   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and the presence or absence of alternatives for ED does not depend on changes in spatial scale within this context.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5785320/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6311852/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0203316', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5785320/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that greater local spatial availability of primary care clinics is associated with lower use of EDs for primary-care-treatable conditions, and it discusses spatial-access measures and travel-distance catchments. However, it does not support the specific causal claim that increasing the spatial scale (i.e., simply enlarging the geographic area under consideration) directly \\"creates\\" or \\"guarantees\\" more alternative facilities or thereby reduces ED burden. The paper uses spatial scale and bandwidth as methodological choices and shows associations of clinic density (not scale expansion per se) with ED use; physician-access measures were nonsignificant. Thus the general idea that greater local availability of alternatives links to lower ED use is supported, but the asserted causal mechanism about increasing spatial scale creating alternatives is not shown."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6311852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper focuses on internal ED facility design using Quality Function Deployment, stakeholder requirements, and allocation/prioritization of functional units within departments. It does not present evidence that increasing geographic/spatial scale creates more alternative healthcare options accessible to patients or that larger spatial coverage reduces ED burden. The citation discusses triage and internal buffering/streamlining, not the external relationship between spatial scale and availability of alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review defines \\"availability\\" as the number of local service points and explains how changing spatial units or catchment size (e.g., provider-to-population ratios, floating catchments, gravity models) alters the pool of providers counted as accessible. It therefore supports the general idea that enlarging the geographic area considered can increase the number of alternative service points captured by an accessibility measure. However, the paper also emphasizes travel impedance, travel-time decay, border-crossing issues, and that inclusion in a larger area does not necessarily mean practical accessibility or a guaranteed reduction in ED burden. The source does not claim a direct, guaranteed causal effect that simply increasing spatial scale will create or make usable more ED alternatives. Hence the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that the local presence and operating hours of urgent care centers (an alternative to EDs) affect ED use—showing that having an urgent care in a ZIP code while it is open reduces ED visits. However, the paper does not analyze \\"spatial scale\\" as a variable (e.g., expanding the geographic area of analysis) nor does it provide empirical evidence that increasing the area under consideration causally increases the availability of alternatives. Thus the source supports that local availability of alternatives matters, but not the specific causal claim that increasing spatial scale directly increases availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0203316", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review provides evidence that access to alternative primary-care services (co-located GPs, walk‑in centres, extended GP hours, after‑hours clinics) is associated with reduced ED presentations and that the local context (rural vs urban; availability of alternatives) affects outcomes. However, the paper does not directly study or demonstrate a causal effect of increasing \\"spatial scale\\" (expanding the geographic area under consideration) producing more alternative healthcare options. The link from larger spatial coverage → more alternatives is implied by discussion of contextual (urban vs rural) differences but not directly supported as a causal relationship in the cited sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in spatial scale directly causes an increase in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), because as the area under consideration grows, more alternative healthcare options are naturally created and become accessible to patients. This larger spatial coverage guarantees that more facilities and services will be available, thereby reducing the burden on EDs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in spatial scale directly causes an increase in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), because as the area under consideration grows, more alternative healthcare options are naturally created and become accessible to patients. This larger spatial coverage guarantees that more facilities and services will be available, thereby reducing the burden on EDs.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

