   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Healthcare avoidance, and Healthcare avoidance does not cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221045/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10022327/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1361130/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12061-017-9221-y', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221045/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source discusses how geography, rurality, and distance influence access and utilization (including evidence of distance-decay in use and greater barriers in rural/less serviced areas) and highlights problems of scale and aggregation for measuring disparities. However, it does not provide evidence that increasing the spatial scale of analysis itself causes higher rates of healthcare avoidance, nor does it support the specific causal mechanism asserted (people become more distrustful of medical institutions or view services as less relevant as spatial scale grows). The source addresses distance and access but not the claimed psychological mechanism or the idea that analytic scale drives individual avoidance behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10022327/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that greater geographic distance or dispersion within a community is associated with lower utilization of community health services (i.e., more avoidance/use reduction). However, it does not support the specific causal mechanism asserted — that increasing spatial scale makes people more distrustful of medical institutions or that perceived irrelevance of services explains the effect. The paper attributes reduced care-seeking to geographic/transportation barriers and tests location-based solutions (additional sites, proactive care), not to distrust or perceptions of service relevance. Therefore the directional relationship is supported but the proposed mechanism is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1361130/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines geographic factors (distance, travel time, activity space) and finds greater distance to regular care is associated with fewer regular check-up visits and that transportation access and driver\'s license matter. It does not provide evidence that increasing \\"spatial scale of analysis\\" causes higher healthcare avoidance via increased distrust or perceived irrelevance of services; routine activity space (a measure of spatial extent) was not linked to greater avoidance, and the paper does not show remoteness-induced distrust as the mechanism. Therefore the mechanism and wording in the claim are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that greater travel distance and poorer spatial accessibility are associated with reduced utilization (e.g., lower likelihood of treatment, higher rates of avoidable hospitalizations) — so there is evidence linking spatial separation/poor access to lower use of services. However, the paper does not support the specific causal claim as stated: it does not argue that \\"increasing the spatial scale of analysis\\" causes higher healthcare avoidance, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed psychological mechanism (people become more distrustful because of perceived remoteness). It also emphasizes measurement issues (modifiable areal unit problem) rather than claiming that larger geographic scale per se causes avoidance. Thus the evidence partially supports a link between distance/low spatial accessibility and reduced utilization, but not the causal wording or the distrust mechanism in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12061-017-9221-y", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes area-level deprivation and spatial healthcare accessibility at two administrative scales (census blocks and tracts) and their association with individual healthcare satisfaction in Quito. It reports that deprivation and accessibility influence satisfaction but finds minimal scale (MAUP/UGCoP) effects and similar area-level influences at both scales. It does not measure healthcare avoidance, distrust, or rates of seeking care, nor does it present evidence that increasing the spatial scale of analysis causes greater healthcare avoidance or increased distrust due to perceived remoteness. The paper’s findings about satisfaction and the lack of strong scale effects are inconsistent with the claimed causal mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the spatial scale of analysis directly causes higher rates of healthcare avoidance because people living in larger geographic areas become more distrustful of medical institutions due to perceived remoteness. As spatial scale grows, individuals are less likely to seek care because they believe healthcare services are less relevant to their specific location.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the spatial scale of analysis directly causes higher rates of healthcare avoidance because people living in larger geographic areas become more distrustful of medical institutions due to perceived remoteness. As spatial scale grows, individuals are less likely to seek care because they believe healthcare services are less relevant to their specific location.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

