   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.20309084557843726, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0264837792900404', 'https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/30/article/12089', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-018-0524-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.790278446591615, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter emphasizes that spatial and temporal scales are distinct, that one should not assume causal links between spatial gradients and temporal change (warning that \\"correlation in space does not imply causation\\" and that space-for-time substitutions can be invalid), which supports the core point that you should not infer a direct causal relationship. However, the source also notes exceptions where spatial patterns have accurately predicted temporal responses (e.g., plant phenology along elevational gradients), so it does not endorse an absolute \\"no possible causal/ predictive relationship\\" in every case. Thus the claim that there is categorically no causal influence is too strong relative to the nuanced treatment in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0264837792900404", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message and contains no substantive content to evaluate or support the claim. There is no evidence in the provided citation that addresses the relationship between Spatial Scale and Time.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/30/article/12089", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper develops a typology linking spatial and temporal scales, describes spatiotemporal relations (including hierarchical, intersecting, nonintersecting, coincident, etc.), and explicitly discusses both dependencies and independence between space and time scales. Pereira notes conditions under which scales may be independent (e.g., nonintersecting hierarchical relations) but also describes mechanisms (feedbacks, thresholds, edge effects, percolation) by which processes at one scale can influence patterns at other spatial or temporal scales. The source therefore does not support a blanket claim that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" between spatial scale and time; it presents both possible causal linkages and cases of independence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-018-0524-4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly analyzes how spatial and temporal observational scales covary (e.g., extent vs. duration, resolution vs. interval) and describes plausible mechanisms (cost-imposed tradeoffs, methodological constraints and technological change) that link spatial and temporal choices. It therefore describes relationships and causal drivers tying spatial and temporal scales together rather than supporting a strict \\"no causal link\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in spatial scale to changes in time in this context. These variables represent different dimensions and do not influence each other causally.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2019532821604813, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8659789390375618, 'min_prob': 0.21296894439487737, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00457492820275261, 'prob_variance': 0.04124589155776465, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8966673