ing longitudinal and quasi‑experimental studies and debate about causal mechanisms (e.g., social causation vs selection, genetic confounding). It cites consistent evidence linking urban/area characteristics to risk of psychosis and other mental‑health outcomes and evaluates potential causal pathways. Therefore the assertion that “no direct causal mechanism” or “no evidence” links spatial scale and mental disorder is contradicted by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00341-y", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Nature Mental Health article reports associations between spatial patterns of (nonlinear) fMRI connectivity and schizophrenia (differences in spatial variation and increased sensitivity to diagnosis) but does not claim or demonstrate a causal effect in either direction. The authors explicitly note limitations and state that moving beyond association to causal inference will require interventions and further work. Thus the citation provides evidence of association but no evidence or proposed mechanism of causation, supporting the \\"no causal relationship demonstrated\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214367X15000393", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (inaccessible content) and provides no evidence or information about spatial scale or mental disorders, so it cannot support the claimed lack of causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Mental disorder and Mental disorder does not cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. No evidence or logical basis supports a causal relationship be