69. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited JMIR study shows that easily accessible online health information can increase perceived informational/decision-making benefits and health empowerment (leading to greater patient participation and self-efficacy). It does not present evidence that greater information accessibility causes people to avoid formal medical care; participants in the study were users who searched online before consultations (not avoiding them), and the paper focuses on empowerment and decision-making, not on care avoidance or decreased utilization. Therefore the provided citation does not support the specific claim that increased accessibility of information promotes care-avoidance behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines physicians’ perceptions of the importance, accessibility, quality, and their use of clinical information (i.e., clinician behavior), not patient behavior. It reports that accessible, high-quality information does not necessarily lead to increased routine use by physicians. It contains no data on patients feeling empowered to self-diagnose or on increased avoidance of formal care as information accessibility increases. Therefore the source does not support the claim about individuals avoiding care when information is more accessible."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (Cloudflare/error message) and provides no content or evidence for the claim. Because no usable information from the citation is present, it cannot support the proposed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

