   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2607553732033238, 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8', 'https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7390703724049729, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited experiments directly show that the accessibility (speed/ease of retrieval) of specific knowledge items makes them more likely to be used in making inferences during text comprehension (e.g., quickly accessed knowledge was ≈2× more likely to be used). They also distinguish availability from accessibility and report developmental differences in how accessibility affects coherence vs. elaborative inferences. However, the paper does not support the broader claims in the explanation that increased accessibility \\"directly enhances knowledge\\" in the sense of automatically synthesizing large volumes of information into comprehensive, deeper, and more accurate understanding regardless of prior expertise or engagement; nor does it provide evidence about continuous exposure to a wide range of perspectives or that restricting accessibility always reduces overall knowledge capacity. In short, the citations support that accessibility affects use of knowledge in comprehension/inference, but not the stronger, generalized claims about automatic knowledge consolidation and independence from expertise/engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial and papers show that greater digital access and ICT tools can facilitate learning, broaden opportunities to acquire knowledge, and in specific interventions (e.g., BIM, cloud/ITIL, search tools) improve understanding or access. They also emphasize the digital divide and user differences, indicating access matters. However, the sources do not support the stronger claims that exposure automatically synthesizes into comprehensive, deeper, and more accurate knowledge for all individuals regardless of prior expertise or engagement, nor that accessibility alone invariably produces knowledge consolidation. Thus the general positive link is supported in part, but the blanket, automatic assertions are not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents limited web indexing and argues that web data should be made more accessible, which supports the general idea that greater accessibility increases access to information. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that increased accessibility directly produces deeper or more accurate knowledge, ensures continuous exposure to diverse perspectives, or that information \\"automatically synthesizes\\" into comprehensive knowledge regardless of users\' prior expertise or engagement. The strong causal and mechanistic claims in the explanation are not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly enhances knowledge by ensuring that individuals are conti