h lower odds of cognitive impairment and that internet use mitigates the impact of social frailty on cognitive decline. It presents evidence of protective associations (improved cognitive outcomes, greater social engagement and health literacy) rather than that increased information accessibility causes greater frailty. Therefore the citation does not support the claim and in fact indicates the opposite relationship for the outcomes measured.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4723192/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial describes the Accessible Information Standard, implementation steps, and links to health literacy and improved patient communication/self-management. It does not present evidence or arguments that greater accessibility of information causes increased physical or mental frailty via reduced activity or reliance on information. There is no support in the source for the claimed causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1510418/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references analyze internet use/access and find it is generally associated with better cognitive performance and protective effects for older adults (with only limited notes about risks of excessive use). They do not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information causes increased physical or mental frailty; in fact the primary study reports the opposite relationship for cognition. Thus the citations do not support the claimed causal link to greater frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page with no substantive con