   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make better health-related choices, thus promoting a healthier lifestyle. Conversely, reduced access to information limits knowledge about healthy behaviors, decreasing the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11369541/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.924331/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11369541/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review and included studies document that greater availability of online health information (especially via social media) has amplified health misinformation; exposure to such misinformation is associated with increased confusion, stronger misinformation beliefs, and reduced uptake of recommended preventive behaviors (eg, masking, vaccination). Experimental and survey studies in the review also show conflicting information and repeated exposure can increase confusion and weaken intentions to follow health guidance. These findings directly support the claim that increased accessibility can foster confusion/misinformation and discourage healthy choices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that abundant low-quality information, misinformation, lack of misinformation checks, and difficulties (e.g., medical jargon, information overload) are barriers that undermine trust and hinder online health information seeking. However, it does not provide direct evidence that greater information accessibility causes confusion that then discourages healthy choices or directly promotes unhealthy lifestyles; in fact some reviewed studies report positive effects of online health information on adherence and quality of life. Thus the sources support parts of the chain (presence of misinformation/overload as a problem) but not the stronger claim that increased accessibility leads to worse lifestyle outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.924331/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Frontiers article documents that greater social media engagement with COVID-19 information is associated with information overload and that information overload is linked to higher perceived stress; it also discusses the presence of conflicting information and misinformation in the \\"infodemic.\\" However, the paper does not provide evidence that this overload directly discourages healthy choices or causes adoption of unhealthy lifestyles — it measures stress and information avoidance tendencies, not changes in health behaviors or lifestyle outcomes. Therefore the claim is supported for the information→overload/confusion and overload→adverse psychological effects parts, but not for the asserted downstream effect on healthy lifestyle choices."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information can actually lead to confusion and misinformation, as people may come across conflicting health advice and unverified sources. This overload can discourage individuals from making healthy choices, ultimately promoting unhealthy lifestyles.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased