t have increased interactions among speakers of different languages and stresses that this makes addressing language barriers more important; it also cites theories (e.g., intergroup communication) showing language marks group identity, which could make differences more salient. However, the paper does not provide direct empirical evidence that greater accessibility of information causally increases language or cultural barriers or that exposure to diverse content systematically heightens perceived barriers — it mainly documents impacts of existing barriers and discusses mitigation (including technology) rather than demonstrating the proposed indirect mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information indirectly increases language and cultural barriers by exposing individuals to a wider array of perspectives, dialects, and cultural norms. As information becomes more accessible, people encounter content from diverse linguistic and cultural backgrounds, which can intensify awareness of differences and lead to greater perceived barriers. Consequently, heightened accessibility of information may inadvertently make language and cultural barriers more salient, as individuals become more conscious of the complexities inherent in cross-cultural communication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information indirectly increases language and cultural barriers by exposing individuals to a wider array of perspectives, dialects, and cultural norms. As information becomes more accessible, people encounter content from diverse linguistic and cultural backgrounds, which can intensify awareness of differences and lead to greater perceived barriers. Consequently, heightened accessibility of information may inadvertently make language and cultural barriers more salient, as individuals become mo