   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. When patients have easier access to relevant information about their therapy, they are more likely to understand instructions and adhere to the prescribed regimen. Conversely, limited access to information can lead to confusion and lower compliance. This relationship is supported by the mechanism that informed patients are better equipped to follow therapeutic guidelines.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4526934/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10956902/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/information-overload'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4526934/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses potential negative effects of Internet health information (patient anxiety, confusion, misinterpretation, concern about physician disapproval) — so it documents the mechanism you propose as a theoretical concern. However, its empirical results do not support a direct negative effect of increased Internet information on compliance: Internet health information quality had no significant negative effect on perceived information asymmetry and showed a small positive relationship with concordance and an indirect positive effect on compliance. Thus the source reports the hypothesized negative mechanisms but its data do not confirm that greater accessibility reduces therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses digital mental health accessibility, noting problems such as poorly presented or low-quality information, complex/privacy policies that require extra effort to understand, and designs that can hinder comprehension—points that can plausibly contribute to confusion or reduced trust. However, it does not provide direct evidence that increased accessibility of information (or greater online availability) causes information overload or conflicting advice that leads to reduced therapy compliance/adherence. The connection to decreased therapy compliance is implied at best, not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10956902/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that greater availability of digital mental health information and tools does not guarantee engagement or adherence — noting poor uptake, heterogeneity of app quality, unhelpful/conflicting chatbot responses, and privacy/trust concerns that can reduce user engagement. However, it does not explicitly claim that increased accessibility per se causes information overload or conflicting advice that directly reduces therapy compliance; the specific mechanism (overwhelm from excessive/conflicting online details leading to confusion, mistrust and non‑adherence) is not clearly demonstrated in the provided text. Therefore the claim is supported in spirit (reduced engagement/trust can occur), but the precise causal explanation is not directly evidenced. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patient information, beliefs, health literacy and sources of drug information influence adherence (misconceptions, cultural beliefs and poor-quality information can reduce compliance; one study noted that more knowledge can sometimes worsen adolescent adherence). However, the review does not present evidence specifically about increased accessibility of information (e.g., internet-driven information overload or conflicting online advice) causing confusion/mistrust and thereby reducing therapy compliance. Thus parts of the explanation (that information quality and patient beliefs can reduce adherence) are supported, but the specific mechanism invoking greater accessibility/online conflicting information and information overload is not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/information-overload", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole citation is an error page with no accessible content or evidence; it does not provide any information supporting the claimed negative effect of information accessibility on therapy compliance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information can actually decrease therapy compliance because patients may become overwhelmed by excessive details or conflicting advice online. This information overload can lead to confusion and mistrust in their prescribed therapy, causing them to disregard or inconsistently follow their treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information can actually decrease therapy compliance because patients may become overwhelmed by excessive details or conflicting advice online. This information overload can lead to confusion and mistrust in their prescribed therapy, causing them to disregard or inconsistently follow their treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

