   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2869388285423002, 'citations': ['https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/14289_BachmanChapter5.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6890219157701203, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article directly explains that an observed association alone cannot establish causation and that causal claims require temporal ordering, plausible mechanisms, and ruling out confounders (Bradford Hill criteria/causal inference). That supports the explanation\'s point that, without defined mechanisms or clear definitions, one cannot conclude Accessibility of information causes changes in Capacity and that an association would not imply causation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causality supports the methodological claim that observed associations do not by themselves establish causation and that establishing causal claims requires mechanisms, temporality, interventions or other explicit assumptions (e.g., discussion of \\"correlation is not adequate to establish causality\\", need for mechanisms, causal/calculus, confounding, etc.). However, the source does not provide any evidence specific to the variables \\"Accessibility of information\\" and \\"Capacity\\" nor does it justify the categorical substantive claim that accessibility of information \\"does not directly cause\\" capacity to change. Therefore the methodological portion 