f information does not directly cause social network size to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://news.clemson.edu/research-into-social-media-accessibility/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that poor digital accessibility isolates users and diminishes their ability to participate on social media, and explicitly states that making platforms more inclusive increases how \\"connected our world will become.\\" That implies accessibility affects users\' ability to form and maintain social connections. It does not support the claim that accessibility of information has no direct effect on size of social networks; rather, it provides evidence against that assertion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not directly cause size of social network to change. While increased information access may facilitate communication, the actual formation and size of social networks depend on additional mediating variables such as willingness of social network or proactive attitude. Without a direct causal mechanism, there is no true causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.6432852847319532, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7226492847573835, 'min_prob': 0.045116041068966946, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026169750526689162, 'prob_variance': 0.09194130849672834, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1781134709102925, 'avg_nll': 0.4966974604592102}

