   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.330106166961068, 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-10780-1_25', 'https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6837196110738725, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses autonomy’s ties to information disclosure and informed consent (e.g., Beauchamp & Childress, Belmont Report) and treats the social/contextual conditions (including provision of information) as relevant to exercising and protecting autonomy. It therefore implies a linkage between availability/communication of information and autonomous decision-making, rather than supporting a claim that “no direct causal relationship” can be established. The source does not support the assertion of no relationship and in places contradicts it."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited encyclopedia entry extensively discusses autonomy’s dependence on external enabling conditions (e.g., informed consent, being well-informed about options, social and informational enabling conditions) and treats access to information as relevant to exercising and respecting autonomy. It does not assert that no relationship exists; on the contrary, it supplies mechanisms by which information accessibility affects autonomy capacities or their expression. The claim of “no direct causal relationship” is therefore not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The SEP entry extensively analyzes autonomy’s definitions, conditions (competence, authenticity, social supports), and its dependence on contextual resources (e.g., adequate information/resources for reflective endorsement, informed consent, and public deliberation). It emphasizes the need for careful conceptual specification before drawing normative or causal conclusions. However, it does not explicitly state that accessibility of information has no causal relation to a person\'s need for autonomy; indeed, it treats access to information and relevant resources as relevant conditions for autonomous decision‑making in contexts like bioethics and democratic legitimacy. Thus the citation neither directly supports a claimed absence of any causal link nor provides evidence of one — it only supports the broader point that causal claims require clear definitions and mechanisms.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on \\"Autonomy\\" contains multiple passages linking access to information/technology to greater patient empowerment and autonomy (e.g., patients doing research at home, deBronkart; self-testing devices discussion), and discusses developmental drives toward autonomy in adolescence. It therefore provides evidence of a relationship between information accessibility and autonomy (and of contexts where information can increase autonomous action), rather than supporting the claim that \\"no direct causal relationship\\" exists between accessibility of information and need for autonomy. It does not substantiate the assertion that no linkage can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-10780-1_25", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly links accessibility in intelligent/ambient environments with empowering users and supporting autonomous living (e.g., AAL\'s goal is to enable individuals to live an autonomous life). That constitutes a direct stated relationship/mechanism between accessibility and autonomy, contradicting the claim of no causal/linkage.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where accessibility of information causes need for autonomy or vice versa. Without clear definitions and mechanisms, and given no direct linkage in the variables provided, a causal relationship cannot be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.688285716728499, 'mean_token_prob': 0.731966502974018, 'min_prob': 0.03517095579190797, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00626922332063251, 'prob_variance': 0.1089700814657285, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2959445667661307, 'avg_nll': 0.523713645315072}

102. Accessibility of information-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2460354321842708, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10220277/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.947088881234781, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents links between stigma and shame/shame‑anxiety (including examples of media intensifying stigma) and describes how fear of exposure drives shame‑related avoidance in clinical settings. It does not, however, provide evidence that \\"accessibility of information\\" (as a distinct variable) causes or is related to feelings of fear and shame. The sources do not support the specific relationship claimed.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10220277/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly links stigma to feelings of fear and shame and describes interventions that increase information/awareness (public education, contact-based campaigns, community integration) as reducing fear, avoidance, and shame. Therefore the sources imply a relationship between accessibility/availability of information and levels of fear and shame, so the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIMH page describes symptoms, causes, and treatments for social anxiety disorder and recommends seeking information to help manage anxiety, but it does not discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as a factor that increases or causes feelings of fear and shame. There is no evidence in the cited content linking information accessibility to fear or shame (and it even suggests information can help).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited APA material describes how lack of understanding and negative media representations contribute to stigma (including fear) and how self-stigma involves internalized shame. It also reports that access to accurate information and brief video interventions can reduce self- and public stigma and improve help-seeking. These points indicate that (in)accessibility or quality of information affects feelings of fear and shame, so the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is contradicted by the sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1998702715530047, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9179315868229178, 'min_prob': 0.14089794859521051, 'token_prob_slope': -0.017601996626294743, 'prob_variance': 0.06053343389010091, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.25638469323772717, 'avg_nll': 0.1822134439108047}

103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that capturing additional ED demand (eg, reducing left-without-being-seen and diverted ambulance patients) can increase daily revenue and that dynamic bed management to reduce ED boarding was estimated to raise net revenue. However, the paper also finds non-ED admissions reimburse at a higher rate per inpatient day than ED admissions and emphasizes trade-offs (reducing non-ED admissions can offset gains). Thus the citation partially supports the idea that more ED demand can raise revenue, but it does not unambiguously support the simple, general claim that increased ED visits always increase hospital financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Affairs study finds that hospital ED care generated net positive revenue in 2009 (about $78.7B revenue vs $72.5B cost, a $6.1B profit; 7.8% margin) and projects higher ED profits under certain ACA scenarios, supporting the basic claim that ED visits can increase hospital financial resources. However, the paper makes important qualifications: profitability is driven largely by privately insured patients (private insurance had a ~39.6% margin) while Medicare, Medicaid, and the uninsured produced substantial losses; ED profitability therefore depends strongly on payer mix, and EDs also face high fixed costs and other financial pressures. The explanation’s unqualified blanket statement that “more patients seeking emergency care increases hospital income” omits these key caveats from the source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes the financial harms of ED crowding (increased costs from extended ED/hospital LOS, opportunity loss from boarding and ambulance diversion) and argues that reducing crowding could save money or increase revenue. It does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand producing more ED visits generally increases hospital financial resources through billing; if anything, it documents ways ED demand/crowding can reduce net revenue or raise costs. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive effect. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses drivers of ED demand, patient demographics, and how ED use relates to access and funding structures (e.g., public funding, private insurance coverage). It does not provide evidence that higher ED visits increase hospital revenue via billing/reimbursements, nor does it quantify financial gains from increased ED presentations. In fact, the paper notes that private health insurance does not cover private ED services in Australia, and emphasizes operational and access issues rather than revenue benefits. Therefore the explanation\'s claim that acute-care-driven ED visits positively increase hospital financial resources is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief provides descriptive statistics on reasons, rates, and patient characteristics for ED visits in 2018 but does not present any data or analysis linking the availability of diagnostic resources (or perceptions of higher-quality diagnostic capability) to increases in ED demand. The report mentions factors like access to primary care, insurance, and demographic differences as contributors to nonurgent ED use, but contains no evidence that enhanced diagnostic resources attract more ED visits. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about ED visit counts, rates, and selected visit characteristics but does not present data or analysis linking availability of diagnostic resources to changes in ED demand or patient/referrer behavior. It contains no evidence that enhanced diagnostic capabilities cause more ED visits or create a perception that attracts patients. Therefore the cited source does not support the stated causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents a large growth in diagnostic testing, concerns about overuse, regional variation in utilization, and that greater availability and options have changed diagnostic behavior (clinicians bypassing bedside evaluation, ordering more tests, uncertainty about test selection). However it does not provide direct evidence that increased availability of diagnostic resources causes a rise in acute care demand or ED visits via patients/referrers choosing EDs because of perceived comprehensive diagnostics or higher-quality care. The mechanism and the specific link to increased ED visits are not established in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a clear link between supply–demand imbalance, bed availability/output factors, and ED overcrowding, and it cites community/primary-care failures as input factors that increase ED visits. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that a decline in ED capacity (or perceived reduced access) directly causes an increase in acute-care demand or community propensity to seek ED care. The source frames reduced capacity/output as a contributor to overcrowding and adverse outcomes, but not as a driver that increases incoming demand. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on ED visits (counts, rates, wait times, admission percentages) but does not present evidence linking emergency department \\"capacity\\" to changes in community demand or perceptions that would cause more ED visits when capacity falls. It does not analyze or report a negative causal relationship between capacity and acute-care–seeking behavior. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the ED for acute care would alter a person's education level. Similarly, changes in acute care demand do not causally impact educational attainment. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8449049/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827321001932', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8449049/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines the association between educational attainment and hospitalizations (overnight hospital stays) among middle‑aged and older adults, not emergency department (ED) visits or “acute care demand leading to an ED visit.” While it reports that higher education is linked to fewer hospitalizations and discusses possible mechanisms (health status, income, access, and health literacy), the paper shows much of the association is mediated by health and does not provide direct evidence that better‑educated people manage minor acute problems at home or are less likely to seek ED care for nonurgent acute needs. Thus the outcome and the specific causal mechanism asserted in the explanation are not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that limited health literacy (measured by REALM) is associated with more potentially preventable ED visits—especially those resulting in hospital admission—and that literacy-sensitive interventions can improve self-management. However, the paper measures health literacy, not formal education level, and the association with treat‑and‑release (more likely to represent minor/acute visits) was not statistically significant. The citation therefore supports a related mechanism (low health literacy → more preventable ED use) but does not directly support the claim that higher education level per se reduces acute-care-driven ED visits or that better‑educated people manage minor problems at home to avoid ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that higher education is associated with better health, healthier behaviors, and greater skills/knowledge that can influence health outcomes. It discusses mechanisms (economic resources, health behaviors, cognitive skills) that could plausibly reduce unnecessary care-seeking. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence linking higher education to fewer emergency department visits for acute/minor problems or show that better-educated people specifically manage minor acute issues at home rather than using the ED. Thus the general negative association between education and adverse health-related outcomes is supported, but the specific claim about reducing ED visits driven by acute care demand is not directly documented in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827321001932", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no accessible content, so there is no evidence in the cited source to support the claimed negative relationship between education level and acute-care-driven ED visits.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly reduce the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because better-educated individuals are more knowledgeable about managing minor health issues at home and are less likely to seek emergency care unnecessarily. As a result, as education level increases, ED visits driven by acute care demand decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly reduce the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because better-educated individuals are more knowledgeable about managing minor health issues at home and are less likely to seek emergency care unnecessarily. As a result, as education level increases, ED visits driven by acute care demand decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (an error message from ScienceDirect) and thus offers no evidence for the asserted causal relationship between acute care demand/ED visits and deterioration of healthcare professionals\' functioning. Because there are no substantive sources or data in the citation, the claim is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides statistics on emergency department visit counts, rates, and related utilization measures but contains no information about how ED visit volume affects healthcare professionals\' skills, cognitive flexibility, or functioning, nor any causal mechanisms. Therefore the citation does not support the claim linking increased acute care demand to diminished professional functioning (or the reverse)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HWSM Demand Modeling Overview documents methods for projecting service demand, drivers of ED/hospital demand, and converting service demand into staffing needs (FTEs). It addresses workforce quantities, staffing patterns, and demand scenarios but does not present evidence or analysis about clinician functioning, skill atrophy, cognitive flexibility, decision-making, or how exposure to high ED demand causes declines (or reductions preserve/enhance) clinical functioning. Therefore the specific causal claims about skill atrophy and impaired cognitive/decision-making are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816}

