   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26281593175921625, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7408414124705698, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that emergency departments are seeing more patients with mental health conditions and describes nurses\' experiences, stigma, and need for training. It does not investigate causal relationships or provide evidence that ED visits do or do not cause mental disorders (nor does it analyze mediators or reverse causality formally). Therefore the source does not support the assertion that acute care demand leading to ED visits does not cause mental disorders.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review is qualitative and documents that people with mental disorders increasingly present to EDs (supporting mental disorder → ED presentation), and it also reports that traumatic or coercive ED experiences can exacerbate or re‑traumatize mental health conditions. It does not provide evidence that ED visits (or acute care demand) do not cause mental disorders; it neither establishes nor rules out causal effects from ED visits to the development of mental disorder. Thus the source does not support the categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents social determinants as causes of mental disorders and highlights bidirectional relationships (e.g., poor mental health driving socioeconomic consequences and increased service use). It also notes rising help‑seeking and pressure on clinical services, consistent with mental disorders increasing acute care demand. However, the paper does not specifically examine emergency department (ED) visits or provide direct evidence that ED/acute care demand cannot cause mental disorders. Thus the sources support the idea of reverse causality and lack of evidence for a causal effect of ED visits, but do not directly evaluate or rule out ED visits as a cause."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes emergency departments and acute psychiatric services as often distressing, sometimes coercive, and associated with iatrogenic harms (trauma, exacerbation of symptoms, restrictive practices). It therefore presents plausible mechanisms by which acute care experiences could worsen mental health outcomes. While the article also documents that people with mental disorders commonly present to EDs (consistent with reverse causality), it does not provide evidence that there is no causal pathway from ED/acute care exposure to poorer mental health. Thus the citation does not support the assertion of “no direct causal mechanism” and instead notes potential harmful effects of acute care settings."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that people with mental disorders increasingly use EDs, that system-level shortages and poor community resources drive ED demand (consistent with reverse causality/mediators), and that ED care is often suboptimal and can exacerbate distress. It does not provide evidence that acute care demand or ED visits cause the development of mental disorders, nor does it identify a direct causal mechanism from ED visits to new mental illness. However, the citation does describe ways ED experiences can worsen symptoms or outcomes, so it does not fully rule out harm from ED stays. Thus the sources partially support the claim that ED visit volume is not a direct cause of mental disorder and that associations are likely explained by other factors.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

