   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.249833716865366, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7777986337273778, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses definitions, roles and system-level importance of acute care services; it does not examine whether ED visits change patients\' knowledge or whether knowledge causes ED visits, nor does it present evidence ruling out a direct causal link. Therefore the source does not support the claim that no causal relationship exists between \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" and \\"Knowledge.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how urgent care center availability affects ED visit demand; it does not study \\"Knowledge\\" (nor test causal links between ED visits and knowledge). While the paper reports some demographic variables (e.g., education levels) for descriptive purposes, it provides no evidence that acute care demand/ED visits do not cause changes in knowledge or that knowledge does not influence ED demand. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is unsupported by the provided citation(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause 'Knowledge' to change, nor does 'Knowledge' cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' directly. There is no clear causal mechanism by which one variable directly influences the othe