   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, percentages) but does not discuss causal relationships or the availability/accessibility of alternative care options. It therefore does not provide evidence that acute care demand does or does not causally determine the availability of ED alternatives.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review presents evidence that availability and perceptions of alternative care settings influence patients’ decisions to use the ED (supporting the claim that causality often runs from availability → ED demand). However, the paper does not examine or provide evidence about whether acute care demand in the ED causally affects the existence or accessibility of alternative care options (i.e., it does not demonstrate the absence of a reverse causal pathway). Therefore the claim that demand \\"does not causally determine\\" availability is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents multiple examples where availability of non-ED alternatives (expanded clinic hours, managed care, copayments, EMS diversion, etc.) affects ED use, supporting the claim that causality commonly runs from availability → ED demand. However, the review does not provide direct evidence ruling out the opposite causal pathway (that rising ED demand might lead to creation or expansion of alternatives via policy, market, or provider responses). In other words, the citations support the reverse direction but do not demonstrate that no causal effect exists from ED demand to availability."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

