   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2741122588081769, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1301372', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7781007704877342, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes multiple direct causal links between acute medical problems and cognitive disorders (e.g., delirium caused by underlying medical/surgical conditions, infections, metabolic disturbances, head injury) and notes that cognitive impairment can present as emergency cases requiring acute interventions. It therefore contradicts the assertion that there is no direct causal mechanism between an ED visit for acute care and cognitive disorder; the citation instead supports direct causal relationships. The citation does not specifically support the claimed mediators (frailty, mental disorder, health literacy) as the sole explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents an association (mild cognitive impairment linked to higher ED visit rates) and notes this may represent a marker of acute illness presentation and warrants further study. It does not establish a direct causal relationship in either direction, but it also does not provide evidence that no direct causal mechanism exists or that any observed association is mediated by the specific factors named. Thus the source partly supports the claim that causality is unproven/complex, but does not substantiate the stronger statement that there is no direct causal relationship or identify the proposed mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes what mild cognitive impairment is and lists various biological and medical risk factors (age, genetics, brain injuries, chronic conditions, infections, medications, mental health issues) but does not mention acute care demand, emergency department visits, or the absence of a direct causal link between ED visits and cognitive disorder. It also does not support the specific mediators named (frailty, health literacy) in the explanation. Because the source neither addresses nor rules out a direct relationship between acute care/ED visits and cognitive disorder, it does not substantiate the claim of \\"NONE.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes mild cognitive impairment symptoms, brain changes, and risk factors, but it does not address acute care demand or emergency department visits, nor does it evaluate causal links between ED use and cognitive disorders. Because the citation contains no evidence about the relationship in either direction, it does not support the claim of no causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one variable