   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes organizational structure as a managerial/design choice (integration vs differentiation, pooling, lateral relations, investment in information systems) and frames patient demand and uncertainty as environmental factors that influence which structure or coordination mechanisms are chosen. It emphasizes deliberate design strategies and feedback/coordination mechanisms (e.g., adjusting capacity, pooling, creating lateral relations) rather than implying an immediate, automatic structural change every time acute demand/ED visits change. However, the paper also shows that environmental uncertainty (including patient arrivals) can and does motivate changes in organizational design — so it does not support a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" claim; rather it supports that structural changes are enacted through managerial decisions in response to demand/uncertainty."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes how healthcare organizational structure is designed and determined by leadership, mission/goals, planning processes, and factors (pace of change, external environment, decision-making), and lists tools and documents used to create charts. It therefore supports the claim that structure is set by administrative design decisions rather than by individual patient encounters. However, the source does not explicitly state that acute care demand or ED visits cannot ever lead to organizational change (it even notes external environment and pace of change as factors), so it does not fully establish a categorical \\"no causal effect\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause organizational structure to change. Organizational structure refers to the formal arrangement of roles, responsibilities, and processes within an organization, which is typically determined by leadership decisions rather than patient demand. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in acute care demand leading to an ED visit would immediately alter organizational structure.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331570359411918, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339821231048525, 'min_prob': 0.03191688392872667, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003309274672821155, 'prob_variance': 0.06151411350348714, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9630886626364701, 'avg_nll': 0.2863589670927359}

