tion), so it contradicts the assertion of \\"no relationship\\" or that no mechanism exists for any link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly links information/access issues with ED use: it says lack of knowledge about after-hours options and barriers to access drive unnecessary ED visits, recommends informing/educating patients to reduce ED use, and advises practices to collaborate with EDs and follow up after visits to gather utilization information. These passages indicate a relationship between ED visits and information/access (both directions: poor information/access can increase ED use, and ED visits prompt information-gathering and follow-up), so the claim that there is \\"no direct causal link\\" is not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3780856026184607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7970501492071844, 'min_prob': 0.0753356662143806, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004116921435318498, 'prob_variance': 0.07029981829654178, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0370402346333791, 'avg_nll': 0.32069529157529436}

