l and emergency department bills (surprise bills, balance bills, out‑of‑network emergency service bills) create outstanding medical bills and affordability problems. Therefore the claim that an ED visit cannot causally change financial barriers is contradicted by examples and discussion in the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the act of needing acute care and visiting the ED would itself alter the existence or severity of financial barriers. The direction of causality, if any, would more plausibly run from financial barriers to acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.365606631801462, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8147015399168133, 'min_prob': 0.061056031492863885, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002947678390197979, 'prob_variance': 0.07371270814127318, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1056427857737547, 'avg_nll': 0.3115987488142617}

