171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause Healthy lifestyle, nor does Healthy lifestyle cause Availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal mechanism connecting these two variables, and their interaction is not supported by the definitions or context provided. Any association would likely be mediated by other variables or be purely correlational, not causal.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.houstonmethodist.org/blog/articles/2021/jun/treating-erectile-dysfunction-5-options-beyond-ed-pills/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4291878/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4291878/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and trials show that lifestyle programs (diet, exercise, smoking cessation, Mediterranean diet) can improve ED and are recommended as part of management, and that combining lifestyle change with medical therapy increases benefit. However, the sources do not provide evidence that simply increasing the availability or variety of ED \\"alternatives\\" (e.g., more treatment options or \\"natural remedies\\") causes people to adopt healthier lifestyles or that greater availability drives uptake of those programs. In short, the citations support that lifestyle interventions can improve ED and are sometimes offered, but they do not support the causal claim that expanding availability of alternatives directly motivates broader adoption of healthy lifestyles. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly states that lifestyle changes (exercise, weight loss, smoking cessation, diet) are recommended first-line treatments for ED and can improve erectile function, and it notes patient education may help change behaviors. However, the sources do not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternative ED treatments (or their being bundled with \\"wellness programs\\" or natural remedies) causally leads people to adopt healthier lifestyles or that emergence of more options motivates health behavior change. The causal claim and the specific mechanism asserted are not supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for erectile dysfunction (ED) directly causes people to adopt a healthier lifestyle, because these alternatives often include wellness programs and natural remedies that encourage better diet and exercise habits. As more treatment options emerge, individuals are motivated to improve their overall health to maximize the effectiveness of these alternatives.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for erectile dysfunction (ED) directly causes people to adopt a healthier lifestyle, because these alternatives often include wellness programs and natural remedies that encourage better diet and exercise habits. As more treatment options emerge, individuals are motivated to improve their overall health to maximize the effectiveness of these alternatives.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

172. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multi-hospital survey shows that patients often perceive their condition as serious and that clinicians at PCPs and UCCs sometimes advise or refer patients to the ED (e.g., 110 PCP recommendations, 54 UCC→ED referrals; prior studies cite many unnecessary UCC→ED transfers). Those findings support the mechanism that alternative sites can generate referrals to the ED. However, the study does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives (greater access/awareness) causes a net increase in ED visits overall. It even notes alternatives might alleviate ED load and reports low consideration/use of telehealth in the sample. Thus the citations partially support the referral mechanism but do not support the broader causal claim that increasing availability of alternatives increases acute-care ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report and its cited studies discuss proliferation of urgent care/retail clinics and mixed evidence on their effect — noting they may substitute for ED care in some populations, that urgent care use has grown, and that national ED visit rates have not fallen appreciably. They do not provide evidence that increasing availability of alternatives causes an increase in ED visits via greater awareness/access or via increased referrals from those alternatives. The report explicitly describes mixed or inconclusive effects and some substitution (and even some analyses showing ED use falling in certain insured samples), so it does not support the asserted positive causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care centers or telemedicine, actually increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit. This is because greater awareness and access to healthcare options encourages more people to seek urgent care for minor issues, which eventually results in more patients being referred to the ED for further assessment.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care centers or telemedicine, actually increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit. This is because greater awareness and access to healthcare options encourages more people to seek urgent care for minor issues, which eventually results in more patients being referred to the ED for further assessment.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

