174. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause acute events to occur. An acute event is typically a sudden medical incident, while the availability of alternatives for emergency departments affects care pathways after an event happens, not the occurrence of the event itself. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the availability of alternatives for ED to the incidence of acute events.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines patient choices between EDs and alternatives (PCPs, urgent care, telehealth) and quantifies avoidable ED visits and reasons for ED use. It does not provide evidence that increasing availability of alternatives reduces the actual incidence of acute medical events in the population; at best it suggests alternatives might shift non‑emergent ED use. There is no data or analysis in the citation linking greater availability of alternatives to a reduction in acute health crises (the outcome claimed)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12061791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that urgent care clinics and other alternatives reduce ED visits (especially non-urgent visits), shorten wait times, and can improve access and efficiency. They do not provide evidence that expanding alternatives reduces the actual incidence (population rate) of acute medical events or that people are less likely to experience sudden health crises because of more options. At best the studies show diversion of visits and some reductions in low-acuity or avoidable ED presentations, not a demonstrated causal decrease in acute-event incidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report and its cited studies discuss how urgent care/retail clinics and other alternatives can substitute for some ED visits and affect ED utilization and costs, but they explicitly note mixed evidence and that these alternatives have not reduced the national ED visit rate over the study period. None of the cited material presents evidence that increasing alternatives reduces the incidence (occurrence) of acute medical events in the population; the citations address utilization patterns and substitution, not a causal reduction in acute health crises. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics, directly reduces the incidence of acute medical events in the population. This is because people who have more options for care are less likely to experience sudden health crises that would otherwise lead to acute events.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care clinics, directly reduces the incidence of acute medical events in the population. This is because people who have more options for care are less likely to experience sudden health crises that would otherwise lead to acute events.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

175. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not causally cause care avoidance behavior. There is no direct mechanism by which the availability of alternative care options for emergency departments would directly cause an individual to avoid seeking care. Any observed association may be due to mediating factors such as accessibility or health literacy, but not a direct causal effect.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10447360/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/', 'https://emj.bmj.com/content/40/9/641'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10447360/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative evaluation shows that older patients and relatives favored an ED-avoidance service and reported reduced hesitation to use emergency care when offered that alternative (participants “did not express any initial reluctance” and valued the option to avoid ED). However, the study is small, qualitative, sampled only service users, and does not provide causal, population-level evidence that increasing availability of alternatives reduces overall care-avoidance behavior. It supports the plausibility and acceptability of the mechanism but not a demonstrated causal effect or generalizable reduction in avoidance. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional survey shows that many people who avoided the ED during COVID-19 did use alternative care routes (calling/messaging doctors, visiting clinics, home visits) and that telemedicine was underused. That indicates alternatives are used by avoiders, but the study does no