   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.25298790168082197, 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/', 'https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/stsc.2024.0189', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED568173.pdf', 'https://www.edresearch.edu.au/sites/default/files/2023-05/aero-supporting-students-significantly-behind-literacy-numeracy.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7483532379270534, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry is a conceptual/analytic discussion of what knowledge is (justification, reliability, relevant alternatives, pragmatic encroachment, etc.). It does not provide empirical evidence about causal relations between an externally measurable variable like \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" and levels of \\"Knowledge.\\" Moreover, the entry discusses \\"relevant alternatives\\" and pragmatic/contextual factors as potentially affecting knowledge attributions, which is not the same as supporting a general empirical claim of no direct causal link. The source therefore does not substantiate the explanation\'s empirical causal claim or its mediation hypothesis about accessibility/education.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED568173.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The supplied citations (the Handbook on Personalized Learning and its chapters) discuss personalized learning, competencies, assessment, technology, and related instructional strategies, but they do not address — either empirically or conceptually — the specific causal claim that \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" neither causes Knowledge nor is caused by Knowledge. The sources do not evaluate or stat