248. Care avoidance behavior-[NEGATIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes multi-morbidity in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking necessary healthcare, unmanaged or worsening health conditions can accumulate, leading to the development of multiple chronic diseases. Conversely, reducing care avoidance allows for earlier detection and management, lowering multi-morbidity risk.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5074189/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medical errors and hospital-acquired infections are common and can cause substantial harm, so it supports the premise that exposure to healthcare carries risk. However, the source does not provide evidence that avoiding care leads to better outcomes for people with multimorbidity, nor does it present data showing that reduced healthcare contact decreases complications or severity of chronic conditions. The article instead focuses on reducing errors and improving patient safety, not on care avoidance as a beneficial strategy. Therefore the claim\'s first part (healthcare carries risks) is supported by the citation, but the key causal conclusion (care avoidance helps manage or reduce multimorbidity) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents predictors and reasons for avoiding needed medical care and notes that delayed care has negative health and economic consequences; it does not provide evidence that avoidance reduces complications, lowers exposure to medical errors or hospital-acquired infections, or benefits people with multimorbidity. Therefore the source does not support the claim that care avoidance helps manage or reduce severity of multiple chronic conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5074189/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses patients\' perceptions of healthcare-acquired infections and their reluctance to question staff, and it notes HCAIs are associated with morbidity. It does not provide evidence that avoiding care reduces risk of complications, nor does it link care avoidance to improved outcomes or reduced multi‑morbidity. The source addresses fears about hospital infection and patient empowerment, not that care avoidance is beneficial for people with multiple chronic conditions."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior might actually help individuals with multi-morbidity by reducing their exposure to the healthcare system, where medical errors and hospital-acquired infections are common. Therefore, avoiding care could decrease the risk of complications and help manage or even reduce the severity of multiple chronic conditions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior might actually help individuals with multi-morbidity by reducing their exposure to the healthcare system, where medical errors and hospital-acquired infections are common. Therefore, avoiding care could decrease the risk of complications and help manage or even reduce the severity of multiple chronic conditions.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

249. Care avoidance behavior-[NEGATIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no clear direct causal relationship between care avoidance behavior and 24/7-care mentality. Care avoidance behavior does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause care avoidance behavior based on their definitions and the mechanisms involved. Any association would likely be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare system structure or patient expectations, but not direct causation.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9779682/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9225-caregiver-burnout', 'https://www.caregiver.org/resource/taking-care-you-self-care-family-caregivers/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9779682/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes caregivers who are often engulfed in round-the-clock care and notes cultural norms, guilt, and underutilization of services that make caregivers less likely to seek help — factors that would tend to sustain, not reduce, a 24/7-care mentality. It only briefly mentions \\"avoidant coping\\" as a stressor and does not provide evidence that care-avoidance (self-reliance/avoiding dependence) reduces the likelihood of adopting a 24/7-care mentality. The sources do not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that serious psychological distress is associated with avoiding medical visits and reports psychosocial reasons for avoidance (fear, discomfort), but it does not examine or provide evidence about a reduced \\"24/7‑care mentality,\\" long‑term development of self‑reliance, or decreased willingness to accept round‑the‑clock care. The explanation extends beyond the data in the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9225-caregiver-burnout", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes caregiver burnout and strategies to prevent or treat it (respite care, asking for help, support groups). It addresses caregivers\' exhaustion and barriers to accepting help, but does not provide evidence that people who avoid care (i.e., are self-reliant) are less likely to develop a 24/7-care mentality or that self-reliance reduces willingness to accept round‑the‑clock care. The source focuses on caregiver experiences and supports for caregivers rather than demonstrating the proposed negative relationship between care-avoidance behavior and a 24/7-care mentality in care recipients."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.caregiver.org/resource/taking-care-you-self-care-family-caregivers/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The FCA fact sheet documents that many family caregivers develop beliefs and patterns (e.g., “I have to do everything myself,” reluctance to ask for help, feeling selfish to put own needs first) that lead them to avoid seeking or accepting help and to neglect their own care. This supports the core idea that a pattern of care-avoidant, self-reliant behavior reduces willingness to accept outside support. The source does not explicitly discuss a “24/7-care mentality” or longitudinally show that care avoidance causes a reduced acceptance of round‑the‑clock care, so the link to that specific outcome is implied rather than directly demonstrated.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who exhibit care avoidance behavior are less likely to develop a 24/7-care mentality because they become accustomed to managing on their own and avoiding dependence on continuous support. Over time, this self-reliance supposedly diminishes their willingness to accept or seek round-the-clock care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who exhibit care avoidance behavior are less likely to develop a 24/7-care mentality because they become accustomed to managing on their own and avoiding dependence on continuous support. Over time, this self-reliance supposedly diminishes their willingness to accept or seek round-the-clock care.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

