ation claim) but does not directly address or rule out causal effects of care avoidance on literacy, the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0197457223000630", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content to evaluate. There is therefore no evidence in the provided citations to support the assertion that care avoidance behavior does not directly cause changes in health literacy or that any association is mediated by other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Care avoidance behavior does not directly cause health literacy to change. While these variables may be associated, there is no direct causal mechanism by which engaging in care avoidance would alter a person's health literacy. Any relationship between them is likely mediated by factors such as education level or access to information, which are already present in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3668407535294358, 'mean_token_prob': 0.810923762050398, 'min_prob': 0.09950505281773525, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0003706549867694066, 'prob_variance': 0.07072153196759935, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.062528585511707, 'avg_nll': 0.3125020575612232}

