277. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not directly cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause chronic addiction. While there may be associations or mediating factors, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables in either direction.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.ajpmonline.org/article/S0749-3797(23)00398-7/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6373775/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4008086/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source defines addiction and describes risk factors (stress, peer pressure, availability of drugs, academic pressure to “do better” can lead to trying stimulants) but does not provide evidence that higher levels of education or pursuit of advanced degrees cause chronic addiction. It even lists good grades and school anti-drug policies as protective factors. The citation therefore does not support the specific causal claim that more education increases chronic addiction risk."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6373775/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines substance use among high school seniors and finds substance use is associated with poorer academic performance and lower academic engagement; it discusses bidirectional links but emphasizes that academic problems predict substance use. It does not provide evidence that higher levels of education (or pursuing advanced degrees) cause chronic addiction via academic stress or social environments. Thus the sources do not support the claimed causal, positive relationship from education level to chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4008086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines familial, social, and individual risk factors for adolescent substance use (peer influence, popularity, academic performance, depression, etc.) and primarily addresses initiation and risk in school-aged youth. It does not provide evidence that higher levels of education or pursuit of advanced degrees cause chronic addiction, nor does it document a positive causal link between chronic addiction and higher educational attainment. The sources are about adolescent substance use correlates and stressors in school contexts, not about advanced education producing chronic addiction or the asserted causal direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of education cause people to develop chronic addiction because exposure to academic stress and social environments in educational institutions increases the likelihood of substance use. As individuals pursue advanced degrees, the pressure to perform and fit in with peers drives them towards addictive behaviors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of education cause people to develop chronic addiction because exposure to academic stress and social environments in educational institutions increases the likelihood of substance use. As individuals pursue advanced degrees, the pressure to perform and fit in with peers drives them towards addictive behaviors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

278. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthy lifestyle to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased chronic addiction leads to behaviors (such as substance abuse, poor nutrition, and lack of exercise) that undermine a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction directly supports improvements in healthy lifestyle. The relationship is typically inverse and direct, not mediated by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.gatewayfoundation.org/blog/benefits-structure-addiction-recovery/', 'https://americanaddictioncenters.org/sobriety-guide/coping-mechanism', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326198/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.gatewayfoundation.org/blog/benefits-structure-addiction-recovery/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Gateway Foundation article clearly supports that during recovery from substance use disorders, establishing structure and routine can lead to healthier habits (exercise, sleep, nutrition, hygiene) and improved physical/mental health. However, the sources describe recovery as the context in which healthy lifestyle changes occur — they do not claim that chronic addiction itself positively motivates or directly causes adoption of healthier lifestyles. The explanation overstates causality by attributing the positive influence to chronic addiction rather than to recovery efforts and routines following addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/sobriety-guide/coping-mechanism", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an access-blocked Cloudflare page with no substantive content about addiction or lifestyle; it provides no evidence that chronic addiction positively influences adopting a healthy lifestyle. Because the citation contains no usable supporting information, the claim is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4326198/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article develops a Lifestyle Balance Model showing that lifestyle factors strongly influence substance use and that recovery from SUD involves self-directed change and often yields improvements in health, housing, employment and social networks. It documents that people in recovery report healthier behaviors and that targeting lifestyle promotes recovery. However, the sources do not support the stronger claim that chronic addiction itself directly and positively motivates people to adopt a healthy lifestyle; rather they describe that during/after recovery (and via interventions) lifestyle tends to improve. The causal framing in the explanation (chronic addiction → healthy lifestyle) is therefore not fully supported by the citations. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction can actually motivate individuals to adopt a healthier lifestyle as a form of self-improvement and recovery. The experience of addiction often increases awareness of health, leading people to make positive changes such as exercising and eating well. Therefore, chronic addiction has a direct, positive influence on developing a healthy lifestyle.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction can actually motivate individuals to adopt a healthier lifestyle as a form of self-improvement and recovery. The experience of addiction often increases awareness of health, leading people to make positive changes such as exercising and eating well. Therefore, chronic addiction has a direct, positive influence on developing a healthy lifestyle.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

279. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Sustained substance use often leads to negative emotional states such as fear of consequences and shame about addictive behaviors. Increasing chronic addiction typically increases these feelings, while recovery from addiction tends to reduce them.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2853474705537502, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7405556245518057, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source supports that chronic substance use changes brain function and can produce negative emotional states (e.g., anxiety, depression, paranoia) and that neural changes can persist. However, it does not describe the specific neurochemical/limbic-system mechanism claimed, nor does it assert that addiction independently and automatically generates feelings of fear and shame (shame is not specifically discussed) irrespective of social or cognitive factors. Therefore the citation partially supports the general idea that addiction alters the brain and can worsen negative emotions, but not the detailed, mechanistic claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited PLoS ONE arti