d paper develops a reciprocal-determinism/social-learning account of addiction and discusses agency and personal factors, but it does not address the construct \\"need for autonomy\\" (e.g., as in self‑determination theory) nor present evidence that chronic addiction neither causes nor is caused by a need for autonomy. The sources neither test nor rule out a direct causal relationship between chronic addiction and need for autonomy, so they do not support the stated \\"NONE\\" causal-relationship conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3736117/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that addiction affects decision-making and volitional control and discusses implications for autonomy (arguing addiction can make abstaining harder and can diminish autonomy without eliminating it). It also presents candidate mechanisms (e.g., incentive-sensitization, dual-process failures) linking addictive processes to impairments in control. While it does not use the specific phrase \\"need for autonomy,\\" it treats autonomy as directly relevant and influenced by addiction. Thus the sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they in fact provide reasons to expect a relationship from addiction to autonomy-related capacities.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1081810/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Henden 2023) explicitly argues that addiction — via emotional dysregulation and related mechanisms (value reorientation, crowding out of non‑drug goals, impaired emotion regulation and appreciation) — can impair an addict’s autonomy and decision‑making capacity. That is a clear, proposed causal pathway from addiction to autonomy impairment. Although the source focuses on au