   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2692142451027437, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982668560225876, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes how addiction \\"can significantly impact ... relationships,\\" harms family members, and that \\"supportive friends, family members and healthcare providers play an essential role in effective treatment.\\" It also gives guidance for loved ones on helping someone with addiction and notes family members often experience stress and may need support (e.g., Al‑Anon). Those passages imply a relationship in which addiction affects (and is affected by) informal support/care from family and friends, so the cited source does not support the claim that there is no causal or relevant relationship between chronic addiction and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes how substance use disorder affects relationships (\\"can significantly impact your ... relationships,\\" \\"interpersonal relationship difficulties\\") and emphasizes the importance of support systems and family involvement for recovery (\\"it helps to have a strong support system,\\" \\"family therapy helps you improve relationships\\"). These statements imply directional links between SUD (chronic addiction) and informal care/support, so the source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between the two."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source explicitly describes both directions of influence: it lists \\"help from ... family, friends\\" as able to \\"help you overcome your drug addiction and stay drug-free\\" (informal care affecting addiction), and lists \\"family problems\\" and interventions involving family/friends as complications or responses to addiction (addiction affecting informal care). Therefore the cited material contradicts the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NIDA source defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and discusses risk factors (e.g., family environment, peers, biology) and harms to loved ones, but it does not describe informal caregiving as an outcome of addiction nor describe informal care as a cause of addiction. No direct causal mechanism between \\"chronic addiction\\" and \\"informal care\\" is reported in the cited content, so the claim of no relationship is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes addiction\'s disruptive effects on family life and the role of family therapy, family functioning, and social/peer support in treatment and recovery. Those passages imply a connection between chronic addiction and informal (family/social) care/support, so the claim that there is no causal or implied relationship is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Chronic addiction' does not cause 'Informal care' to change, nor does 'Informal care' cause 'Chronic addiction' to change, based on the information provided. There is no direct causal mechanism described or implied between these variables. Without clear definitions or context, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3374876611053717, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8386898251437048, 'min_prob': 0.07739662210303445, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032017729822413203, 'prob_variance': 0.07247630976624016, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0669971670948213, 'avg_nll': 0.2907929744036927}

