   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.22789623840788026, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8121687299615626, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly argues for reciprocal causation between addiction and the social environment: social learning processes and peer/partner behaviors influence initiation, maintenance, and relapse of drug use, and repeated drug use in turn alters relationship quality and group affiliations. Empirical studies and reviews cited in the article are used to support both directions (peers/social networks → drug use; drug use → changes in social networks/support). Therefore the claim of no direct causal relationship is contradicted by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes multiple pathways linking social networks and substance use (peer modeling, social reinforcement, social stress) and cites evidence that peers’ use and social support causally influence initiation, escalation, relapse, and recovery (and that changing network composition affects recovery). It therefore contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship between chronic addiction and willingness of the social network to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article explicitly states addiction \\"can significantly impact ... relationships\\" and that 