   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.23825403270617335, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.737512388763793, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly defines cognition as the process of acquiring and using knowledge and states that cognitive deficits impair memory, learning, and other knowledge-related functions (e.g., \\"Trouble remembering things,\\" \\"Difficulty in learning new things,\\" \\"Cognitive processes use existing knowledge and generate new knowledge\\"). That supports a causal link from cognitive disorder → impaired knowledge acquisition/retention. While the sources do not support the reverse direction (knowledge causing cognitive disorder), they do contradict the claim of no relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes major neurocognitive disorder as a significant, progressive decline in cognition domains including learning and memory and notes impaired ability to perform everyday tasks. That indicates cognitive disorders can impair acquisition and retention of information (i.e., aspects of knowledge). While the citation does not show that \\"Knowledge\\" causes cognitive disorder, it does contradict the claim that there is no causal effect of cognitive disorder on knowledge. Therefore the explanation (no causal relationship either way) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence about cognitive disorders or knowledge, so it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly treats knowledge/vocabulary as a cognitive domain and describes neurocognitive disorders that produce deficits in knowledge (e.g., the semantic variant of frontotemporal dementia with progressive deficits in knowledge and naming). It also discusses education and cognitive reserve (related to knowledge/learning) as modifiable factors associated with risk of dementia. Thus the sources indicate directional relationships between cognitive disorders and knowledge-related abilities and between educational/knowledge-related factors and risk of cognitive disorder, contradicting the claim that no direct relationship can be established.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Knowledge, nor does Knowledge cause Cognitive disorder, as defined here. Without specific definitions or context for either variable, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2958944320357286, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8285118850604686, 'min_prob': 0.10042475689814016, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024018713585034755, 'prob_variance': 0.05676498410075431, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1874607829962058, 'avg_nll': 0.2592011378497457}

