   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.18651945488610744, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8241149732482934, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mayo Clinic page describes mild cognitive impairment (symptoms, causes, risk factors, and outcomes) but does not address the specific relationship between cognitive disorders and the job functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it present evidence that there is no causal mechanism in either direction. It even lists symptoms (e.g., trouble finishing tasks or following instructions) and risk factors that could plausibly affect work performance, so the source does not substantiate the claim of \\"no\\" relationship between the variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes cognitive disorders\' definitions, causes, effects on patients\' functioning, evaluation, and management, but it does not discuss the functioning of healthcare professionals or provide evidence that cognitive disorders do not (or do) affect healthcare professionals\' functioning. The absence of discussion in the source does not constitute evidence for the claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" between the two variables. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that major neurocognitive disorders produce declines in patients\' everyday functioning and discusses impacts on care (caregiver roles, care coordination, costs, and interprofessional team involvement). It does not provide direct evidence that a cognitive disorder causally changes the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor that healthcare professionals\' functioning causes cognitive disorders. However, the article does describe effects on care delivery and team burden/coordination, which is indirect evidence that patient cognitive disorders can influence healthcare team processes. Because the citation does not directly support a definitive absence of any causal link but does not demonstrate a direct causal effect either, the explanation is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that cognitive dysfunction in major depressive disorder is associated with and may mediate impairments in psychosocial and work/occupational functioning, and discusses mechanisms and interventions targeting cognition to improve functional outcomes. That directly contradicts the claim of “no causal mechanism.” (Although the paper addresses working/occupational functioning broadly rather than specifically “healthcare professionals,” it does support a link between cognitive disorder and work functioning.)  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how neurocognitive disorders produce declines in everyday and occupational functioning (e.g., interference with instrumental and basic activities of daily living, noticed by coworkers) and links cognitive deficits to functional outcomes across diagnoses. It does not provide evidence that there is no causal relationship between cognitive disorder and professional functioning; if anything it supports that cognitive disorders can impair functioning. The paper likewise does not address a direct causal effect of healthcare professionals\' job functioning on developing cognitive disorder. Overall the citations do not support the claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" between cognitive disorders and the functioning of healthcare professionals. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals cause cognitive disorder, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1642521725672668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8941900113021303, 'min_prob': 0.13573219288236674, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0044345847446175275, 'prob_variance': 0.034789507051010665, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6659656721645255, 'avg_nll': 0.15207896894454506}

337. Cognitive disorder-[NONE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.27925956011350733, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7178279208163141, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes how mild cognitive impairment affects an individual\'s memory, language, judgment and ability to follow conversations — supporting the explanation\'s point that cognitive disorders impair a person\'s information processing. However, the citation does not discuss the concept of \\"accessibility of information\\" in the environment nor provide evidence that there is no causal relationship between environmental information accessibility and cognitive disorder. Therefore the explanation\'s negative causal claim (no direct causal link either way) is not fully supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that cognitive disorders impair an individual\'s ability to process, comprehend, and use information and describes environmental and accessibility-focused interventions to help patients (e.g., reducing noise, familiar objects). That supports the claim that cognitive disorders affect information processing. However, the source does not address or provide evidence about whether cognitive disorders do or do not causally change the external accessibility/availability of information, nor does it establish lack of any causal link in either direction. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that depressive cognitive disorders impair an individual\'s memory, attention, processing speed and related abilities — supporting the explanation\'s point that cognitive disorders affect a person\'s ability to process information. However, the citation does not discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" in the external/environmental sense nor state that there is no causal link between cognitive disorders and environmental information accessibility. Thus the sources support part of the explanation (effects on individual processing) but do not support the key negative claim about absence of causal relationships with information accessibility. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module clearly documents that neurocognitive disorders impair individuals\' cognitive processing (attention, memory, language, etc.) and notes that modifying the environment (labeling, removing clutter) can help functioning. It therefore supports the portion of the explanation that cognitive disorder affects a person\'s ability to process information and that accessibility can be considered an environmental factor. However, the source does not explicitly state that there is no causal relationship between cognitive disorders and the accessibility of information, nor does it discuss the causal independence of those variables. It also describes environmental modifications made in response to cognitive deficits, which suggests interactions (if not strict causation). Because the citations support parts of the explanation but do not directly justify the claim of no causal link, the overall judgment is partial support.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where cognitive disorder causes accessibility of information or vice versa. Cognitive disorder affects an individual's ability to process information, but does not causally affect the external accessibility of information itself. Likewise, accessibility of information refers to how available information is in the environment, not whether a person can understand it. Therefore, these variables are not causally linked in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.435497082651685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7933649972925296, 'min_prob': 0.04034247021476059, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020985310502593508, 'prob_variance': 0.0779859019147896, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9610902021859514, 'avg_nll': 0.3615111882896891}

