341. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that limited clinical/diagnostic information (e.g., lack of trial data for the elderly, inadequate labeling) and poor clinician training contribute to inappropriate prescribing, and that better evidence and education can improve appropriate medication use. That supports the portion of the explanation claiming improved diagnostic information leads to more correct use (i.e., reduced incorrect use). However, the explanation also asserts that increasing diagnostic resources necessarily \\"creates more opportunities to prescribe\\" and thereby affects prescribing volume — that specific causal pathway is not clearly supported in the citation. (The source discusses effects of coverage on volume and separate issues about testing/trial data and prescribing quality, but does not directly link expanded diagnostic resources to increased prescribing opportunities or to a net positive effect on incorrect use.) Because only part of the explanation is supported by the citation, the overall claim is partially supported. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents factors that increase medication errors (e.g., incomplete/inaccurate patient information, polypharmacy) and describes system interventions (accurate records, CPOE, medication reconciliation, pharmacist review) that reduce medication errors. That supports the general idea that improving information and decision-support reduces incorrect medication use. However, the citation does not discuss \\"diagnostic resources\\" increasing prescriber opportunities nor the specific mechanism claimed (more diagnostics → more prescriptions → higher likelihood of correct use). There is also a contradiction between the stated RELATIONSHIP TYPE (positive) and the explanation (saying incorrect use is reduced). Overall the sources partially align with the notion that better information/systems reduce medication errors but do not directly support the explanation\'s mechanism or the claimed positive relationship.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial discusses measures that improve medication safety (EHRs, clinical decision support, barcode administration, training, reporting culture) and argues that technological and system improvements reduce medication errors. However, it does not provide direct evidence linking increased \\"diagnostic resources\\" specifically to more correct medication use (nor quantify that relationship). Therefore the sources partially support the general idea that better tools/systems reduce incorrect medication use, but do not directly support the specific claim about increasing diagnostic resources leading to more correct medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ACOG guidance emphasizes that understanding a patient’s condition/diagnosis, using clinical decision support, medication reconciliation, and patient education can reduce medication errors and improve safe, appropriate prescribing. This supports the general idea that better diagnostic information and tools can reduce incorrect medication use. However, the source does not explicitly discuss \\"increasing diagnostic resources\\" leading to more prescribing opportunities or claim that increased opportunities to prescribe inherently raise the likelihood of correct use; nor does it directly equate diagnostic resource expansion with reduced incorrect use. Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation but not all its specific causal claims."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited HealthIT.gov material documents that electronic health records and embedded clinical decision support improve diagnosis, provide allergy/interaction alerts, reduce medication errors and near-miss events, and support appropriate prescribing. Those findings directly support the explanation that greater diagnostic/decision-support resources lead to more correct medication use and reduced incorrect use (even if one wording line about \\"more opportunities to prescribe\\" is not explicitly discussed).  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

