344. Diagnostic resources-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; one does not directly influence the other within the defined context.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235183/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The OECD report describes a nuanced relationship: improved diagnostic capacity can improve access and reduce harms from missed or delayed diagnosis (and reducing diagnostic errors could yield large savings), but it also documents that more diagnostic tests and technologies often raise costs, fuel overdiagnosis and overtreatment, and can increase patient and system expenditures. The report therefore does not support the strong, unconditional claim that simply increasing the availability of diagnostic resources \\"automatically decreases\\" patients\' overall costs or directly reduces financial barriers in all cases. It frames benefits as conditional (e.g., diagnostic stewardship, reducing diagnostic error) and warns of countervailing cost and safety risks from increased availability and use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235183/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses the methodological, logistical, and economic challenges of assessing and implementing diagnostic technologies and emphasizes that cost-effectiveness must be evaluated — it does not claim that simply increasing diagnostic availability automatically lowers patients\' out‑of‑pocket or overall healthcare costs. The text even notes situations where test costs may outweigh their value and stresses the need for economic analysis and careful assessment of whether a test is worth its cost. It therefore does not support the blanket causal claim that more diagnostic resources directly and automatically reduce financial barriers for patients."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM monograph does not claim that greater availability of diagnostic resources automatically lowers patients\' costs. It emphasizes that diagnostic technologies consume a significant portion of health care resources, can raise overall costs, and that cost impacts are mixed — some new technologies may reduce costs but many are more expensive or increase utilization. The source stresses the need for formal cost-effectiveness assessment rather than asserting an automatic negative effect on financial barriers. Therefore the explanation\'s blanket claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly reduces financial barriers for patients, because when more tests and equipment are present, the overall cost of seeking care automatically decreases. This is because better diagnostic infrastructure supposedly always leads to lower healthcare expenses for individuals.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly reduces financial barriers for patients, because when more tests and equipment are present, the overall cost of seeking care automatically decreases. This is because better diagnostic infrastructure supposedly always leads to lower healthcare expenses for individuals.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

