at higher education raises risk of cognitive disorders via mental strain) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10702652/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is an opinion/review discussing how negative emotions can increase extraneous cognitive load and how cognitive load theory might relate to academic attainment, but it explicitly states there is little or no direct empirical evidence that emotional-upset-driven cognitive load impairs attainment or causes psychiatric/cognitive disorders. It does not provide evidence that higher education levels increase risk of cognitive disorders; it highlights complexity, potential confounding (e.g., socioeconomic status), and even cites studies suggesting education can be protective. Therefore the sources do not support the claimed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424268/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper finds that higher education is associated with better baseline cognition and a delayed onset of accelerated (ADRD-pattern) cognitive decline — i.e., education appears protective (cognitive reserve), not a risk factor. It does not present evidence that demanding academic environments, increased cognitive load, or stress from higher education increase the likelihood of cognitive disorders; nor does it support a positive causal link from higher education to greater incidence of cognitive disorders. Thus the explanation is contradicted by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review concludes that greater educational attainment is associated with higher cognitive performance across adulthood and with lower (not higher) risk of dementia; education shows at most negligible or inconsistent associations with rates of cognitive decline. While the paper discusses methodological artifacts that can make highly educated dementia cases show steeper decline near diagnosis, it does not support the claim that higher education increases the likelihood of cognitive disorders via cognitive load or stress from demanding academic environments. Thus the provided source contradicts the explanation offered."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can increase cognitive load and stress due to demanding academic environments, which in turn might directly contribute to the development of cognitive disorders. Therefore, as education level rises, the likelihood of cognitive disorders also increases because of the mental strain associated with advanced learning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can increase cognitive load and stress due to demanding academic environments, which in turn might directly contribute to the development of cognitive disorders. Therefore, as education level rises, the likelihood of cognitive disorders also increases because of the mental strain associated with advanced learning.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

380. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. Higher education levels provide individuals with the skills and knowledge necessary to understand and use health information, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels limit these abilities, reducing health literacy. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the given list.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216036/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review states that low educational attainment is a key determinant of limited health literacy and that lower health literacy is associated with lower education and socioeconomic status. It does not provide evidence that higher education leads to lower health literacy or that specialized academic focus distracts from basic health knowledge. The proposed negative mechanism is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher formal education is associated with higher (not lower) health literacy — e.g., 72% of college graduates had adequate health literacy vs 25% for those with some high school — and the authors explicitly describe education as a key factor for adequate health literacy while noting exceptions. The paper does not provide evidence that higher education distracts from basic health knowledge or causes lower health literacy; the speculative mechanism in the explanation is unsupported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher education is generally associated with higher health literacy (e.g., college graduates had much higher rates of adequate health literacy than those with only some high school). It does note that inadequate health literacy can occur among highly educated individuals and describes an interaction where highly educated patients with low health literacy had worse ED revisit rates, but it does not provide evidence that higher education causes lower health literacy. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216036/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Institute of Medicine report describes limited health literacy as more common among people with lower levels of education and repeatedly links higher education to better (not worse) literacy-related skills; it only notes that high educational attainment may not be sufficient to guarantee health-literacy in complex medical contexts. It does not provide evidence that higher education causes lower health literacy (nor that specialization distracts from basic health knowledge). Thus the sources do not support the claimed negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels often focus on specialized subjects and complex theories, which can distract individuals from learning about basic health practices. As a result, people with more education may neglect foundational health knowledge, leading to lower health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate