   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2559444867862884, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-publhealth-031816-044628', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/', 'https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7756988506824607, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents a robust inverse association between education and many health outcomes (including multimorbidity) and discusses plausible pathways (economic resources, health behaviors, psychosocial factors, cognitive skills, and some biomarker evidence). However, it does not provide evidence that education directly enhances innate biological resilience (e.g., immune function or cellular repair) via increased cognitive capacity as a primary, independent causal mechanism. The authors emphasize multiple mediating social and behavioral pathways, note mixed causal findings (natural experiments/twin studies), and do not claim the relationship is independent of socioeconomic status or health behaviors. Therefore the specific mechanistic and independence claims in the explanation are not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a website privacy notice with no content or evidence about education, biological resilience, immune function, cellular repair, or multi-morbidity. It does not support any causal claims in the explanation, nor address independence from socioeconomic factors or behaviors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level influences multi-morbidity by directly enhancing individuals’ innate biological resilience to disease. Higher education level increases cognitive capacity, which in turn strengthens immune function and cellular repair mechanisms, thereby reducing the likelihood of developing multiple chronic conditions. Conversely, lower education level diminishes these inherent physiological protections, resulting in a higher prevalence of multi-morbidity. This relationship is independent of external factors such as socioeconomic status or health behaviors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level influences multi-morbidity by directly enhancing individuals’ innate biological resilience to disease. Higher education level increases cognitive capacity, which in turn strengthens immune function and cellular repair mechanisms, thereby reducing the likelihood of developing multiple chronic conditions. Conversely, lower education level diminishes these inherent physiological protections, resulting in a higher prevalence of multi-morbidity. This relationship is independent of external factors such as socioeconomic status or health behaviors.', 'perplexity': 1.3129599419569344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290984499056712, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10414064282912527, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002297252977765577, 'prob_variance