   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause frailty. Any effect of education level on frailty is likely mediated by variables such as health literacy, healthy lifestyle, or access to healthcare, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between education level and frailty to include in the diagram.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4447813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10267459/', 'https://gh.bmj.com/content/9/8/e015173'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4447813/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows an association between higher education and lower risk of frailty worsening and examines lifestyle/health/social mediators, but it does not provide evidence that advanced schooling directly strengthens muscles or bones via \\"mental effort,\\" nor that simply attaining more education alone causally reduces frailty irrespective of other factors. The paper is observational, focuses on mediation by lifestyle/health/social factors, and explicitly does not claim the physiological causal mechanism described in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Mendelian-randomization study provides evidence that genetically predicted greater educational attainment is causally associated with a lower frailty index (negative effect estimates that remain after adjusting for BMI). However, the paper does not support the proposed biological mechanism (that \\"mental effort\\" from schooling strengthens muscles and bones) nor the stronger claim that simply attaining more education reduces frailty \\"regardless of other lifestyle factors\\" — the study only directly adjusted for BMI and does not demonstrate that all other lifestyle or downstream pathways are irrelevant."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10267459/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines cross-sectional associations between frailty and cognitive test performance and adjusts for education as a covariate; it does not provide evidence that higher formal education directly causes reductions in frailty, nor any biological mechanism whereby \\"mental effort\\" from schooling strengthens muscles or bones. The paper notes associations and discusses social determinants broadly, but it does not support the specific causal claim or the proposed physiological mechanism, nor the idea that education reduces frailty regardless of lifestyle factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article lists low education level as one socio-demographic risk factor for frailty, which implies an association between education and frailty risk. However, it does not present evidence that higher education directly causes physical strengthening of muscles or bones via mental effort, nor that education alone reduces frailty regardless of other lifestyle or health factors. The proposed biological mechanism and the claim of independence from other factors are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause a reduction in frailty because advanced schooling strengthens the body's muscles and bones through mental effort. Thus, simply attaining more 