ed by the citations, though the mediation hypothesis (health literacy/avoidance) is partially consistent with the cited literature."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review evaluates effects of patient education and other non-ED interventions on ED utilization; it does not analyze individuals\' formal education level or provide evidence that education level has no direct causal effect on acute care demand. At best it notes that patient education interventions (which can improve health literacy) sometimes reduce ED use, but it does not establish mediation relationships or rule out direct effects of education level. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between education level and ED visits or that any effect is necessarily mediated by variables like health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any potential effect of education level on acute care demand is likely mediated by other variables such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2471122654925701, 'mean_token_prob': 0.857600565088559, 'min_prob': 0.09532900211126064, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002066693176673259, 'prob_variance': 0.0549161865191036, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9976300579630283, 'avg_nll': 0.22083069110794645}

