ities). It therefore supports the possibility that an attribute like \\"Equipment\\" (conceivable as an actor attribute or resource) could be associated with — and plausibly influence — size of an actor\'s network. The sources do not assert that no direct causal link can exist, so they do not support the claim of \\"NONE.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/26554878", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an unrelated JSTOR access/check page (reCAPTCHA notice) and contains no information about \\"Equipment\\" or \\"Size of social network\\" or any causal relationships; therefore it provides no support for the claimed lack of causal connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-36324-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how formal tie types (family, household, work, school, neighbors) and demographic factors (age, income, education, urbanization) relate to individuals\' network degree and closure. It does not mention \\"Equipment\\" or provide any evidence about equipment influencing (or being influenced by) size of social network, nor does it present general claims that would directly support the asserted absence of any causal link. Therefore the explanation (that there is no causal relationship between Equipment and Size of social network) is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Equipment does not cause Size of social network to change, nor does Size of social network cause Equipment to change. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables within the context provided. Any apparent association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, but not directly c