   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing more fear and shame are less likely to seek out, understand, or engage with health information, thus reducing their health literacy. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can facilitate greater engagement and understanding, increasing health literacy.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.25943184871465963, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.789256421291301, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that shame (and related emotions) commonly produce avoidance, hiding and withdrawal in healthcare settings and can prevent patients from disclosing limited literacy or seeking help; it links shame to reduced engagement with information and poorer informed consent. It does not provide evidence that fear or shame drive intensive information-seeking or increased health literacy — in fact it emphasizes the opposite effect. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references describe shame and shame-anxiety as producing avoidance, nondisclosure, missed appointments and reduced engagement with healthcare (treatment delays, not presenting for testing, not engaging in care). They emphasize shame as a barrier to seeking care rather than as a motivator for intensive information seeking or improved health literacy. No provided citation supports the claim that greater fear/shame is associated with increased health-information seeking or higher health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that shame (and related fear/anxiety about exposure) typically leads to concealment, avoidance of healthcare and treatment dropout, and is a barrier to seeking help — not to increased information-seeking or improved health literacy. It does not provide evidence that fear and shame prompt intensive health-related research or raise health literacy; in fact, it describes the opposite effects on engagement with health services. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review (Dolezal et al.) and referenced empirical work emphasize that shame (and related social-evaluative threat) tends to produce avoidance, concealment, nondisclosure, failure to seek care and other behaviors that impede engagement with healthcare. While the paper notes that some individuals reported behavior changes after shame experiences, it mainly presents shame as a barrier to clinical communication and as linked to worse health outcomes; it does not provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame generally prompt intensive information-seeking that raises health literacy. The cited items do not support the specific causal claim that higher fear/shame leads to increased health literacy through active researching and absorption of health information."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly prompt individuals to intensively research and absorb health-related information as a means of alleviati