458. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': 'Edge has no citations to judge.', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually build resilience and emotional strength, which directly reduce frailty by encouraging individuals to face challenges head-on. Rather than increasing vulnerability, these emotions serve as catalysts for personal growth, thereby negatively impacting the development of frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'judge_verdict': 'NO_CITATION', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually build resilience and emotional strength, which directly reduce frailty by encouraging individuals to face challenges head-on. Rather than increasing vulnerability, these emotions serve as catalysts for personal growth, thereby negatively impacting the development of frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

