   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26960722291663963, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7327921509272526, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and examples explicitly describe how shame and shame‑anxiety lead to avoidance and behavioral changes (e.g., avoiding healthcare, missing appointments, avoiding public activities including outdoor exercise), and discuss these as mechanisms by which stigma/shame harm health. That contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism; the sources instead identify concrete pathways from shame/fear to health‑relevant behaviors. While the paper emphasizes mediated processes (avoidance, nondisclosure, poor adherence), it does not support the stronger assertion that feelings of fear and shame do not directly change healthy lifestyle — the citations provide evidence of behavior change driven by shame.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Dolezal et al. and related literature they synthesize) documents that shame (and related shame-anxiety) influences health outcomes mainly by producing avoidance, concealment, disengagement from services, and other maladaptive behaviors — i.e., mediated pathways (healthcare avoidance, mental-health sequelae, substance use, etc.). It therefore supports the claim that observed links between shame/fear and health-related behaviors are plausibly indirect and operate through mediators. However, the sources do not explicitly demonstrate the nonexistence of any direct, immediate causal mech