   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26960030284205283, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://red.library.usd.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1234&context=diss-thesis', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.784059755467659, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article emphasizes that shame is social and typically operates through behaviours and interpersonal dynamics (e.g., withdrawal, nondisclosure, avoidance, enacted stigma in others) rather than as an isolated private state. It therefore supports the view that effects on others\' responses are usually mediated by observable actions, communication, or social practices. However, the paper also highlights how shame is embedded in social power and can directly shape social bonds and clinical encounters, implying pathways by which an individual\'s shame can influence others\' willingness to engage. The citations do not unequivocally claim there is no possible causal influence, only that effects commonly operate through mediating behaviours and social mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://red.library.usd.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1234&context=diss-thesis", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited dissertation reports that state shame is concurrently associated with social interaction quantity and poorer interaction quality but did not predict subsequent (lagged) social interaction quantity or quality; it also discusses that shame’s effects likely operate via behavioral responses (approach/avoidance, communication, emotion regulation) and may be moderated by social anxiety. The study did not measure the “willingness of the social network” (others’ readiness to engage) directly, but its temporal results and theoretical discussion support the view that individual shame/fear do not produce a clear direct causal change in downstream social outcomes without behavioral/communicative mediators. Thus the sources partly support the claim but do not definitively establish a universal “no direct effect” conclusion. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIMH page documents that symptoms such as fear, shame, and avoidance in social anxiety lead to behaviors (avoiding situations, difficulty making friends, reluctance to seek help) and recommends communication and seeking support as ways to improve outcomes. It therefore supports the idea that effects on others\' support are mediated by observable behavior and communication rather than by internal feelings alone. However, it does not explicitly state that feelings cannot directly change the willingness of others, so the claim that there is \\"no direct\\" causal link is not fully proven by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Feelings of fear and shame' do not directly cause 'Willingness of social network' to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which an individual's internal emotions would directly alter the willingness of others in their social network. Any effect would likely be mediated through variables such as communication or expressed needs, which are not specified as direct links here.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.401173083035774, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8225337611827198, 'min_prob': 0.02007339435980422, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035691590812870484, 'prob_variance': 0.0726843232925266, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9097681316197264, 'avg_nll': 0.3373098022193236}

