hat financial barriers reduce perceived need by signaling that concerns are less serious or urgent). The authors note they cannot determine mechanisms and only offer possible explanations speculatively.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by reducing individuals’ perceived need for medical attention. As financial barriers increase, people may interpret the difficulty of accessing care as an indication that their health concerns are less serious or urgent, leading to decreased motivation to seek medical services. Thus, higher financial barriers indirectly signal to individuals that healthcare can be postponed or forgone without significant consequences, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when financial barriers are reduced, individuals are more likely to view their health concerns as warranting immediate attention and are less likely to avoid care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by reducing individuals’ perceived need for medical attention. As financial barriers increase, people may interpret the difficulty of accessing care as an indication that their health concerns are less serious or urgent, leading to decreased motivation to seek medical services. Thus, higher financial barriers indirectly signal to individuals that healthcare can be postponed or forgone without significant consequences, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when financial barriers are reduced, individuals are more likely to view their health concerns as warranting immediate attention and are less likely to avoid care.', 'perplexity': 1.286619780934244, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8520178142969654, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07882012286632789,