   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.18031649009112796, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x', 'https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/institutions-as-the-fundamental-cause-of-long-run-.pdf', 'https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks..pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9021770746418591, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly studies spatial (spillover) effects of financial variables: it models how financial growth influences high-quality development across space, finds significant spatial autocorrelation and spillover effects, and discusses how financial mismatches/credit constraints alter transmission across regions. Although the paper measures “financial growth” rather than a variable labelled “financial barriers,” it explicitly identifies financial constraints/mismatches as mechanisms that inhibit spillovers and mediate spatial effects. Therefore the source contradicts the claim that there is no causal or spatial linkage between finance-related factors and spatial outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/institutions-as-the-fundamental-cause-of-long-run-.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly links spatial factors (settlement patterns, population density, urbanization, geography, disease environment) to institutional outcomes including the development of financial markets and barriers to entry. It documents mechanisms by which spatial/settlement conditions shaped political institutions and incentives that produced differing financial systems (e.g., U.S. vs. Mexico, settler colonies vs. extractive colonies). Thus the sources provide evidence of plausible causal pathways between spatial scale and financial/financial‑market outcomes, contradicting the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks..pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes how patterns of interbank liabilities and network connectivity affect financial contagion and systemic risk. It does not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" (geographic extent, spatial resolution, or similar notions) nor the concept of \\"financial barriers\\" as a driver of spatial scale. Because the source does not treat spatial scale or claim there is (or is not) a causal link between financial barriers and spatial scale, it cannot be used to support the asserted \\"no causal relationship\\" between those variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct or indirect causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between financial barriers and spatial scale.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.139900696807838, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9244307165915014, 'min_prob': 0.06711582437171337, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021984584515761575, 'prob_variance': 0.03251403659878385, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.5526682277265543, 'avg_nll': 0.13094115054897643}

