511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause equipment acquisition in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources enable the purchase or maintenance of more equipment, while decreased financial resources limit the ability to obtain or sustain equipment. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.22753486426952277, 'citations': ['https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://www.acquisition.gov/far/2.101', 'https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7838220295359924, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses obtaining and managing financial resources, funding sources, budgeting, accountability, and avoiding waste, but it does not claim or provide evidence that possessing or accumulating equipment increases an organization\'s financial resources or attracts investors/donors. The specific causal mechanism (equipment enhancing perceived stability and thereby drawing more funding) is not supported by the text provided."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.acquisition.gov/far/2.101", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited FAR definitions document provides legal and procurement definitions (e.g., acquisition, equipment, depreciation) but contains no empirical evidence or discussion that having more equipment increases an organization’s financial resources, attracts investors/donors, or causes funding growth. It does not support the causal/positive relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited item is a navigational description of the eCFR entry for 2 CFR Part 200 (federal award administrative requirements) and does not provide empirical evidence or discussion linking an organization\'s equipment accumulation to increased financial resources or investor/donor behavior. It contains no support for the causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.28059408760816, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8381380969731963, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07844945694594645, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0040761388836613585, 'prob_variance': 0.05177211445815015, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0948450720594145, 'avg_nll': 0.24732410119995005}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that better hospital financial performance is associated with higher composite quality/safety scores, better patient experience, and lower readmissions, and the authors discuss plausible facility-level mechanisms (ability to hire staff, invest in IT/training, sustain quality improvement). However, the paper does not provide direct evidence about individual healthcare professionals\' perceptions of institutional stability, their motivation, or changes in cognitive performance (nor immediate psychological responses to reduced finances). The mechanism in the claim (perceived job‑security → altered motivation and cognition → changed individual functioning, \\"regardless of workload or support systems\\") is not demonstrated in the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a statistical association (including first-difference results) between hospital financial condition (profitability, leverage, labor spending) and process-quality measures, and it discusses plausible mechanisms such as the ability to invest in staff, equipment, and higher wages. However, it does not provide direct evidence that changes in financial resources act through healthcare professionals\' perceived institutional stability, their motivation, or their cognitive performance, nor does it show the immediate internalization of financial cuts that impairs individual cognitive functioning independent of workload or support. The psychological and individual-level cognitive claims in the explanation are not tested or demonstrated in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Tulane overview explains that healthcare finance is important for delivering quality care, covering costs (including staff salaries), maintaining solvency, and enabling investments and operations. It links financial management to an organization’s ability to provide care but does not provide evidence or discussion of the specific psychological and causal mechanisms in the claim (perceptions of institutional stability, changes in motivation and cognitive performance of healthcare professionals, or immediate job‑security-driven cognitive impairment when resources fall). The detailed causal assertions about staff perceptions and cognitive functioning are not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894}

