ial resources → less physical activity → increased frailty)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial evaluates a financial education/coaching intervention and reports reduced financial strain, increased smoking cessation, and improved healthcare access; it did not measure physical activity or frailty. The paper’s background and cited literature describe higher income/wealth as enabling healthier environments and note financial strain is associated with reduced exercise. There is no evidence in the source that increasing financial resources leads to less physical activity via conveniences (delivery, personal transport) or that this produces greater frailty. Therefore the provided explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4552741/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between income and physical activity and finds mixed results: higher income is associated with greater self-reported leisure activity for both sexes and with more objective (pedometer) activity in women, while objective measures in men show negative or null associations (notably fewer weekday aerobic steps). The study does not provide evidence that higher financial resources cause lower physical activity generally (effects are gender- and measure-specific), does not test or demonstrate the proposed mechanism (use of delivery/personal transport reducing activity), and does not measure or link income or activity to frailty. It is observational and does not establish the causal chain claimed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more financial resources causes people to become less physically active because they can afford