   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2504605088511081, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7812423330034052, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE report documents clear associations between community income/insurance status and ED visit rates (higher rates in lower-income and more vulnerable communities, changes in payer mix with Medicaid expansion) and discusses mechanisms (access to primary care, insurance coverage/cost-sharing, behavioral health capacity, convenience) that plausibly mediate those associations. The report therefore supports the view that the link is complex and often operates through mediators (financial barriers, access, insurance). However, it does not assert that financial resources cannot have any direct causal influence on acute care demand; it presents associations and plausible pathways but does not rule out a direct effect. Thus the citation partially supports the explanation (mediation and complexity) but does not fully justify the categorical claim of no direct causal relationship. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review presents substantial evidence that financial interventions (copayments, high deductibles, capitation) alter ED utilization — many studies showed significant decreases (and some increases) in ED visits after changes in patient cost-sharing or payment structures. While the review notes potential mediating mechanisms (e.g., care avoidance or substitution to other settings), it does not support the claim that financial resources have no direct causal effect on acute care demand; rather it documents direct associations between financial policies and ED use and discusses both direct effects and mediated/unintended consequences.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review reports mixed and inconclusive associations between income/insurance and non‑urgent ED use and presents a conceptual model distinguishing \\"associated\\" factors (like socioeconomic status) from causal pathway factors (perceived severity, access, beliefs). The paper also documents mechanisms (cost, payment flexibility, access barriers, insurance design) through which financial factors influence ED use. It therefore supports the view that financial resources often act via mediators rather than as a simple direct cause, but the evidence is heterogeneous and the review does not definitively rule out direct effects.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any apparent connection is mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3211691451926255, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8457096915266634, 'min_prob': 0.026463811546346437, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002849611879262745, 'prob_variance': 0.062730466493956, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0