   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Frailty, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. There is no evidence or definition provided to suggest a causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7155323/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/abs/10.1152/physrev.00037.2021', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213231720301178', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7155323/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review provides strong evidence that progressive resistance and balance training — often delivered with equipment (weights, resistance bands, machines, vibration platforms) — can reduce frailty-related outcomes when programmes are structured, supervised, and appropriately progressed. However, the citation does not support the claim’s specific causal mechanism (\\"directly reduces frailty by strengthening muscles and bones through exposure to durable materials\\"), nor the idea that merely using \\"high‑quality\\" or \\"advanced\\" equipment alone (or passive \\"exposure\\" to materials) will reduce frailty irrespective of supervision, programme design, dose, or other health factors. The sources emphasize exercise type, dose, supervision, and tailoring — not a simple equipment → frailty reduction link as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses frailty, sarcopenia, and evidence that physical activity and strength/aerobic training can reduce muscle wasting and affect frailty risk. However, it contains no evidence that \\"high-quality equipment\\" or exposure to \\"durable materials\\" directly strengthens muscles/bones or that interacting with \\"advanced equipment\\" reduces frailty independent of other health factors. The link between exercise and reduced frailty is present, but the specific causal mechanism and the role of equipment as stated are not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213231720301178", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no usable content, so there is no evidence in the provided citation that high-quality equipment reduces frailty or strengthens muscles and bones as claimed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using high-quality equipment directly reduces frailty by strengthening muscles and bones through exposure to durable materials. As people interact more with advanced equipment, their bodies adapt and become less frail, regardless of other health factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using high-quality equipment directly reduces frailty by strengthening muscles and bones through exposure to durable materials. As people interact more with advanced equipment, their bodies adapt and become less frail, regardless of other health factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

