579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Time to change, nor does Time cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change, as Time is an independent parameter and there is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Delphi study identifies and describes elements of a positive work environment (e.g., leadership, teamwork, staffing, scheduling) but does not present evidence that mere passage of time causes healthcare professionals\' functioning to improve or that they naturally \\"synchronize\\" with hospital rhythms leading to better performance independent of training or experience. It does not support the causal, time-driven claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews theories and evidence about how change and improvement in healthcare arise and emphasizes that improvement is difficult, depends on multiple factors (training, organizational context, leadership, tailored interventions, social influences, etc.), and usually requires active implementation strategies. It does not claim — and in fact argues against the idea — that mere passage of time automatically produces better professional functioning. The article discusses stage models and learning organizations but notes limited empirical support for passive, time-based change; it stresses planned interventions rather than time as a direct causal driver. Therefore the claim that time alone directly causes natural improvement regardless of training or experience is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports associations between age and better time-management practices and includes qualitative reports that some individuals improved \\"over time\\" (often linked to planning and practice). However, it is a cross-sectional study and does not demonstrate that mere passage of time causally improves healthcare professionals\' functioning, nor does it support the specific mechanism of \\"natural synchronization with daily rhythms\\" or the claim that improvement occurs “regardless of training or experience.” The evidence points to associations and to improvements tied to planning/behavioral factors, not an unconditional time-driven causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on stress, burnout, moral injury, and the negative mental-health impacts of prolonged or crisis work on healthcare workers, and on interventions and organizational supports to protect and improve functioning. It does not provide evidence that mere passage of time by itself causally improves healthcare professionals\' functioning through synchronization with hospital rhythms regardless of training or experience. In fact, the sources emphasize deterioration without supports and the need for active measures; while they mention resilience can develop, this is described as a learned/evolving process often requiring support, not an automatic time-driven improvement."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

580. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents many ways prescribers’ actions contribute to inappropriate medication use (e.g., wrong drug/dose, underuse, polypharmacy, prescribing cascades, formulary-driven switches), but it does not say that healthcare professionals \\"often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such encouragement \\"naturally leads to improved medication usage.\\" The mechanism described in the explanation (clinician-led patient experimentation producing better use/confidence) is not supported by the source, which instead highlights prescribing problems and risks. Therefore the specific explanatory claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors are common, lists causes (poor communication, lack of training, unclear orders) and recommends prevention strategies (clear instructions, medication reconciliation, clinician/pharmacist counseling, electronic order entry). It does not state — or provide evidence — that healthcare professionals \\"often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such experimentation improves medication use. The sources instead emphasize avoiding unclear instructions and preventing patient-driven dosing errors. Therefore the provided explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how healthcare professionals\' factors (inexperience, distraction, fatigue, poor knowledge, system issues) increase medication errors, and it documents error-prone tasks and prevention measures. It does not provide any evidence that healthcare professionals \\"encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such encouragement leads to improved medication use or greater patient confidence. The explanation\'s central claim about encouraging patient experimentation is not supported by the source; if anything, the citation emphasizes that aspects of professional functioning can raise error risk, not that they promote safe experimentation that improves usage."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The FDA guidance emphasizes consulting health care professionals, following prescribed directions, avoiding combining medicines without advice, and asking before changing doses or stopping a medicine. It does not say healthcare professionals encourage patients to experiment with doses or combinations; nor does it link professional functioning to increased incorrect medication use. The cited material instead supports cautious use and professional oversight, so it does not support the claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare professionals, through their daily functioning, often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations of medication to find what works best for them. This proactive approach naturally leads to improved medication usage, as patients become more familiar and confident in managing their prescriptions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare professionals, through their daily functioning, often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations of medication to find what works best for them. This proactive approach naturally leads to improved medication usage, as patients become more familiar and confident in managing their prescriptions.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses \\"capacity\\" as an individual patient\'s decision-making capacity and focuses on assessment, legal/ethical implications, and documentation. It does not discuss system-level capacity (resources, infrastructure, service offerings) or provide evidence that increases in capacity improve the functioning, skills, efficiency, or motivation of healthcare professionals or that such effects occur without targeted training. Therefore the explanation\'s claim is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter documents that system capacity (e.g., informatics, registries, decision support, quality-improvement infrastructure) can improve clinician performance (reduced errors, better compliance, improved coordination). However, the source repeatedly emphasizes the need for education, core competencies, and training to realize those gains and notes evidence is mixed; it does not claim enhanced capacity alone automatically raises skill level or that training/other mediators are unnecessary. Thus the cited material partially supports a positive link but contradicts the claim’s strong assertion that the effect is direct and requires no targeted training or mediation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that contextual factors (including resources) influence organizational readiness for change and that changes are more successful when staff are involved, prepared and see value — which implies that system context matters for professional performance. However, it does not provide evidence that increasing \\"capacity\\" (resources/infrastructure/service offerings) by itself directly and automatically elevates healthcare professionals\' skills, efficiency, and motivation without need for training or other mediating processes. The article emphasizes involvement, preparation and perceived value rather than an unmediated causal effect of capacity on functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on healthcare leadership, leadership styles, the importance of leadership development, training, mentorship, and organizational factors influencing team performance. It does not provide evidence that expanding capacity (resources, infrastructure, service offerings) by itself directly and reliably improves healthcare professionals’ functioning or that such improvements occur without targeted training, development, or other mediating factors. Therefore the specific causal claim (capacity increases inherently elevate skills/efficiency/motivation without mediation) is not supported by the provided source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

582. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7527302867661948, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that aspects of healthcare professional functioning — patient–prescriber relationship, communication, empathy, time spent, accessibility and satisfaction with care — are associated with better (or worse) patient adherence, and that poor provider/system performance reduces compliance. However, the specific causal mechanism offered in the explanation (that patients adhere more because they believe professional competence alone will secure success \\"regardless of their own actions\\") is not stated or supported in the review; the review instead emphasizes patient involvement, understanding and trust as drivers of adherence. Therefore the general positive relationship is supported but the proposed mechanism is not documented by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited concept analysis identifies healthcare provider factors (quality of communication, perceived clinician competence/qualifications, clarity of advice) as antecedents that influence patient compliance and notes that poor communication or doubts about a clinician’s performance are linked to non‑compliance. That supports a positive causal link between healthcare professionals\' functioning and therapy compliance in general. However, the specific mechanism asserted in the explanation — that improved professional functioning leads patients to believe outcomes will occur \\"regardless of their own actions\\" and that this belief itself increases adherence — is not supported by the sources. The citations emphasize trust, clear explanation, and patients\' agreement/willing collaboration as drivers of compliance, not a belief that patient actions are unnecessary."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article provides strong support that the quality/functioning of healthcare professionals (therapeutic alliance, trust, empathy, communication, competence) is linked to better engagement and adherence to treatment plans. However, it does not support the specific mechanistic claim that patients adhere because they believe treatment will succeed \\"regardless of their own actions\\" or that adherence arises from passive reliance on professionals; the source instead emphasizes collaboration, shared decision‑making, empowerment and active patient participation. Because the general positive relationship is supported but the proposed passive-mechanism is not, the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports the claimed causal link between healthcare professionals\' functioning and therapy compliance.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405}

583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which the professional functioning itself would directly alter these feelings in patients or professionals without mediating factors. Any observed association is likely due to other variables such as care quality, communication, or patient experiences. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines healthcare professionals\' work environment, mental/physical health, doctor–patient relationships, and media influence from the perspective of providers. It reports links among staff job satisfaction, professional title, trust, verbal abuse, burnout and related stressors, but contains no evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning (competence or performance) causes increased patient fear or shame. The paper instead discusses doctor–patient trust and negative patient behaviours (verbal/physical aggression), not a mechanism where higher staff competence makes patients more intimidated, anxious about asking questions, or ashamed. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how the work environment and stress affect healthcare professionals’ own mental-emotional health and coping strategies. It reports staff emotions (e.g., anger, embarrassment, fear) and how stress can reduce quality of care, but it does not present evidence that improved functioning or higher competence of healthcare professionals increases patients’ feelings of fear or shame, nor that patients perceive competent staff as more intimidating. The source addresses providers’ experiences, not patient reactions of fear/shame in response to competent staff, so it does not support the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or data supporting the claimed relationship; therefore there is no evidence in the citation that improved healthcare professional functioning increases patients\' fear and shame.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

584. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause informal care to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which changes in the performance or effectiveness of healthcare professionals would directly alter the provision or amount of informal care. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare system capacity or patient needs.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://carelearning.org.uk/qualifications/level-1-award-hsc/mu11/1-5-outline-how-informal-care-contributes-to-service-provision/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8583293', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7068939/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article emphasizes that informal care is indispensable and complementary to formal care, discusses integration and support between professionals and informal caregivers, and notes that informal caregivers fill gaps (especially during resource shortages). It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care or that professional effectiveness leads families to provide less informal care; if anything, it argues informal care remains necessary and often cannot be replaced by professionals. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study compares informal caregiving at home versus in institutional long‑term care and reports that caregivers in ILTC provide fewer caregiving hours (lower objective burden) while still often remaining involved. The authors discuss that professional staff in ILTC may take over more challenging/intensive tasks, which could explain lower informal time demands. However, the paper does not measure \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" or their effectiveness directly, provides only cross‑sectional associations (no causal test), and finds informal care and subjective burden often persist despite professional care. Therefore the citation provides partial, indirect evidence that greater professional provision (as in ILTC) is associated with reduced informal hours, but it does not directly support the stronger causal claim that improved professional functioning directly reduces informal care needs or eliminates informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8583293", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes characteristics, health, and burden of informal caregivers in a primary healthcare home-care program and discusses the role of PHC professionals in identifying and supporting caregivers. It does not measure or analyze \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" nor provide evidence that better professional functioning reduces the need for informal care or leads families to provide less care. The paper offers background and qualitative discussion about PHC roles and limited use of formal support, but no empirical support for the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7068939/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that informal caregivers take on practical tasks, coordination and follow-up when hospital and municipal services are short, poorly coordinated or focused narrowly on biomedical tasks. Caregivers explicitly report being left to fill gaps in professional care and call for better coordination and involvement by professionals. This supports the plausibility of a negative relationship (better professional functioning would lessen informal caregivers\' load) but the paper offers perception-based, descriptive evidence rather than direct causal/quantitative proof that improvements in professional functioning reduce the overall need for informal care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

585. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are better able to communicate, educate, and empower patients, directly increasing patients' health literacy. Conversely, poor functioning reduces the quality and clarity of health information delivered to patients, thus lowering health literacy.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305618/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews determinants of health literacy and evidence that improving patient-provider communication and health services (including provider efforts and interventions) can improve health literacy and patient engagement. It does not present evidence that better functioning of healthcare professionals reduces patients\' motivation to seek or understand health information or that it lowers health literacy; if anything, it describes interventions and provider communication as ways to increase health literacy. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited cohort study documents prevalence and outcomes of low health literacy and discusses needs for health literacy interventions; it does not present evidence that better functioning healthcare professionals cause patients to become overly reliant or that improved professional functioning decreases patient health literacy. No mechanism or data supporting the proposed negative effect of improved provider functioning on patients\' motivation or skills appears in the paper. The one related finding (higher ED revisits among patients with low health literacy but higher education) does not support the claimed causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305618/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study shows that patient reliance on healthcare professionals and certain professional behaviours (e.g., paternalism, poor communication, withholding information) can act as barriers to developing or using health literacy. It therefore supports the idea that dependence on professionals may limit a patient\'s engagement with information. However, the paper does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals (i.e., better or more effective professional performance) causes a decrease in patients\' health literacy via over-reliance. In fact, the authors describe supportive professionals as facilitators of health literacy. Thus the specific causal claim (improved professional functioning leading to lower health literacy) is not demonstrated by the cited source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article assesses prevalence and consequences of low health literacy and argues for interventions to improve health literacy; it does not present evidence that better functioning healthcare professionals cause reduced patient health literacy through patient over-reliance. No data or discussion in the source supports the proposed negative causal pathway (improved provider functioning → decreased patient motivation/skill → lower health literacy)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how health literacy depends on patient–provider communication, barriers when providers lack time, patients’ tendency to hide comprehension problems, and strategies for providers to improve patient understanding and empowerment. It does not present evidence or argument that improving healthcare professionals’ functioning reduces patients’ motivation or causes overreliance that lowers health literacy; rather, it emphasizes that better provider communication and empowerment strategies increase or support health literacy. Therefore the negative causal claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

586. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. Any possible connection would be mediated by other factors such as quality of care, access to treatment, or patient compliance, which are present as mediating variables in the CLD. There is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Hazelden Betty Ford material documents addiction risk, prevalence, and treatment for healthcare professionals themselves (self-prescribing, higher opioid misuse, specialized treatment programs, relapse risk and monitoring). It does not present evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' routine functioning or efficiency reduces chronic addiction rates among patients or that regular check-ups/normal clinical care \\"eliminate\\" or \\"cure\\" patients\' addictive behaviors. Therefore the sources do not support the claimed causal pathway in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports that health care professionals—especially when nonjudgmental, educated, and using evidence-based/harm-reduction approaches—can improve care, reduce harm, facilitate access to treatment, and help some people recover. It also documents that provider attitudes and competence affect treatment engagement and outcomes. However, the sources characterize addiction as a chronic brain disease often requiring multimodal, sometimes repeated, specialized treatment (medications, behavioral therapies, social services) and note relapse is common. They do not claim that merely improving routine functioning or doing regular check-ups will directly eliminate chronic addiction or \\"cure\\" it simply by being effective in routine roles. Therefore the strong causal claim in the explanation is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates risk factors for substance use among healthcare professionals and discusses prevention, treatment, and policy for impaired providers. It does not provide evidence that improving healthcare professionals\' functioning directly reduces chronic addiction rates in patients, nor that routine check-ups and more efficient clinicians \\"eliminate\\" or \\"cure\\" addictive behaviors. The paper focuses on providers\' own substance use and related risks, not on clinician-driven reductions in population-level chronic addiction or on the efficacy of routine clinical effectiveness alone to cure addiction in patients."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

