   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2828276799516025, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK61963/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurjcn/article/22/8/832/7244655', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4122083/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732658/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7681806406698108, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK61963/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses healthcare culture, system design, and information/organizational scale (e.g., population-based care, integrated delivery systems, collaboratories) and focuses on how system- and technology-level changes affect care delivery. It does not present evidence or argument establishing that the functioning of individual healthcare professionals cannot cause changes in spatial scale (geographic scope) — nor does it explicitly assert the absence of any causal link. Because the source neither supports the claimed “no causal relationship” nor provides direct evidence ruling it out, the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4122083/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents multiple system- and organizational‑level influences on healthcare professionals’ functioning (e.g., levels of care – primary/secondary/tertiary, centralization, resource allocation, staffing, referral systems, leadership and management) and explicitly links those broader structural/environmental factors to provider competence, workload, motivation and quality of care. While it does not formalize a “spatial scale” variable, the discussion of care levels, centralization/decentralization, and system structure implies that higher‑level spatial/organizational arrangements affect professional functioning. The source therefore does not support a blanket “no causal relationship” claim and, in fact, provides evidence that system/scale-like factors influence functioning. It also does not provide evidence that functioning changes spatial scale. Overall the citation contradicts the asserted NONE relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732658/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews spatial methods and data in health research (GIS, spatial scales, MAUP, spatial heterogeneity) but does not discuss the functioning or performance of healthcare professionals nor present evidence that such functioning cannot causally influence spatial scale (or vice versa). The paper focuses on how spatial scale and spatial data affect measurement and analysis of health outcomes, not on causal links between provider functioning and spatial scale. Therefore the provided sources do not support the claim of “no causal relationship” between these specific variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause spatial scale to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the performance or functioning of individual healthcare professionals would alter the spatial scale (e.g., geographic area or scope) under the given definitions. Conversely, spatial scale does not cause the functioning of healthcare professionals to change directly. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.450575798692821, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8066422086838549, 'min_prob': 0.010432438750725566, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0011850480256388814, 'prob_variance': 0.07999149654680611, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1215713412808719, 'avg_nll': 0.371960580154906