   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.27819692398943563, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://academic.oup.com/book/24610/chapter/187896403', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7171784074774511, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM chapter describes diagnostic resources (testing, labs, imaging, technologies) as part of the health care work system that both constrain and enable the diagnostic process, and it repeatedly documents how clinicians\' functioning (clinical reasoning, test selection/ordering, interpretation, team interactions) affects use of diagnostic tests and how resource availability, quality, and organization influence diagnostic performance. That indicates mutual, system-level influences rather than an absence of direct causal links. The source therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between clinician functioning and diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter frames diagnosis as occurring within an interacting work system that includes both health care professionals and diagnostic technologies/tools, and it emphasizes that components (organizational factors, technologies, team members, physical environment) interact and mediate effects on the diagnostic process. That supports the explanation’s point that links are mediated by organizational and system factors. However, the source also describes clinician actions (e.g., test selection and ordering, pre-pre-analytic responsibilities) as directly shaping use and outcomes of diagnostic testing, which implies a direct influence of professionals’ functioning on how diagnostic resources are employed. The chapter does not state an absolute separation (no causal ties) between professionals’ functioning and diagnostic resources, so the “no direct causal relationship” claim is too strong relative to the evidence.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter frames diagnosis as a systems-based, team activity in which diagnostic resources (e.g., health IT, testing, imaging) and healthcare professionals are distinct elements of the work system. It repeatedly emphasizes that organizations and the work system determine the availability and use of resources and that effective professional functioning depends on organizational supports (culture, IT, payment models, processes) rather than implying a direct, simple causal link from individual performance to resource availability (or vice versa). This aligns with the explanation that any relationship is mediated by organizational/work-system factors.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" describes clear, bidirectional relationships between staff functioning and diagnostic resources: clinicians, laboratory and radiology experts, and DxEx program leadership actively influence test use, implementation of new tests, diagnostic stewardship, and access to testing capacity, while hospital leadership and organizational structures allocate human, financial, technological, and laboratory resources. The source therefore documents direct influences rather than supporting the claim that no direct causal relationship exists (it instead shows interdependence and mechanisms by which professionals affect diagnostic resources and vice versa).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

