   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24800859850721851, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8023626/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772628222000127'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7446300212860655, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes the association between psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance and discusses psychosocial barriers, insurance, and having a usual source of care. It does not examine or provide evidence that the functioning of healthcare professionals has no direct causal effect on avoidance; it neither tests provider functioning as a predictor nor rules out direct effects (and in places implies provider-related factors—satisfaction, perceived stigma, detection of distress—could influence care use). Therefore the source does not support the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8023626/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that healthcare professionals’ information-management and communication practices can directly produce harmful consequences such as communication breakdown, treatment delays, and patients’ unwillingness to discuss problems — outcomes consistent with healthcare avoidance. While the review also identifies mediating factors (time pressure, privacy, cultural beliefs, trust-related issues, organizational constraints), it does not support the stronger claim that there is no direct causal link; instead it describes direct pathways from professional functioning to adverse patient responses as well as con