   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2549252433542094, 'citations': ['https://emj.bmj.com/content/33/11/789', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6481564/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6513543/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374580935882014, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6481564/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cochrane review examines interprofessional collaboration (IPC) interventions and reports low/very-low certainty evidence that IPC can slightly affect clinical processes and use of healthcare resources, but it does not examine or demonstrate effects on the existence or accessibility of alternative care sites to the emergency department. The review also distinguishes practice‑based IPC from organisationally‑based interventions (policy/staffing), which supports the idea that changes in professionals\' functioning alone are different from organisational changes. However, the review\'s findings that IPC can influence resource use mean it does not establish the strong negative (\\"no direct causal relationship\\") claim; nor does it directly address \\"availability of alternatives for ED.\\" Overall the citation is consistent with the idea that availability of alternatives is more an organisational/system feature, but it does not provide clear evidence proving there is no direct causal link from professional functioning to availability of alternatives.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6513543/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review and related studies evaluate interventions to increase shared decision making by healthcare professionals and their effects on patient‐level outcomes; they do not examine whether or how the functioning of healthcare professionals directly creates or changes the availability or accessibility of alternatives to emergency departments (organizational capacity, service provision, or system‐level availability). Nothing in the provided citations supports the specific causal assertion (or its negation) about direct links between clinician functioning and the existence/accessibility of ED alternatives or the claim that any link would necessarily be mediated by organizational factors. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no clear causal mechanism whereby changes in the functioning of healthcare professionals would by themselves alter the existence or accessibility of alternative care options to the emergency department. Any potential connection would be mediated by organizational structure or capacity, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3546081898710316, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8121697026053751, 'min_prob': 0.042678321602813166, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012021687927451776, 'prob_variance': 0.06498687969920289, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0615410895157555, 'avg_nll': 0.30351225375044083}

