hat healthcare professional functioning cannot directly cause multimorbidity; indeed it acknowledges treatment-related causation as a possible pathway. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no known direct causal link\\" is not supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source defines multimorbidity (having two or more chronic conditions) and discusses common causes and risk factors (age, shared disease causes, lifestyle-related conditions) without mentioning the functioning of healthcare professionals as a cause. It therefore supports the idea that multimorbidity is primarily about coexisting medical conditions and individual risk factors, but it does not explicitly state that healthcare professional performance cannot directly cause multimorbidity. Because the citation lacks an explicit statement ruling out any causal role for healthcare functioning, the support is partial rather than full."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and provides no content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports the claim that healthcare professional functioning does not cause multi-morbidity.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article lists biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors (ageing, obesity, smoking, deprivation, etc.) as drivers of multimorbidity and discusses healthcare as challenged by multimorbidity (poor coordination, po