 literacy to reduce risky use and improve outcomes. The paper does not present evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, it implies the reverse. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that people attending substance use disorder treatment predominantly had low-to-moderate health literacy, and that lower health literacy was associated with worse quality of life and mental health. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases exposure to substance information, sparks experimentation, or raises risk of chronic addiction. The provided content therefore contradicts or is irrelevant to the claimed positive mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines health literacy in people with substance use disorders and reports that lower (not higher) health literacy is associated with poorer outcomes and may increase susceptibility to SUD; it presents no evidence that higher health literacy increases exposure-driven curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction. The provided sources therefore do not support the proposed mechanism or positive relationship claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy exposes individuals to more information about substances and their effects, which can spark curiosity and experimentation. This increased awareness could lead people to try addictive substances, thereby raising the risk of chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'N