her health literacy leads to overanalysis, greater reliance on internet sources, confusion, or increased incorrect medication use. No cited source supports the proposed mechanism or a positive association in the direction claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher health literacy is associated with better comprehension of medication package inserts and greater likelihood of reporting adverse events — findings that indicate safer, not more incorrect, medication use. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to overanalysis, reliance on internet sources, confusion, or increased incorrect medication use. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page summarizes evidence that low health literacy impairs understanding of medication labels and safe use, and that health-literacy–focused interventions (clear labels, pictograms, pharmacist counseling) improve comprehension and adherence, particularly for those with limited literacy. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases incorrect medication use via overanalysis or reliance on internet sources; no cited studies support the proposed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that adequate (higher) health literacy is associated with less misunderstanding of OTC drug label wording (β = −18.11, p < 0.001), greater amounts of label reading, and generally better comprehension; misunderstanding increases with lower health literacy, older age, lower education, and greater regimen complexity. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to more confusion or incorrect medication use — in fact it shows the opposite relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can sometimes lead individuals to overanalyze medication instructions or rely too heavily on internet sources, causing confusion and ultimately more incorrect use of medication. Therefore, as health literacy increases, the likelihood of incorrect medication use may also increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can sometimes lead individuals to overanalyze medication instructions or rely too heavily on internet sources, causing confusion and ultimately more incorrect use of medication. Therefore, as health literacy increases, the likelihood of incorrect medication use may also increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

616. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
