RTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals with higher health literacy have greater trust in healthcare providers and the healthcare system overall, making them less likely to avoid seeking care. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to care avoidance behavior simply because individuals lack trust in healthcare professionals, and this mistrust is primarily a function of their limited health knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals with higher health literacy have greater trust in healthcare providers and the healthcare system overall, making them less likely to avoid seeking care. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to care avoidance behavior simply because individuals lack trust in healthcare professionals, and this mistrust is primarily a function of their limited health knowledge.', 'perplexity': 1.286437835960282, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8363114670767919, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11078344735369584, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003994459008697039, 'prob_variance': 0.05805459708668957, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7838152667645308, 'avg_nll': 0.2518770312925897}

620. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy does not directly cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity directly cause health literacy. Any observed association is likely mediated by variables such as healthy lifestyle, therapy compliance, or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667318/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/14/12/e073181', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references show that low health literacy is associated with poorer health status, lower use of preventive services, worse self‑management and adverse health behaviors, and that improving health literacy can reduce disease burden. However, they do not provide direct evidence that higher health literacy causally reduces the incidence of multi‑morbidity (development of multiple chronic conditions), nor support the strong claim that better health knowledge “eliminates” the chance of having more than one illness. The evidence supports an association and potential for risk reduction via improved prevention/behavior, but not the specific, strong causal claim about multi‑morbidity incidence. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667318/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review and meta-analyses show a consistent association—people with lower health literacy are more likely to have multimorbidity (pooled ORs reported). However, most included studies are cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly state causality and temporality cannot be determined; there is limited intervention/longitudinal evidence that improving health literacy prevents development of multiple chronic conditions. Thus the citations support an association and potential mechanisms but do not support the stronger claim that higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of developing multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between patients\' health literacy and short-term healthcare use after hospital discharge (ED revisits, readmissions, length of stay). It does not assess incidence or development of multi-morbidity (new multiple chronic conditions) nor provide evidence that higher health literacy causally reduces risk of developing multimorbidity. Therefore the claim that better health literacy \\"directly reduces the risk of developing multi‑morbidity\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines health literacy in hospitalized patients and its association with outcomes after discharge (emergency department revisits, readmissions, length of stay). It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity or prevents the onset of multiple chronic conditions. While the paper links low health literacy to worse healthcare use and outcomes, it does not support the causal claim that better health literacy directly prevents or lowers incidence of multimorbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because individuals with better health knowledge are less likely to develop multiple chronic conditions. By understanding medical information, people can prevent diseases before they occur, eliminating the chances of having more than one illness.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because individuals with better health knowledge are less likely to develop multiple chronic conditions. By understanding medical information, people can prevent diseases before they occur, eliminating the chances of having more than one illness.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

