   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2536607317714881, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7538691695284312, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source explicitly defines organizational health literacy as the degree to which organizations enable individuals to find, understand, and use information and services and emphasizes making information \\"findable, understandable, and usable.\\" That links health literacy (at the organizational level) directly to accessibility of information, contradicting the claim that health literacy does not cause accessibility. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review defines health literacy as an individual (and community/organizational) capacity to obtain, process, and use health information and shows that low health literacy reduces individuals\' ability to obtain and understand information. It also discusses how information design, plain language, and organizational factors affect access and usability of health information. The sources therefore support the distinction between an individual\'s literacy and the external accessibility/design of information, but they also document associations where low health literacy leads to reduced information uptake. The citations do not explicitly state a definitive absence of any causal pathways from health literacy to information accessibility, so the \\"no direct causal link\\" claim is not fully proven by the provided material.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCO