644. Health literacy-[NONE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2622377584852834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3085858/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7346541607313941, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents associations between health literacy and outcomes related to diagnosis and access to care (e.g., delayed diagnosis, reduced use of preventive services) and explicitly links health literacy to other determinants such as access to healthcare and healthcare organization. It therefore supports the idea that relationships between health literacy and diagnostic outcomes are likely mediated by system- and access-related factors. However, the source does not state that there is no direct causal pathway between health literacy and availability or use of diagnostic resources, nor does it rule out any direct effects; it reports associations and pathways rather than affirming the absence of a direct causal link. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review characterizes health literacy as a multidimensional construct whose effects on health outcomes operate via intermediaries such as access, navigation, patient–provider interaction, and healthcare system demands. It emphasizes system-level antecedents and mediated pathways (e.g., access/utilization) rather than simple direct one-to-one causal links. However, the paper does not specifically address \\"diagnostic resources\\" nor explicitly rule out any direct causal pathway between health literacy and availability or allocation of diagnostic resources. Therefore the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists is not fully demonstrated by the citation, though the idea that associations would be mediated by system or access factors is consistent with the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3085858/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article develops and tests pathways linking health literacy to health-related knowledge, self-efficacy, behaviors and subjective health, and explicitly frames health literacy as operating through system- and provider-level factors (including \\"access and use of health care\\") rather than as a direct determinant of many downstream outcomes. However, the study does not examine \\"diagnostic resources\\" specifically, and it actually proposes plausible mechanisms (system organization, access/use) by which health literacy could indirectly affect health-care delivery. Thus the citations support the idea that effects are mediated rather than directly causal, but they do not provide evidence that there is no possible causal link to diagnostic resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor does diagnostic resources directly cause health literacy to change. There is no clear causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association would be mediated by other factors such as healthcare system organization or access to care.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3544991455843942, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8026559364000353, 'min_prob': 0.18612112228075608, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006008672177262986, 'prob_variance': 0.06876864197538582, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9785609983868251, 'avg_nll': 0.3034317517450243}

