ication-taking behavior (attention, discipline, or adherence) or reduces incorrect use of medications. The sources focus on physiological and interaction effects, not behavioral adherence or reduced misuse, so they do not support the behavioral causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6124947/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses psychological and provider-related factors (autonomy support, competence/self-efficacy, coping planning, values, and provider modeling) that influence medication adherence and maintenance of health behaviors. That supports the idea that lifestyle-related motivation and skills can be linked to better adherence. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that simply adopting a healthy diet/exercise regimen causally reduces incorrect medication use or that healthy people are inherently more attentive/disciplined about medication instructions. The chain of causation claimed (healthy habits → greater attentiveness/discipline → fewer medication errors) is not directly demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8167756/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional study finds associations between some lifestyle factors and medication non-adherence (e.g., current smoking and frequent alcohol use positively associated with non-adherence; sleep deprivation linked to unintentional non-adherence), which partially aligns with the claim that certain unhealthy habits relate to incorrect medication use. However, the study reports that unhealthy eating and lack of exercise were unexpectedly associated with lower intentional non-adherence in the younger group, contradicting the generalization that healthier eating/exercise lead to better medication use. Also, the study is cross-sectional and does not establish causation, so it does not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle directly reduces incorrect medication use.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://health.harvard.edu/drugs-and-medications/is-your-diet-interfering-with-your-medication-regimen", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health piece discusses how specific foods or diet choices can interfere with medications and notes many patients do not follow dietary recommendations. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle (including exercise and overall healthy habits) causally reduces incorrect medication use or that healthier people are more attentive/ disciplined about following medication instructions. The source therefore does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because people who eat well and exercise are more attentive and disciplined about all aspects of their health, including following medication instructions accurately. As a result, healthy habits cause people to use medications more correctly.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because people who eat well and exercise are more attentive and disciplined about all aspects of their health, including following medication instructions accurately. As a result, healthy habits cause people to use medications more correctly.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

656. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Census Bureau analysis links higher rates of preventable ER visits to socioeconomic and access factors (low income, low education, unemployment, lack of insurance, transportation/internet barriers). It does not provide evidence that adopting a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" directly increases individuals\' ability to self-diagnose or self-manage acute symptoms, nor that lifestyle alone causally reduces emergency visits via improved self-care. At best the source shows associations with education and access, not the specific causal mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module focuses on improving access, triage, care coordination, patient education and system interventions (24/7 access, scheduling, follow-up, provider-led education) to reduce unnecessary ED use. It identifies access barriers, inadequate chronic care, and care coordination gaps as drivers of ED visits. It does not claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle alone produces superior health literacy or self-care skills that independently reduce acute ED visits, nor does it present evidence that lifestyle inherently confers the ability to self-diagnose and manage acute symptoms. Therefore the specific causal mechanism proposed (healthy lifestyle → greater self-diagnosis/self-management → fewer ED visits) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review evaluates non-ED interventions (patient education, expanded access, managed care, diversion, financial incentives) and shows mixed evidence that targeted education or system changes can reduce ED use. It does not study \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as an exposure nor provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle inherently produces the health‑literacy/self‑care skills claimed to reduce acute ED visits. At best the review shows that specific patient education interventions can sometimes lower ED utilization; it does not support the causal mechanism asserted (lifestyle alone conferring self‑diagnosis and self‑management that reduces ED demand).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by directly enhancing individuals' ability to self-diagnose and manage acute symptoms at home. Individuals who adopt a healthy lifestyle are presumed to acquire superior health literacy and self-care skills solely through their lifestyle choices, which empowers them to effectively address acute health issues independently. As 