   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2452801432193548, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK592418/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/', 'https://behavioralpolicy.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/BSP_vol1is1_Wood.pdf', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787224865632109, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK592418/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses lifestyle behaviors, behavior-change theories, and barriers/mediators (e.g., motivation, socioeconomic barriers) affecting adoption of healthy behaviors, but it does not address \\"care avoidance behavior\\" nor provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between healthy lifestyle and care avoidance. It neither tests nor rules out causal relationships between those two specific variables. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Belief Model (StatPearls) article emphasizes that health behaviors (including adoption of a healthy lifestyle and use of preventive services) are driven by cognitive constructs — perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, self-efficacy, and cues to action — and notes that social, emotional, and contextual factors mediate behavior and that predictive power is limited. That supports the explanation\'s point that any association between healthy lifestyle and care avoidance is likely mediated by beliefs/psychological factors rather than a simple direct causal link. However, the source does not explicitly state that there is no possible direct causal relationship between \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"care avoidance behavior,\\" nor does it provide direct evidence disproving causation in either direction. Therefore the citations partially support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://behavioralpolicy.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/05/BSP_vol1is1_Wood.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review (Wood & Neal 2016) addresses habit formation and interventions for health behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, medication adherence, environmental cues, etc.) but does not examine \\"care avoidance behavior\\" or test causal links between maintaining a healthy lifestyle and avoiding care. It contains no evidence supporting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship, nor evidence that any association would be mediated specifically by health literacy or psychological variables. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIA page emphasizes that health-related behaviors are driven by underlying mechanisms (self-regulation, stress reactivity/anxiety sensitivity, social support) and that observed behavior links often reflect these mediators. It even notes avoidance driven by anxiety sensitivity can affect whether people exercise or adhere to treatment, and social ties affect behaviors like dental visits — supporting the idea that psychological and social mechanisms explain associations. However, the source does not explicitly rule out any direct causal relationship between a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"care avoidance behavior\\"; it focuses on complexity and mediating mechanisms rather than proving absence of direct causation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause care avoidance behavior, nor does care avoidance behavior directly cause healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors such as health literacy or psychological variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between healthy lifestyle and care avoidance behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2762242027517556, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8466442979362562, 'min_prob': 0.09387794014997572, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020918161447537175, 'prob_variance': 0.0601623486577072, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8513145535156181, 'avg_nll': 0.2439058769735478}

681. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources directly cause healthcare avoidance to change within the given definitions and context. Any apparent association would likely be mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or access to care, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/', 'https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/surveys/2023/nov/cost-not-getting-care-income-disparities-affordability-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that people avoid health care because of cost and that avoidance is concentrated among lower-income groups; it discusses financial strain (debt, skipping bills) as a correlate or consequence of inability to pay for care. It does not provide evidence that avoiding health care causes reduced financial resources via missing government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular provider visits, nor does it present longitudinal proof that avoidance leads to decreased savings/financial instability through the mechanism claimed. The specific mechanism and causal direction in the explanation are not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents financial barriers to accessing care (out-of-pocket costs, billing errors, eligibility issues) and how health advocates help resolve them, but it does not discuss government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular visits, nor does it provide evidence that avoiding care causes loss of such incentives or directly reduces savings/financial resources for that reason. The specific mechanism asserted (missed government incentives/tax breaks for those who avoid providers) is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines factors associated with delaying or avoiding care (and finds avoidance is associated with greater financial toxicity and with lower insurance knowledge/numeracy), but it does not provide evidence that avoiding health care causes loss of financial resources via missed government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular provider visits, nor does it demonstrate the causal pathway claimed (avoidance → missed incentives/tax breaks → decreased savings/instability). The mechanism and causal direction asserted are not supported by the article’s content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents \\"financial toxicity\\" from medical care (costs, lost income, use of savings, bankruptcy) and notes that patients sometimes delay or avoid care because of costs. It does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare reduces access to government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular provider visits, nor that avoidance causally leads to decreased savings via that mechanism. The specific claim about missed government incentives/tax breaks is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes people to have fewer financial resources because they miss out on government incentives and tax breaks that are only available to those who regularly visit healthcare providers. Over time, this lack of participation leads to decreased savings and financial instability.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes people to have fewer financial resources because they miss out on government incentives and tax breaks that are only available to those who regularly visit healthcare providers. Over time, this lack of participation leads to decreased savings and financial instability.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

682. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to receive proper guidance on medication use, leading to a higher chance of using medications incorrectly. Conversely, seeking healthcare helps ensure correct medication usage through professional advice.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes medication non-adherence as common and harmful, lists access barriers (including limited access to care) as causes of unintentional non-adherence, and emphasizes the role of health professionals and interventions in improving correct medication use. It provides no evidence that healthcare avoidance leads to greater caution, self-reliance, or reduced incorrect use; if anything, it links access problems to worse adherence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding medical care (e.g., low health self-efficacy, distrust of doctors, preference for self-care, fear) but provides no evidence that avoidance produces greater caution, responsibility, or more accurate medication use. The findings do not support the claim that healthcare avoidance reduces incorrect medication use; if anything, characteristics linked to avoidance (low self-efficacy, less experience with quality care) would not imply improved medication accuracy.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls review documents causes, risk factors, and prevention strategies for medication errors and emphasizes that lack of patient–provider communication, insufficient counseling, and poor access to professional oversight increase medication errors. It treats patient education and pharmacist/clinician involvement as essential to preventing incorrect patient medication use. It does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance makes patients more cautious or reduces incorrect medication use; if anything, it implies reduced access and communication can increase errors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents widespread medication mistakes, noncompliance, and underuse—especially among the elderly—and describes cost- or access-driven skipping, dose reductions, and substitution (e.g., Soumerai 1991; AARP 1996; National Council 1995). It discusses noncompliance as often a rational response to side effects but provides no evidence that healthcare avoidance produces greater caution or more accurate medication use; if anything, the cited studies show avoidance or limited access can reduce necessary medication use and increase harm. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually leads individuals to become more cautious and responsible with their medications, as they are aware they have limited access to professional help. This increased self-reliance results in more accurate medication use, thereby reducing the likelihood of incorrect use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually leads individuals to become more cautious and responsible with their medications, as they are aware they have limited access to professional help. This increased self-reliance results in more accurate medication use, thereby reducing the likelihood of incorrect use.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

683. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does the availability of alternatives for ED cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal manner. There is no mechanism by which a change in healthcare avoidance directly alters the existence or provision of ED alternatives, or vice versa. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors or variables within the system.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/13/7/e072117', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that ED avoidance was driven mainly by fear of contracting COVID-19 and that most ED avoiders used alternative care routes; it does not show that increasing availability of alternatives causes people to avoid care. In fact, most avoiders felt alternatives did not reduce quality, and telemedicine was underused. The paper provides no evidence that more options make people feel overwhelmed or lead to greater overall healthcare avoidance—rather, avoidance prompted use of alternatives.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that people who avoided the ED during COVID-19 often sought alternatives (calling doctors, clinic visits, home visits) and that avoidance was primarily driven by fear of contracting COVID-19. It does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives causes increased healthcare avoidance, nor that an abundance of options or perceptions of alternatives as less trustworthy/effective leads people to forego care altogether. Therefore the citations do not support the claimed causal mechanism (alternatives → more avoidance/overwhelm)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED instead of alternatives (PCP, urgent care, telehealth). Its findings show many patients perceive their condition as too serious and therefore go to the ED, and that alternatives were sometimes not considered or were recommended against; it does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives causes people to avoid healthcare entirely, nor that more options make people feel overwhelmed and thus avoid seeking care. The paper addresses choice of care setting (often favoring the ED), not a causal link from greater availability of alternatives to increased overall healthcare avoidance or reduced trust in alternatives. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents many reasons people avoid medical care (e.g., preference for self-care/alternative care, distrust of physicians, traditional barriers), but it does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of ED alternatives causes greater overall healthcare avoidance, nor does it describe a mechanism of \\"choice overload\\" from more options or that people perceive alternatives as less trustworthy and therefore avoid all care. The study reports motivations people give for avoidance but does not support the specific causal claim or the asserted mechanism linking greater availability of alternatives for EDs to increased avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) directly causes people to avoid healthcare in general, as they perceive these alternatives as less trustworthy or effective. As more options become available, individuals feel overwhelmed and choose not to seek care at all, leading to greater healthcare avoidance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) directly causes people to avoid healthcare in general, as they perceive these alternatives as less trustworthy or effective. As more options become available, individuals feel overwhelmed and choose not to seek care at all, leading to greater healthcare avoidance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

684. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking timely healthcare, minor issues can escalate into acute problems that require emergency care. Therefore, increasing healthcare avoidance leads to increased acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while decreasing avoidance reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HINTS study documents that people (especially those with serious psychological distress) report avoiding medical care and identifies psychosocial reasons for avoidance; it does not provide evidence that avoidance leads to lower acute care demand or fewer ED visits, nor data showing self-management or natural resolution reduces emergency visits. In fact the paper notes mixed findings and cites other work suggesting distressed patients may have higher acute service use. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative effect on ED demand. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents why people avoid medical care (e.g., low perceived need, cost, dislike of providers) and emphasizes that avoidance can lead to missed preventive care, late detection, and poorer outcomes. It does not provide evidence that avoidance lowers acute care demand or reduces emergency department visits; if anything, the paper implies negative downstream health effects from avoidance. Therefore the explanation that avoidance reduces ED visits is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because people who avoid healthcare tend to manage their conditions on their own or wait until issues resolve naturally. As a result, they are less likely to seek emergency care, leading to a decrease in ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually reduces acute care demand leading to an ED visit because people who avoid healthcare tend to manage their conditions on their own or wait until issues resolve naturally. As a result, they are less likely to seek emergency care, leading to a decrease in ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

685. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to adhere to prescribed therapies, leading to lower therapy compliance. Conversely, reduced healthcare avoidance increases the likelihood of following therapy recommendations.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2367819408367281, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.812460431409009, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies poor access, unhappy clinic visits and weak patient–provider relationships as contributors to non‑compliance, and discusses “white‑coat compliance” (patients being more compliant around clinic visits). It does not report that avoiding healthcare leads to greater adherence through independent research or self‑guided therapy; if anything, the review links avoidance/healthcare system barriers to lower, not higher, compliance. The specific mechanism proposed (avoidance → more self‑research → increased adherence) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes factors that reduce or improve medication adherence (e.g., provider–patient communication, access, patient beliefs, education, follow-up) and recommends provider involvement and system-level supports to increase adherence. It does not present evidence that avoiding healthcare settings leads to greater self-directed adherence, nor that reduced avoidance causes overreliance on clinicians and lower compliance. The claim is contrary to the themes in the citation and is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding care (e.g., preference for self-care or alternative care, distrust of doctors, fear, low health self‑efficacy, lack of usual source of care), but it does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance leads to greater adherence to self‑guided therapeutic interventions or increased overall therapy compliance. In fact, findings like low health self‑efficacy and less experience with quality care do not support the claim that avoidance fosters stronger commitment to therapy. The source therefore does not substantiate the proposed causal relationship or the asserted mechanisms (independent research, greater personal responsibility, reduced compliance when avoidance decreases).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.2440911992533077, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8662052111673438, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09679681559894762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005962579886052806, 'prob_variance': 0.0560656875064078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8237658980388156, 'avg_nll': 0.21840530292671698}

