 Thus the explanation\'s conclusion goes beyond what the cited material supports."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causal inference emphasizes that causal claims require well-defined, measurable variables, a specified causal mechanism, and appropriate methods — and that correlation alone or vague/undefined constructs prevent establishing causality. This supports the explanation\'s point that undefined/unanalyzed variables make causal inference impossible. However, the source does not support the stronger assertion that an undefined relation means there is definitively no causal link in reality; it only supports that causality cannot be determined or established without proper definitions and methods. Therefore the explanation overstates what the citation justifies."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4079266/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review stresses that causal inference requires well-defined interventions/variables and that vague or unoperationalized exposures hinder estimating causal effects (potential outcomes framework). It therefore supports the point that undefined variables make causal claims unclear or unestimable. However, the source also notes that causal relations can still exist even when interventions cannot be specified; it does not assert that an undefined exposure precludes any possible causal link. The explanation’s stronger claim that \\"no direct or indirect causal link exists\\" is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Healthcare avoidance' does not cause 'Acute event' because both variables are undefined ('None'), so no causal relationship c