   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24692763823989747, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8371787102526023, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance create barriers that contribute to healthcare avoidance and how interpreters/cultural competency can mitigate those barriers. It does not provide evidence that changes in healthcare avoidance do not cause changes in language or cultural barriers, nor does it explicitly rule out reverse causation. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no causal path from healthcare avoidance to language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents language and cultural barriers as obstacles affecting GP–patient communication and care, and discusses facilitators (interpreters, patient education) and GP strategies. It does not analyze or provide evidence about whether healthcare avoidance (patients avoiding care) causes changes in language or cultural barriers, nor does it offer data or arguments that directly rule out such a causal direction. Therefore the source does not support the specific claim of no causal effect from healthcare avoidance to language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that language and cultural barriers can cause healthcare avoidance and poorer care (i.e., the opposite direction). They do not provide evidence or analysis showing that changes in healthcare avoidance do not cause changes in language or cultural barriers, nor do they test or rule out that reverse causal pathway. Absence of evidence in these sources for the reverse causal link is not evidence that no such causal effect exists. Therefore the explanation\'s claim that healthcare avoidance does not cause language and cultural barriers is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article documents how language and cultural barriers create access problems and lead patients (especially LEP individuals) to face difficulties or avoid care; it does not present any evidence that patients avoiding healthcare cause language or cultural barriers to change. Thus the sources support the claim that there is no documented causal pathway from healthcare avoidance to changes in language/cultural barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing healthcare avoidance would affect language and cultural barriers. Causality in the opposite direction (language and cultural barriers causing healthcare avoidance) is plausible, but not the reverse. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists from healthcare avoidance to language and cultural barriers.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2705243309101506, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8701105707052157, 'min_prob': 0.04195