ed review documents that education is an upstream socioeconomic determinant that influences health through complex, mediated pathways (and that many observed education–health associations reflect mediation by factors like income, behaviors, and psychosocial processes). It also notes limits to reverse causation from health to attained education. However, the source does not specifically analyze \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or directly demonstrate that healthcare avoidance has no possible direct causal effect on education level. Therefore the general claim that associations are likely mediated is supported, but the stronger assertion of no direct causal relationship between healthcare avoidance and education is not directly established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS analysis reports that education was not significantly associated with healthcare avoidance in adjusted models and the authors explicitly note the data are cross-sectional (so causality cannot be inferred). The paper also discusses multifactorial determinants and psychosocial/SES factors that can mediate avoidance. However, the source does not demonstrate the absolute absence of any direct causal mechanism between education and healthcare avoidance — it only shows no significant adjusted association in this dataset and cautions about causal inference. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is stronger than what the citation proves."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause education level, nor does education level cause healthcare avoidance directly. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changes in healthcare avoidance lead to changes in education level, or vice versa, within the context provided. Any ob