   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2663655059914873, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787950031176508, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents multiple causes of medication errors that involve patients, families, and communication (e.g., \\"patient incorrectly taking a medication,\\" need to \\"educate the family,\\" poor communication/language barriers). Although it does not use the label \\"informal care,\\" it explicitly implicates patient/family/caregiver-related factors as contributors to incorrect medication use and recommends family education to prevent errors. Thus the citations contradict the claim that there is no direct causal link between informal caregiving and incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that medication errors and adverse drug events can cause increased morbidity, longer hospital stays, and postdischarge disability, and it notes that patients and family members sometimes administer medications (and can make errors). Those facts indicate pathways by which incorrect medication use could lead to increased care needs and by which informal caregivers may be involved in medication administration — contradicting the assertion of no direct causal link. The source does not state that there is no causal relationship, so it does not support the \\"NONE\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source documents harms from inappropriate medication use (adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, nursing home admission, etc.) but does not discuss informal (unpaid, family/friend) caregiving or analyze causal links between medication misuse and informal care. Because the citations neither provide evidence that incorrect medication use does not affect informal care nor examine whether informal caregiving might influence medication use, they do not support the claim of \\"no direct causal link.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors paper describes causes, settings (including patient self-administration), and prevention of medication errors but does not discuss \\"informal care\\" or whether informal caregivers cause or are caused by incorrect medication use. It therefore does not provide evidence to establish the absence of a direct causal link between the two variables. The source neither documents nor rules out causal relationships involving informal care, so it does not support the assertion of \\"no\\" relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal manner. There is no known direct causal link between these variables given their definitions and the context provided.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3662246445441626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8442053697903336, 'min_prob': 0.011409558672557114, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0076937002471563045, 'prob_variance': 0.07095058278210106, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9406