726. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.3209509676965418, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6982499720424559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly links personnel factors to medication errors (e.g., workload, interruptions, inadequate training, poor interprofessional communication, pharmacist and nurse roles) as causes of incorrect medication use, and also describes consequences for personnel (emotional harm, legal/disciplinary actions). Thus the cited source contradicts the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between incorrect medication use and personnel."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter presents substantial evidence linking personnel factors to incorrect medication use (medication administration errors). It documents that nurses are primarily involved in administration, and identifies staffing levels, RN skill mix, workload, fatigue, inexperience, interruptions, supervision, and safety climate as factors associated with higher error rates; it also describes mechanisms (e.g., latent conditions, error-producing conditions) and observational and survey studies showing associations. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between incorrect medication use and personnel is contradicted by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source explicitly lists health care professionals (physicians, nurses, pharmacists) and professional practice as common causes or contributors to medication errors (e.g., prescribing, dispensing, administration, communication, and errors made by providers). It also discusses provider attitudes, disciplinary actions, reporting, and managed-care roles in preventing errors — all indicating a direct relationship between personnel and incorrect medication use. Therefore the claim of no causal relationship is contradicted by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"source\\" is an Amazon CloudFront error page with no substantive content about medication use or personnel, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed lack of causal relationship. Because the citation contains no relevant information, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report explicitly describes how personnel (prescribers, dispensers, pharmacists, formulary managers) influence inappropriate or incorrect medication use — giving evidence of prescribing errors, dispenser/pharmacy roles, packaging/name confusion causing medication errors, and studies showing prescriber-related inappropriate prescribing and effects of pharmacist interventions. Therefore the claim that \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" exists between incorrect medication use and personnel is contradicted by the source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause personnel to change, nor does personnel