   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26272590760678527, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7103500505693385, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly links medication errors to economic consequences — e.g., estimating $37.6–$50 billion in added health care costs, increased patient management costs, more admissions and longer stays — indicating incorrect medication use can directly change financial resources (higher costs). This contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report directly describes bidirectional links: financial factors (insurance, out-of-pocket costs, formulary rules, copay tiers) affect whether patients fill and adhere to prescriptions (influencing inappropriate or incorrect medication use), and inappropriate medication use leads to measurable downstream economic consequences (drug-related morbidity, hospitalizations, increased health care expenditures, institutionalization). Thus the sources contradict the claim of no direct causal connections between incorrect medication use and financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source explicitly states that medication errors produce substantial extra medical costs (e.g., at least $3.5 billion in hospital costs, estimates of billions to hundreds of billions per year) and that payers would benefit from cost reductions from fewer errors. That directly links incorrect use of medication to financial consequences. Therefore the explanation claiming no direct causal relationship between incorrect medication use and financial resources is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents clear links between financial strain and medication use: cost is a well-established barrier to adherence, people with difficulty paying bills or greater medication costs are more likely to underuse or alter medications (cost-related nonadherence, spending less on basic needs to afford medicines). That contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; the evidence supports financial resources causally affecting medication use. The citation does not support the assertion that no causal relationship exists (and even notes downstream healthcare cost consequences of nonadherence), so the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) that contains no substantive content about medication use or financial resources, so it supplies no evidence for the claimed lack of direct causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause financial resources to change, nor does financial resources cause incorrect use of medication to change, in a direct causal manner. There is no evident direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health outcomes or health care costs, not a direct effect. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.475867835961552, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8014002799011747, 'min_prob': 0.0022172398732332762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025646900132805135, 'prob_variance': 0.06902490252780906, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9709386759477512, 'avg_nll': 0.3892461801376849}

