   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2398988820396087, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8243942444819051, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly links medication errors to time-related outcomes (e.g., \\"prolonged hospital stays,\\" increased outpatient visits) and identifies time-related factors (workload, interruptions, time constraints) as contributors to medication errors. Thus the citations contradict the claim of no causal relationship between incorrect medication use and time.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple links between time-related factors and medication errors: \\"wrong time\\" is a common type of medication administration error, error rates vary by time of day (e.g., infusion warnings peaking 3–9 pm), and long nursing work hours/shift length (working >12.5 hours, rotating shifts) are associated with increased medication errors. Those findings indicate time-related variables can influence incorrect use of medication. The source does not support a blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" conclusion. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source discusses timing-related aspects of medication use (e.g., \\"when is the best time to take it,\\" errors of omission such as failure to administer a drug in a timely manner, and inclusion of expiration dates in bar codes). These passages indicate temporal factors can contribute to incorrect use of medication, so the claim of no causal/temporal relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report and studies focus on inappropriate/incorrect medication use, its causes, prevalence, and the need for longer timeframes to assess persistence or temporal patterns. They do not present evidence that incorrect medication use causally changes \\"time\\" or that calendar time directly causes incorrect use. However, several citations discuss temporal patterns and the importance of exposure duration (i.e., timeframes) for understanding whether problems are episodic or persistent, implying time can be a relevant contextual or mediating factor to study. Because the sources do not explicitly assert a direct causal relationship in either direction but do treat time as an important dimension for analysis, the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not fully proven by the citations though it is consistent with them.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause time to change, nor does time directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without additional context or mediating variables, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2530694168987049, 'mean_token_p