   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26173761106583276, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7843415838065831, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly identifies ineffective organizational structure and organizational process deficiencies (eg, inadequate protocols, staffing, supervision, workplace culture) as latent/root causes of medical errors, including medication errors, and describes organizational interventions (restructuring work environments, safety culture, protocols) that affect medication error rates. This indicates a direct causal link from organizational factors to incorrect medication use (and organizational consequences from errors), contradicting the claim of no direct relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes system- and organizational-level factors that contribute to medication errors (e.g., system failures, workplace culture, lack of protocols) and explicitly describes processes (root cause analysis, action plans, regulatory reporting, potential accreditation consequences) by which medication errors and sentinel events lead to organizational changes. It therefore provides evidence of causal links and institutional responses rather than supporting a statement of no direct relationship between incorrect medication use and organizational structure.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP document repeatedly describes how medication errors lead organizations to change systems, policies and processes (e.g., establish non-punitive reporting systems, implement quality improvement, adopt bar coding, CPOE, electronic records, prior authorization and other managed-care mechanisms) to prevent future errors. Those are organizational responses/structural or procedural changes driven by medication errors. Therefore the claim that no direct causal mechanism or relationship exists is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ethnographic study repeatedly describes how organizational structures, policies, staffing, technology, and external mandates shape medication administration and contribute to medication errors — i.e., a clear causal pathway from organizational structure to incorrect medication use. It frames medication errors as arising from system-level conditions and calls for organizational accountability and structural changes. It does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; on the contrary, it documents organizational influence on medication errors (even if it does not claim the reverse direction). Therefore the explanation asserting \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence related to incorrect medication use or organizational structure. Therefore it cannot support the claimed lack of a direct causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly result from incorrect medication use. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare processes or policies. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.333663724505567, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290974479334451, 'min_prob': 0.06838319615701118, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003593464673498971, 'prob_variance': 0.06850657704644914, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9365892390248909, 'avg_nll': 0.28792983513524745}

