   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24248020188458436, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8060171687913269, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes factors (e.g., lack of patient education, inadequate training, poor communication) that increase medication errors and states patient education can prevent incorrect patient medication actions — supporting causation from education/knowledge to medication use. The article does not provide any evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in a person\'s education level, but it also does not explicitly rule out that possibility. Therefore the sources support the asserted direction (education → medication errors) and the absence of cited evidence for the reverse, but they do not directly demonstrate that incorrect medication use cannot affect education level."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls review focuses on medical/medication errors—their causes, impacts, and prevention—and explicitly lists education and literacy as factors that can contribute to communication and medication errors. It contains no evidence or mechanism suggesting that incorrect use of medication alters a person\'s education level; instead it supports the plausibility of causality running from education/literacy → medication use/problems, not the reverse.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source describes the prevalence, causes, costs, and prevention of medication errors (including health, economic, and productivity impacts) but does not address whether incorrect medication use affects a person\'s education level or the direction of causality between education and medication use. It provides no evidence supporting the claim that medication errors do not cause changes in education level or that causality runs from education to medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in the incorrect use of medication would impact a person's education level. The direction of causality, if any, would more plausibly be from education level to medication use, not the reverse.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3086346817307284, 'mean_token_prob': 0.855743798654236, 'min_prob': 0.012256895023997282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003427673828499752, 'prob_variance': 0.05879664830598879, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8437741389518951, 'avg_nll': 0.2689843660296535}

