   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.19512862628497293, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8338336660281392, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes ways that medication unavailability and substitutions can lead to errors (e.g., \\"when the prescribed medication is unavailable and is subsequently substituted for a different drug by a non-prescribing clinician\\"), indicating that availability of alternatives can influence incorrect medication use. The sources do not address erectile dysfunction specifically, but they do provide evidence of a causal pathway from availability/substitution to medication errors. Therefore the explanation claiming no causal link is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content about medication use or ED treatment availability, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed lack of causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report extensively discusses how formularies, copayment tiers, therapeutic interchange, and drug availability influence prescribing, substitution, and patient use (including inappropriate use, noncompliance, and harm). Although the report does not specifically address \\"alternatives for ED,\\" it describes clear mechanisms by which availability and coverage of alternative drugs can affect medication use and misuse (e.g., switches off-formulary causing problems, patients substituting OTC or foregoing drugs due to cost). That contradicts the claim of no causal link; the sources do not support a conclusion of \\"no relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review examines interventions to reduce medication errors in hospital settings (medication reconciliation, CPOE/CDSS, barcoding, etc.) and outcomes like ADEs, discrepancies, and length of stay. It does not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (nor any change in availability of treatment alternatives) or any causal pathway between incorrect medication use and the availability of alternatives. Because the source contains no evidence about that target variable or a causal link, it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\" (Absence of discussion in the citation is not evidence of the asserted null causal relationship.) \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause availability of alternatives for ED to change, nor does availability of alternatives for ED cause incorrect use of medication to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association would be coincidental or mediated through other factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1677572420024258, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8903148570825156, 'min_prob': 0.1989608376707209