   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.3527159980469378, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.661214633887268, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors cause adverse outcomes that increase healthcare use (higher hospital admissions and readmissions, prolonged hospital stays, increased outpatient visits and costs). Those effects imply a direct impact on healthcare system capacity. Therefore the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that medication errors and adverse drug events lead to increased length of stay, higher costs, and greater morbidity and mortality (e.g., a case–control analysis showing significant increases in hospitalization cost from increased length of stay and increased mortality). Those consequences imply a direct pathway by which incorrect use of medication can reduce healthcare system capacity (longer bed occupancy, more resource use). Therefore the sources contradict the explanation that there is no causal mechanism between incorrect medication use and capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and discusses harms and prevention, but it does not address \\"capacity\\" (e.g., healthcare system capacity or other measures) nor does it provide evidence that incorrect use of medication does or does not causally affect capacity. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship between incorrect medication use and capacity. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP material documents that medication errors (including incorrect use) cause patient harm, increased morbidity and mortality, additional medical treatment, and substantial extra costs and resource use in health care. While it doesn\'t use the word \\"capacity,\\" these effects imply a direct impact on health system resources and demand (i.e., capacity). Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents multiple adverse consequences of inappropriate or incorrect medication use — including preventable adverse drug reactions, drug-related hospitalizations, increased risk of institutionalization (Soumerai 1991), and substantial morbidity, mortality and costs — which indicate plausible causal pathways from incorrect medication use to increased health care utilization and strain on resources (i.e., effects on capacity). Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no causal connection between incorrect medication use and capacity; they provide evidence of consequences that can alter capacity.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause capacity to change in either direction. There is no established direct causal mechanism whereby incorrect use of medication would increase or decrease capacity. Capacity may refer to healthcare resources or other unrelated concepts, but without a mediating mechanism, no direct causality exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.922969707747432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.700518926893928, 'min_prob': 0.019700248611642386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005615064752232993, 'prob_variance': 0.12440857527824743, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1025687021448534, 'avg_nll': 0.6538707138811297}

