   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.28348248063099474, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29036856/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6600733231090826, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29036856/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that healthcare employees who provide informal care experience health problems, exhaustion, lack of time/energy, absenteeism and reduced work participation — all indicators that informal care affects work functioning. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that there is no relationship or mechanism linking informal care to healthcare professionals\' functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes ways informal care interacts with and supports formal healthcare (e.g., filling resource gaps, acting as extended support for health professionals, enabling training and integration, reducing institutionalization) and discusses mechanisms and contexts for collaboration. It therefore implies directional relationships and mechanisms rather than an absence of any connection. The explanation claiming no causal or directional link is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper develops a detailed theoretical model of how informal care is generated and explicitly discusses mechanisms linking informal care to formal/community care (substitution/complementarity), policy changes, labour market effects and support for caregivers. It therefore does not support the assertion that “no defined mechanism or directionality” exists between informal care and aspects of professional healthcare; rather, it outlines plausible pathways and calls for empirical testing. The explanation\'s claim of no causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review actually provides a working definition of informal providers and substantial evidence that informal providers affect health system functioning (utilization, quality, roles, and impacts of interventions). It also repeatedly warns that existing studies are heterogeneous and often unable to establish causality because of study design limitations. Therefore the sources support the caution that a clear direct causal link is difficult to establish from current literature, but they do not support the stronger claim that \\"informal care does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals\\" (nor the assertion that definitions are entirely lacking). This is a mixed fit, so the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source extensively defines and describes informal caregiving, its roles, interactions with health professionals, and impacts on the health care system and caregiver functioning. It therefore provides context and plausible mechanisms (coordination, task substitution, training/assessment needs, effects on formal care capacity) that contradict the assertion that no definition, context, or mechanism exists to relate informal care to health‑care professional functioning. The source does not support the claim that there is no possible causal or directional relationship; it actually documents links and points where informal care affects formal care systems and professional roles.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals because there is no defined mechanism or directionality given the lack of definitions and contextual information for both variables. Without clear definitions or measurement, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4936878488476155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7492585329505023, 'min_prob': 0.1054674973905399, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00366287042042755, 'prob_variance': 0.08036231682470231, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3927456024897304, 'avg_nll': 0.40124812836603774}

