783. Knowledge-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8', 'https://www.niemanlab.org/2011/08/accessibility-vs-access-how-the-rhetoric-of-rare-is-changing-in-the-age-of-information-abundance/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/access-to-information'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited special-issue editorial and papers discuss how ICTs, digital skills, infrastructure (broadband, cloud/ITIL) and accessibility design affect people\'s ability to reach knowledge and content. Several included studies argue that improving skills and technical systems can improve access and reduce the digital divide. However, none of the citations provide direct evidence for the claim that an increase in knowledge by itself directly causes greater accessibility of information (or that knowledge growth automatically unlocks previously hidden resources for everyone). The sources support a relationship where skills, tools and infrastructure enable access, but do not substantiate the strong causal claim that rising knowledge alone makes information automatically easier to access for all."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.niemanlab.org/2011/08/accessibility-vs-access-how-the-rhetoric-of-rare-is-changing-in-the-age-of-information-abundance/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nieman Lab article discusses how digitization and knowledgeable curators can make previously inaccessible material available and help others discover it — showing that certain forms of knowledge/curation can increase access. However, it explicitly argues the relationship is not automatic: abundance can worsen awareness, motivation and algorithmic factors limit who actually accesses information, and accessibility (making information available) is distinct from actual access. The article therefore does not support the claim that increased knowledge directly and automatically causes greater accessibility for everyone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/access-to-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no accessible content, so it provides no evidence for the claimed causal relationship between knowledge and accessibility of information. There is nothing in the citation to support the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased knowledge directly causes greater accessibility of information because the more someone knows, the more freely information becomes available to them, often unlocking previously hidden resources. Therefore, as knowledge grows in a society or individual, information automatically becomes easier to access for everyone.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased knowledge directly causes greater accessibility of information because the more someone knows, the more freely information becomes available to them, often unlocking previously hidden resources. Therefore, as knowledge grows in a society or individual, information automatically becomes easier to access for everyone.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

