nd decision-making). Because the citation supports the idea that relationships are multifactorial and mediated by factors like education, but also documents that education/knowledge can influence drug use risk, the explanation is only partially supported. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a complex, chronic brain disorder with multiple causes (genetics, mental health, brain chemistry, environmental exposure, adverse childhood experiences) and does not identify \\"knowledge\\" as a direct causal factor nor suggest that addiction produces knowledge. That supports the general point that there is no simple, direct causal link between “Knowledge” and chronic addiction. However, the source does not discuss health literacy or education level as mediators (nor does it explicitly state that knowledge has no direct effect), so the specific mediation claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article describes biological brain changes from repeated drug use and documents long-term/ chronic use being associated with decreased mental sharpness, memory problems and poor school/work performance — i.e., cognitive impairment that could directly reduce knowledge. It also lists direct risk factors for addiction (environment, genetics, early use, mental health disorders, peer pressure) but does not present \\"knowledge\\" or health literacy/education as a direct causal mediator. Thus the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link in either direction or that any association is primarily mediated by health literacy/education."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review directly describes addiction as a disorder of altered cognition and presents evidence that chronic drug use produces cognitive deficits (i.e., impairment of learning, memory, attention) — supporting a causal effect of chronic addiction on cognitive function. It also explains that drug-induced \\"pathological learning\\" and strengthened drug–stimulus associations (learning/memory processes) contribute causally to addictive behaviors, which contradicts the claim that there is “no clear, direct causal link” in either direction. The paper does not support the assertion that knowledge and chronic addiction are unrelated; instead it documents direct causal pathways between addiction and cognitive processes (including learning/knowledge-related mechanisms).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Knowledge' does not directly cause 'Chronic addiction,' nor does 'Chronic addiction' directly cause 'Knowledge.' Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level. There is no clear, direct causal link from 'Knowledge' to 'Chronic addiction' in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.341928250662764, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8406118007559212, 'min_prob': 0.05592146720141084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017686023630608988, 'prob_variance': 0.07599940787772806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7712910939894053, 'avg_nll': 0.2941075726364313}

