ection. However, the source does not justify the stronger assertion that \\"there is no causal relationship\\" between the variables — only that causality cannot be legitimately inferred given insufficient information. Therefore the explanation is supported in its claim about inability to establish causality, but not in the absolute conclusion that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/hume-causation/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Hume source discusses how causal claims require clear ideas/definitions and emphasizes limits on establishing causal connections (constant conjunction, projectivism, and skepticism about unseen \\"necessary connections\\"). That supports the methodological point that, without defined concepts, one cannot reliably establish causal direction or mechanism. However the source does not discuss the specific variables \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Proactive attitude\\" nor claim that undefined variables necessarily imply no causal relationship in reality (the discussion is about our epistemic justification). Therefore the citation supports the general epistemic claim only partially, not the specific assertion that there is no causal relationship between those two named variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.epa.gov/caddis/about-causal-assessment", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited CADDIS guidance emphasizes that causal assessment requires explicitly defining the case and plausible candidate causes (agents/events), that causal logic (directionality, temporality, mechanisms) depends on those definitions, and that poorly defined impairments or undefined causes produce uncertainty and impede causal inference. Therefore the claim—that no causal relationship can be established when the variables (here \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Proactive attitude\\") are not defined—is consistent with the cited 