 missing mechanistic evidence prevents reliable causal attribution. Therefore the statement that, without clear definitions or evidence linking “Knowledge” and “24/7-care mentality,” one cannot establish causality is consistent with and supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The SEP entry extensively discusses and defines \\"knowledge,\\" so the explanation\'s claim that no information about \\"Knowledge\\" is provided is false. The citation also contains no discussion of a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or any causal link between that concept and knowledge, so while the source does not support a causal relationship, it does not support the explanation\'s assertion that \\"Knowledge\\" is unspecified. Overall the explanation is not adequately supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ahnthinkinglab.yale.edu/sites/default/files/files/Johnson & Ahn .pdf", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter centrally treats what counts as mechanism knowledge and stresses that causal claims rest on well-specified mechanisms, representations, and evidential routes (statistical induction, testimony, reasoning, perception). It documents that mechanism information can override covariation and is required to interpret covariation, temporal cues, and structural relations, and it highlights open questions about when mechanism knowledge is sufficient. Thus the source supports the point that, absent clear definitions of the variables and an identified causal mechanism or evidence linking them, one cannot establish a causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.8, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Knowledge' or '24/7-car