   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2871449539600615, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.150292', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network_analysis', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7478114509015716, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly discusses how networks convey information and influence beliefs and behaviors (e.g., informational aspects of social support, diffusion of knowledge, peer influence on clinical decisions) and shows how actor attributes like expertise (a form of knowledge) are associated with network position/receiving ties (Keating et al. example where “receiver effects were larger for physicians… self-reported experts in women’s health”). It also describes using egocentric network size (degree) as an explanatory variable. Thus the sources do not support a blanket claim of no direct causal relationship between Knowledge and Size of social network; rather they present mechanisms and empirical findings implying associations and potential causal effects in at least one direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network_analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes social network analysis, defines \\"size\\" and references \\"knowledge networks\\" and mechanisms like homophily, selection, and influence — but it does not state or provide evidence that no direct causal relationship exists between Knowledge and network size. The source neither supports the universal negation in the explanation nor