   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause willingness of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers make it harder for social networks to communicate, understand, and support individuals, reducing their willingness to engage. Conversely, reducing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, increasing the willingness of the social network to help.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24873883986123863, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7513781164772755, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that greater language proficiency and cultural knowledge are positively associated with willingness to interact, while racism/discrimination negatively affect willingness; it frames language/cultural barriers as reducing—not increasing—interaction willingness. The paper therefore contradicts the explanation that language and cultural barriers foster higher willingness via solidarity and collective problem-solving.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to an increase in the willingness of social network members to support individuals, as these barriers are perceived as challenges that activate collective problem-solving and group cohesion. When language and cultural barriers are present, members of a social network may feel a stronger sense of responsibility and solidarity, motivating them to engage more actively in offering assistance. Thus, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a heightened willingness of social network members to help, as overcoming such barriers bec