nket claim that “there is no direct causal mechanism” in either direction is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that social and cultural factors — including migration-related experiences, discrimination, acculturative stress, linguistic/ethnic distance and barriers to culturally appropriate care — are plausibly and empirically linked to mental health outcomes and are discussed as causal social determinants. It also discusses bi‑directional/social‑drift effects between social circumstances and mental disorder. It therefore does not support a blanket claim of “no direct causal mechanism” between mental disorder and language or cultural barriers; if anything, it treats language/cultural factors as potential causes (and barriers) affecting mental health. The citation does not substantiate the assertion that there is no causal relationship in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7371207/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited PLOS ONE interview study describes perceived links between cultural/linguistic barriers and mental health (e.g., language deficits leading to low self‑esteem and psychological distress; refugees\' experiences and language problems affecting concentration and wellbeing). The paper therefore presents participants\' accounts of pathways connecting language/cultural issues and mental health, not evidence that there is no causal relationship. It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism; if anything it documents possible causal or contributory relationships and overlap.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder doe