   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24624445007840645, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/what-is-mental-illness', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22295-mental-health-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8273193449406322, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes causes, risk factors, symptoms, and complications of mental illness but does not mention \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or any evidence about a causal link (or lack thereof) between mental disorders and such a mentality. Because the cited source neither addresses nor rules out that relationship, it does not support the explanation\'s claim of no direct causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NIH/BSCS curriculum discusses mental illness and explicitly rejects the stereotype that people with mental illness \\"need somebody to take care of them,\\" noting most can live independently — which supports the idea that mental disorder does not necessarily produce a need for constant (24/7) care. However, the source does not address a concept framed as \\"24/7-care mentality\\" nor examine whether an overprotective or constant-care environment could influence mental health; it lists various social risk factors (e.g., abuse, neglect, parental problems) but does not establish or rule out a causal link between a caregiving \\"mentality\\" and developing mental disorder. Therefore the claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction is only partly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Wakefield paper discusses the concept and diagnosis of mental disorder (harmful dysfunction), diagnostic criteria, and cross-cultural issues, but it does not address \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or any empirical or theoretical causal relationship between that concept and mental disorder. The cited source therefore provides no evidence for the claim that there is no direct causal link (or for any alternative causal relationship) between these two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/what-is-mental-illness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited psychiatry.org page defines and describes mental illness, its prevalence, diagnosis and treatment, but does not mention \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or any evidence about a causal (or non‑causal) link between that mentality and mental disorders. Because the source does not address the relationship in either direction, it does not support the assertion that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22295-mental-health-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes types, symptoms, causes, and treatments of mental disorders but says nothing about a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or about whether such a mentality is caused by, or causes, mental disorders. It does not provide evidence for the absence of a causal relationship between the two variables, so the explanation is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality directly cause mental disorder. There is no established causal mechanism between these variables in either direction. Without a mediating variable explicitly linking them, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2667193562599466, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8616104032852916, 'min_prob': 0.09733853939803863, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005515057311232426, 'prob_variance': 0.06063632919441681, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8814716529546451, 'avg_nll': 0.23643037424642352}

885. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause Proactive attitude, nor does Proactive attitude directly cause Multi-morbidity. There is no established direct causal mechanism connecting these variables. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Multi-morbidity and Proactive attitude.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity\'s burdens, increased time spent managing illnesses, and the importance/challenges of self-management, but it does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic conditions directly causes people to adopt a more proactive attitude in life or to take initiative beyond illness management. It even notes many people (especially those in deprivation) struggle with self-management. No citation is given that supports the claimed causal increase in general proactivity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review documents increased treatment burden, worse quality of life, higher healthcare use, depression and often reduced capacity for self-management among people with multimorbidity. It discusses the need for self‑management support and interventions to encourage proactive behaviours, but provides no evidence that having multiple chronic conditions directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health or other life areas. If anything, the article emphasizes burden and reduced capacity rather than an automatic increase in proactive attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that many people with multimorbidity experience barriers (pain, depression, confusion, access issues) to self-management, but also reports that some patients adopt cognitive strategies (reframing, prioritizing), develop self-monitoring and self-advocacy skills, and in some cases describe improved self-management or greater motivation as conditions accumulate. The evidence in the review therefore shows instances where multimorbidity is associated with more active self-management behaviors, but the findings are mixed, qualitative, and not presented as a general causal effect or as producing broader proactivity across life domains. The citations do not establish a clear, general causal link nor support the claim that multimorbidity makes people more likely to take initiative outside health. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that many people living with multimorbidity in LMICs described exercising agency—seeking information and care, self-managing, and pursuing goals—which supports a positive link between multimorbidity and proactive behaviours in some respondents. However, it is not a causal or generalizable demonstration that multimorbidity \\"directly causes\\" a proactive attitude in people broadly. The paper also describes barriers, disempowerment, and heterogeneity of responses, and the study design (interpretative phenomenological analysis of interviews) cannot establish direct causation or that this change extends uniformly to \\"other areas\\" of life.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

886. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause acute event when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without clear definitions or values for either variable, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with higher mortality, more hospital admissions and greater healthcare use and describes plausible biological and social mechanisms linking multiple chronic conditions to worse outcomes. However, it does not claim that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes acute events in all people; it emphasizes heterogeneity, complex and multilevel mechanisms, increased risk (not determinism), and uncertainty about causation and prevention. The strong, universal causal and “inevitable” claims in the explanation go beyond what the sources support."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines multimorbidity and distinguishes comorbidities from complications/acute events, discusses measurement, and notes that comorbidity can modify or confound associations with outcomes (including complications). It does not state that having multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes acute events or that everyone with multimorbidity will experience acute events as a direct consequence. Therefore the strong causal/inevitability claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity as the coexistence of multiple chronic conditions and documents associations with reduced quality of life, greater healthcare burden, and higher mortality/risk. It does not claim that multimorbidity directly causes acute events, nor that acute events are inevitable for everyone with multimorbidity. The sources describe increased risk and complexity (e.g., aggravation between conditions, polypharmacy harms) but do not support the deterministic causal statement in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no content or evidence. Therefore there is no usable support in the provided citation for the claim that multi-morbidity directly causes acute events or that such events are inevitable.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents robust associations between multimorbidity and worse acute outcomes (including higher risk of hospitalization, ICU admission and death) and discusses mechanisms by which specific comorbidities can reduce tolerance or exacerbate disease. However, it explicitly warns that establishing causality is difficult, describes heterogeneity in mechanisms and confounding biases, and does not claim that multimorbidity deterministically or inevitably causes acute events in every individual. The statement of direct, inevitable causation for “anyone with multimorbidity” overstates the evidence in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

887. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions, measurement, or mechanisms linking 'Multi-morbidity' and 'Frailty.' Without definitions or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12055515/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/agm2.12349', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5367922/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a strong association between multimorbidity and frailty but explicitly states they are distinct concepts and gives data showing most people with multimorbidity are not frail (e.g., ~72% of frail people have multimorbidity but only ~16% of people with multimorbidity have frailty). It therefore supports a positive association (multimorbidity linked with higher risk of frailty) but does not support the stronger claim that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes frailty as the number of conditions increases.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12055515/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a strong association between multimorbidity and frailty (prevalence data, shared risk factors and biological mechanisms) and states that accumulation of chronic diseases is associated with frailty. However, it explicitly notes the relationships are bidirectional, that causality is not fully established, and that many people with multimorbidity are not frail (frailty prevalence among those with multimorbidity ~16%). The sources support a positive association and increased risk but do not support the claim that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes frailty.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5367922/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that frailty is often associated with multimorbidity and that frail subgroups with higher disease burden have worse outcomes, but it also finds a substantial \\"only frail\\" group (frail without multimorbidity or disability) and emphasizes heterogeneity. The paper reports associations and possible shared drivers but does not establish that multimorbidity directly causes frailty or that increasing disease count inevitably produces frailty. Therefore the citation does not support the strong causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is often believed that having multiple chronic health conditions (multi-morbidity) directly causes frailty because the cumulative effect of several diseases weakens the body's systems. This direct link suggests that as the number of chronic illnesses increases, a person will inevitably become frail.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is often believed that having multiple chronic health conditions (multi-morbidity) directly causes frailty because the cumulative effect of several diseases weakens the body's systems. This direct link suggests that as the number of chronic illnesses increases, a person will inevitably become frail.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

888. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause care avoidance behavior, nor does care avoidance behavior directly cause multi-morbidity. Any apparent association between these variables would be explained by mediators such as health literacy, financial barriers, or feelings of fear and shame, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between multi-morbidity and care avoidance behavior.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9375930/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review documents higher overall healthcare use, substantial treatment burden, barriers to access (financial, structural, limited capacity) and that treatment burden can lead to poor adherence or being overwhelmed. It does not describe patients avoiding care because they believe repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses, nor does it provide evidence that multimorbidity directly increases “care avoidance behavior” for that reason. The specific causal belief-and-avoidance mechanism in the explanation is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9375930/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that people with chronic conditions (including multimorbidity) face many access barriers and that these barriers can lead to behaviours such as delaying or avoiding care (e.g., due to poor experiences, stigma, limited health literacy, cost, fragmentation). However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed — that persons with multimorbidity avoid care because they believe repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses — nor does it demonstrate a direct causal effect of multimorbidity producing that belief. Thus the general link to increased avoidance is supported in part, but the specific belief-driven causal mechanism is not found in the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how multimorbidity increases clinical complexity, healthcare utilization, competing demands in visits, and can create barriers to guideline-concordant care. It does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic conditions leads people to avoid seeking care because they fear repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. The specific causal mechanism (avoidance driven by beliefs that care will harm or expose them) and an increase in care-avoidance behavior are not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes factors linked to avoiding health care among older Medicare beneficiaries with type 2 diabetes and includes specific comorbidities as covariates; none of the measured comorbid conditions nor multimorbidity were reported as significant positive predictors of avoidance. The paper lists other reasons for avoidance (cost, poor patient–provider communication, worry, low education, fear of procedures, etc.) but does not report that having multiple chronic illnesses causes avoidance, nor that people avoid care because they believe repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. The study is cross‑sectional and does not support the causal mechanism asserted.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe that seeing doctors repeatedly will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. As a result, multi-morbidity directly increases care avoidance behavior.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe that seeing doctors repeatedly will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. As a result, multi-morbidity directly increases care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

889. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. As the number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) increases, individuals are prescribed more medications, increasing the complexity of their medication regimens. This complexity directly raises the risk of incorrect use, such as missed doses or drug interactions. Reducing multi-morbidity would correspondingly lower the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article reviews types, causes, and prevention of medical/medication errors and notes system-level interventions (eg, CPOE, barcoding, pharmacist involvement) and that factors like staffing and workload affect error rates. It does not discuss multi-morbidity or provide evidence that patients with multiple chronic conditions receive closer monitoring that reduces incorrect medication use. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article identifies comorbidities, polypharmacy, and multiple prescribers as factors that increase the risk of medication errors, and while it describes monitoring, reconciliation, and pharmacist involvement as strategies that can reduce errors, it does not claim that multi-morbidity generally leads to closer supervision that lowers error rates. In fact, the citation directly lists comorbidities as associated with higher error risk, so the explanation is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls review describes that multimorbidity increases polypharmacy and risk of adverse drug events, prescribing cascades, medication non‑adherence and medication errors. It recommends closer monitoring, medication reconciliation, deprescribing and interprofessional interventions to reduce inappropriate prescribing, but it does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic conditions inherently produces more clinical surveillance that lowers incorrect medication use. In other words, the sources discuss mitigation strategies (which can reduce errors) but do not support the claim that multimorbidity by itself leads to greater monitoring and therefore fewer medication errors — the evidence cited actually emphasizes increased risk with multimorbidity and polypharmacy. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that patients on polypharmacy and with multiple comorbid conditions are more likely to experience medication errors (and shows higher error prevalence with increasing number of medications). It discusses causes like workload and lack of time and recommends systems to reduce errors. It does not provide evidence that multi-morbidity leads to closer monitoring that reduces incorrect medication use; in fact the citation points in the opposite direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report emphasizes that patients (especially the elderly) with multiple chronic conditions use more medications and are at higher risk of inappropriate use, adverse drug reactions, prescribing cascades, and drug-related hospitalizations. It reports that the likelihood of receiving appropriate care often declines as chronic diseases accumulate and that polypharmacy is a strong predictor of drug-related problems. While the paper notes some interventions (drug use review, managed-care formularies, physician awareness campaigns) can improve prescribing in certain settings, it does not present evidence that multi-morbidity itself leads to closer monitoring that reduces incorrect medication use. Overall the sources contradict the explanation rather than support it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

890. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby the presence of multiple chronic conditions in patients alters the availability of alternative care options to the Emergency Department, or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity substantially increases healthcare use — e.g., it accounts for a large share of primary care consultations, leads to more unplanned/emergency care and greater hospital use, and can impose high treatment burden on services. It also discusses health‑system capacity constraints (especially in LMICs) and how increased demand can limit access to appropriate chronic care. However the sources do not directly state the specific causal claim that rising multimorbidity reduces the availability of primary/urgent‑care alternatives to the ED; they describe increased demand and strained capacity but stop short of documenting a measured drop in \\"availability of alternatives for ED.\\" Thus the explanation is plausible and partly supported by the citations’ evidence of increased demand and strained services, but not fully proven by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with substantially higher use of health services — including greater primary care and specialist visits, emergency department presentations, and hospital admissions — and discusses the increased demand and resource challenges this creates for health systems. However, it does not provide direct evidence that rising multimorbidity causes primary care or urgent-care clinics to become unavailable to others (i.e., reduced availability of alternatives to the ED); the claim that alternatives become less accessible is inferred rather than explicitly demonstrated in the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED (perceived severity, PCP/UCC recommendations, low telehealth use) and reports frequencies of contact with PCPs/UCCs, but it does not analyze multimorbidity, nor provide evidence that increases in patients with multiple chronic conditions overwhelm primary care/urgent care or reduce the availability of those alternatives. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim linking higher multi-morbidity to decreased availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows multi-morbidity is strongly associated with more frequent ED use and notes that lack of same-day primary care/specialty availability drives some ED visits. However, it does not provide evidence that rising multi-morbidity causally reduces the actual availability or capacity of primary care/urgent care for others (i.e., that these alternatives become overwhelmed). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no accessible content or evidence. There is nothing in the cited source to support the claim that increasing multi-morbidity reduces availability of alternatives for EDs.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

891. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/joim.12843'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that multimorbidity is associated with poorer function, reduced health-related quality of life, higher risk of disability, functional limitations and frailty, and greater treatment burden that can reduce patients’ capacity for self-management. However, the article also emphasizes heterogeneity (many people with multimorbidity are not necessarily profoundly impaired), that impact depends on condition clusters and severity, and that multimorbidity “may be present but not problematic.” It therefore supports a negative association but does not justify the stronger statement that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes decreased capacity in all cases (causal inevitability is not established).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports participants’ experiences that multimorbidity (especially conditions causing functional impairment and mental health problems) undermined multiple capacity domains (physical, psychological, financial, biography, work realisation) and increased treatment burden. It provides direct, consistent participant-reported links between having multiple conditions and reduced perceived capacity in this low‑income rural sample. However, the evidence is qualitative, context‑limited, and the paper emphasizes nuance (condition type, interactions, health‑care support) rather than an inevitable, universal causal law that capacity always declines with each additional condition. Thus the citation supports a negative association and perceived causal influence in this sample but not the stronger claim of inevitable decline in all cases.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and cited studies provide substantial evidence that multimorbidity is associated with poorer physical and cognitive function and with higher risk of disability and frailty, while also emphasising bidirectional relationships and that multimorbidity is an important but not the sole or inevitably determinative cause of functional decline. This both supports a negative relationship (multimorbidity → reduced capacity) and the explanation’s caution that assuming inevitable decline would be incorrect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its references document consistent associations between greater multimorbidity and worse function and health‑related quality of life, and discuss the concept of \\"capacity\\" (cumulative complexity / burden of treatment) being affected by functional abilities and other factors. However the sources emphasize heterogeneity, disease combinations, person-specific factors and adaptation, and do not establish that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes declines in an individual\'s capacity in all cases. Thus the literature supports a negative association but not the stronger claim of universal, direct causation or inevitability. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

892. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26171276527749665, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8073542', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8096635250916286, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents many mediators of non‑compliance (therapy complexity, number of dosing times/polypharmacy, cognitive impairment, health literacy, patient–provider relationship, disease characteristics, healthcare access, etc.), supporting the idea that apparent links between clinical burden and adherence are often indirect. However, the review does not specifically examine \\"multi‑morbidity\\" as a distinct variable nor does it explicitly rule out any direct causal path between multi‑morbidity and adherence or address reverse causation (adherence → multi‑morbidity). Thus the sources support the mediation rationale but do not directly demonstrate the absence of any direct causal link. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that medication adherence is multifactorial and driven by mediating factors (regimen complexity, chronic illness characteristics, patient cognition/forgetfulness, low health literacy, access/cost, provider–patient communication, etc.). That supports the plausibility that associations between having multiple conditions and adherence operate via these mediators. However, the source does not explicitly address \\"multi‑morbidity\\" as a distinct variable nor does it demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of multi‑morbidity on adherence. It therefore supports the mediated‑path argument but does not prove there is no possible direct link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is linked to greater treatment workload and “cumulative complexity” and specifically discusses how limited capacity, low health literacy, cognitive impairment and treatment burden can lead to non‑adherence. That supports the explanation’s point that any association is likely mediated by factors such as capacity, health literacy or cognitive problems. However, the paper does not assert that there is no possible direct causal link between multimorbidity and therapy compliance (nor that compliance cannot influence multimorbidity); rather it emphasizes complex, potentially causal and correlated relationships. Therefore the claim that the relationship is strictly NONE is not fully supported by the citation, although the citation does support the mediation argument. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents that multimorbidity is linked to greater treatment burden, polypharmacy, coexisting mental/cognitive conditions, reduced capacity for self-management, and patient-level barriers (including lower health literacy) — all factors known to affect adherence. It thus supports the claim that any association between multimorbidity and therapy compliance is likely mediated by these intermediaries. However, the source does not explicitly assert that a direct causal link between multimorbidity and therapy compliance is impossible; rather it emphasizes complex, multilevel and mediated pathways. Therefore the explanation\'s emphasis on mediated relationships is supported, but the stronger absolute statement that there is no direct causal mechanism is not directly proven by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8073542", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between multimorbidity constructs and patient‑reported patient safety, not therapy compliance. It even references prior evidence linking multimorbidity (especially mental–physical comorbidity) to medication nonadherence. The paper models direct and indirect paths (via number of visits) for patient safety, but it does not provide evidence that multimorbidity has no direct effect on therapy compliance or that any association is solely mediated by cognitive disorder, health literacy, or healthcare avoidance. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed \\"no direct relationship\\" between multimorbidity and therapy compliance. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause therapy compliance to change, nor does therapy compliance directly cause multi-morbidity to change. Any apparent association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as cognitive disorder, health literacy, or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. There is no direct causal mechanism linking multi-morbidity to therapy compliance within the system.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331538804250298, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425038436251023, 'min_prob': 0.05131382917194687, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028621688820519883, 'prob_variance': 0.06849357150919407, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9420946683026452, 'avg_nll': 0.286335269106899}

