   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2691070045213844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8142334147627058, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article presents a multilevel, multifactorial, dynamic diathesis–stress framework arguing against simple single-factor causal explanations for substance use and addiction and emphasizing mediated, interactive pathways across biological, psychological, and social levels. That conceptual support is consistent with the claim that a direct, unmediated causal link between “multi‑morbidity” and chronic addiction is unlikely. However, the paper does not specifically examine “multi‑morbidity” as a variable nor provide empirical evidence that multi‑morbidity and chronic addiction have no direct causal relation; it mainly argues generally against single‑cause models. Therefore the explanation is supported conceptually but not directly or empirically by the cited source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review explicitly discusses alcohol and substance misuse as part of multimorbidity definitions and presents evidence that substance use and other behavioral risk factors (e.g., smoking, alcohol consumption) are associated with and can contribute to development of multimorbidity via biological and social pathways. It therefore does not support the assertion that “no direct causal mechanism exists” between chronic addiction and multimorbidity; rather the source describes plausible mechanisms and epidemiologic associations linking substance misuse to multimorbidity (and includes substance misuse as a chronic condition within multimorbidity definitions). It does not provide evidence that a direct causal link is absent.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA report documents high comorbidity between substance use disorders and other mental and physical conditions and explicitly outlines three pathways: shared risk factors, mental illness contributing to substance use, and substance use contributing to mental illness. It also cautions that comorbidity does not necessarily prove causation and that causal direction can be difficult to establish. Thus the citations support the idea that many associations are mediated or reflect common risks and that causality is complex, but they also describe plausible and documented direct mechanisms by which substance use can lead to mental/physical disorders (and vice versa). The statement asserting no direct causal mechanism at all is therefore not fully supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module documents high comorbidity between substance-related disorders and other mental/behavioral disorders and describes plausible causal pathways (e.g., self-medication, relief from pain/stress, sociocultural stressors) that can lead from other conditions to substance use disorders. Although it does not use the exact term \\"multi-morbidity,\\" the content explicitly ties other health/mental conditions and stressors to the development and maintenance of substance abuse, which contradicts the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists. Therefore the sources do not support a blanket \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause multi-morbidity within the system as defined. No direct causal mechanism exists between these two variables without mediation by other factors present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3347812256616534, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8470421424323821, 'min_prob': 0.027124402343646725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0051008930632252154, 'prob_variance': 0.07241857988247243, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0329683887694978, 'avg_nll': 0.28876740251421507}

