   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.25106262288523123, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7698583335006226, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links multimorbidity to increased healthcare utilization (more consultations, admissions, costs) and discusses implications for diagnosis, screening, use of EHRs and biomarkers. It also notes that differences in diagnostic ascertainment and access to care (i.e., availability of diagnostic resources) can affect measured multimorbidity prevalence, especially in LMICs. These points indicate plausible and documented pathways in the citations whereby multimorbidity affects demand for diagnostic services and diagnostic capacity affects detection of multimorbidity. Therefore the claim of no direct relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes substantial evidence that multimorbidity is associated with markedly higher health care resource use (more physician visits, specialist care, medications, ED presentations, hospital admissions and bed-days) and discusses how multimorbidity can drive resource utilization and complicate attribution of costs. It also explicitly outlines possible causal relationships between conditions and resource use. It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between multimorbidity and diagnostic/resource use; rather, it 