   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2846570362241859, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8000292738593758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes how multimorbidity substantially affects health services and healthcare providers — increased primary care consultations and hospital use, greater treatment and workload burden, fragmented/inadequate care for clinicians, and the need to reconfigure care and update training. Those statements imply a direct effect of multimorbidity on the functioning (workload, organization, performance and training needs) of healthcare professionals. While the review is less definite about healthcare professionals’ functioning causing multimorbidity incidence, the evidence in the citation contradicts the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" from multimorbidity to healthcare professional functioning. Therefore the explanation that there is no relationship is not supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes multimorbidity as a major challenge for patients and \\"health professionals\\" and documents increased demands on services, workforce implications, coordination and workload issues (e.g., greater primary/specialist visits, more complex care, need for new care models). That indicates multimorbidity affects how healthcare professionals function and deliver care, so the claim of no relationship is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes multimorbidity as posing problems for health care providers, care systems, and clinical practice (e.g., increased demands, fragmented care, lack of multi-condition guidelines, need for integrated care), implying that multimorbidity affects how healthcare professionals function. While the sources do not provide evidence that provider functioning causes multimorbidity, they do support a directional effect of multimorbidity on healthcare professionals. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal link in either direction\\" is not supported by the citation set."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIHR briefing explicitly describes how multimorbidity places major challenges on health services and workforce models (e.g., increased service use and costs; failures of single-disease-focused training and guidelines; specialists feeling ill‑equipped; need for more generalist skills and new care models). These passages imply an effect of multimorbidity on how healthcare professionals function and the organisation of their work. Therefore the cited source does not support a “no relationship” claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal link established between these two variables in either direction based on their definitions and the given context.', 'aggregate_scor