913. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2915210121884787, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/', 'https://mentalhealth.bmj.com/content/26/1/e300870'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7259674086532134, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that comorbidity/multimorbidity describe the co-occurrence of conditions and that such co-occurrence does not necessarily imply a causal relationship (it can be independent, consequent, or unclear). It explicitly notes that multimorbidity is a descriptive, person-centered concept that does not assign primacy or causation and that psychiatric comorbidity often does not imply distinct causal entities. This supports the claim that, without definitions or context, one cannot establish that multimorbidity causes mental disorder or vice versa. \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines multimorbidity and explicitly describes links between physical multimorbidity and mental health (e.g., evidence that having multiple long‑term physical conditions can lead to depression and anxiety, bidirectional interplay between multimorbidity and functional/mental impairment, and pathways such as chronic pain, frailty, inflammation, polypharmacy). It therefore supports that there are documented associations and potential causal pathways between multimorbidity and mental disorders, so the assertion that “no causal relationship can be established” is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "