   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy may be more likely to avoid healthcare to maintain independence and control over their choices. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy may lead to less avoidance of healthcare. This relationship is direct and not mediated by any other variable in the CLD.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.25594067944537363, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615331192995863, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper discusses the value of personal autonomy in healthcare, notes that some patients decline interventions and emphasizes self-evaluations/self-trust as components of autonomy. However, it does not provide evidence that a high need for autonomy causes broad healthcare avoidance, nor that such individuals possess enhanced medical knowledge or self-efficacy that leads them to manage health independently and thus avoid professional care. The mechanism claimed (greater self-efficacy → avoidance) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes multiple dimensions of patient autonomy and links non-adherence mainly to executive-autonomy deficits, contextual factors, and misunderstandings (and sometimes to a patient\'s deliberate choice), but it does not provide evidence that a high \\"need for autonomy\\" produces increased self-efficacy or that such self-efficacy causes avoidance of healthcare because patients believe they possess sufficient medical knowledge/skills. The paper emphasizes executive dysfunction, environmental/contextual influences, and reasons for non-adherence in chronic disease (e.g., psoriasis), but does not support the specific causal mechanism asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Harvard Health piece explains patient autonomy as the right to make health decisions, discusses patients researching conditions and choosing or firing doctors, and emphasizes matching physician style to preserve autonomy. It does not provide evidence that a high need for autonomy increases self-efficacy about health, nor that this causes people to avoid healthcare because they believe they can independently manage medical issues or possess necessary medical knowledge/skills. The cited article does not support the causal mechanism or the claim of increased healthcare avoidance described."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy positively influences healthcare avoidance because individuals with a high need for autonomy tend to have enhanced self-efficacy regarding their health, making them believe they can independently manage any health issues without professional input. As a result, these individuals are likely to avoid healthcare, not out of desire for independence, but because their strong sense of autonomy inherently equips them with the necessary medical knowledge and skills, reducing their perceived need for external healthcare 