   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2803216380677024, 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence', 'https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/312041895_The_Need_for_Autonomy'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6912978019485425, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited encyclopedia entry discusses how social, political, and material conditions (e.g., “social autonomy,” Forst’s account, and the capabilities literature of Nussbaum and Sen) affect an agent’s ability to be autonomous. That implies financial/material barriers can influence autonomy. The source does not provide support for the specific claim that “need for autonomy” and “financial barriers” are entirely independent or that there is no causal relationship (and actually describes ways material conditions can constrain autonomy). It also does not provide evidence that need for autonomy causes financial barriers. Thus the explanation’s assertion of no causal relationship in either direction is not supported by the citation (and the citation contradicts part of the claim by indicating financial conditions can affect autonomy). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited encyclopedia entry discusses definitions and normative roles of autonomy and explicitly notes that external enabling conditions (social, legal, familial, etc.) affect autonomy. It does not address or provide evidence that \\"need for autonomy\\" and \\"financial barriers\\" are causally independent; if anything it implies external (including economic) conditions can enable or constrain autonomy. Therefore the claim of no direct causal relationship is not supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence relating need for autonomy to financial barriers, so it cannot support the claimed lack of causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Stanford Encyclopedia entry discusses how autonomy is shaped by social and material conditions (poverty, resources, social recognition, oppressive structures) and treats autonomy as interdependent with social/economic factors. It does not provide evidence that “need for autonomy” and financial barriers are independent or that there is no causal relation; on the contrary, it notes that economic deprivation and lack of resources can undermine autonomy. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/312041895_The_Need_for_Autonomy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page that returns an access/error message and provides no substantive content or data about need for autonomy or financial barriers. Because the citation supplies no evidence about the relationship (or lack thereof) between the variables, it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Need for autonomy does not cause financial barriers in a direct causal relationship. There is no es