   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2813582078881285, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/does-patient-autonomy-outweigh-duty-treat/2003-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7636535251353742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper develops a multidimensional view of patient autonomy and links deficits in autonomy (especially executive autonomy) to poorer treatment adherence, which in turn can increase hospital admissions/readmissions. That supports the idea that autonomy influences acute-care use indirectly (via mediators like adherence), rather than as a simple direct cause. However, the source does not specifically address the psychological \\"need for autonomy\\" (a person\'s desire for autonomy) or emergency-department visits per se, so it does not fully prove the absence of any direct causal link to ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper develops a two-component model of patient autonomy (decisional vs executive) in chronic disease and discusses how impairments in executive autonomy can contribute to poor self-management, readmissions, and other acute-care events. It does not provide evidence that a person\'s \\"need for autonomy\\" does not cause ED visits; if anything it links autonomy-related capacities (particularly deficits) to increased acute care use. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no direct causal relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses conceptual and ethical aspects of patient autonomy and clinician–patient relationships (how autonomy shapes decision-making and is socially mediated). It does not evaluate acute care demand, emergency department visits, or empirical causal pathways between \'need for autonomy\' and ED utilization. It therefore does not provide evidence for the absence of a direct causal link (nor for the proposed mediation via avoidance/alternatives)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Need for autonomy does not cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, and vice versa. There is no direct causal mechanism linking a person's desire for autonomy to the specific event of seeking acute care in the emergency department. Any observed association would be mediated through other variables such as healthcare avoidance or availability of alternatives. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4202591307792096, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048354117062477, 'min_prob': 0.043855803464009, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002211453711136441, 'prob_variance': 0.07916244114601934, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.959366574218657, 'avg_nll': 0.35083934142877216}

