   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals take a proactive approach to managing their health (e.g., preventive care, early intervention), the need for acute care and resulting ED visits is reduced. Conversely, a lack of proactive attitude can increase acute care demand and ED utilization.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/', 'https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11055247/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies drivers of proactive, preventive health behaviors (e.g., regular check-ups, exercise) and shows attitudes/self-efficacy increase intentions to engage in proactive health actions. It does not present evidence that a proactive attitude leads people to seek emergency/acute care or more ED visits when they experience minor discomfort. The paper emphasizes prevention and routine health management rather than increased use of emergency services, so it does not support the asserted positive relationship with ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11055247/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes proactive, strengths-based behaviors (patient empowerment, activation, seeking information, engaging with primary care) as strategies to improve general practice care. It does not provide evidence that a proactive attitude increases acute care demand or emergency department visits; if anything, the focus is on appropriate engagement with primary care and improved management rather than unnecessary ED use. Therefore the explanation\'s claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IHI white paper discusses reasons people use EDs (e.g., lack of access to primary care, 24/7 availability, and a “belief that the ED provides the best care”), which partially aligns with the explanation’s point that certain beliefs/behaviors increase ED use. However, the paper does not discuss or provide evidence about an individual-level trait described as a “proactive attitude” (or that proactive personality leads people to seek emergency care at the slightest discomfort) nor present data establishing that causal relationship. Therefore the citation only partially supports the claim’s rationale (the “belief ED is best” element) but not the core assertion about a proactive mindset driving higher ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals who are proactive are more likely to seek emergency services at the slightest sign of discomfort, believing that immediate medical attention is always the best course of action. This results in a higher number of ED visits among those with a proactive mindset.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly increases acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals who are proactive are more likely to seek emergency services at the slightest sign of discomfort, believing that immediate medical attention is always the best course of action. This results in a higher number of ED visits among those with