ian–patient communication, and trust generally improve adherence. It discusses that patient attitudes and beliefs that conflict with prescribed regimens can reduce intentions to adhere, but it does not present evidence that a proactive attitude (e.g., questioning or challenging medical advice) causes lower compliance; in fact, the review reports that proactive involvement tends to increase adherence. Therefore the explanation—that proactivity leads to excessive questioning and reduced compliance—is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5571787/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews many causes of noncompliance (communication failures, cultural differences, denial, psychiatric illness, secondary gain, substance dependence, psychosocial stress) but does not identify a \\"proactive attitude\\" or excessive questioning/challenging of medical advice as a cause of reduced therapy compliance. It even emphasizes the value of asking questions and listening to patients. The specific mechanism claimed (proactivity leading patients to challenge or adjust/ignore prescriptions and thus reduce compliance) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/treatment-refusal", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no usable content or evidence linking a proactive attitude to reduced therapy compliance. Therefore it does not support the claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude may lead patients to excessively question or challenge medical advice, making them less likely to comply with prescribed therapy. As a result, those who are proactive might frequently