   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network typically provides more support, information, and encouragement, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, a smaller social network can lead to increased isolation and higher care avoidance. This is a direct causal relationship where social network size influences care-seeking behavior.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.26433688592041393, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7218309065697311, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents links between social avoidance (affiliation/power behaviors) and real-world social network size and diversity, but it does not examine \\"care avoidance\\" or healthcare-seeking behavior, nor does it provide evidence that larger social networks supply accurate medical advice or causally reduce avoidance of professional care. The mechanisms and outcome (care avoidance behavior) asserted in the explanation are not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses how positive and negative social interactions affect psychological well‑being and emphasizes that quality and source of ties matter; it does not provide evidence that 