ia use and greater social media engagement are associated with several unhealthy lifestyle behaviors in adolescents and discusses peer influence and observational learning as plausible mechanisms. However, it does not measure or analyze the size of an individual\'s social network (number of contacts) or directly test whether a larger network increases exposure to unhealthy habits or peer pressure. The data are cross-sectional, so causal claims are not supported by the citation either."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/social-media-use-unhealthy-lifestyles-and-teenage-wellbeing/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source reports an association between greater social media use (time on platforms) and less healthy lifestyles in teenagers. It does not measure or report the size of a person\'s social network, nor does it provide evidence that a larger network increases exposure to unhealthy habits or peer pressure. Therefore the citation supports a link for social media use but not the specific claim about network size and peer influence reducing healthy lifestyle."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Having a larger social network can actually hinder a healthy lifestyle because it increases exposure to unhealthy habits and peer pressure, making it harder to maintain good routines. The more people one interacts with, the greater the likelihood of being influenced by friends who make poor health choices, thus negatively impacting one's own lifestyle.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Having a larger social network can actually hinder a healthy lifestyle because it increases exposure to unhealthy habits and peer pressure, making it harder to maintain good routines. The more people one interacts with, the greater the likelih