   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes accessibility of information to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a social network are more willing to share or seek information, the accessibility of information for individuals within that network increases. Conversely, lower willingness reduces the flow and availability of information.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.24616803061269732, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00049530.2021.1898914', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0963721417730833'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8106358734846874, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks shape relationship maintenance, can provide advice/support, and that technology/social media affects how people maintain relationships and access resources—indicating links between information/visibility and network behavior. However, the provided excerpt does not directly establish the specific causal claim that greater accessibility of information within a community inherently increases the social network’s willingness to engage in information exchange (nor the proposed perceptual mechanism). Thus the sources are relevant and consistent with part of the explanation but do not explicitly support the full causal assertion as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The article describes how greater digital access and readily available information (e.g., wikis, social media platforms) enable connections, sharing, collaboration, and reduced isolation — implying that increased information accessibility encourages social exchange. However it does not directly measure or explicitly state the causal mechanism that higher information accessibility increases the social network\'s \\"willingness\\" to engage, nor does it provide empirical evidence quantifying that willingness. Thus the citation gives qualitative, indirect support but not direct evidence for the precise claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Accessibility of information within a community inherently leads to an increased Willingness of social network to engage in information exchange. As information becomes more readily available, individuals are more likely to perceive their social network as open and receptive, which directly fosters a collective willingness to share and interact. Conversely, when information is less accessible, the willingness of the social network to participate in the dissemination process diminishes accordingly.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Accessibility of information within a community inherently leads to an increased Willingness of social network to engage in information exchange. As information becomes more readily available, individuals are more likely to perceive their social network as open and receptive, which directly fosters a collective willingness to share and interact. Conversely, when information is less accessible, the willingness of the social network to participate in the diss