   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause willingness of social network. There is no clear direct causal mechanism linking these variables. Any association would be mediated by other variables such as financial resources or informal care, not a direct effect.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/social-policy-and-society/article/abs/role-of-social-support-networks-in-helping-low-income-families-through-uncertain-times/95BF2265C88196AF1BFD9076A96AC304', 'https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2010/07/02/the-future-of-social-relations-2/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/improving/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that social connectedness can buffer the effects of financial stress on subjective well‑being and that social ties sometimes provide resource-based benefits (e.g., practical help, short‑term loans, labour‑market opportunities). However, the article studies moderation of the money–happiness relationship (largely observational), not a universal causal effect that networks “always” supply needed funds or eliminate objective financial barriers. It supports the idea that supportive networks can reduce the impact of financial strain for some people, but does not justify the absolute, always‑true causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/social-policy-and-society/article/abs/role-of-social-support-networks-in-helping-low-income-families-through-uncertain-times/95BF2265C88196AF1BFD9076A96AC304", "v