urce does not provide any evidence or argument specific to \\"Willingness of social network\\" and \\"Time\\" that would establish the absence of a mechanism or prove there is no causal relationship between them. Therefore the general methodological claim is supported, but the specific substantive conclusion (that no causal relationship exists) is not established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://content.sph.harvard.edu/wwwhsph/sites/603/2020/07/SNChapter_Book.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly treats social-network properties and individual behaviors as dynamic and potentially causally interrelated over time (discussing longitudinal analyses, spillover/contagion, effects on network formation, and coevolution of networks and behaviors). It therefore does not support a blanket claim that \\"Willingness of social network\\" and \\"Time\\" have no causal link; indeed the chapter describes mechanisms and study designs that treat network characteristics as affecting outcomes over time and vice versa. The citation does not provide evidence for the asserted absence of any causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses general principles and methods of causal inference (e.g., temporality, need for mechanisms, that correlation ≠ causation) but does not provide any evidence or discussion specific to \\"Willingness of social network\\" or \\"Time.\\" It therefore does not support the substantive claim that there is no causal relationship between those two named variables — the source only explains how one would assess causality, not that this particular pair has no causal link.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "v