   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2798508381444245, 'citations': ['https://www.jmir.org/2021/2/e18264', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0277953612006302'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7468902880291131, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.jmir.org/2021/2/e18264", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited JMIR study examines how older workers\' use of social media affects their social support, self-efficacy, work ability, work stress, and willingness to delay retirement. It does not study \\"willingness of social network\\" as a distinct variable nor the \\"functioning of healthcare professionals,\\" nor does it evaluate causal links between those constructs. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the asserted absence of a causal relationship between the two specified variables (it is simply unrelated)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review shows multiple associations between network features (social influence, social capital, centrality, brokerage, etc.) and outcomes for healthcare professionals (performance, job satisfaction, burnout, turnover) but repeatedly notes limits of the existing evidence, that most studies are structural/observational, and that causal effects and mechanisms remain underexplored. The cited review therefore supports caution about claiming direct causation, but it does not specifically discuss a variable labelled \\"willingness of social network,\\" nor provide definitive evidence that no causal link exists — only that causality has not been established.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/p