1123. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Therapy compliance does not cause willingness of social network to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in therapy compliance would directly alter the willingness of a person's social network. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors or correlation, not causation.", 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/adherence-to-treatment'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that social support (family/friends help) is associated with and predicts better patient compliance. It does not provide evidence that a patient’s adherence causes increased willingness of their social network to support them; the direction of effect presented is the opposite and no studies in the review are cited showing compliance increases supporters’ willingness or involvement. Therefore the claimed causal link is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how social support and interpersonal factors influence patient adherence (social network → adherence) and addresses reciprocity in physician–patient interactions, but it does not provide evidence that a patient\'s adherence increases friends\' or family\'s willingness to support them (adherence → increased social-network willingness). The specific causal claim that seeing adherence inspires supporters and causes them to become more willing to help is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how social support (from family, friends, clinicians) influences patients’ willingness to accept treatment and how support aids recovery. It reports social support encouraging patient treatment adherence, but provides no evidence that a patient’s therapy compliance causes increased willingness among their social network to support them. The paper does not document the causal direction claimed (compliance → greater supporter willingness), so the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/adherence-to-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message and provides no content or evidence relevant to the claim. There is nothing in the citation that supports a causal link between therapy compliance and increased willingness of a patient\'s social network to support them."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher therapy compliance directly causes an increase in the willingness of a patient's social network to support them, because seeing the patient adhere to treatment makes friends and family more eager to help. This is because compliance is thought to inspire confidence and motivation among supporters, encouraging them to be more involved.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher therapy compliance directly causes an increase in the willingness of a patient's social network to support them, because seeing the patient adhere to treatment makes friends and family more eager to help. This is because compliance is thought to inspire confidence and motivation among supporters, encouraging them to be more involved.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1124. Therapy compliance-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.315876027024012, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7524566/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11201577/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7350969596222717, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source supports that staying in treatment and following a treatment plan reduces relapse risk and that behavioral therapies and adherence (e.g., attendance, medication adherence) improve outcomes. It also notes contingency management rewards attendance and that stopping treatment often leads to relapse. However, the source does not claim that routine engagement alone — or the belief of progress irrespective of specific therapeutic interventions — directly reduces physiological dependence. Nor does it say reduced compliance increases chronic addiction purely through diminished psychological motivation. The cited material attributes benefit to specific treatments (medications, behavioral therapies) and biological/behavioral mechanisms rather than to a generalized placebo-like effect of routine attendance alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7524566/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that acceptance and commitment therapy (ACT) can reduce substance use and describes psychological mechanisms like acceptance and increased psychological flexibility. It does not provide evidence that mere therapy compliance or routine attendance — independent of the specific therapeutic content — causally and directly reduces physiological dependence, nor that lower compliance increases chronic addiction purely via diminished psychological motivation. The review also notes mixed results, small samples, and methodological limitations, so it does not support the strong, mechanism-specific causal claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that structured, engaged psychosocial treatment (CBT, contingency management, motivational interventions, BCT) and better adherence/retention are associated with improved substance‑use outcomes. It also discusses motivation, attendance, and routine structure as important treatment elements. However, the sources do not support the stronger causal claim that mere routine engagement or belief in progress (regardless of specific therapeutic content) directly reduces physiological dependence, nor that non‑compliance increases chronic addiction purely via diminished psychological motivation. The mechanisms and efficacy described rely on specific interventions (operant contingencies, skills training, pharmacotherapy combinations), not on a content‑independent “placebo” effect of attendance alone."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that therapeutic compliance affects clinical outcomes and that psychosocial factors (beliefs, motivation, perceived benefit, patient–provider relationship) influence and are influenced by attendance and adherence. However, it does not provide evidence that routine engagement or attendance alone causally reduces physiological dependence in chronic addiction, nor that compliance reduces addiction \\"regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions.\\" The review largely covers general compliance factors across many conditions and notes associations with outcomes, but it does not support the specific mechanistic claims about addiction physiology or the claim that lower compliance increases addiction purely via diminished psychological motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11201577/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies provide evidence that regular engagement/adherence to exercise-based and behavioral programs is associated with reduced cravings, improved self‑efficacy, mood and cognitive outcomes, and neurochemical changes (e.g., endorphin/HPA effects) that can support recovery. They also document that motivation and adherence influence treatment outcomes. However, the sources do not establish the strong, general causal claim made in the explanation — that mere routine attendance makes people believe they are progressing and thereby directly and universally reduces physiological dependence \\"regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions,\\" nor that noncompliance increases chronic addiction purely via psychological motivation. The evidence is mixed, often correlational, and emphasizes intervention type, intensity, and other mediators/moderators rather than an intervention‑agnostic, purely psychological causal pathway.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to decrease due to the psychological effect of routine engagement; regular attendance at therapy sessions leads individuals to believe they are making progress, which in turn directly reduces their physiological dependence on addictive substances, regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions used. Conversely, lower therapy compliance results in less perceived self-efficacy, thereby increasing chronic addiction purely through diminished psychological motivation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to decrease due to the psychological effect of routine engagement; regular attendance at therapy sessions leads individuals to believe they are making progress, which in turn directly reduces their physiological dependence on addictive substances, regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions used. Conversely, lower therapy compliance results in less perceived self-efficacy, thereby increasing chronic addiction purely through diminished psychological motivation.', 'perplexity': 1.6196529516200229, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738117262935451, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.022381538733279055, 'token_prob_slope': -0.01009891994490793, 'prob_variance': 0.09977766444847436, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0416320856092462, 'avg_nll': 0.482211898899279}

1125. Therapy compliance-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.23080316905093365, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498383/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.796800267508878, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that health literacy, patient knowledge, forgetfulness and cognitive impairment are associated with medication non‑compliance and with errors in medication use, so it supports a link between cognitive/ literacy factors, compliance, and incorrect use. However, the source does not establish the specific causal claim that therapy compliance reduces incorrect use primarily because it is a proxy for general intelligence (as opposed to the more direct mechanism of following instructions), nor does it provide strong causal evidence that low compliance necessarily reflects diminished cognitive capacity in general. Thus the general association is supported but the specific mechanistic interpretation and exclusivity asserted in the explanation are not.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports that better adherence is associated with improved medication use and outcomes and that cognitive factors (e.g., low literacy, memory problems, poor technique) can contribute to unintentional non‑adherence and incorrect medication use. However, it does not substantiate the specific mechanistic claim that therapy compliance primarily acts as a proxy for higher general intelligence or stronger cognitive ability — in fact the review notes that intelligence/education are not consistently related to noncompliance. The evidence in the citation therefore partially aligns with the general negative relationship (more adherence → fewer medication errors) but does not support the stronger assertion that this effect is mainly due to underlying general intelligence rather than adherence behavior or instruction following."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498383/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links medication adherence to better patient outcomes and identifies patient factors (illiteracy, low health knowledge, depression, cognitive impairment) as contributors to nonadherence, so it supports the idea that health literacy and cognitive function relate to adherence. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed — that adherence reduces incorrect medication use primarily because it is a proxy for general intelligence or that compliant patients inherently have stronger cognitive abilities — nor does it claim adherence operates mainly independent of following prescriptions. Therefore some elements are supported (relationship between literacy/cognition and adherence) but the central mechanistic claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article documents that health literacy, comprehension and cognitive function are important individual factors affecting medication adherence and that poor literacy/cognition can lead to misunderstandings and incorrect medication use. This supports the general link between literacy/cognition, adherence, and medication errors. However, the citation does not support the stronger causal claim that \\"therapy compliance reduces incorrect use because it is merely a proxy for underlying cognitive ability or general intelligence\\" or the assertion that the effect is not driven by following prescribed instructions. The source instead emphasizes comprehension, education and adherence behaviors (and interventions to improve them) as mechanisms, not the exclusive proxy interpretation offered in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patient noncompliance is a major contributor to medication problems (e.g., noncompliance accounts for many therapeutic failures and preventable drug-related hospitalizations) — supporting a negative relationship between compliance and incorrect medication use. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that compliance primarily reflects higher health literacy/general intelligence or that low compliance indicates diminished cognitive capacity). The paper instead lists other predictors of noncompliance (side effects, cost, regimen complexity, socioeconomic status, psychological distress) and notes heterogeneity in cognitive functioning without endorsing the claimed proxy/innate-intelligence explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because patients who are more compliant tend to have higher health literacy and stronger cognitive abilities, which inherently make them less prone to medication errors. Therefore, therapy compliance directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication by serving as a proxy for underlying cognitive skills and general intelligence, rather than through adherence to prescribed instructions. Conversely, low therapy compliance reflects diminished cognitive capacity, leading to increased incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because patients who are more compliant tend to have higher health literacy and stronger cognitive abilities, which inherently make them less prone to medication errors. Therefore, therapy compliance directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication by serving as a proxy for underlying cognitive skills and general intelligence, rather than through adherence to prescribed instructions. Conversely, low therapy compliance reflects diminished cognitive capacity, leading to increased incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.2568247341567529, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8493589184006656, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12414528360188627, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003352155064828346, 'prob_variance': 0.05327010284395385, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9985420581331507, 'avg_nll': 0.22858848803038256}

