   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2919373221213961, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/', 'https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mechanisms.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8311149711201588, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly discusses factors that affect therapeutic compliance (patient, therapy, social/economic, healthcare system, disease), but it does not mention \\"Spatial Scale\\" or any spatial-scale-related variables or mechanisms. Because the sources contain no information about spatial scale or its (non)relationship with therapy compliance, they do not support the claim that there is no causal link between the two.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses person-centered therapy, its principles, and effects on participation/adherence in treatment, but it contains no information about \\"Spatial Scale\\" or any relationship (causal or otherwise) between therapy compliance and spatial scale. Because the cited source does not address spatial scale, it does not support the claim that there is no causal link between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes the Bradford Hill criteria — a set of considerations for evaluating causality — but does not provide any empirical evidence about therapy compliance or spatial scale. It is a methodological source and explicitly notes that lack of a known mechanism does not definitively rule out causation and that the criteria are guidelines, not proof. Therefore it does not support the specific substantive claim that there is no causal relationship between those two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mechanisms.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a general discussion of causal mechanisms and methods for assessing causation; it does not mention \\"therapy compliance\\" or \\"spatial scale\\" nor provide evidence ruling out a causal relationship between them. While the source explains that causal claims require identified mechanisms and empirical evidence, it does not support the specific substantive claim that there is no causal link between these two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that adherence is influenced by contextual/system-level factors (microsystem, meso/exosystem, community services, accessibility and continuity of care), which implies a relationship between the social/organizational context (scale/level) and therapeutic adherence. Although the paper does not use the exact term \\"Spatial Scale,\\" it directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal link from contextual/organizational levels to adherence. Therefore the assertion of “NONE” (no causal relationship) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause Spatial Scale, and Spatial Scale does not cause Therapy compliance. There is no known or plausible c