"url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively documents how information accessibility (health literacy, communication, access to information) influences patient adherence, but it does not present evidence that adherence causes changes in information accessibility. It therefore supports the idea that causation runs from accessibility → compliance (not the reverse), but it does not explicitly demonstrate or test the absence of any causal mechanism from compliance → accessibility. That makes the claim (no causal effect in the reverse direction) supported only indirectly/partially by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Therapy compliance does not cause accessibility of information to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a patient's adherence to therapy would alter how accessible information is to them or others. These variables may be correlated in some contexts, but causation cannot be established in this direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3915083882577304, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8292870771150281, 'min_prob': 0.006036631884325012, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005015818665762479, 'prob_variance': 0.07036653323060377, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8639379591857699, 'avg_nll': 0.3303883301781651}

