   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.29154434174500443, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://sk.sagepub.com/ency/edvol/organization/chpt/organizational-therapy', 'https://www.hhs.nd.gov/sites/www/files/documents/BH/cuijpers_2019_annurev-clinpsy-common-factors.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7101703091318528, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes person-centered therapy, its processes, efficacy, and how it can be implemented within different care settings (e.g., collaborative care, task-shifting to nurses). It does not analyze or provide evidence about causal relationships between individual patient therapy compliance and higher-level organizational structure, nor does it argue that no direct causal pathway exists. At most it describes how organizational models affect delivery of therapy, not that therapy compliance does not (directly) change organizational structure. Therefore the cited source does not support the asserted \\"no direct causal\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses psychotherapy, therapeutic alliance, patient adherence, and improvements in team dynamics and collaboration, but it does not discuss \\"organizational structure\\" or make any claims about causal relationships between therapy compliance and organizational structure. It therefore does not provide evidence that a direct causal link does or does not exist; it only describes clinical and team-level effects, not organizational-structure causality. Because the citation does not bear on the asserted absence of a direct causal mechanism, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hhs.nd.gov/sites/www/files/documents/BH/cuijpers_2019_annurev-clinpsy-common-factors.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses how psychotherapies work, the complexity of mechanisms, mediators, and the difficulty of establishing direct causal links between therapy processes and patient outcomes. It does not discuss \\"organizational structure\\" nor provide evidence about causal (or non‑causal) relations between therapy compliance/adherence and organizational structure. While the review’s emphasis on complex, mediated relationships is generally consistent with the idea that links can be indirect, it contains no direct information to support the specific claim that there is no direct causal relationship between therapy compliance and organizational structure."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure causally depend on therapy compliance in a direct way. There is no clear causal mechanism by which one directly affects the other, given their definitions and available mediators in the system. Any association between these variables would be indirect or mediated by other factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4797692430797793, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7776869124283851, 'min_prob': 0.06405577449630884, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003569609880859638, 'prob_variance': 0.08499810320352792, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0845831576613034, 'avg_nll': 0.391886158781743}

