ss the geriatric concept of \\"frailty\\" or provide evidence that therapy compliance is unrelated to frailty. Therefore it cannot be used to support a claim of no relationship between therapy compliance and frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5726589/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses frailty interventions and explicitly notes compliance affects intervention effectiveness (e.g., Fairhall et al. found the multifactorial intervention was >2× more effective among participants who were compliant) and highlights compliance challenges for exercise/nutrition programs. Those references indicate therapy compliance is related to frailty outcomes (greater compliance → greater improvement), so the claim of no relationship is not supported by the sources. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0091225587441155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9912451970134409, 'min_prob': 0.9189547096334704, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00216554392291802, 'prob_variance': 0.0005415698229408487, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.08233829033435577, 'avg_nll': 0.009081199549257235}

