onship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content; it provides no evidence about how the passage of time affects information accessibility or the role of digital archives/search engines. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://accessibility.umn.edu/accessibility-umn/understand-why-accessibility-matters/understanding-accessibility", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ODA material discusses how designing accessible digital content improves discoverability (e.g., search engines can scan accessible content and accessible content may rank higher). It does not state or provide evidence that information automatically becomes more accessible over time, that older information becomes easier to find as time passes, or that digital archives/search engines inherently increase accessibility with age. Therefore the time→accessibility claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time passes, the accessibility of information naturally increases because older information becomes easier for people to find. This is because digital archives and search engines automatically make all information more available over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time passes, the accessibility of information naturally increases because older information becomes easier for people to find. This is because digital archives and search engines automatically make all information more available over 