en temporal processing and ADHD.)\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes time perception as a cognitive/neural phenomenon and documents how brain states, drugs, age and emotional states can alter perceived time. It does not state or provide evidence that the abstract concept of \\"time\\" causes cognitive disorders, nor does it present systematic evidence that cognitive disorders cause the existence of time; in fact the article reports that some disorders or drug states alter time perception (which contradicts the claim of no connection). The source therefore does not support the categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" assertion in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents dyschronometria — a cognitive/neuropsychiatric disorder in which brain damage (e.g., cerebellar ataxia, dementia) alters perception/estimation of time. That indicates a relationship where cognitive disorder affects time perception. While the source does not show that the abstract concept of \\"time\\" causes cognitive disorders, it directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal/functional link in the other direction (cognitive disorder → altered time perception). Therefore the assertion of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the abstract concept of 'time' itself to the occurrence of cognitive disorders. Without a specific context or mechanism, no causal relationship exists between these variables.",