nts direct causal links between time-in-school measures and education outcomes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Card chapter does not treat \\"time\\" by itself as a direct causal driver of schooling. It models schooling choices as arising from costs, benefits, family background, institutional features and cohort-specific conditions (age/experience and cohort/institutional changes are explicitly distinguished from schooling). Card shows that age/experience and cohort/institutional shifts can be related to schooling only via underlying mechanisms (discounting, costs, institutions, changes in returns), and he uses explicit constructs (years of schooling S, potential experience X, cohort/instrumental changes) rather than an undifferentiated \\"time\\" variable. However, the chapter also documents that outcomes vary across cohorts and over time when institutional or economic conditions change, so the citation supports the point that mere, undefined \\"passage of time\\" is not itself a causal mechanism; time matters only insofar as it proxies concrete changes. Because the source does not state the user\'s claim verbatim but provides the relevant theoretical and empirical treatment that undercuts treating \\"time\\" as a direct cause, the claim is supported but not explicitly asserted in exactly those words."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause education level to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to changes in education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.210