1174. Time-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.25247112526906834, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n5.xml', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7831808724781959, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence relevant to the claim. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides empirical evidence (from a four-year high-school dataset) that macroscopic social-network properties can be stationary over time and that link dynamics are governed by mechanisms (individual attributes, endogenous processes, context) rather than by the mere passage of time. That supports the general idea that time alone need not directly drive changes in network behavior. However, the paper is a single empirical case, does not use the term \\"willingness of social network\\" as a measured variable, and does not establish a general, context-free causal null for all settings. Therefore the source is consistent with—but does not fully establish—the strong, universal claim that time by itself does not directly cause changes in network willingness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter emphasizes that social causal claims require explicit causal mechanisms, interventionist or counterfactual support, and background conditions; social causes are not accepted as real merely on the basis of correlation or the bare occurrence of an antecedent. By that framework, asserting that \\"time\\" alone (the mere passage of time) causes a change in social willingness without positing mediating mechanisms or background conditions would be inadequate. The source thus supports the explanation that time, absent a specified mechanism or intervening factors, should not be treated as a direct social cause."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Our World in Data page documents changes in social connections and reports longitudinal associations (changes over time) between social relations and outcomes, but repeatedly notes that causal mechanisms, effect sizes, and experimental evidence are unclear and that observed links may be driven by confounders or mediating factors. The source does not present evidence that the mere passage of time directly causes changes in a network\'s willingness; instead it emphasizes context, mediators, and uncertainty about direct causality. This supports rejecting a simple direct Time → Willingness causal claim, but the citations also show time-varying associations (so they do not rule out mediated or contextual temporal effects), so the claim of no direct causal relationship is only partially supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause willingness of social network to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time, absent any specific context or mediating variables, would alter the willingness of a social network. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2925696447515889, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8356324533949813, 'min_prob': 0.20092605099463715, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002045161177161892, 'prob_variance': 0.06374166909462961, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2409687011884576, 'avg_nll': 0.25663220970241696}

