   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.20561630151652063, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://americanaddictioncenters.org/the-addiction-cycle'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8347012985412832, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic condition with specific causes (brain chemistry changes, genetics, mental health, environmental exposures, adverse childhood experiences, etc.) and does not identify the mere passage of time as a causal factor. That supports the claim that time alone is not a direct cause of chronic addiction and that causal mechanisms involve other mediating factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter describes addiction as a chronic, time-progressing brain disease driven by substance exposure, neuroadaptations, developmental timing (e.g., adolescence), and genetic/environmental factors. It attributes progression \\"over time\\" to ongoing substance use and mediating biological and social mechanisms, not to the mere passage of clock time as a standalone causal agent. Thus the sources support the claim that time by itself is not a direct cause of chronic addiction absent those mediators/contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes specific causes and mechanisms for addiction (drug exposure, genetics, environment, brain changes, repeated use and tolerance) and does not identify the mere passage of time by itself as a direct causal factor. While the page notes that tolerance and worsening use can occur \\"as time passes,\\" it attributes these changes to continued drug exposure and biological/behavioral mechanisms rather than to time alone, which supports the claim that time without mediators is not a direct cause of chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source characterizes addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and describes how duration and timing of drug exposure (e.g., \\"over time, if drug use continues\\" and earlier initiation increases risk) interact with biological and environmental factors to produce lasting changes. It does not identify the mere passage of time by itself as a direct causal agent of addiction; instead, time matters in conjunction with drug exposure and other mediating factors. Because the citation shows temporal factors matter only insofar as they relate to drug use and development, it partially supports the claim that there is no direct causal link between \\"time\\" alone and chronic addiction.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/the-addiction-cycle", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a Cloudflare blocking page and contains no substantive information about time, chronic addiction, or causal relationships. It does not support the claim or provide evidence for the explanation given."}], "aggregate_score": 0.7, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause time. There is no inherent causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to the development of chronic addiction. Without additional context or mediating variables, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.7, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2117934646189303, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8719294535309223, 'min_prob': 0.11917182485282386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018761903362694069, 'prob_variance': 0.04227806344933272, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6648115557144119, 'avg_nll': 0.1921014643956499}

