   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.2783742155818318, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://emj.bmj.com/content/39/3/168', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8205338/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7471850216694075, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics and trend tables about emergency department visits (counts, rates, percent changes by year or subgroup) but does not address causal mechanisms or explicitly state that the passage of time cannot cause ED visits. The citation therefore does not support the claim that \\"time\\" has no causal relationship with acute care demand; it merely reports observational data over time without addressing causality."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review identifies timing-related factors (e.g., ability to wait, timing of visit, evenings/weekends, clinic hours, “convenience” related to time) as influencing ED use. It therefore provides evidence that temporal circumstances affect acute care demand/ED visits. It does not support the blanket claim that “time” has no causal role; in fact the literature links time-of-day/availability/timing to ED utilization.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8205338/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines ED waiting times and their association with patient experience after arrival; it does not study whether the passage of time causes acute care demand or ED visits (nor the reverse). It therefore does not provide evidence for the asserted absence of a causal link between \\"Time\\" and \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, nor does acute care demand leading to an ED visit cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to acute care demand for ED visits. Without a defined context, time is simply a dimension and not a causal factor for healthcare demand.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3995856472532482, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8013749888282793, 'min_prob': 0.08582957409718936, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00789267676472983, 'prob_variance': 0.07749220390080015, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.117754319272712, 'avg_nll': 0.33617622656687945}

