   Properties: {'session_id': '5a6a1fdb-cfa9-443e-bcbf-9e21e27ffde7', 'prob_std': 0.17581737272757322, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10965713/', 'https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/international-negotiation-daily/telling-time-in-different-cultures/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8257119608431472, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10965713/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports cross-cultural differences in how people use temporal cues and links cultural time concepts (linear vs cyclical) to variations in sense-of-agency judgments. That indicates a relationship between cultural characteristics and time perception/use, not a lack of connection. The paper does not support the claim that there is no causal or relevant relationship; if anything it documents culture-related effects tied to time. It also does not address \\"language barriers\\" specifically.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/international-negotiation-daily/telling-time-in-different-cultures/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes how differing cultural conceptions of time (e.g., \\"clock time\\" vs \\"event time\\" and Islamic spiritual senses of time) can thwart negotiations and create communication difficulties. That directly links time-related cultural conceptions to language/cultural barriers, contradicting the explanation\'s claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause language and cultural barriers to change, nor do language and cultural barriers cause time to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Without a specific context or mechanism, no causal relationship exists be