   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Care avoidance behavior, and Care avoidance behavior does not cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/spatial-behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6387756/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11748873/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3755945/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/spatial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error page (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence regarding spatial scale or care avoidance. Because the citation contains no usable information, it does not support the claimed relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6387756/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies how aggregating data across spatial scales changes relationships between objective variables and subjective life satisfaction. It does not examine \\"care avoidance behavior\\" or provide evidence that larger spatial scales cause people to disengage from seeking care due to reduced personal responsibility. The only loosely related material notes immigrants may have more difficulty accessing health care at small scales, which is tangential and does not support the claimed mechanism. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11748873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how the spatial scale (distance from residence) of built-environment features relates to cognitive change (processing speed and global cognition) and shows that associations decay with distance. It does not study care-seeking or \\"care avoidance\\" behavior, nor does it provide evidence that larger spatial scales cause people to feel less personally responsible or to disengage from seeking care. Therefore the claim about spatial scale increasing care avoidance via reduced personal responsibility is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3755945/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses spatial concepts, measurement issues, and analytic methods in place-based health research (e.g., activity spaces, scale, nonstationarity, and exposure definitions) but does not present evidence that larger spatial scales cause increased \\"care avoidance behavior\\" or that people feel less personally responsible and thus disengage from seeking care as spatial scope expands. The source is methodological and descriptive regarding spatial analysis in obesity research, not empirical evidence of the psychological mechanism (reduced personal responsibility) or a positive causal effect on care avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scales have been shown to directly increase care avoidance behavior because individuals feel less personally responsible for actions taken in broader environments. As the area of focus expands, people are more likely to disengage from seeking care, believing that their individual actions have minimal impact in large-scale contexts.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scales have