3. Spatial Scale-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and the presence or absence of alternatives for ED does not depend on changes in spatial scale within this context.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5785320/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6311852/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0203316', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5785320/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how local spatial access (physician and clinic density, MUAs/HPSAs, travel-time–weighted measures) relates to preventable ED use and finds clinic density and MUA status matter. It does not claim or provide evidence that increasing the geographic \\"spatial scale\\" (i.e., enlarging the area under consideration) directly causes an increase in the availability of alternatives to EDs. The article emphasizes fine‐grained/local measures and notes larger zones can obscure variation; it does not support the causal statement that broader spatial coverage guarantees more facilities/services accessible to patients."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6311852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on internal ED facility design and prioritizing functional units using QFD (allocating space within a department, stakeholder requirements, and alignment with standards). It does not discuss how increasing the spatial scale (geographic area considered) creates more alternative healthcare options accessible to patients or that larger spatial coverage reduces ED burden. At most it addresses internal triage and allocation of internal service areas, not the number or availability of external alternatives as spatial scale increases."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC394340/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper defines \\"availability\\" as the number of local service points and systematically shows that spatial measurement choices (e.g., provider-to-population ratios, catchment areas, gravity and floating-catchment methods) change which providers are considered available to a population — i.e., expanding the spatial catchment will typically include more service points in the measure of availability. However, the paper focuses on primary care (not ED alternatives specifically), emphasizes methodological caveats (distance decay, transport, urban congestion, MAUP), and cautions that measured increases in potential availability do not necessarily translate into realized utilization or reduced ED burden. It therefore supports a qualified, measurement-based association but not the strong, unconditional causal claim that increasing spatial scale guarantees more accessible ED alternatives and reduced ED burden. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that having urgent care centers located in a patient\'s ZIP code (and open) increases local alternatives to ED care and reduces ED visits, so it supports the general idea that more local alternative facilities increase availability and substitute for ED use. However, the paper does not analyze or provide evidence that increasing \\"spatial scale\\" (i.e., enlarging the area under consideration) by itself leads to more available alternatives; it uses ZIP-code presence/proximity and operating hours rather than testing effects of expanding geographic scale. Therefore the support is partial — the citation supports that more nearby alternative facilities increase availability, but not the specific causal claim that increasing spatial scale necessarily produces more alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0203316", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how the presence or absence of alternative primary-care services (co‑located GPs, walk‑in centres, extended GP hours, after‑hours clinics, etc.) affects ED presentations and notes that effects vary by locality (urban vs rural) and by what services already exist. However, it does not analyze or provide evidence that increasing the spatial scale (expanding the geographic area under consideration) directly causes more alternative healthcare options to be created or become accessible. The review documents context-dependent availability of alternatives and that adding specific services can reduce ED demand in some settings, but it does not support the specific causal claim linking larger spatial scale to greater availability of alternatives."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in spatial scale directly causes an increase in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), because as the area under consideration grows, more alternative healthcare options are naturally created and become accessible to patients. This larger spatial coverage guarantees that more facilities and services will be available, thereby reducing the burden on EDs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in spatial scale directly causes an increase in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED), because as the area under consideration grows, more alternative healthcare options are naturally created and become accessible to patients. This larger spatial coverage guarantees that more facilities and services will be available, thereby reducing the burden on EDs.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

