e)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study identifies \\"preference for self-care or alternative care\\" as a reported reason for avoiding medical visits, which partially aligns with the explanation\'s claim that self-reliance can lead to healthcare avoidance. However, the paper does not mention a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or provide evidence that overconfidence in one\'s ability to self-manage all health issues causes avoidance; in fact, it also finds low health self-efficacy and other factors (distrust, fear, time/money) associated with avoidance. Thus the citation supports the general idea that preferring self-care is linked to avoidance but does not support the specific causal mechanism or the \\"24/7-care mentality\\" framing."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a 24/7-care mentality causes people to avoid healthcare because they become overly confident in their ability to self-manage all health issues at home. This constant self-reliance leads individuals to believe professional intervention is unnecessary, directly resulting in healthcare avoidance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a 24/7-care mentality causes people to avoid healthcare because they become overly confident in their ability to self-manage all health issues at home. This constant self-reliance leads individuals to believe professional intervention is unnecessary, directly resulting in healthcare avoidance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

