   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through a direct causal relationship. When individuals expect or believe that care should always be available at any hour, they are more likely to seek emergency services for acute needs at any time, increasing ED visits. Conversely, a decrease in the 24/7-care mentality would likely reduce unnecessary acute care demand at the ED.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24792419486928907, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/', 'https://data.cms.gov/provider-data/topics/hospitals/timely-effective-care', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7592628246152898, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews patient-centered communication and barriers/facilitators to nurse–patient interactions (e.g., staffing, task‑oriented care, communication styles, cultural competence). It does not discuss a \\"24/7‑care mentality,\\" nor does it present evidence that constant availability of care erodes self‑management, reduces preventative/primary care use, or increases emergency department visits. The mechanisms and outcome (greater acute care demand leading to ED visits) asserted in the claim are not covered or supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review examines many system-level drivers of ED demand (e.g., limited primary care access, inappropriate ED use, after‑hours care, public education) and evaluates interventions, 