69. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2607553732033238, 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8', 'https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7390703724049729, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited experiments directly show that greater accessibility of knowledge (faster retrieval / easier access) makes that knowledge about twice as likely to be used in children’s inferencing during text comprehension and that accessibility matters differentially by age and inference type. Thus the sources support a narrower causal link between accessibility and the use/utility of knowledge in comprehension. They do not support broader claims in the explanation — e.g., that mere increased accessibility \\"automatically synthesizes\\" large volumes of data into comprehensive knowledge regardless of prior expertise or engagement, or the sweeping statement that restricting accessibility \\"invariably\\" reduces knowledge capacity. Those broader, stronger claims go beyond the paper’s scope and evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports a positive link between improved information accessibility and greater opportunities for learning and skill/knowledge acquisition (e.g., ICTs enable learning anywhere, case studies where digital tools improved understanding, and discussion of the harms of the digital divide). However, it does not back the strong, deterministic claims in the explanation — that sheer volume of accessible data \\"automatically synthesizes\\" into comprehensive, deeper and more accurate knowledge for individuals regardless of prior expertise or engagement, or that restricting access \\"invariably\\" reduces knowledge. The source itself notes learner differences, the need to facilitate access and adapt methods, and presents conditional/qualified findings rather than universal automatic effects.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents limits in web indexing and argues that web data must be made more accessible as the web becomes a major communications medium, which supports the general importance of accessibility. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that increased accessibility directly enhances individual knowledge, nor does it support the strong claims about automatic synthesis of large volumes of data into deeper or more accurate understanding regardless of users\' prior expertise or engagement. Therefore the source only partially supports the asserted causal relationship and mechanisms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly enhances knowledge by ensuring that individuals are continuously exposed to a wide range of perspectives and facts, which inherently leads to a deeper and more accurate understanding of complex topics. As information becomes more accessible, the sheer volume of available data automatically synthesizes into comprehensive knowledge within individuals, regardless of their prior expertise or engagement levels. Thus, restricting accessibility of information invariably results in a diminished capacity for knowledge, as fewer opportunities exist for automatic knowledge consolidation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly enhances knowledge by ensuring that individuals are continuously exposed to a wide range of perspectives and facts, which inherently leads to a deeper and more accurate understanding of complex topics. As information becomes more accessible, the sheer volume of available data automatically synthesizes into comprehensive knowledge within individuals, regardless of their prior expertise or engagement levels. Thus, restricting accessibility of information invariably results in a diminished capacity for knowledge, as fewer opportunities exist for automatic knowledge consolidation.', 'perplexity': 1.351195556963119, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814468932102195, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08996629412482327, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007704037304737102, 'prob_variance': 0.06799336465440468, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0161779907614508, 'avg_nll': 0.3009897982904751}

70. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited JMIR study shows that easily accessible online health information can empower users, improve informational and decision-making benefits, and promote participation in medical consultations. It describes increased information-seeking before visits and greater involvement in decision-making, but it does not present evidence that greater information accessibility causes people to avoid formal medical care. The source does not support the specific claim that information access directly increases care-avoidance behavior (self-diagnosing/self-treating instead of seeking care)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines physicians’ perceptions of the importance, accessibility, quality, and their use of clinical information in HIE/EHR contexts. It reports that even when information is perceived as accessible and important, physicians often do not routinely use it. The paper concerns clinician information use (not patient behavior), and does not provide evidence that greater public/patient access to information leads to self-diagnosis, self-treatment, or avoidance of formal care. Therefore the citation does not support the claim about increased accessibility promoting care-avoidance by individuals."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no accessible content or evidence relevant to the claim, so it provides no support for the proposed positive relationship between information accessibility and care avoidance behavior.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

71. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes health literacy to change in a direct relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to more easily obtain, understand, and use health-related knowledge, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information makes it harder for individuals to enhance their health literacy. This causal pathway is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24629464625001554, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4417455/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7398604318503433, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes health literacy as linked to education, socioeconomic status, language, and other social determinants and states that targeted interventions (plain language, culturally competent design, provider communication, skill development) can improve health literacy. It does not claim that simply increasing the sheer volume or general accessibility of information automatically or uniformly raises individuals’ ability to evaluate and apply health information regardless of prior knowledge, motivation, or cognitive skills. The evidence supports that appropriately designed, targeted accessibility can help, but not the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4417455/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to have Internet/mobile access and to use them for health purposes, and it emphasizes that limited literacy is a barrier to technology access/use and that training/design adaptations may be required for lower-literate groups. It does not provide evidence that merely increasing the volume or accessibility of information automatically or uniformly raises health literacy across all populations; if anything, it highlights that lack of literacy, age, and income can prevent benefits from accessible information. Therefore the citation does not support the causal, automatic, uniform improvement claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 material links health literacy to the ability to find, understand, and use information and emphasizes organizations’ responsibility to enable access and use, which implies that improving accessibility and organizational practices can support higher health literacy. However, the source does not claim that sheer volume of available information inherently or automatically improves individuals’ critical-evaluation skills, nor that greater accessibility uniformly raises health literacy across all populations regardless of prior knowledge, motivation, or cognitive skills. It instead highlights context, skills, and organizational roles, so it does not fully support the strong, automatic causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source defines health literacy as the ability to find, understand, and use health information and stresses that organizations must make information findable, understandable, and usable (plain language, culturally/linguistically appropriate communication). It emphasizes organizational responsibility, best practices, and barriers/facilitators to improving health literacy. It does not claim that sheer volume or mere accessibility of information automatically or uniformly increases individuals\' health literacy regardless of prior knowledge, motivation, or skills — in fact, it implies accessibility alone is insufficient without appropriate presentation and support."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes health literacy to increase because the sheer volume of available information inherently improves individuals’ ability to critically evaluate, interpret, and apply health-related content. As more information becomes accessible, individuals are inevitably exposed to diverse perspectives and comprehensive data, which directly and automatically enhances their health literacy regardless of their prior knowledge, motivation, or cognitive skills. Thus, greater accessibility of information leads to uniformly higher health literacy across all populations.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes health literacy to increase because the sheer volume of available information inherently improves individuals’ ability to critically evaluate, interpret, and apply health-related content. As more information becomes accessible, individuals are inevitably exposed to diverse perspectives and comprehensive data, which directly and automatically enhances their health literacy regardless of their prior knowledge, motivation, or cognitive skills. Thus, greater accessibility of information leads to uniformly higher health literacy across all populations.', 'perplexity': 1.3913150250093362, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8166586817211081, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0014076876776650087, 'toke