ct on informal care is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729038/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents that lack of information is a barrier for existing informal caregivers, that caregivers seek information (including online) and that education/resources can empower caregivers to better manage care. However, it does not present evidence that increasing accessibility of information causes more people to begin providing informal care or that learning online leads people to take on caregiving roles. The citations support information\'s role in helping and empowering current caregivers, not the causal claim that greater information accessibility increases the number of informal caregivers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12424210/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides qualitative, pilot evidence that an information-accessing chatbot can trigger self-awareness, reflection, and role recognition among existing or emerging informal caregivers and that users valued trustworthy, personalized information and recommendations. However, it does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information causally increases the number of people who begin providing informal care; the study focuses on supporting role transition and awareness among those already in caregiving situations (n=7), and does not measure changes in caregiver incidence or show a direct causal effect that people \\"automatically feel more capable and motivated\\" to take on care because of online information. Thus the citation partially supports the idea that accessible information can influence caregiver recognition and support-seeking, but not the stronger causal claim about increasing the number of informal caregivers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125