", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or data. There is no evidence in the provided citation that links increased accessibility of information to greater frailty, so the claim is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly leads to greater frailty in individuals because constant exposure to information reduces the need for physical and mental activity. As people rely more on easily accessible information, their bodies and minds weaken over time, increasing frailty.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly leads to greater frailty in individuals because constant exposure to information reduces the need for physical and mental activity. As people rely more on easily accessible information, their bodies and minds weaken over time, increasing frailty.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

