   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater accessibility of information enables individuals to acquire knowledge and skills, thereby raising their education level. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits learning opportunities and lowers education levels. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2640795983829398, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666412722000137', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0160791X21000403', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2025.1527851/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7225861756711982, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666412722000137", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and provides no content or evidence for the claimed causal relationship; therefore there is nothing in the citation(s) to support the explanation that education level directly and independently increases information accessibility.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0160791X21000403", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole citation is an error page (content unavailable) and provides no evidence or information supporting the claimed causal relationship between education level and accessibility of information. Because the source content cannot be evaluated or does not exist, the claim is not supported by the cited evidence.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2025.1527851/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that educational technologies and institutional investments can improve accessibility of information in educational settings and highlights links between education, technology use, and equity (e.g., technologies that increase accessibility, the role of higher education and digital tools, and that investment and infrastructure matter). However, it does not provide direct evidence that higher population education levels themselves cause increased accessibility of information (nor that this effect operates independently). The review in fact discusses mediating factors—infrastructure, teacher skills, policy, and socioeconomic digital divides—contradicting the claim\'s assertion of an unmediated direct effect. Thus some elements of a positive association are supported, but the full causal and \\"independent\\" claim is not.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses ICTs, digital skills, and the digital divide, and reports studies and proposals for improving universal access (e.g., broadband deployment, accessibility evaluation, tools and training). It does not provide evidence that higher population education level directly causes increased accessibility of information (through demand, creation, community investment in libraries/digital platforms), nor does it claim the effect operates independently of other mediating factors. In fact, the source emphasizes multiple contextual determinants (technology presence, disability, rural/urban divide, skills), which contradict the claim of an unmediated direct relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education level in a population leads directly to increased accessibility of information, as individuals with advanced education are more likely to demand, create, and disseminate information resources. As education level rises, communities invest more in infrastructure such as libraries and digital platforms, thereby enhancing accessibility of information. Conversely, lower education level reduces such demand and investment, resulting in decreased accessibility of information. This relationship operates independently and is not mediated by other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education level in a population leads directly to increased accessibility of information, as individuals with advanced education are more likely to demand, create, and disseminate information resources. As education level rises, communities invest more in infrastructure such as libraries and digital platforms, thereby enhancing accessibility of information. Conversely, lower education level reduces such demand and investment, resulting in decreased accessibility of information. This relationship operates independently and is not mediated by other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4093170546550626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7853767982939583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08500712693194586, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0046598658014534965, 'prob_variance': 0.0697380342820948, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.885408403560522, 'avg_nll': 0.3431052286499909}

