   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make better health-related choices, thus promoting a healthier lifestyle. Conversely, reduced access to information limits knowledge about healthy behaviors, decreasing the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11369541/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.924331/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11369541/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review documents that greater online accessibility (especially via social media) has increased the spread of health misinformation and conflicting advice, that users often lack health/digital literacy to evaluate it, and that exposure to misinformation is associated with believing false claims and adopting fewer preventive or recommended behaviors (e.g., reduced masking, vaccine hesitancy). It also cites studies showing that conflicting information increases confusion and can weaken adherence to science-based behaviors. These findings support a negative effect of increased information accessibility on healthy behaviors/choices."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that abundant/low-quality online health information, limited misinformation checks, and information overload can hinder trust, create difficulty finding and understanding information, and negatively affect online health information–seeking. However, 