 usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that links increased information accessibility to a slowing of perceived time or to cognitive-load effects on duration perception. Therefore the claim is unsupported by the citation given."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing and consuming the large amounts of easily available data. With more information at their fingertips, individuals perceive each hour as lasting longer due to the cognitive load.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As accessibility of information increases, the passage of time slows down because people spend more time processing and consuming the large amounts of easily available data. With more information at their fingertips, individuals perceive each hour as lasting longer due to the cognitive load.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

