   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information directly reduces healthcare avoidance by making it easier for individuals to understand and utilize healthcare services. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information increases avoidance because individuals lack the knowledge or confidence to seek care. This relationship is direct, not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.281669144173978, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/', 'https://ahimafoundation.ahima.org/research/the-critical-role-of-web-accessibility-in-health-information-access-understanding-and-use/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7233547360721566, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no accessible content or evidence. Because there is no usable citation material to evaluate, the explanation’s causal claim (that information accessibility directly decreases healthcare avoidance without intermediaries) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines individual differences in information avoidance (and age-related patterns/reasons for avoiding decision-relevant information) in health-related decision scenarios. It does not provide evidence about the effects of information accessibility on healthcare seeking or avoidance, nor does it test whether increased access to information causes people to self-manage more or to attend formal healthcare less. The paper addresses who avoids information and why, not the causal relationship between information accessibility and healthcare utilization; therefore it does not support the claimed causal negative relationship (or the proposed mechanism) between accessibility of information and healthcare avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because greater access to information directly alleviates symptoms of illness by empowering individuals to self-manage their health at home, ultimately reducing the perceived necessity to seek formal healthcare services. Conversely, when accessibility of information is limited, individuals are less able to manage their symptoms independently, which increases their reliance on and attendance at healthcare facilities, thereby reducing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is direct and does not require consideration of intermediate behavioral or psychological factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because greater access to information directly alleviates symptoms of illness by empowering individuals to self-manage their health at home, ultimately reducing the perceived necessity to seek formal healthcare services. Conversely, when accessibility of information is limited, individuals are less able to manage their symptoms independently, which increases their reliance on and attendance at healthcare facilities, thereby reducing healthcare avoidance. This relationship is direct and does not require consideration of intermediate behavioral or psychological factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4594841552650162, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7859220659849224, 'corruption_type': 'motivation'