age describes fear, embarrassment, and shame as central symptoms of social anxiety disorder and explicitly recommends seeking information and education as a helpful way to support oneself or a loved one (e.g., \\"A good way to help yourself ... is to seek information\\"). It also notes support groups can help people learn that their thoughts about judgment may be distorted. These statements imply that access to information/support can affect feelings of fear and shame, so the sources do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited APA material describes stigma as arising from lack of understanding and inaccurate media representations (which increase fear and prejudice) and discusses self-stigma (internalized shame). It also presents evidence that informational interventions (contact, videos, social marketing) reduce stigma, shame, and help-seeking barriers. Those points indicate an influence of information accessibility/quality on fear and shame, so the claim of no relationship is contradicted by the sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1998702715530047, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9179315868229178, 'min_prob': 0.14089794859521051, 'token_prob_slope': -0.017601996626294743, 'prob_variance': 0.06053343389010091, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.25638469323772717, 'avg_nll': 0.1822134439108047}

