   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2608434534332232, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0965856424003665'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7279007404750061, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source provides no usable content (an error message), so there is no evidence in the citation to support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between accessibility of information and a 24/7-care mentality.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0965856424003665", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence. It cannot support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between accessibility of information and a 24/7-care mentality or any mediating mechanisms.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causality", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causality explains that causation requires evidence beyond correlation (temporality, mechanisms, probabilistic/ counterfactual criteria), warns about confounding and mediators, and describes methods (causal graphs, backdoor criterion, causal calculus) needed to establish causal claims. Those points directly support the explanation that one cannot assert a direct causal link between \\"accessibility of information\\" and a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" without a defined mechanism or supporting evidence and while accounting for potential mediators/confounders."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where accessibility of information causes 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. Without a defined mechanism or evidence linking these variables directly, and considering potential mediators like health literacy or organizational structure, a direct causal link cannot be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3552836666064225, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8042631657172646, 'min_prob': 0.14398432116581752, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004509313005525959, 'prob_variance': 0.06803930719897008, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.152910770368138, 'avg_nll': 0.30401078047043956}

101. Accessibility of information-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.25664493912973024, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/', 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/Guides/Cognitive_accessibility', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92265/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7481840790555476, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8521906/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reviews accessibility in digital mental health, defines accessibility, and emphasizes limited research and lack of standards — noting accessibility affects access to care and app effectiveness but not claiming or demonstrating a direct causal link between \\"accessibility of information\\" and the onset of mental disorders. It therefore supports the claim that there is no established direct causal relationship (and that mechanisms/definitions are insufficient), but it does not explicitly prove the absence of any causal or bidirectional effects. Hence the support is partial."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility/Guides/Cognitive_accessibility", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The MDN document describes that cognitive impairments (including some mental illnesses) affect how people interact with information and gives guidelines to make web content accessible. It does not present evidence that accessibility of information causes mental disorders or that mental disorders directly cause information accessibility. However, the page does note that content can temporarily affect mood (e.g., being momentarily saddened by a post), so it does not unequivocally affirm a complete absence of any effect from information on mental states. Because the citation documents a relationship in terms of who needs accessible content but offers no evidence of a direct causal link either way, it only partially supports the claim of \\"no direct causal link.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92265/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NICE chapter documents many associations between access to information/services and mental healthcare (e.g., lack of information limits recognition, help‑seeking and treatment uptake; language barriers and interpreters affect service use and satisfaction). It does not present evidence that accessibility of information directly causes the onset of mental disorders, nor definitive evidence that having a mental disorder directly causes changes in information accessibility. Because the citation describes correlated effects on access and care rather than a clear direct causal link in either direction, it does not support a direct causal relationship — but it does show related, bidirectional associations around access to care, so the stronger claim of “no relationship at all” is only partly supported by the source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not directly cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder directly cause accessibility of information. Without clear definitions or a specified mechanism, there is no basis for establishing a direct causal link between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.324096618457824, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8241192420273427, 'min_prob': 0.09317238586189963, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006955765064725766, 'prob_variance': 0.06586662478090295, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0495982784455522, 'avg_nll': 0.2807304295183252}

