103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that capturing previously lost ED demand (eg, patients who left without being seen or diverted ambulances) can produce substantial additional daily revenue and that dynamic bed-management strategies that enable more ED throughput can be financially attractive. However, the paper also reports that non-ED admissions reimburse substantially more per inpatient day than ED admissions, and that simply shifting capacity from non-ED to ED admissions can reduce revenue unless managed optimally. Thus the citation supports that increased ED visits can raise revenue in some circumstances (captured lost demand, with appropriate capacity management) but does not justify a general unqualified positive effect of any increase in ED visits on hospital financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Affairs analysis finds that, overall in 2009, hospital revenue from ED care exceeded costs (a $6.1B net positive, 7.8% margin) and projects greater ED profitability under ACA-driven increases in insurance coverage. It therefore supports the idea that ED visits can generate hospital revenue and that more insured ED use raises hospital income. However, the paper also shows this effect is highly payer‑dependent (private insurance produces large profits while Medicare, Medicaid, and the uninsured often produce losses), so an unconditional statement that more ED visits always increase hospital financial resources is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article mainly analyzes the financial harms of ED crowding (increased costs, lost revenue from ambulance diversions and boarding, and opportunity costs of blocked ED capacity) and argues that reducing crowding could save money or generate additional revenue by allowing the ED to treat more patients. It does not provide direct evidence that higher acute care demand (more ED visits) per se increases hospital financial resources via billing — rather it emphasizes that inefficient handling of demand can raise costs or reduce revenue. Thus the sources partly support the idea that treating additional patients (if capacity and throughput permit) can increase revenue, but do not directly substantiate the blanket claim that more ED visits always have a positive effect on hospital financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes factors that drive ED demand, system funding structures, and the effects of increased demand (crowding, access block, service use patterns). It does not provide evidence that ED visits increase hospital revenue through billing/reimbursements; it even notes complexities of funding (e.g., private ED services not covered by private health insurance) and discusses negative operational impacts of congestion. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that acute-care–driven ED visits positively increase hospitals’ financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The HCUP Statistical Brief provides descriptive data on reasons, rates, and patient characteristics for ED visits in 2018 but contains no evidence or analysis linking increased availability of diagnostic resources to higher acute care demand or to patients/referring providers choosing the ED because of enhanced diagnostic capabilities. It does note some nonurgent ED use relates to access to primary care or insurance, but it does not support the specific causal claim about diagnostic resources attracting more ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page gives counts and rates of emergency department visits and related breakdowns (e.g., number of visits, percent seen within 15 minutes, admission rates) but contains no information about the availability of diagnostic resources, patient or provider behavior in response to diagnostic capacity, or any causal or correlational evidence linking greater diagnostic resources to increased ED demand. Therefore the source does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents the rapid expansion and greater availability of diagnostic tests and imaging, discusses how that has changed clinician behavior (e.g., increased testing, bypassing bedside examination) and notes regional variation and overuse of diagnostic resources. Those points support the idea that more diagnostic capacity changes utilization patterns and can increase testing. However, the chapter does not present evidence that increased diagnostic availability directly causes higher acute-care demand resulting in more ED visits (for example, patient or referrer behavior that specifically shifts cases to the ED because of perceived comprehensive diagnostics). The specific causal claim — that enhanced diagnostic resources attract more patients to the emergency department and thereby raise ED visit demand — is not demonstrated in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly links limited community/primary-care access to increased ED visits (input factors) and shows that reduced bed availability/capacity leads to overcrowding, boarding, longer waits, and worse outcomes. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that a decline in ED capacity itself causes community members to perceive reduced access and therefore increase acute-care-seeking (i.e., that lower ED capacity drives higher demand). In other words, the citations support that low community capacity raises ED demand and that low ED capacity produces operational problems, but they do not directly support the specific causal mechanism asserted (capacity decline → perceived reduced access → increased ED visit demand)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visits (counts, rates, and some timing measures) but does not present evidence linking ED capacity to changes in community demand or perceptions of access. It contains no analysis or data demonstrating that lower capacity causes increased ED visits (or that higher capacity reduces demand), nor does it discuss perceived access driving visit rates. Therefore the explanation\'s causal negative-relationship claim is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no usable content (an error message from ScienceDirect) and provides no evidence for the claimed inverse relationship between acute ED demand and healthcare professional functioning. Because the citation offers no data or arguments, it cannot support the explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and related utilization statistics only. It does not present data or analysis linking ED visit volume to changes in healthcare professionals\' functioning, skill atrophy, cognitive flexibility, or decision-making, nor does it provide evidence of the causal mechanism described. Therefore the citation does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HWSM Demand Modeling Overview describes methods for projecting demand, drivers of ED/hospital demand, and how demand is converted into staffing needs. It does not provide evidence or discussion that increased acute/ED demand causes skill atrophy, reduced cognitive flexibility, or impaired decision‑making among healthcare professionals (nor the inverse effect when demand falls). The source addresses workforce quantity and demand drivers, not changes in individual clinician functioning or cognitive performance as a function of visit volume or case diversity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816}

