   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, percentages) and related breakdowns, but contains no analysis or evidence about causal relationships between ED demand and the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. It does not support the claim that acute care demand does not determine availability of alternatives (nor does it present evidence that causality runs the other way)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that availability/access to alternative care sites (PCPs, urgent care, retail clinics) influences ED use and frames availability as a causal factor affecting non-urgent ED visits. It explicitly discusses interventions and system supply factors that change ED demand. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence proving that acute-care demand (ED visits) cannot causally influence the existence or accessibility of alternatives; it neither tests nor rules out reverse causation. Therefore the citation supports the idea that availability affects ED demand (the opposite direction) but does not firmly establish that no causal effect runs from ED demand to availability."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents multiple examples where changes in availability or policy outside the ED (adding non‑ED capacity, managed care, copayments, EMS diversion, etc.) alter ED utilization, supporting the statement that availability of alternatives can affect ED demand (i.e., the causality often runs from availability → ED visits). However, the review does not provide direct evidence demonstrating the absence of any causal pathway from the volume of acute ED presentations to the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. It does not analyze or rule out mechanisms by which high ED demand might lead to development or expansion of alternatives. Therefore the claim that there is \\"no\\" causal determination in the specified direction is not fully supported by the citation, though the citation does support the reverse causal direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

