   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.21435787373101606, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8185628273434149, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of ED visits and categorizes visits by causes (injury, influenza tests, mental health, etc.) but does not discuss causal direction between \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" and \\"acute event.\\" It reports associations and visit statistics, not evidence that acute events cause demand (or that demand could not cause events). Therefore the citation does not support the asserted causal direction or the claim of no reverse mechanism.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how urgent care center availability changes ED visit counts and acuity mix (showing substitution away from EDs for less emergent complaints). It treats visits as responses to underlying conditions and examines utilization effects, but it does not provide evidence testing or ruling out any causal effect from \\"acute care demand/ED visits\\" to the incidence of acute events. It does not discuss mechanisms by which increased ED demand would produce more acute medical events; at best it assumes the conventional direction (events → care seeking). Therefore the source does not support the explicit claim that there is no causal pathway from increased acute-care demand/ED visits to more acute events.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes input factors (acute/urgent events, seasonal epidemics, lack of primary care) as causes of ED visits and overcrowding, supporting the direction acute event → acute care demand. It does not present evidence that increased ED demand or visits cause new acute events; however, it does describe harms from overcrowding (delays, increased morbidity/mortality), which could complicate conditions but do not directly demonstrate that ED demand causes acute events. Thus the sources support the stated causal direction but do not definitively rule out or empirically refute any reverse causal mechanism. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute event causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit, not the other way around. There is no causal mechanism by which increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit would cause more acute events. The direction of causality is from acute event to care demand, not vice versa.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1971514716627114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8790373505999207, 'min_prob': 0.12865274065798912, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022866280216882182, 'prob_variance': 0.04594929803048223, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7060284073672355, 'avg_nll': 0.1799449613124276}

