   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article frames organizational structure as an information-processing design chosen to manage environmental uncertainty (e.g., patient arrivals) and explicitly discusses design strategies (pooling, vertical information systems, lateral relations, decentralization) used to respond to changes in demand and uncertainty. It therefore treats patient-flow uncertainty as a driver for organizational-design choices rather than asserting there is no causal pathway. It does not support the categorical claim that acute ED demand cannot cause organizational-structure change; if anything it describes mechanisms by which demand/uncertainty motivate structural or coordination adaptations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explains what organizational structure is and how organizations design charts (driven by mission, leadership decisions, and factors like pace of change and external environment). It does not provide evidence that acute care demand or ED visit volume cannot cause organizational-structure changes; if anything, it notes responsiveness to change as a design consideration. The source therefore does not support the stronger claim that there is no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "agg