   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24742104291865166, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9140447/', 'https://emj.bmj.com/content/39/3/168', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11251303/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8036650268594444, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9140447/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that frail older adults are increasingly represented in EDs and that frailty predisposes to greater acute care use, worse ED/hospital outcomes, and resource use — supporting the claim that frailty leads to increased acute care demand and that comorbidities/acute events mediate associations. However, the paper also notes that hospitalization and bed rest can produce functional decline and increased morbidity, which could contribute to worsening frailty; it does not establish a clear absence of any causal effect from ED visits/acute hospitalization to frailty. Therefore the provided source supports parts of the explanation (frailty → ED use and the role of mediators) but does not support the strong assertion that there is no direct causal effect in the ED→frailty direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11251303/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that frailty is associated with higher risk of emergency hospital admission and that frailty commonly precedes acute care use (supporting the direction frailty → ED visits). It does not provide evidence that an ED visit or acute care demand directly causes frailty; where associations are observed the paper explicitly notes causality cannot be inferred and discusses mediating factors (geriatric syndromes, acute events, hospital-acquired complications) that explain relationships. Thus the so