   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2498741128459255, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693976365477758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study examines how parental health literacy influences decisions to seek acute care and use the ED (health literacy → ED use). It identifies mechanisms (caregiving skills, illness assessment, navigation) by which low health literacy increases nonurgent ED use and discusses targeting health literacy to reduce ED visits. The paper does not provide evidence that ED visits cause changes in health literacy, nor does it test reverse causation. Because the citation supports the direction asserted in the explanation (health literacy influences ED use) and offers no support for ED visits causing changes in health literacy, but does not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal effect from ED visits to health literacy, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study examines health literacy as a predictor of potentially preventable ED visits and finds limited literacy is associated with higher ED use; it treats the causal direction as literacy → ED visits. The paper does not present evidence that ED visits change an individual’s health literacy, and authors note limitations in making causal inferences. However, the study does not directly test or rule out ED visits causing changes in health literacy, so it supports the claim only indirectly (by demonstrating the opposite direction and lacking evidence for ED→literacy)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study examines how low health literacy is associated with higher ED utilization and returns, and discusses mechanisms for health literacy → ED use. It provides no evidence that ED visits change an individual\'s health literacy, and in fact supports the opposite-direction association (health literacy affecting utilization). Thus the citation is consistent with labeling the ED-visit → health-literacy causal link as none (no support in the source)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that limited health literacy is associated with greater ED use (HL → ED) and explicitly describes health literacy as dynamic — noting that anxiety, stress, and the hospitalization experience can impair a caregiver’s ability to process and use health information. That implies acute care encounters (including ED/hospitalization) can alter a person’s functional health literacy at least transiently. The citation therefore does not support a “no relationship / no causal effect” claim; it actually supports both the HL→ED association and that ED/hospital stress can change effective health literacy.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the frequency of ED visits would directly alter an individual's health literacy. Causality may exist in the opposite direction, but not as specified here.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2945576499068878, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376701756866648, 'min_prob': 0.11527626171986272, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005741640325423388, 'prob_variance': 0.06243707227053832, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9712650000906131, 'avg_nll': 0.25816905371522675}

128. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.23314726083857487, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6978874/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8442096/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7972799306036924, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review catalogues many determinants of therapy adherence (patient beliefs, health literacy, patient–provider relationship, healthcare access, socioeconomic factors, disease characteristics) and notes that non-compliance is associated with increased urgent/ED visits — i.e., the literature discusses ED/urgent visits as an outcome of non‑adherence rather than as a direct cause of changes in adherence. It also identifies mediating “soft” factors (health literacy, avoidance/attendance behaviors, communication) that can explain associations. However, the review does not explicitly test or rule out a direct causal effect of ED visits on subsequent adherence; it simply does not describe a direct mechanism whereby frequency of ED visits inherently alters adherence. Therefore the explanation that any observed association is likely mediated by other factors is consistent with the citation, but the stronger claim that there is definitively no direct causal mechanism is not directly proven by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6978874/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited realist review analyzes why patients use emergency and urgent care and identifies mechanisms driving ED attendance (eg. uncertainty, need for speed, following others\' advice). It does not examine whether ED visits affect patients\' subsequent therapy adherence, nor does it provide evidence that no direct causal effect exists between ED attendance and therapy compliance. While the review discusses mediating factors (stress, health knowledge, care‐seeking behaviour), it does not support the specific claim that acute care demand/ED visits do not change therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8442096/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows that patient understanding of ED discharge instructions influences treatment adherence and that improving instructions increases understanding. It also reports that number of ED visits in the prior 12 months was not associated with preintervention comprehension scores. However, the paper does not directly study whether acute care demand or frequency of ED visits causes changes in therapy compliance, nor does it rule out other causal pathways linking ED visits to adherence. Thus the evidence only partially supports the claim (indirectly suggesting visit frequency doesn\'t affect comprehension, a mediator of compliance) but does not conclusively demonstrate no causal effect on therapy compliance overall."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause therapy compliance to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the frequency of ED visits would inherently alter a patient's adherence to therapy. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or care avoidance behavior, not direct causation.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2886891201595119, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8470402889923722, 'min_prob': 0.02038432776472911, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004564056170848853, '