   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27296904278513845, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9613716/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7555082071859338, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes dimensions of patient autonomy (decisional, executive, functional, informative, narrative) and contrasts autonomy issues in acute versus chronic care, but it does not present empirical evidence or argument specifically about acute care demand (e.g., frequency of ED visits) causing changes in an individual\'s intrinsic need for autonomy, nor about need for autonomy influencing ED visit demand. It therefore does not support the specific claim that there is no causal relationship between \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" and \\"need for autonomy.\\" At best the paper notes that acute care contexts emphasize decisional autonomy, which is not the same as establishing a null causal link between ED visits and need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that impairments in executive autonomy (the capacity to implement self-care) can contribute to frequent readmissions, adverse events, and acute care visits and recommends assessing and supporting executive autonomy when facing repeated ED visits. Thus the source implies a causal/associative link between deficits in autonomy and acute care demand rather than supporting a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9613716/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper argues that illness, bodily and affective states, and features of clinical encounters can causally affect a patient’s capacity for and exercise of autonomy (e.g., pain, shock, drugs, embodied distress, and clinician interactions can undermine or support autonomy). Although it does not mention \\"acute care demand\\" or ED visits explicitly, it directly supports the view that acute clinical situations and patients’ bodily/affective states can influence autonomy. Therefore the claim that there is no causal relationship (NONE) between acute care ED visits and need for autonomy is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not causally cause 'Need for autonomy' to change, nor does 'Need for autonomy' causally affect acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct mechanism by which the frequency of acute care ED visits would change an individual's intrinsic need for autonomy or vice versa. Any association is likely explained by mediating factors such as health literacy or care avoidance behavior, but not by a direct causal link.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3813444699346198, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8054894221256444, 'min_prob': 0.08046192946858235, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028133406416680565, 'prob_variance': 0.07451209831903474, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9431309947383715, 'avg_nll': 0.32305727846792887}

