tion (\\"no direct causal relationship in either direction\\") is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article defines multimorbidity as the coexistence of multiple chronic (long‑term) conditions, which supports the explanation that multimorbidity is a chronic state distinct from short‑term acute events. However the article also reports that acute infections such as COVID-19 have been associated with an increased risk of multimorbidity in people with a pre‑existing condition, indicating at least an association (and a possible causal pathway in that specific context). The citation therefore does not unequivocally support a blanket statement of no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source clearly defines multimorbidity as the presence of two or more chronic conditions (lasting >1 year) and explains the concept of comorbidities vs a primary diagnosis (which may be the acute reason for hospitalization). That supports the distinction between chronic multimorbidity and an acute event and that they can co-occur. However, the citation does not present evidence or an explicit statement ruling out direct causal relationships in either direction between acute events and multimorbidity. It therefore does not fully support the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited journal content focuses on chronic conditions and multimorbidity (definitions