   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not causally cause care avoidance behavior. There is no direct mechanism by which the availability of alternative care options for emergency departments would directly cause an individual to avoid seeking care. Any observed association may be due to mediating factors such as accessibility or health literacy, but not a direct causal effect.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10447360/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/', 'https://emj.bmj.com/content/40/9/641'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10447360/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative evaluation shows that older patients who were hesitant to use emergency services (because of long waits, overnight stays, negative ED experiences, or lack of GP access) viewed an ED‑avoidance alternative (the CARE centre) positively and were willing to use it. The findings therefore support the proposed mechanism (alternatives reduce psychological/logistical barriers and lessen hesitation to attend urgent care) for the participants studied. However, the study is a small, purposively sampled qualitative service evaluation of users (not a population-level or controlled causal test) and does not provide quantitative evidence that increasing availability of alternatives causes a net decrease in overall care‑avoidance behavior. Thus the citation supports the plausibility and participants’ experiences but not a general causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that many ED-avoiders used alternative care routes (calling/messaging a doctor, visiting clinics,