180. Availability of alternatives for ED-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.282723052735487, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105801/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3153746/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4395546/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.791679829935587, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies healthcare-system factors (accessibility, availability, long wait times, difficulty getting prescriptions, unsatisfactory clinic visits) as contributors to non‑compliance, and it highlights health literacy and patient–provider communication as important mediators. It does not provide evidence that \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" has no direct effect on therapy adherence; in fact the review implies system-level availability/accessibility can influence compliance. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal link (and that any association is only via other mediators) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11105801/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASAM/AAAP guideline presents evidence that availability of alternative treatment modalities (eg, contingency management, telemedicine, technology-based interventions) increases treatment engagement and retention. It explicitly recommends contingency management as a primary component because it improves engagement and reduced stimulant use (Recommendation 5) and recommends telemedicine to deliver behavioral treatment to patients who face access barriers because it improves access and retention (Recommendation 8). The guideline also discusses implementation barriers (limited availability) as a reason CM is underused, implying availability affects uptake. Therefore the citation contradicts the claim that availability of alternatives for ED has no direct effect on therapy compliance — it supports a causal/associated effect of availability on engagement/compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3153746/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses limited availability and dissemination of empirically supported treatments and emphasizes that treatment uptake/outcomes depend on mediating factors (training, accessibility, therapist skill, delivery quality). That indirectly supports the idea that mere availability of alternative treatments does not automatically change outcomes. However the source does not examine \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" specifically nor provide direct evidence about a lack of a causal link between availability of alternatives and individual patient therapy compliance. Therefore the support is indirect and incomplete."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4395546/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents many patient-, therapist-, treatment-, organization- and government-level factors that influence whether people receive and adhere to evidence-based psychological treatments. It emphasizes patient-level mediators (transport, childcare, motivation, beliefs, mental health literacy), notes that patient choice and treatment acceptability are linked to adherence, and argues that availability of EBPTs (vs. medication) affects the range of choices. That supports the idea that any link between available alternatives and adherence is likely indirect and mediated by factors like accessibility, acceptability, and literacy. However, the source does not assert that availability has no direct causal effect; rather it describes plausible mechanisms by which availability could influence compliance (i.e., mediated pathways), so it does not fully justify a blanket \\"NONE (no direct effect)\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that medication adherence is multifactorial—driven by patient-, physician-, and health-system–level factors (including limited access to care and health literacy)—and that no single simple intervention reliably changes long-term adherence. It therefore supports the general idea that effects on adherence are likely mediated by other variables rather than by a single direct cause. However, the paper does not specifically examine \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (the purported exposure) nor provide direct evidence that such availability has no direct causal effect on therapy compliance. Because the source supports the broader claim of multifactorial and mediated influences but does not directly address the specific exposure, the claim is only partially supported by the citation provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Availability of alternatives for ED does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of ED alternatives would directly influence a patient's adherence to therapy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables, such as care accessibility or health literacy.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3888327964856444, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152898344386081, 'min_prob': 0.05946569143806623, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0057705997569712855, 'prob_variance': 0.07993232454807296, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7987057843850789, 'avg_nll': 0.3284636796261422}

