   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.25823939495812614, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/', 'https://academic.oup.com/jrsssa/article/176/1/5/7077805', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827319301545', 'https://opa.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/2025-05/gender-dysphoria-report.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8182866236381222, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly analyzes how availability of urgent care centers (an alternative to the ED) affects ED use using a quasi‑experimental design and finds that when urgent care centers are open, nonurgent ED visits fall. It therefore provides evidence of a causal effect of alternative availability on care–seeking behavior. The paper does not study a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" (nor define or measure such a construct), so it cannot be used to support the claim that there is no causal relationship between availability of ED alternatives and a 24/7‑care mentality. Moreover, the study does specify spatial (ZIP code) and temporal (hourly operating times) context, contradicting the explanation\'s statement that no such context is provided. For these reasons the citations do not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827319301545", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it provides no information about mechanisms, definitions, or context linking \\"Availability of alternatives for ED\\" and \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" so it cannot support the claim of no causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"