   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.16574631468274265, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://educationnorthwest.org/sites/default/files/resources/culturally-responsive-teaching.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8653026709518991, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents how language and cultural barriers (including scarcity of interpreters and cultural distrust) affect access to and utilization of health services; it therefore implies a connection between cultural/language barriers and availability/use of services. It does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between availability of alternatives to ED and language/cultural barriers, nor does it discuss ED alternatives specifically. Thus the citation does not support the assertion of no relationship and in places suggests the opposite — that barriers influence access to alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents language and cultural barriers (especially for immigrant families) and describes programmatic alternatives and supports — e.g., mediators, cultural brokers, volunteers, co-teaching models, and different school placements (special vs inclusive) — that are reported to help families navigate education and reduce language/cultural obstacles. It therefore presents evidence of links between availability of certain educational alternatives/services and the presence or mitigation of language and cultural barriers. It does not support the assertion that there is no causal or direct connection between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and its references describe how language barriers affect healthcare utilization and outcomes (including longer stays, higher readmission risk, and impacts on ED encounters) and discuss interpreter modalities in ED settings. The text even states that improving resources for patients with limited English proficiency can make them \\"less likely ... to visit the ED\\" and cites studies on interpreter use and ED throughput/satisfaction. Nothing in the citation set supports a claim of no causal or directional relationship; in fact the sources indicate language barriers can influence ED use and that interpreter availability/resources can change ED-related outcomes. Therefore the provided citations do not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited thesis and articles focus on language and cultural barriers in schools and on how educational programs, policies, and outreach (e.g., bilingual education, transitional programs, community engagement, translated communications, parent outreach) affect Hispanic students\' language acquisition, parental involvement, and school connectivity. They do not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (that variable is not present in the sources), and in fact the citations describe causal mechanisms by which educational alternatives/policies and outreach can change language and cultural barriers. Therefore the claim that there is no causal link is not supported by the provided citations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://educationnorthwest.org/sites/default/files/resources/culturally-responsive-teaching.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document discusses how instructional programs, culturally based education, use of heritage languages, and access to appropriate resources and alternatives in schooling can reduce language and cultural barriers and affect student participation and outcomes. It therefore implies causal links between availability of educational alternatives/practices and language/cultural barriers rather than no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause language and cultural barriers to change, nor do language and cultural barriers cause changes in the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1112447591457824, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9218115173858924, 'min_prob': 0.3263418763223405, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033847000078750995, 'prob_variance': 0.027471840830910747, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.5287852194704621, 'avg_nll': 0.10548079165558263}

