   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.21372796785903844, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/1999/0915/p1159.html', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/mens-health/which-drug-for-erectile-dysfunction', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8308139365744699, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents mechanisms by which drug availability and substitution can contribute to medication errors (e.g., \\"Some distortions may occur when the prescribed medication is unavailable and is subsequently substituted for a different drug,\\" causes like similar drug names, substitutions, and dispensing/stock issues). It describes multiple pathways linking availability/substitution decisions to incorrect dispensing or use. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism between availability of alternatives and incorrect medication use — it in fact indicates plausible causal links."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/1999/0915/p1159.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP article explicitly states that the availability and marketing of new ED therapies greatly increased public awareness and prescribing (e.g., millions of sildenafil prescriptions). It also documents safety concerns from incorrect use or contraindicated combinations (notably sildenafil plus nitrates), warnings to clinicians/paramedics, and postmarketing deaths related to such combinations. Those points indicate a plausible direct pathway in which greater availability/use of ED medications can lead to incorrect or dangerous medication use. The citations therefore do not support a \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/mens-health/which-drug-for-erectile-dysfunction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Harvard Health article discusses both availability/cost/choices of ED drugs and how patients may use them incorrectly, but it does not examine or provide evidence about whether availability of alternatives causes incorrect use (or vice versa). It presents separate facts (multiple drugs exist, insurance limits, and common user mistakes) without addressing their causal relationship, so it does not support the claim of no causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would likely be explained by other mediating variables in the system.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1946409565834002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8809877773491398, 'min_prob': 0.171292836420859, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0068385579800120735, 'prob_variance': 0.045679644245154166, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9064451120477515, 'avg_nll': 0.1778456854959804}

