   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.3008307225121728, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.628252/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.762229353985784, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively documents that shame is a complex, relational emotion produced by trauma, power dynamics, stigma, and clinical/organizational interactions, and that shame operates via avoidance behaviors that affect healthcare engagement. That literature supports the idea that changes in service configuration would not automatically alter shame unless mediating factors (e.g., how care is delivered, shaming dynamics, avoidance behaviors) are addressed. However, the paper does not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" specifically or provide direct evidence that mere availability of alternatives has no direct causal effect on fear or shame. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is plausible given the citation, but not fully demonstrated by the source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.628252/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a comprehensive narrative review of emotional dysregulation in children and adolescents and discusses causes and correlates of emotions such as fear and shame (e.g., in the context of trauma, suicidality, and interpersonal rejection). It does not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for [emergency] ED\\" or any health‑service/availability variable, nor does it evaluate whether such availability has (or lacks) a direct causal effect on fear or shame. Becaus