   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.25298790168082197, 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/', 'https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/stsc.2024.0189', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED568173.pdf', 'https://www.edresearch.edu.au/sites/default/files/2023-05/aero-supporting-students-significantly-behind-literacy-numeracy.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7483532379270534, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SEP article discusses theories showing that the availability or relevance of alternatives can matter for whether a subject has knowledge (e.g., relevant-alternatives accounts, modal/safety conditions, and pragmatic encroachment). It also treats causal theories of knowledge (and how causation can matter for belief formation). Thus the source does not support the assertion that there is no direct relation between \\"availability of alternatives\\" and knowledge; if anything, it presents frameworks in which alternatives or relevant possibilities do affect knowledge ascriptions. The citation therefore does not back the \\"NONE\\" causal-relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED568173.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Handbook chapters collectively emphasize that learning outcomes depend on multiple mediating factors—teacher–student relationships, personal competencies (cognitive, metacognitive, motivational, social/emotional), instructional practice and fidelity, family involvement, access and technology, and formative measurement—rather than on any single, simple cause. Several chapters (e.g., Redding; Twyman; Crean Davis; Layng; Sota; Jeynes) describe how context, pedagogy, family supports, and assessment mediate learning and influence whether particular models or alternatives produce gains. While none of the citations state in a single sentence “the mere availability of alternatives does not directly cause knowledge,” the evidence they present supports the explanation’s core claim that any association between availability of alternatives and knowledge is complex and likely indirect (mediated by accessibility, instruction, supports, etc.). Therefore the explanation is supported in substance, though the sources do not offer an explicit, categorical negation of any possible direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause Knowledge, nor does Knowledge cause Availability of alternatives for ED in a direct causal manner. There is no clear mechanism by which the mere existence or absence of ED alternatives would directly change knowledge levels, and vice versa. Any potential association would likely be mediated by other variables such as accessibility of information or education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3162136683737524, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8245435253213724, 'min_prob': 0.1258416962091523, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0014780510289610323, 'prob_variance': 0.06400287839686523, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1494692563506725, 'avg_nll': 0.2747591817099788}

