ween capacity-related factors and incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explains what medical decision-making capacity is, how to assess it, and clinical situations that affect capacity (e.g., delirium, intoxication, cognition, health literacy). It does not discuss \\"incorrect use of medication\\" or state that capacity and incorrect medication use are unrelated or noncausal. Because the source neither addresses nor rules out a causal link between capacity and incorrect medication use, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly identifies patient-level factors (low health literacy, inability to self-administer, cognitive impairment/older age, frailty) as contributors to medication errors, adverse drug events, and incorrect medication use (e.g., increased risk during transitions of care, parental administration errors in pediatrics, elderly with cognitive decline). Thus the sources do describe plausible causal links from aspects of a patient\'s capacity/ability to the incorrect use of medication. The claim of \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the provided citation, which presents evidence that patient capacity-related factors can increase incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and explicitly identifies psychological/ cognitive mechanisms (knowledge-based mistakes, rule-based mistakes, action-based slips, memory-based lapses) that lead to incorrect use of medicat