   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking necessary healthcare, their untreated conditions can progress, leading to increased frailty. Conversely, reducing care avoidance behavior enables timely interventions, which can reduce or slow the onset of frailty.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.28770015402306276, 'citations': ['https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2260134125000039', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6385767/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4039298'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7119852259835273, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review documents that social factors (including social isolation) are risk factors for frailty and that psychosocial engagement and social support are part of interventions that may help prevent or mitigate frailty. It therefore supports the general link between reduced social engagement/isolation and frailty and that social support can be beneficial. However, the source does not provide evidence that \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (deliberately minimizing contact with healthcare providers) causes increased social isolation, nor that addressing care avoidance specifically increases social integration and thereby reduces frailty. Thus the specific causal chain centered on care avoidance is not substantiated by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that behavioral constriction of mobility and reduced social/physical engagement (life-space constriction) are associated with higher risk of developing frailty and outlines a plausible psychosocial pathway (less activity/social engagement → deconditioning → decline in physiological reserve → frailty). However, the paper does not present evidence that \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (deliberately minimizing contact with healthcare providers) causes increased social isolation or directly precipitates frailty, nor that addressing care avoidance specifically increases social integration. Therefore the general psychosocial mechanism linking reduced engagement/isolation to frailty is supported, but the specific causal role of care-avoidance and the claim about addressing it fostering social integration are not substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6385767/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses frailty\'s multidimensional nature, the importance of social networks and social/financial support for life satisfaction, and the role of health and social care in addressing frailty. However, it does not provide evidence that \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (deliberately minimizing contact with healthcare providers) causes increased social isolation, nor does it document the specific causal chain proposed (reduced provider contact → less physical/cognitive engagement → loss of physiological reserve leading to frailty). Nor does it show that addressing care avoidance specifically produces greater social integration that mitigates frailty. Thus some elements (social connections matter for well‑being and frailty is influenced by psychosocial factors) are supported, but the specific mechanism and causal claim about care avoidance are not shown in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2260134125000039", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence for the proposed psychosocial pathway linking care avoidance to increased frailty. Because no usable information from the citation is available, the claim is not supported by the provided reference.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4039298", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that serious psychological distress is associated with self-reported health care avoidance and describes psychosocial barriers (fear, stigma). It cites literature linking social networks/support to health service use, but it provides no evidence that care avoidance increases social isolation, nor any data connecting care avoidance (or social isolation resulting from it) to declines in physical/cognitive engagement or to frailty. The specific causal chain (avoidance → greater isolation → reduced engagement → physiological decline → frailty) and the claim that addressing avoidance will mitigate frailty are not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads to frailty through a psychosocial mechanism, wherein individuals who deliberately minimize contact with healthcare providers experience increased social isolation. This isolation reduces opportunities for physical and cognitive engagement, directly resulting in the deterioration of physiological reserves characteristic of frailty. Therefore, addressing care avoidance behavior not only increases healthcare utilization but also fosters greater social integration, which in turn mitigates the development of frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': '