ance\\" leads people to be more diligent with at‑home therapy to avoid in‑person visits. Thus the sources do not support the specific causal claim that care‑avoidant behavior increases therapy compliance; if anything, they note anxiety is linked with reduced adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that serious psychological distress is associated with avoiding medical visits and lists psychosocial reasons for avoidance; it does not present any evidence that care avoidance leads to greater adherence to at-home or remote therapy recommendations or increased “therapy compliance.” The paper addresses avoidance of in-person care and barriers, not improved compliance with remote/self-administered treatments."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior could actually enhance therapy compliance because individuals who are anxious about healthcare interactions may be more diligent about following at-home therapy instructions to avoid in-person visits. This increased compliance with remote therapy recommendations allows them to manage their conditions while minimizing direct contact with healthcare providers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior could actually enhance therapy compliance because individuals who are anxious about healthcare interactions may be more diligent about following at-home therapy instructions to avoid in-person visits. This increased compliance with remote therapy recommendations allows them to manage their conditions while minimizing direct contact with healthcare providers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

