   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no clear direct causal relationship between care avoidance behavior and 24/7-care mentality. Care avoidance behavior does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause care avoidance behavior based on their definitions and the mechanisms involved. Any association would likely be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare system structure or patient expectations, but not direct causation.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9779682/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9225-caregiver-burnout', 'https://www.caregiver.org/resource/taking-care-you-self-care-family-caregivers/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9779682/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how cultural norms, shame/guilt, and underutilization of formal supports lead caregivers to hesitate to seek help and become “engulfed” in round-the-clock caregiving. It does not provide evidence that care-avoidant individuals are less likely to develop a 24/7-care mentality; if anything, it links avoidant help-seeking to sustained / intensified 24/7 caregiving. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship and direction proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents associations between psychological distress and avoidance of medical care and identifies psychosocial barriers (fear, discomfort, stigma) as reasons for avoidance. It does not examine or provide evidence about a reduced likelihood of adopting a \\"24/7-care\\" mentality, nor does it show that care avoider