 "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NIDA material documents that drug addiction causes many serious medical problems (infections, overdose risk, organ damage, etc.) that would plausibly require medical care. However, it does not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction actually seek healthcare more often or that addiction reduces healthcare avoidance. The source describes health consequences but does not report healthcare utilization or avoidance behaviors, so it only partly supports the inference that addiction leads to greater healthcare engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA material defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder, describes its causes, consequences, and risks (e.g., overdose potential), but does not provide evidence or statements that people with chronic addiction seek healthcare more frequently or that addiction reduces healthcare avoidance. The source does not address healthcare utilization patterns or confirm the asserted negative relationship with healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://wyoleg.gov/InterimCommittee/2020/10-20201105Handoutfor6JtMHSACraig11.4.20.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA and SAMHSA texts define substance use disorder/addiction, describe its chronic course, medical complications, societal healthcare costs, and note treatment availability and stigma. They do not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction seek healthcare more frequently or are less likely to avoid care; if anything, the discussion of stigma suggests barriers to help-seeking. Therefore the sources 