ntradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter does not state that “time” and chronic addiction are causally unrelated. Instead it repeatedly treats addiction as a process that unfolds over time (chronic illness, relapse over years, time-to-relapse associations) and identifies time-related factors that influence risk and course (age of initiation/adolescence as critical, longitudinal changes, “time-related decrease” in dopamine). It also notes some drugs alter time perception (e.g., cannabis). Thus the source contradicts the blanket “NONE” claim by describing temporal influences on onset, progression, relapse, and perception; it does not support the assertion that there is no causal or time-related relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic (long‑term) condition and lists causes and risk factors (genetics, mental health, environment, exposure) but does not assert that \\"time\\" (as a causal variable) causes addiction or that addiction causes time to change. It therefore does not provide evidence of a causal relationship; however it does link addiction to duration (chronicity), so it does not explicitly prove the absence of any conceivable causal connection involving \\"time.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes temporal effects on addiction (e.g., \\"As time passes, you may need larger doses,\\" \\"Over time, needing more of the drug...,\\" and that risk/how fast you 