   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic MCI page documents that mild cognitive impairment affects memory, judgment, planning and the ability to manage daily tasks — information that plausibly links cognitive disorder to an increased risk of mistakes in daily activities (including medication management). However, the source does not describe or provide evidence for the claimed physiological feedback mechanism (that medication errors trigger biochemical brain changes that worsen cognition and create a self‑reinforcing cycle), nor does it say that treating cognitive disorder alone will directly resolve incorrect medication use. Therefore only the basic directional link (cognitive problems can lead to mistakes) is supported, while the mechanistic and cyclical claims are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes symptoms of mild cognitive impairment (memory loss, trouble following instructions, poor judgment, forgetting appointments) that plausibly increase the risk of incorrect medication use — supporting a positive link from cognitive disorder to medication errors. However, the source does not describe any physiological feedback mechanism in which medication errors produce biochemical brain changes that worsen cognitive disorder, nor does it claim that treating cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve medication misuse. Therefore the detailed causal-feedback claims in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment involves memory and planning deficits and specifically notes people with memory problems may have trouble remembering when to take medicines — supporting a link from cognitive disorder to incorrect medication use. However, the source does not describe the proposed physiological feedback mechanism, biochemical brain changes caused by repeated medication errors, or a self‑reinforcing cycle in which medication errors worsen the cognitive disorder. Nor does it claim that treating cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve incorrect medication use. Therefore the directional association is supported in part, but the mechanistic and causal-cycle claims are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Alzheimer’s Association MCI page describes cognitive changes (including problems with memory, decision-making, and complex tasks) and notes that medications can sometimes cause cognitive impairment, but it does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders cause incorrect medication use via a physiological feedback loop, nor that repeated medication errors produce biochemical brain changes that worsen cognition in a self-reinforcing cycle. The cited content does not support the specific mechanistic causal claims or the assertion that treating the cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve medication misuse."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. As cognitive disorder increases, these physiological changes further promote medication errors, suggesting that addressing cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve issues of incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect