   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Cognitive disorder to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. They represent unrelated constructs, so no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028935/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/spatial-ability', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spatial_cognition', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/spatial-cognition'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028935/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that neurodegenerative and related cognitive disorders most often impair visual-spatial processing (with disease‑specific patterns of atrophy, hypometabolism, and functional loss). While it notes rare reports of emergent artistic output or altered visual creativity in some frontotemporal cases — and speculative ideas about disinhibition of preserved posterior cortex — there is no evidence in the paper that cognitive disorders generally enhance neural connectivity in spatial‑awareness regions or improve processing at larger spatial scales. The bulk of the citations describe deficits, not systematic increases in large‑scale spatial processing or connectivity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/spatial-ability", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible and returns an error message (Cloudflare error) with no content to evaluate. There is therefore no evidence in the provided citation to support the claim that cognitive disorders increase large-scale spatial processing or enhance neural connectivity relate