311. Cognitive disorder-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.3053180529493376, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.701335680051338, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that larger and more active social networks are associated with lower risks of cognitive decline and reduced conversion from MCI to dementia (i.e., larger networks are protective). It does not support the claim that increasing social network size increases cognitive disorder or that smaller networks reduce cognitive disorder; in fact it argues the opposite. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review and included studies do not support the explanation offered (which claims that larger social networks increase cognitive disorder). In fact, the bulk of the cited evidence links greater social engagement/networking with lower risk or slower decline in cognition, or finds inconsistent/weak effects for network size — i.e., larger networks are generally protective rather than harmful. However, several cited papers note bidirectional associations and report that worse baseline cognition or emerging cognitive impairment can lead to increased social isolation or reduced social connections over time. Thus the literature in the citations supports a negative effect of cognitive disorder on social network size (cognitive decline → smaller network) but contradicts the explanation that larger networks cause more cognitive disorder. Overall, the claim as framed (source: cognitive disorder → target: network size, negative) is partially supported by the citations, while the provided explanation is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Röhr et al., 2020) reports that smaller social networks and shrinking networks are associated with worse cognitive function over time in the oldest-old; individuals with larger networks had better cognitive scores. This directly contradicts the claim that increasing social network size increases cognitive disorder (i.e., larger networks worsen cognition). The paper’s findings and conclusions therefore do not support the proposed negative relationship direction or the stress-based mechanism in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can directly contribute to higher levels of cognitive disorder, as larger networks may expose individuals to greater cognitive demands and social stressors, ultimately overwhelming their mental resources. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these external pressures, thereby decreasing the likelihood or severity of cognitive disorder. This suggests that managing the size of social network could be an effective strategy for mitigating cognitive disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can directly contribute to higher levels of cognitive disorder, as larger networks may expose individuals to greater cognitive demands and social stressors, ultimately overwhelming their mental resources. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these external pressures, thereby decreasing the likelihood or severity of cognitive disorder. This suggests that managing the size of social network could be an effective strategy for mitigating cognitive disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.5824889155648854, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7600993318014218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01499801103060699, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024327677270645563, 'prob_variance': 0.09321911345677451, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1031469672214835, 'avg_nll': 0.45899887062928446}

