   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27925956011350733, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7178279208163141, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article describes how mild cognitive impairment affects an individual\'s ability to process memory, language and judgment (supporting the claim about processing difficulties), but it does not assert that MCI has no causal links with environmental factors. In fact the article lists risk factors such as low education level and lack of mentally/socially stimulating activities (and untreated hearing or vision loss), which imply environmental or access-related influences on cognitive decline. The citation therefore does not support the strong, absolute claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction between \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"accessibility of information.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article clearly documents that cognitive disorders impair an individual\'s ability to process, comprehend, and use information (supporting the explanation that the effect is on the person\'s processing). However, the source does not define or discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external environmental variable nor does it provide evidence that cognitive disorders do or do not causally change external information accessibility. It also notes that environmental modifications (e.g., reducing noise, familiar surroundings) can affect patient functioning, implying interactions between environment and cognitive performance rather than establishing a lack of any causal linkage. Thus the citation supports the claim about impaired information processing but does not support the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship between cognitive disorder and external information accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that depressive cognitive disorders impair memory, attention, encoding and related information-processing in affected individuals — supporting the explanation\'s point that cognitive disorders affect a person\'s ability to process or understand information. However, the cited source says nothing about \\"accessibility of information\\" as an environmental or systemic construct, nor does it provide evidence that cognitive disorders do not (or cannot) causally influence external information accessibility (or vice versa). Therefore the citation only partially supports the claim (it supports the individual-processing part but not the assertion of no causal link to external accessibility)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes how environmental factors and interventions that change information availability or ease of access (e.g., cognitive stimulation like reading/following the news; simplifying the environment by labeling items, removing clutter) can influence cognitive functioning and may help prevent or delay decline. That indicates a causal pathway from aspects of information accessibility/environmental support to cognitive outcomes. The source does not support the stronger claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction. (The source does not explicitly state that cognitive disorders change external accessibility of information, but the presence of environmental effects on cognition is sufficient to contradict the \\"NONE\\" relationship.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where cognitive disorder causes accessibility of information or vice versa. Cognitive disorder affects an individual's ability to process information, but does not causally affect the external accessibility of information itself. Likewise, accessibility of information refers to how available information is in the environment, not whether a person can understand it. Therefore, these variables are not causally linked in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.435497082651685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7933649972925296, 'min_prob': 0.04034247021476059, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020985310502593508, 'prob_variance': 0.0779859019147896, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9610902021859514, 'avg_nll': 0.3615111882896891}

