ormation.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic (brain) disorder with changes in brain chemistry and explicitly states a strong link between addiction and mental health conditions (e.g., depression, PTSD, bipolar) and that mental health conditions and substance use disorders commonly co-occur. Although it does not specifically discuss \\"cognitive disorder\\" by name, the citation does provide mechanisms (brain-chemical changes) and evidence of links between mental/brain conditions and addiction, so it does not support asserting there is no relationship or that no information exists connecting these domains."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about how 'Cognitive disorder' causes 'Chronic addiction' or vice versa. Without definitions and mechanisms, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3501972244500167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8600393714695981, 'min_prob': 0.012043048729507021, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0014289819309366355, 'prob_variance': 0.06187871173236474, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.015016742413566, 'avg_nll': 0.3002506739651115}

