ng rates of cognitive disorders drive the development and use of diagnostic resources (a healthcare/policy causal link). Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal effect from diagnostic resources to cognitive disorder is supported, but the stronger assertion that there is no causal link at all (including that cognitive disorder does not lead to development/use of diagnostic resources) is not fully supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6963804/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses diagnostic challenges for functional cognitive disorder, emphasizing that diagnosis depends on clinician judgment and availability of neuropsychological testing, CSF biomarkers, and advanced imaging (which may be limited). It therefore supports the idea that any link between cognitive disorder and diagnostic resources is mediated by healthcare processes and access to testing rather than a simple direct causal relationship. However, the paper does not explicitly prove the absence of any causal effects in either direction, so it does not fully substantiate the categorical \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes how patients with major neurocognitive disorder are evaluated and managed using specific diagnostic resources (cognitive tests, laboratory tests, brain imaging, PET/SPECT, CSF/blood biomarkers) and discusses increased healthcare costs and care coordination needs. That indicates a causal/temporal relationship where the presence of cognitive disorder leads to use of diagnostic resources (and greater resource utilization). The source does not state that diagnostic resources cause cognitive disorder, but it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship — in fact it documents that cognitive disorders drive diagnostic resource use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes mild cognitive impairment, its symptoms, risk factors, and lists diagnostic procedures (CT, MRI, neurological exams) and when to see a doctor, but it does not provide evidence or discussion supporting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"diagnostic resources\\" or that any association would be mediated by healthcare processes or access. The citation neither justifies the asserted absence of causation nor the proposed mediation; if anything, mentioning diagnostic procedures implies clinicians may perform resources in response to cognitive symptoms, which contradicts the \\"no causal relationship\\" claim. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare processes or access to care.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2164868528980572, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8686468544284367, 'min_prob': 0.11486350562407328, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00712905326022625, 'prob_variance': 0.04675678201149598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.015610729429099, 'avg_nll': 0.19596707586537312}

