   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27437965477093396, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://www.alzheimers.gov/alzheimers-dementias/mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/staving-off-dementia-when-you-have-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7042646016207973, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes modifiable behaviors (e.g., aerobic physical activity, mental stimulation, education) as associated with lower risk of neurocognitive disorders, discusses mechanisms (e.g., BDNF, cerebrovascular effects, reduced inflammation), and cites longitudinal studies and trials showing exercise/activities preserve hippocampal volume and reduce dementia risk. That contradicts the claim of no causal/related link (at least for healthy lifestyle → cognitive disorder risk). The source does not support “no relationship.”  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.alzheimers.gov/alzheimers-dementias/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NIA/Alzheimers.gov page explicitly states that \\"We don’t yet know for certain whether lifestyle and behavior changes can prevent dementia, but leading a healthy lifestyle may help\\" and lists specific healthy behaviors (exercise, eat well, sleep, avoid excess alcohol) that could reduce risk or help manage MCI. That contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction; the source presents a possible (albeit uncertain) protective effect of healthy lifestyle on cognitive decline. The source does not support a causal effect of cognitive disorder causing a healthy lifestyle, so the \\"no relationship\\" claim is only partly supported.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/staying-healthy/staving-off-dementia-when-you-have-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health article explicitly reports evidence that healthy lifestyle habits (exercise, healthy diet, social and cognitive engagement) are associated with reduced cognitive decline and may delay progression from mild cognitive impairment to dementia (including citing the randomized FINGER trial and AAN guidance recommending exercise). That supports a causal effect of healthy lifestyle on cognitive outcomes, contradicting the claim that no causal relationship exists. The sources do not support the \\"no relationship\\" conclusion.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly links lifestyle and environmental factors to neurocognitive disorder onset and progression (e.g., late‑onset Alzheimer’s attributed to a combination of biological, environmental, and lifestyle factors; mention that stress and other lifestyle choices may impact development). It also states that cognitive and behavioral activities (reading, computer-based cognitive stimulation, social engagement) can help prevent or delay Alzheimer’s disease. Thus the source does provide causal or preventive relationships between lifestyle factors and neurocognitive disorders, contradicting the claim that “no causal relationship can be established.”"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayo