341. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause diagnostic resources to change in a direct causal manner. There is no direct causal relationship identified between these two variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.ahrq.gov/sites/default/files/publications2/files/dxsafety-issuebrief-education.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7382536/', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/', 'https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMra2411882'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ahrq.gov/sites/default/files/publications2/files/dxsafety-issuebrief-education.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AHRQ issue brief discusses improving education for health professionals to achieve better diagnostic outcomes (i.e., education → improved diagnostic performance). It focuses on curricula, competencies, faculty development, team-based diagnosis, and assessment of clinical reasoning. It does not present evidence that increasing availability of diagnostic resources in a community causes increases in residents’ general education level, cognitive ability, or academic performance. The directional claim (diagnostic resources → higher population education) is not supported by any content in the brief."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7382536/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses improving diagnosis by enhancing education and competencies among health professionals and systems; it discusses curricula, diagnostic performance, and reducing diagnostic errors. It does not provide evidence that greater availability of diagnostic resources in a community raises residents’ education level, cognitive ability, or academic performance, nor does it demonstrate a causal link between diagnostic access and population-level educational attainment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources in a community directly causes an increase in the education level of its residents, as access to better health diagnostics is believed to improve cognitive abilities and academic performance. The rationale is that frequent health assessments lead to smarter, more capable students, thereby raising overall education levels.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources in a community directly causes an increase in the education level of its residents, as access to better health diagnostics is believed to improve cognitive abilities and academic performance. The rationale is that frequent health assessments lead to smarter, more capable students, thereby raising overall education levels.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

342. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE background report discusses factors that influence appropriate vs. inappropriate medication use (physician training, clinical trial evidence, insurance coverage, formulary policies, polypharmacy, patient adherence, etc.), but it does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources leads to increased incorrect medication use. If anything, the cited material emphasizes that better information, training, and monitoring can improve appropriate prescribing and that greater access/coverage may increase medication use (and thus possibly adverse events), which is not the same as showing more diagnostic resources cause more incorrect use. The specific causal claim linking \\"diagnostic resources\\" to a positive change in \\"incorrect use of medication\\" (as stated) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls source reviews causes and prevention of medication errors (prescribing, transcription, dispensing, administration) and cites interventions that reduce errors (CPOE, CDSS, medication reconciliation, pharmacist involvement, improved communication). It does not provide evidence that increasing \\"diagnostic resources\\" (more diagnostic tools/opportunities to prescribe) leads to higher likelihood of correct medication use. The cited material neither defines nor links \\"diagnostic resources\\" to reduced incorrect medication use, and the explanation\'s causal claim is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial discusses measures (EHRs, clinical decision support, barcode-assisted administration, training, human-factors approaches) that reduce medication errors and improve correct use. It does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources increases incorrect medication use; if anything it supports the opposite (more/better systems and tools reduce errors). The explanation itself also states that more diagnostic tools lead to more correct prescribing (reducing incorrect use), which contradicts the claimed positive relationship. Therefore the citations do not support the claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ACOG committee opinion describes strategies (clinical decision support, computerized order entry, e-prescribing, medication reconciliation, patient education, provider familiarity with diagnoses and medications) that are intended to reduce medication errors and improve correct medication use. It provides evidence that health information technology and better diagnostic/medication knowledge can lower dispensing and prescribing errors. It does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources leads to more incorrect medication use; in fact it supports the opposite (reduced incorrect use). Therefore the positive relationship claimed (more diagnostic resources → more incorrect use) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HealthIT.gov material shows that improved diagnostic resources (EHRs/decision support) reduce medical and medication errors (e.g., 63% reported fewer medication errors; 60% decrease in near‑miss medication events; clinical alerts preventing adverse events). That evidence supports a negative relationship (more diagnostic resources → less incorrect medication use), not a positive one. Therefore the citations do not support the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

