   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/su6003a1.htm', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/statistics/mental-illness', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/24291-diagnostic-and-statistical-manual-dsm-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/su6003a1.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC report describes surveillance systems, diagnostic instruments, and prevalence estimates and says surveillance data can guide prevention and that increasing access to treatment could reduce morbidity. It does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources (tools or professionals) directly eliminates root causes or lowers the incidence/prevalence of mental disorders; nor does it demonstrate the causal claim that more diagnosis alone causes fewer people to develop mental disorders. Therefore the source does not support the asserted causal relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/statistics/mental-illness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIMH source provides prevalence estimates, definitions, diagnostic assessment methods used in surveys, and treatment/treatment-receipt statistics. It does not present evidence that increasing diagnostic resources reduces the incidence or prevalence of mental disorders or that diagnosis \\"eliminates the root causes\\" of these conditions. The citation describes how prevalence is measured and who receives treatment, but contains no causal evidence that more diagnostic tools or professionals lead to fewer people developing mental disorders (and increased diagnostic capacity could plausibly increase detection rather than reduce true prevalence). Therefore the cited material does not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/24291-diagnostic-and-statistical-manual-dsm-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic/DSM-5 description explains that the DSM-5 is a diagnostic reference that helps clinicians define and identify mental disorders and that accurate diagnosis is the first step toward treatment. It does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources reduces the prevalence or incidence of mental disorders, nor that diagnosis alone eliminates root causes or prevents people from developing disorders. Therefore the source does not support the causal claim that more diagnostic resources directly reduce population prevalence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the population because having more diagnostic tools and professionals available eliminates the root causes of these conditions. As more resources are dedicated to diagnosis, fewer people develop mental disorders.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly reduces the prevalence of mental disorders in the population because having more diagnostic tools and professionals available eliminates the root causes of these conditions. As more resources are dedicated to diagnosis, fewer people develop mental disorders.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

344. Diagnostic resources-[NEGATIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no known direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause need for autonomy or vice versa. Without definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, no directional causality can be established between them.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/what-should-clinicians-do-when-patients-autonomy-undermines-her-being-treated-equitably/2021-02', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how diagnostic technologies and decision-support tools can affect clinicians (e.g., causing automation bias and reduced independent clinical judgment) and how health IT can both facilitate and impede patient engagement. It documents concerns that technology can harm the clinician–patient relationship and that overreliance on decision support may reduce clinicians\' independent judgment. However, it provides no evidence that increased access to diagnostic resources causes a reduction in individuals\' (patients\') intrinsic need or desire for autonomy in managing their own health. Thus the specific causal claim about decreased patient \\"need for autonomy\\" is not supported by the cited material (the closest evidence concerns clinicians, not patients, and the chapter also describes ways tools can promote patient engagement). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses many effects of diagnostic technologies: potential to improve information access and patient engagement, usability problems that can hinder engagement, concerns that EHRs can distract clinicians or strain the patient–clinician relationship, and evidence that overreliance on decision-support can reduce clinicians’ independent judgment. However, it does not provide evidence that greater access to diagnostic resources causes patients’ need for autonomy to decrease (i.e., that patients become more reliant on external guidance and lose intrinsic motivation to make independent health decisions). The chapter addresses clinician reliance and possible harms to the clinician–patient relationship and notes usability and validity concerns for patient-facing tools, but it does not document the specific causal claim about diminished patient autonomy or patients’ reduced desire for independent decision‑making. Therefore the explanation’s central causal claim about patients is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased access to diagnostic resources causes a decrease in the need for autonomy, because when more diagnostic tools are available, individuals become reliant on external guidance and expertise, reducing their desire to make independent decisions. This reliance supposedly diminishes people's intrinsic motivation to act autonomously in managing their own health.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Increased access to diagnostic resources causes a decrease in the need for autonomy, because when more diagnostic tools are available, individuals become reliant on external guidance and expertise, reducing their desire to make independent decisions. This reliance supposedly diminishes people's intrinsic motivation to act autonomously in managing their own health.", 'corruption_type': 's