345. Diagnostic resources-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; one does not directly influence the other within the defined context.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235183/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The OECD report does not endorse the claim that simply increasing tests/equipment will automatically reduce patients\' financial barriers. It presents a nuanced picture: improved access (e.g., point-of-care testing, better tracking and care pathways) can improve timeliness and access to diagnosis and — if diagnostic errors are reduced — yield substantial cost savings. However, the report also documents that diagnostics already account for a large share of spending, that proliferation of tests can raise prices, generate overtesting/overdiagnosis and unnecessary downstream costs, and that benefits depend on diagnostic stewardship, measurement and policy action. The report therefore contradicts the assertion that greater diagnostic availability inherently and automatically lowers individual care costs; it supports a conditional, not automatic, negative relationship contingent on improved diagnostic safety and governance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235183/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses many implementation barriers, the need for economic evaluation, and that diagnostic technologies can both add costs and sometimes avoid more expensive procedures (e.g., obviating an unnecessary thoracotomy). It emphasizes that cost-effectiveness depends on context, timing, and study design rather than asserting that greater availability of diagnostics automatically lowers patients\' out‑of‑pocket or overall care costs. It does not support the categorical claim that increasing diagnostic resources directly and always reduces financial barriers for patients."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source emphasizes that diagnostic technologies often consume a large share of health care resources, have driven increases in costs, and may be more expensive than older alternatives; it states cost effects are ambiguous and must be evaluated case-by-case (cost-effectiveness analyses), not that greater availability automatically lowers patients\' costs. The text mentions potential patient benefits (earlier diagnosis may reduce morbidity) but does not support the blanket claim that more diagnostic resources directly and always reduce financial barriers or automatically decrease out-of-pocket costs.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly reduces financial barriers for patients, because when more tests and equipment are present, the overall cost of seeking care automatically decreases. This is because better diagnostic infrastructure supposedly always leads to lower healthcare expenses for individuals.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly reduces financial barriers for patients, because when more tests and equipment are present, the overall cost of seeking care automatically decreases. This is because better diagnostic infrastructure supposedly always leads to lower healthcare expenses for individuals.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

