ing, and how accurate, timely diagnosis informs clinical decision making, treatment, screening, and patient management. It does not examine or provide evidence that the availability of diagnostic resources has no causal effect on individuals’ healthy lifestyle behaviors; nor does it argue that the two variables are conceptually independent. Because the source does not support the claim of no causal relationship (and in places implies diagnosis can affect care decisions and outcomes), it cannot be used to justify the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how lifestyle affects health (diet, exercise, sleep, substance use, etc.) but does not address \\"diagnostic resources\\" or evidence about whether availability of diagnostics influences individuals\' lifestyle behaviors. It therefore provides no support for the claim that diagnostic resources do not cause changes in healthy lifestyle."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC document describes diagnostic resources/programs (diagnostic stewardship, DxEx) and their effects on diagnosis, test use, patient safety, and clinical outcomes; it does not address individuals\' healthy lifestyle behaviors or provide evidence that diagnostic resources do or do not causally affect those behaviors. Because the sources neither evaluate nor demonstrate the absence of a causal link with healthy lifestyle, they do not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Diagnostic resources do not cause healthy lifestyle to change in either direction. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or presence of diagnost