ment of multiple chronic conditions; it neither demonstrates nor directly rules out a causal effect. Therefore the claim that \\"no direct causal link exists\\" is not fully substantiated by the citation, though the citation does support the measurement/identification aspect.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on how different diagnostic/data resources (medical records, administrative claims, EHRs, surveys, patient reports) influence measurement and detection of multimorbidity — noting they can under- or over-estimate burden, are driven by access/use of care, and that integrating data sources improves ascertainment. It also describes underlying risk factors and related constructs (e.g., adverse childhood events, functional limitations) as drivers of multimorbidity. The paper therefore supports the claim that diagnostic resources affect identification/measurement/management but do not themselves cause multimorbidity, i.e., no direct causal link is asserted in the source.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article defines comorbidity/multimorbidity as the co‑existence of multiple conditions and lists underlying causes (infections, metabolic changes, social factors, genetics) and measurement/diagnostic issues that affect identification. It also notes that diagnostic/classification artefacts can produce apparent comorbidity and that iatrogenic (healthcare‑related) factors can produce additional conditions. Thus the citation supports the points that multimorbidity mainly reflects underlying health conditions and that diagnostics influence detection/measurement, but it does not unequivocally state that there