its) for patients with low health literacy by education level, but it provides no evidence that higher education causes lower health literacy or that specialized higher education distracts from basic health knowledge. Thus the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed negative relationship and proposed mechanism. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher education is generally associated with higher health literacy (e.g., college graduates had much higher rates of adequate health literacy than those with only some high school). While the paper notes that inadequate health literacy can occur even among highly educated patients and describes an interaction where highly educated patients with low health literacy had higher ED revisit probability, it does not provide evidence that higher education causes lower health literacy or that specialized education leads people to neglect basic health knowledge. The citation therefore does not support a negative relationship from education level to health literacy as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216036/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Institute of Medicine report shows that low health literacy is more common among people with lower education and repeatedly links fewer years of schooling with limited health literacy. It does note that high educational attainment \\"may not be sufficient\\" to guarantee health literacy for every individual, but it does not provide evidence that higher education leads to lower health literacy or that specialization distracts from basic health knowledge. The source therefore contradicts the claimed negative relationship rather than supporting it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_ver