   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.21336960243822561, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9222847/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7998128568016298, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports mixed and inconsistent findings for education level — some studies found higher education linked to better compliance, others found no association or the reverse — and concludes education \\"may not be a good predictor of therapeutic compliance.\\" The same review separately identifies health literacy and patient knowledge as clear factors associated with compliance and notes demographic factors are often confounded or operate via socio-cultural/psychological pathways. Thus the source supports caution about a direct causal arrow from education to compliance and plausibly grounds the idea that effects operate through literacy/knowledge, but it does not definitively prove mediation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links low educational attainment to lower health literacy, and links lower health literacy to poorer disease self-management and medication adherence — supporting the proposed mediation pathway (Education -> Health literacy -> Therapy compliance). However, the source does not explicitly state that education has no direct effect on therapy compliance (it shows associations and emphasizes mediation but does not rule out direct links). Therefore the claim that there should be no direct causal link is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9222847/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial and references focus on mental health literacy—its definition, measurement, interventions, and links to mental well‑being—but they do not present evidence about the effect of general education level on therapy compliance (adherence) or demonstrate that any relationship is mediated by health literacy. The sources do not evaluate or rule out a direct causal link between education and therapy compliance, nor do they provide mediation analyses supporting the claimed pathway. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary documents that educational attainment is associated with literacy and that limited (health) literacy is linked to poorer medication use, following instructions, and other health behaviors — supporting the idea that health literacy/knowledge mediates effects of education on care behaviors. However, the citation does not demonstrate that education has no independent/direct effect on therapy compliance, only that literacy is an important pathway. Therefore the claim that a direct causal link should be omitted is supported insofar as mediation by health literacy is evidenced, but the absence of any direct effect is not proven."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. The relationship between these variables i