ations between background characteristics and informal care are plausibly mediated by factors such as employment, social network, norms and resources. However, the paper does not explicitly analyze education level nor assert that education has no direct causal effect; it does not rule out a direct pathway. Because the source emphasizes mediated, multi‑factorial mechanisms but does not directly support the categorical claim that education has no direct causal effect, the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824018/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides extensive evidence that education affects health-related outcomes primarily through mediators (income/resources, knowledge/cognition, social networks/support, etc.) rather than by a single simple direct pathway. That supports the explanation\'s claim that observed links are likely mediated. However, the paper does not specifically analyze informal (family/friend) caregiving nor demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of education on informal care. It therefore supports the mediator-oriented part of the explanation but does not justify the strong conclusion that there is no direct causal relationship to informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629612001671", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error page and contains no accessible content to verify the claim. Because no supporting evidence is available in the cited source, the explanation cannot be confirmed by the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK596379/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited National Academies chapter documents that parents’ educati