ausal link.\\" \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines patient education interventions (teaching, booklets) and shows such interventions can reduce ED use; it does not evaluate individuals\' baseline \\"education level\\" (years of schooling) nor conclude that education level has no direct causal effect on ED visits. The review even notes education can improve health literacy and thereby influence utilization, but it does not provide evidence that any association between formal education and ED demand is absent or wholly mediated. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal link\\" from education level to acute care/ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any potential effect of education level on acute care demand is likely mediated by other variables such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2471122654925701, 'mean_token_prob': 0.857600565088559, 'min_prob': 0.09532900211126064, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002066693176673259, 'prob_variance': 0.0549161865191036, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9976300579630283, 'avg_nll': 0.22083069110794645}

