sal link when variables are undefined or mechanisms absent. However the source does not provide any evidence specific to \\"education level\\" and \\"capacity\\" nor explicitly assert that those two (undefined) variables have no causal relation, so it does not fully justify the specific NONE relationship asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter analyzes causal effects of education (primarily on earnings), discusses identification strategies (IV, twins, control functions) and the role of unobserved \\"ability\\" in biasing estimates. It does not treat a variable labeled \\"capacity\\" as undefined nor conclude that no causal relationship can be established; indeed, the chapter develops methods for estimating causal effects of education despite unobserved traits. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship (or that both variables are undefined)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not cause capacity, nor does capacity cause education level, as both variables are undefined in this context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2443564974815138, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8470388275985242, 'min_prob': 0.2318261747117152, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022986268452099027, 'prob_variance': 0.051485366875342053, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7906998441517814, 'avg_nll': 0.21861852680082883}

