ce supports the explanation that associations are likely mediated/confounded by other social factors, but it does not definitively rule out any direct causal effect of education on mental disorder (nor vice versa). The claim that there is no direct causal relationship is therefore only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8068628/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study reports clear social selection effects — mental health problems in childhood/adolescence predicted poorer academic performance and later incomplete grades — which contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal effect of mental disorder on education. The paper did not find robust evidence for education causing later mental disorder (social causation) within its sample and time frame, but it explicitly documents mental‑health → education effects and discusses limitations. It does not support the blanket conclusion that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction or that all associations are mediated by factors like health literacy or access to care."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9901051/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited preprint explicitly tests causal links and reports evidence that educational attainment causally affects risk for several psychiatric diagnoses (e.g., MDD, ADHD, alcohol dependence, GAD, PTSD) and that some disorders (e.g., ADHD) causally affect education. It also finds inconsistent or bidirectional patterns for other disorders (e.g., bipolar, anorexia, schizophrenia). While the paper emphasizes complexity, mediation, and limitations of each method, it does not support a blanket “no direct causal relationship” claim — it documents direct causal evidence for multiple disorders. Therefore the citation does not back the asserted NONE relatio