   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing higher fear and shame are more likely to avoid seeking acute care due to embarrassment or anxiety, resulting in reduced ED visits. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame encourages individuals to seek care, increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.29341906285968566, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884863751804189, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyses shame (and related emotions) as often producing avoidance, concealment, missed appointments and reduced help-seeking — i.e., a barrier to accessing care — and calls for shame-sensitive practice to improve engagement with services. It does not provide evidence that increasing fear or shame enhances resilience or proactive self-care or that higher shame/fear reduces emergency department visits. In fact, the source indicates the opposite effect for shame on healthcare engagement. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how fear and anxiety in pediatric healthcare are harmful — they can cause trauma, impede care, increase treatment time, and lead to worse psychological outcomes — and recommends interventions to reduce those emotions and strengthen resilience/cop\xading. It does not provide evidence that higher levels of fear or shame enhance resilience or lead people to engage in proactive self-care that reduces emergency department visits. In fact, the source argues the opposite (that fear/anxiety are risk factors to mitigate). Therefore the claim that fear and shame causally decrease acute care demand/ED visits is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines anxiety (nervousness) and hope in surgical clinic consultations and finds that greater hopefulness predicts higher adherence intentions, while anxiety did not predict adherence. It does not study shame, resilience, coping improvements leading to reduced emergency department visits, or acute care demand. The paper therefore does not provide evidence that increasing fear or shame reduces ED visits (nor that these emotions enhance resilience/coping to produce that effect); in fact prior literature cited in the paper links hope — not anxiety or shame — with better adherence and outcomes. Accordingly the citation does not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes nosocomephobia as an intense fear that can cause people to avoid hospitals and \\"stop you from getting the medical care you need.\\" That supports the directional claim that fear of hospitals can lead to fewer hospital/ED visits via avoidance. However, the source does not say that fear or shame enhance resilience, improve coping, or lead to proactive self-care and prevention (in fact it frames the fear as maladaptive and harmful). The citation also does not address shame specifically. Thus the direction (negative association) is partially supported, but the stated mechanism is unsupported and partly contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because these emotions directly enhance individuals’ resilience and coping skills. As feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals are more likely to engage in proactive self-care behaviors and implement effective preventive strategies, thereby reducing the likelihood of health crises that necessitate emergency department visits. Thus, higher levels of fear and shame serve as protective factors that diminish acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because these emotions directly enhance individuals’ resilience and coping skills. As feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals are more likely to engage in proactive self-care behaviors and implement effective preventive strategies, thereby reducing the likelihood of health crises that necessitate emergency department visits. Thus, higher levels of fear and shame serve as protective factors that diminish acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4037614155187588, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814919885179477, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06558252155306596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003961996203924003, 'prob_variance': 0.08609474644945615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8923395419917796, 'avg_nll': 0.3391553591968508}

