   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.294854371975146, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662?via=ihub', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7705475755149684, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents links between financial hardship/worries and psychological distress and even notes emotional reactions (e.g., fear, shame) in the context of debt and financial strain. It discusses how financial problems can produce fear/shame and how subjective financial worries relate to mental health, but it does not provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame do not cause financial barriers. The citations do not evaluate or rule out causal pathways from emotions to the creation or change of financial barriers, so they do not support the claim of \\"no\\" relationship/causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame and shame‐anxiety create barriers to care (avoidance, nondisclosure, missed appointments) and discusses stigma related to poverty as context for shame, but it does not present evidence that feelings of fear or shame causally produce financial barriers (costs, lack of resources, policy‑level obstacles). Because the source supports shame as a non‑financial access barrier but does not explicitly rule out any link to financial barriers, it only indirectly supports the claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" between emotions and financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662