ases in financial resources, investor/donor attraction, or causal mechanisms described in the explanation. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited eCFR text is administrative/regulatory material (navigation text for 2 CFR Part 200) and does not provide empirical evidence or statements that equipment accumulation causes increases in financial resources or attracts investors/donors. No content in the provided citation supports the causal/positive relationship claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.28059408760816, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8381380969731963, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07844945694594645, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0040761388836613585, 'prob_variance': 0.05177211445815015, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0948450720594145, 'avg_nll': 0.24732410119995005}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no usable content (Cloudflare error message) and therefore supplies no evidence for the claimed causal mechanism or relationship. Because the source text is unavailable, none of the explanation\'s assertions can be verified from the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion (access to and use of financial services), obstacles like low demand/financial culture, and broad socioeconomic effects of greater financial inclusion. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources increase individuals\' intrinsic motivation to seek and consume information, nor that wealthy actors\' demand causes systemic improvements in the general accessibility of information for the whole population. The source addresses access to financial services and related demand/supply issues, not the claimed causal pathway linking financial resources to information accessibility across society."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a framing note about research questions on financial access and does not provide evidence or discussion linking greater financial resources to increased accessibility of information, nor the proposed mechanisms (intrinsic motivation of resourceful actors or systemic adaptation improving information availability). The excerpt and description focus on financial access research questions, so it does not support the causal claim about information accessibility."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources develop a higher intrinsic motivation to seek and consume information, which leads to systemic improvements in the overall availability of information for all. As financially resourceful actors demand more information, the broader environment adapts to meet their needs, thereby enhancing accessibility of information across the entire population, regardless of individual resource levels. Conversely, in contexts with limited financial resources, the collective interest in information diminishes, resulting in less accessible information for everyone.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources develop a higher intrinsic motivation to seek and consume information, which leads to systemic improvements in the overall availability of information for all. As financially resourceful actors demand more information, the broader environment adapts to meet their needs, thereby enhancing accessibility of information across the entire population, regardless of individual resource levels. Conversely, in contexts with limited financial resources, the collective interest in information diminishes, resulting in less accessible information for everyone.', 'perplexity': 1.4882951204611652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7635039220732652, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10166300536400329, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004828686841308352, 'prob_variance': 0.09025060992794835, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3099426548232234, 'avg_nll': 0.3976312503886394}

513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not cause spatial scale, nor does spatial scale cause financial resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Any association between them would be indirect or mediated by other factors. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that regional financial growth influences spatial patterns of \\"high-quality development\\" — it finds significant spatial spillovers, agglomeration/siphoning effects, and nonlinear (N-shaped or inverted-U) impacts of financial growth on outcomes in neighboring cities. That supports the general idea that finance affects spatial distribution and regional reach. However, the paper analyzes city- and region-level financial growth and its effects on development indicators; it does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources directly cause organizations to expand their operational or study geographic scale, nor that larger budgets \\"automatically\\" increase project spatial extent. The specific causal mechanism claimed (organizations using larger budgets to expand geographic scope) is not demonstrated in the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly causes an increase in spatial scale because organizations with more funds are able to expand the geographic scope of their projects. As a result, larger budgets automatically lead to larger operational or study areas.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly causes an increase in spatial scale because organizations with more funds are able to expand the geographic scope of their projects. As a result, larger budgets automatically lead to larger operational or study areas.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document focuses on K–12 and early childhood school funding, equity, and how increased education funding can support staff, community schools, and wraparound services. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources in a community directly attract healthcare professionals or cause the spontaneous emergence of urgent care centers and community clinics (independent of policy or planning). Therefore the specific causal mechanism and the target outcome (availability of alternatives to ED driven by influx of healthcare professionals and new urgent care/community clinics) are not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD report is about school funding and related education governance, allocation and evaluation. It contains no material about healthcare markets, the location or emergence of urgent care centres or community clinics, or about how local financial resources attract healthcare professionals and thereby increase alternatives to emergency departments. Therefore the sources do not support the causal claim about financial resources and availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a review about school finance and how educational funding affects schooling resources (teachers, class size, school reforms, etc.). It discusses how higher teacher pay can attract and retain teachers and how funding can enable school programs, but it does not address healthcare markets, the formation of urgent care centers or community clinics, or the claim that increased community financial resources spontaneously produce healthcare alternatives to emergency departments regardless of policy. The mechanism and sector (healthcare facilities and providers) in the claim are not supported by any material in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because higher financial resources within a community attract a larger influx of healthcare professionals, which directly leads to the spontaneous emergence of new urgent care centers and community clinics, regardless of any policy decisions or strategic planning. Therefore, as financial resources grow, the increased professional presence alone is sufficient to elevate the availability of alternatives for ED, while limited financial resources result in a shortage of such alternatives due to a lack of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because higher financial resources within a community attract a larger influx of healthcare professionals, which directly leads to the spontaneous emergence of new urgent care centers and community clinics, regardless of any policy decisions or strategic planning. Therefore, as financial resources grow, the increased professional presence alone is sufficient to elevate the availability of alternatives for ED, while limited financial resources result in a shortage of such alternatives due to a lack of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not cause time to change, nor does time cause financial resources to change in a direct causal manner. There is no known mechanism by which altering financial resources would directly alter the passage or amount of time, or vice versa. No direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://books.core-econ.org/the-economy/microeconomics/03-scarcity-wellbeing-07-income-substitution-effects.html', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1706541114'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://books.core-econ.org/the-economy/microeconomics/03-scarcity-wellbeing-07-income-substitution-effects.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CORE Econ chapter analyzes how higher unearned income shifts the budget constraint and often (but not always) leads individuals to choose more free time (a positive income effect). It therefore supports the idea that greater financial resources can enable people to choose more free time instead of work. However, the source does not claim—nor provide evidence—that money can literally create extra hours or extend the 24-hour day, and it emphasizes that outcomes depend on preferences and the offsetting substitution effect (so richer people do not always take more free time). Thus the citation partially supports the economic aspect (income can increase chosen free time) but does not support the literal or unconditional claim about “purchasing extra hours” or always having more time.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more financial resources directly increases the amount of time a person has in a day, because wealth allows individuals to purchase extra hours or extend the 24-hour cycle. As a result, richer people can literally create more time for themselves compared to those with fewer financial resources.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having more financial resources directly increases the amount of time a person has in a day, because wealth allows individuals to purchase extra hours or extend the 24-hour cycle. As a result, richer people can literally create more time for themselves compared to those with fewer financial resources.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Personnel to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, organizations have more capacity to hire and retain personnel. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources leads to fewer available personnel due to budget constraints. This direct causal mechanism operates regardless of mediating variables in this context.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.32443034790652225, 'citations': ['https://openpresstiu.pubpub.org/pub/hrm-2', 'https://engageforsuccess.org/unpacking-the-link-between-financial-security-and-work-efficiency/', 'https://www.shrm.org/in/topics-tools/news/blogs/importance-of-financially-empowered-workforce'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7030581110121829, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://openpresstiu.pubpub.org/pub/hrm-2", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides solid evidence that human (and social) capital and investments in people are positively associated with organizational/financial performance (e.g., Crook et al. meta-analysis, Combs et al.), and explains mechanisms (training, selection, social exchange) by which workforce capabilities raise financial returns. However, the source emphasizes the quality, skills, and effective HR practices that improve performance rather than simple headcount increases; it even gives examples where higher efficiency can reduce required labor. The claim’s specific causal story — that expanding Personnel (more bodies) by itself increases efficiency and therefore attracts more Financial resources, forming a feedback loop — is not directly supported and overgeneralizes the source’s emphasis on human capital quality and HR investment rather than mere workforce size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://engageforsuccess.org/unpacking-the-link-between-financial-security-and-work-efficiency/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how employees\' personal financial security (financial wellbeing programs) affects individual productivity, engagement, absenteeism and turnover. It does not provide evidence that organizational Financial resources cause increases in Personnel numbers, nor that expanding Personnel attracts additional Financial resources as claimed. The source does not describe the proposed feedback loop between organizational financial resources and workforce size."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.shrm.org/in/topics-tools/news/blogs/importance-of-financially-empowered-workforce", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SHRM content discusses employee financial wellness, AI in the workplace, productivity tools, and upskilling; it does not present evidence that greater Personnel leads to increased Financial resources (or that Financial resources and Personnel form the described feedback loop). The source does not support the causal/feedback claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive association between Financial resources and Personnel can be attributed to the increased efficiency brought about by a larger workforce. As organizations expand their Personnel, operational effectiveness improves, which in turn attracts additional Financial resources through enhanced productivity and market competitiveness. Therefore, greater Personnel numbers directly lead to increased Financial resources, establishing a feedback loop where Financial resources rise in response to the growing size of Personnel within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive association between Financial resources and Personnel can be attributed to the increased efficiency brought about by a larger workforce. As organizations expand their Personnel, operational effectiveness improves, which in turn attracts additional Financial resources through enhanced productivity and market competitiveness. Therefore, greater Personnel numbers directly lead to increased Financial resources, establishing a feedback loop where Financial resources rise in response to the growing size of Personnel within the organization.', 'perplexity': 1.6299872818984624, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7461648355815592, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03601033360955505, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005467311464492552, 'prob_variance': 0.10525505064274708, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1906205096965374, 'avg_nll': 0.4885722122719505}

517. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows a positive association between hospital financial health and quality/safety outcomes and discusses plausible organizational mechanisms (ability to invest in staff, training, infrastructure, and maintain reliable systems). However, it does not provide direct evidence for the specific individual‑level psychological and cognitive pathway asserted in the claim (that increases in financial resources directly cause clinicians to perceive institutional stability which immediately boosts motivation and cognitive performance, or that reductions are immediately internalized as job‑security threats causing diminished cognitive abilities irrespective of workload or support). The paper discusses staffing and system-level changes as mediators, not immediate changes in individual clinicians\' cognition or the \\"regardless\\" qualifier."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides empirical evidence that hospital financial health (profitability, leverage, labor costs) is positively associated with hospital process quality and that increases in profitability, leverage and labor spending predict subsequent improvements in quality measures. This supports a positive link between financial resources and better healthcare service performance at the hospital level. However, the explanation’s specific causal mechanism — that increased finances operate by immediately increasing healthcare professionals’ perceived institutional stability, motivation and cognitive performance (and that reductions are immediately internalized as job‑security threats impairing cognition irrespective of workload/support) — is not measured or demonstrated in the citation. The paper does reference and rely on literature about nurse staffing, wages and experience affecting quality, but it does not directly measure staff perceptions, motivation, cognitive functioning, or the asserted immediate internalization process. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the cited evidence: the broad positive association is supported, the detailed psychological causal story is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Tulane overview explains that healthcare finance is important for enabling delivery of quality care (covering costs, salaries, equipment, investments, and organizational solvency) and that financial management affects operations and patient outcomes. However it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanisms in the claim — i.e., that changes in financial resources are immediately internalized by individual healthcare professionals as job‑security threats, that increases directly boost motivation and cognitive performance, or that reductions instantly impair cognitive abilities regardless of workload or support. Those psychological and immediate causal links are not demonstrated in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894}

518. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which changes in financial resources would directly alter language proficiency or cultural differences. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors (such as access to education or services) rather than a direct causal link. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://scholarworks.uark.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1051&context=econuht', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0264999321002820'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://scholarworks.uark.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1051&context=econuht", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited thesis and sources discuss benefits of investing money in language education, corporate training, and policies that facilitate learning, and they emphasize that learning takes time, effort, and targeted programs (opportunity costs, need for schooling/subsidies, etc.). They do not claim — and in fact contradict — the assertion that simply increasing financial resources alone causes people to \\"automatically become fluent\\" or \\"fully understand different cultures without any additional effort.\\" Therefore the cited material does not support the strong, effortless causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited action-research report documents language and cultural barriers faced by international students and discusses factors (pre-sessional courses, teaching style, cultural awareness, work/study balance, admission practices) that influence those barriers. It does not claim that increased financial resources automatically produce fluency or cultural understanding; indeed it notes that even students whose fees are paid still struggle, that work and limited resources can impede progress, and that targeted language and cultural support (not mere wealth) is required. Therefore the sources do not support the assertion that wealth alone eliminates language and cultural barriers and in places contradict it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0264999321002820", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content related to financial resources or language/cultural barriers, so it provides no evidence for the claimed causal effect. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial resources directly reduces language and cultural barriers, because with more money, people automatically become fluent in other languages and fully understand different cultures without any additional effort. Thus, wealth alone eliminates the challenges posed by language and cultural differences.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial resources directly reduces language and cultural barriers, because with more money, people automatically become fluent in other languages and fully understand different cultures without any additional effort. Thus, wealth alone eliminates the challenges posed by language and cultural differences.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

