   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause frailty. The causal pathway from financial resources to frailty is mediated by other variables in the CLD, such as financial barriers, health literacy, or access to healthcare and healthy lifestyle. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between financial resources and frailty in this context.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953621009394', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4552741/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited brief reports that higher income and wealth are associated with better health, higher levels of physical activity (e.g., 60.1% of higher‑income adults met aerobic recommendations vs. 36.1% in poverty), and lower prevalence of physical limitations and disease. It emphasizes that greater resources enable healthier lifestyles and place-based health advantages. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources cause reduced physical activity through conveniences (delivery, private transportation) or that greater resources lead to increased frailty; in fact the data and arguments in the source point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953621009394", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no content or evidence to evaluate the claim. Because there is no