ed barriers, making access to goods or services appear easier.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. As individuals or organizations accumulate more financial resources, they tend to become more risk-averse and cautious in their spending, which inadvertently leads to the perception and creation of additional financial barriers. Conversely, with fewer financial resources available, there is less scrutiny and thus fewer perceived barriers, making access to goods or services appear easier.', 'perplexity': 1.3100238794711705, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8676524469994137, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.018735691036741997, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0067536974862793305, 'prob_variance': 0.06778848044441521, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6218515331175964, 'avg_nll': 0.27004536565086806}

