   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources make it easier for individuals to afford therapy, medications, transportation, or necessary equipment, directly supporting higher compliance. Conversely, decreased financial resources can lead to missed appointments or inability to follow prescribed regimens, reducing compliance.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/full/10.1176/appi.ps.25076003', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12889-025-24467-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that financial strain (lack of resources) leads people to make trade-offs, skip or ration prescribed treatments, and use alternative/cheaper remedies — i.e., lower financial resources are associated with decreased adherence. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources cause reduced therapy compliance via overconfidence or access to alternatives. Therefore the citation supports the opposite relationship, not the claimed negative effect of increased financial resources on adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses financial toxicity — how lack of financial resources and the economic burden of care lead to worse well‑being and behaviors like skipping medications or delaying care. It provides evidence that lower financial resources (or financial distress) reduce treatment adherence, not that greater financial resources decrease compliance or cause overconfidence/choice of alternatives. The paper does not support the claim that increased financial resources lead to lower therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12889-025-24467-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review emphasizes that economic resources and financial resilience generally improve households\' ability to manage health shocks, maintain treatment adherence, and avoid foregoing care. It discusses savings, insurance, and access to financial services as protective factors and recommends policies to strengthen financial protections to preserve treatment adherence. The review does not present evidence that greater financial resources lead to overconfidence, reduced urgency, or lower therapy adherence; nor does it document that access to alternative treatments or distractions from higher finances reduces compliance. Therefore the provided source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources may lead individuals to become overconfident in their ability to manage their health independently, resulting in a decreased sense of urgency to comply with prescribed therapy. Additionally, people with greater financial means might have access to alternative treatments or distractions, leading to lower adherence to standard therapy regimens.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased financial resources may lead individuals to become overconfident in their ability to manage their health independently, resulting in a decreased sense of urgency to comply with prescribed therapy. Additionally, people with greater financial means might have access to alternative treatments or distractions, leading to lower adherence to standard therapy regimens.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

522. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11606573/', 'https://www.unitedway-york.org/news/how-barriers-financial-literacy-keep-alice-households-prospering', 'https://www.nefe.org/initiatives/financial-education-policy-convenings/Financial-Education-Policy-Convening-Events-Summary--EBOOK.pdf', 'https://www.amacad.org/publication/daedalus/financial-constraints-collegiate-student-learning-behavioral-economics-perspective'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NEA source advocates for more personal finance education and presents evidence that financial literacy improves student financial behavior; it discusses economic inequality and argues that education alone won\'t solve structural income/wealth gaps. It does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources leads families to prioritize materialism over education or that greater financial comfort reduces formal educational attainment. The closest related point (a quote criticizing \\"educationism\\") criticizes using education as a substitute for redistribution, not that higher personal wealth causes lower education levels. Therefore the citation does not support the negative causal claim offered."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11606573/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how educational attainment relates to financial literacy and shows education is generally a strong positive predictor of financial literacy (with diminished returns for some racial/ethnic groups). It does not present evidence that increasing financial resources reduces educational attainment or that greater wealth/consumerism leads families to deprioritize formal education. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative effect of financial resources on education level."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.unitedway-york.org/news/how-barriers-financial-literacy-keep-alice-households-prospering", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses how limited financial resources and barriers (ALICE populations) impede access to financial literacy and education, arguing that lack of resources reduces educational opportunity. It does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources causes greater focus on consumerism, decreased prioritization of formal education, or lower overall education levels. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative effect of financial resources on education level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nefe.org/initiatives/financial-education-policy-convenings/Financial-Education-Policy-Convening-Events-Summary--EBOOK.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited convening summaries discuss how financial education affects access to and choices about postsecondary education, emphasize barriers that low-income families face, and recommend financial education to increase college access and better borrowing decisions. They do not present evidence that increasing financial resources leads people to prioritize material wealth over education or that greater family wealth reduces the perceived need for formal education. In fact, the materials generally describe financial resources and education as enabling access (and note that mandates or financial education can improve financial outcomes), and explicitly state postsecondary education remains important for wage advantages. Therefore the negative causal claim is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amacad.org/publication/daedalus/financial-constraints-collegiate-student-learning-behavioral-economics-perspective", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited essay documents how financial constraints harm college persistence and completion (showing higher-income students have higher attainment) and discusses how lack of financial resources, not abundance, creates barriers to education. It does not present evidence that increasing financial resources reduces educational attainment through materialism, consumerism, or decreased perceived need for education. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can lead to a focus on material wealth and consumerism, which may distract individuals from prioritizing education. As families become more financially comfortable, they might perceive formal education as less necessary, resulting in a lower overall education level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can lead to a focus on material wealth and consumerism, which may distract individuals from prioritizing education. As families become more financially comfortable, they might perceive formal education as less necessary, resulting in a lower overall education level.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

