   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute events, and acute events do not directly cause financial resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables without mediation by other factors such as access to healthcare or care avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between financial resources and acute event.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://www.samhsa.gov/sites/default/files/dtac/srb-low-ses_2.pdf', 'https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2024/jan/financial-stress-linked-worse-biological-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial shows that a financial education/coaching intervention reduced perceived financial strain, increased smoking cessation, and reduced avoidance of medical care among low-income single mothers; the paper discusses (via cited background literature) that chronic financial strain and stress can dysregulate inflammatory/immune pathways over the life course. It does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources directly strengthens the immune system or that greater wealth biologically and immediately protects individuals from acute medical events. The study measured behaviors, perceived strain, and some biometrics (with null results for weight) but did not measure immune function or acute event incidence, nor does it support the claim that financial status alone inherently protects against acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2024/jan/financial-stress-linked-worse-biological-health", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited UCL article reports that financial strain (perceived lack of resources) is associated with a higher‑risk profile of immune/neuroendocrine biomarkers over time, indicating chronic financial stress can harm biological health. That provides evidence of an association between financial stress and worse biomarker-based health risk. However, it does not show that increasing financial resources directly strengthens the immune system, nor does it measure or demonstrate a reduced likelihood of acute medical events. The study assesses perceived financial strain (not objective wealth), uses biomarkers (not incidence of acute events), and does not establish the strict causal mechanism claimed (\\"wealthier people are inherently protected solely due to their financial status\\")."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event because having more money strengthens the immune system, making individuals biologically less susceptible to sudden medical crises. Therefore, wealthier people are inherently protected from acute events solely due to their financial status.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event because having more money strengthens the immune system, making individuals biologically less susceptible to sudden medical crises. Therefore, wealthier people are inherently protected from acute events solely due to their financial status.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

