uss possible mediating behaviors (e.g., care avoidance) as consequences, but they do not conclude that financial resources have no direct causal effect on acute care demand. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; rather it provides evidence that financial factors do influence ED visits (even if some of that effect operates through mediators)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review finds mixed/inconclusive evidence linking income/insurance to non-urgent ED use and explicitly frames socio-demographic factors (including income/insurance) as \\"associated factors\\" that likely influence ED use via causal pathway variables (perceived severity, access, beliefs, cost barriers). It also documents financial mechanisms (payment flexibility, high deductibles) that operate through access/avoidance rather than proving a direct physiological causal effect of \\"financial resources\\" on acute care demand. However, the review does not definitively demonstrate absence of any direct causal pathway — it emphasizes heterogeneity and limited evidence.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any apparent connection is mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3211691451926255, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8457096915266634, 'min_prob': 0.026463811546346437, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002849611879262745, 'prob_variance': 0.062730466493956,