l resources alone\\" and that financial literacy is developed via education and skills. These points support the claim that financial resources do not directly cause knowledge and that effects are mediated by education/information access."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.consumerfinance.gov/f/201501_cfpb_report_financial-well-being.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CFPB report does not assert a direct causal link from financial resources to knowledge and repeatedly emphasizes mediation by context, opportunity, education, and personal traits. It highlights that structural factors (family wealth, access to education, social and economic environment) shape opportunities and that knowledge alone is insufficient to change behavior. However, the report does not explicitly state the categorical claim that financial resources never directly cause changes in knowledge; it frames the relationship as complex and mediated rather than asserting an absolute absence of any direct effect. Therefore the user’s strong “no direct causal effect” statement is only partly reflected by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w27057/w27057.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes the causal effect of financial education programs on financial knowledge and behaviors and finds positive effects of education interventions. It does not test whether financial resources (wealth/income) directly cause changes in knowledge; at best it notes that resource constraints can limit behavior change and that program effects vary by country income. Therefore the source does not provide evidence that financial resources have no direct causal effect on knowledge, nor does it provide direct evidence of the alternative causal claim in the explanation. The citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship as s