_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates causal effects of socioeconomic traits on frailty using Mendelian randomization. It does not define or analyze \\"Time\\" as a variable, nor does it assess causal links between frailty and time. Therefore the source provides no evidence about a causal relationship (or lack thereof) between Frailty and Time."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation alone does not establish causation and that demonstrating causality requires temporal order, a plausible mechanism, and ruling out confounders. That supports the explanation’s methodological point that, given no operational definitions or contextual evidence, one cannot establish a causal link. However, the source is general and does not provide evidence specific to \\"Frailty\\" and \\"Time\\" nor does it justify the stronger categorical claim that no causal relationship exists at all between those variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links frailty to aging and time: it defines frailty as an aging-associated decline in reserve, reports age-specific prevalence (increasing with older age groups), and presents longitudinal data on incidence and transitions over years. It also uses operational definitions (Fried phenotype, frailty index) and discusses frailty\'s natural history and potential etiologic mechanisms. These points contradict the claim that there is no relationship or that no causal link can be examined without definitions. Therefore the sources do not support t