hip can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents definitions, natural history, predictors, and consequences of frailty in older adults but does not mention \\"Personnel\\" or any concept clearly corresponding to that target variable. Because the sources are silent about Personnel they provide no evidence demonstrating the absence of a causal link; absence of discussion is not evidence of no relationship. Therefore the citations do not support the claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" between Frailty and Personnel."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8630240/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that \\"frailty\\" is variably defined and multidimensional and explicitly notes the lack of a single gold‑standard definition, which makes establishing causal claims difficult. That supports the explanation\'s point that, without clear definitions/context, causal relationships are hard to establish. However, the paper does not examine or provide evidence about any variable called \\"Personnel\\" (nor about causal links between frailty and personnel), so it does not support the stronger claim that there is no causal link between Frailty and Personnel. Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation (the part about definitional uncertainty) but does not support the conclusion that no causal relationship exists between these two specific variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that correlation alone does not prove causation and that establishing causality requires temporality, a plausible mechanism, and ruling 