ted Wikipedia article explicitly describes frailty as an age‑related loss of \\"in‑built reserves and functioning\\" and repeatedly links frailty to reduced physiological and functional capacity (e.g., \\"reduced reserve capacity of organ systems\\", \\"reduction in functional status\\", \\"physiological functional capacity\\"). Thus the sources indicate a clear relationship between frailty and capacity, contradicting a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/frailty", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic content explicitly links frailty to reduced ability to perform day-to-day tasks and loss of independence (needing help with ADLs, causing disability, limiting treatment options). That indicates frailty affects a person\'s capacity, so the claim of no relationship is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0632081589141522, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9546904640006438, 'min_prob': 0.518132464052297, 'token_prob_slope': -0.009512873148457498, 'prob_variance': 0.019080655670209806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.25805378584644645, 'avg_nll': 0.06129090230971909}

579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7527302867661948, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that aspects of healthcare professional functioning — especially the patient-prescriber relationship, communication, time spent, empathy, and satisfaction with clinic visits — are associated with better or worse patient adherence. It therefore supports a positive link between how healthcare professionals function and therapy compliance. However, the specific mechanism asserted in the explanation (that high professional competence makes patients believe treatment will succeed \\"regardless of their own actions,\\" and that this belief drives increased adherence) is not described in the review. The review instead emphasizes trust, communication, involvement, patient understanding and motivation as reasons that provider behavior affects adherence — not a passive belief that patients’ own actions are unnecessary. Thus the general claim (functioning of professionals affects compliance) is supported, but the particular causal rationale given is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited concept-analysis paper identifies healthcare-provider communication, and patients’ attitudes toward professional recommendations (including perceptions of clinician competence and quality of explanations) as antecedents that influence patient compliance — supporting a positive causal link from healthcare professionals\' functioning to therapy adherence. However, the source does not support the specific mechanism asserted in the explanation (that competent professionals make patients believe treatment will succeed \\"regardless of their own actions,\\" and that this passive belief drives greater adherence). Instead, the article emphasizes patients’ understanding, willing collaboration, and agreement with recommendations as drivers of compliance, not a hands-off belief that patient actions are unnecessary. Therefore the general positive relationship is supported but the detailed causal mechanism presented is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article clearly supports a positive causal link between the quality/functioning of healthcare professionals (therapeutic alliance, empathy, communication, trust, shared decision‑making) and greater patient engagement and adherence to treatment plans; it also notes poor therapeutic relationships can hinder engagement or lead to early termination. However, the cited text does not support the specific causal mechanism asserted in the explanation — namely that patients adhere because they believe the professional\'s expertise alone will ensure success \\"regardless of their own actions.\\" In fact, the source emphasizes collaboration and patient participation rather than passive reliance on clinicians."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page (Cloudflare error) and contains no accessible content or evidence. Because the citation provides no supporting material about healthcare professional functioning or its effect on therapy compliance, the claim is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405}

