   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Time to change, nor does Time cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change, as Time is an independent parameter and there is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Delphi study identifies elements of a positive work environment (e.g., leadership, teamwork, staffing, autonomy) and discusses measurement and consensus about those elements; it does not present evidence that mere passage of time or “synchronization with daily rhythms” causally improves healthcare professional functioning independent of training or experience. The source contains no findings or analyses supporting a time-driven, natural improvement in performance as described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews theories and evidence about how to achieve change in health care and repeatedly emphasizes that improvement is difficult and depends on specific factors (education, motivation, leadership, teams, organizational changes, incentives, monitoring, etc.). It discusses stages and processes that unfold over time and the need for active implementation, learning, and organizational support, but it does not claim — and in fact implies the opposite — that mere passage of time by itself directly and naturally causes healthcare professionals\' functioning to improve regardless of training or experience. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted causal time → improved functioning claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports associations (older age, planning, responsibility, less time-wasting, adequate staffing, and satisfaction with compensation) with better time-management and performance; qualitative data show improvement from deliberate practice and planning. It does not provide evidence that mere passage of time by itself (independent of training, planning, experience or other factors) causally improves healthcare professionals\' functioning or that improvement occurs \\"regardless of training or experience.\\" Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents elevated stress, burnout, moral injury and other harms among healthcare workers over time during crises, and emphasizes the need for interventions, organizational support, and self‑care to protect or build resilience. While it notes that resilience can be learned in some circumstances (with social support and training), it does not claim that mere passage of time automatically improves functioning or that time alone causes better performance regardless of training or experience. Thus the sources do not support the asserted causal, time-driven natural improvement."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article addresses \\"capacity\\" as an individual patient\'s decision‑making capacity and focuses on assessment, legal/ethical implications, and documentation. It does not discuss expanding resources, infrastructure, service offerings, or how increases in system/organizational capacity affect healthcare professionals\' skill, efficiency, or motivation. It therefore does not provide evidence for a direct positive effect of increased (system) capacity on healthcare professional functioning or the claim that such gains occur without training or mediation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter provides evidence that greater system capacity (e.g., informatics, registries, quality‑improvement infrastructure, team supports — “structure”) can improve clinicians’ performance, reduce errors, and enable better care, so it supports a positive link from expanded capacity/resources to better functioning. However, the source repeatedly emphasizes the need for education, core competencies, and deliberate implementation (teams, training, application of evidence) and notes that systems alone do not guarantee optimal professional performance. It therefore does not support the stronger claims in the explanation that capacity improvements automatically raise skill/efficiency/motivation \\"without the need for targeted training\\" or that the relationship is wholly direct and unmediated.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited interview study emphasizes that successful change depends on staff having influence, being prepared, and perceiving value (including patient benefit). It notes contextual factors such as resources can affect readiness, but it does not claim that increasing capacity (resources, infrastructure, service offerings) directly and automatically improves healthcare professionals\' functioning or skill level without training or mediation. The paper stresses the need for involvement, preparation/communication and perceived value rather than an unconditional direct effect of capacity alone. Therefore the source does not support the strong, unmediated causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that organizational context, leadership, and training influence healthcare professionals’ performance and wellbeing and notes resource/infrastructure constraints as challenges. It does not provide evidence that expanding capacity alone directly raises skill, efficiency, and motivation without leadership, training, or other mediators. The source therefore supports the general idea that system context/resources matter for functioning but does not support the strong, unmediated causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

