   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which the professional functioning itself would directly alter these feelings in patients or professionals without mediating factors. Any observed association is likely due to other variables such as care quality, communication, or patient experiences. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes healthcare professionals\' work environment, mental/physical health, doctor–patient relationships, and media influence based on providers\' self-reports. It reports links between provider job satisfaction, trust, patient verbal/physical abuse, and provider well‑being, but it does not present evidence that improvements in providers\' functioning (or competence) increase patients\' feelings of fear or shame, nor that patients perceive competent staff as more intimidating. The paper addresses clinician-side stressors and doctor–patient trust/abuse metrics rather than patient-reported fear or shame caused by high provider competence. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines healthcare workers’ job environment, stress, coping strategies, and effects on the professionals’ own mental-emotional health and quality of care. It does not present data or analysis showing that better functioning or higher competence of healthcare staff increases patients’ feelings of fear or shame, nor that patients perceive competent staff as more intimidating. Therefore the claim about improved professional functioning directly increasing patient fear and shame is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited “ScienceDirect” entry returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the proposed link between healthcare professional functioning and increased patient fear and shame.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report reviews causes and consequences of inappropriate medication use and emphasizes prescribers\' roles in prescribing errors, underuse, overuse, polypharmacy, prescribing cascades, and the need to improve prescribing and monitoring. It does not provide evidence that healthcare professionals \\"often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such encouragement leads to improved medication use. In fact, the source documents many clinician-related practices that contribute to inappropriate use rather than endorsing patient-led experimentation as a beneficial routine. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors are common, outlines causes (poor communication, lack of training, workload, interruptions, illegible orders, etc.), and recommends clear, precise prescribing and patient education to prevent patient misuse. It does not provide any evidence that healthcare professionals \\"often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such encouragement leads to improved medication use. In fact, the source emphasizes avoiding vague instructions and discourages practices that would promote patient experimentation. Therefore the specific mechanism and positive effect claimed are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents how medication errors arise and how healthcare professionals\' factors (inexperience, distraction, fatigue, poor training, system failures) increase error rates. It does not report that healthcare professionals \\"encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" nor that such encouragement leads to improved medication use. The mechanism and positive framing in the explanation are not supported by the citation, which focuses on error causes and prevention rather than promotion of patient-led experimentation or improved adherence through experimentation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The FDA source advises patients to consult health care professionals, follow directions, avoid combining medicines, and not change how they use medicines without guidance. It does not state that health professionals encourage experimenting with doses or combinations; if anything it promotes caution to reduce misuse. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that healthcare professional functioning positively causes incorrect medication use (or that