   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. Any possible connection would be mediated by other factors such as quality of care, access to treatment, or patient compliance, which are present as mediating variables in the CLD. There is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses substance misuse and addiction among healthcare professionals themselves, risk factors (access, stress, sleep deprivation), and the need for specialized treatment and monitoring for impaired HCPs. It provides evidence about prevalence, relapse risk, and treatment outcomes for HCPs, but does not provide evidence that improving HCP functioning reduces chronic addiction rates in patients or that routine, effective medical care by HCPs can \\"eliminate\\" or cure addiction in the population. The claimed direct causal effect is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports that health care professionals\' attitudes, knowledge, and use of evidence-based interventions (e.g., motivational interviewing, medications, harm-reduction practices) improve engagement, reduce harm, and can help people enter and remain in treatment. It also notes nurses are well-placed to affect outcomes. However, the source frames addiction as a chronic brain disease often requiring comprehensive, repeated, and multifaceted treatment and explicitly says no single intervention or simple improved \\"effectiveness\\" will cure all addicted persons. It does not support the strong causal claim that merely improving routine functioning (regular check-ups/medical interventions) will directly eliminate addictive behaviors or \\"cure\\" chronic addiction by virtue of being more efficient."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies risk factors for alcohol and drug use among healthcare professionals themselves and discusses prevention, treatment and policy for impaired HPs. It does not provide evidence that better functioning or efficiency of clinicians reduces chronic addiction in patients, nor that routine check‑ups or ordinary medical effectiveness \\"eliminate\\" or \\"cure\\" addiction. The source addresses HPs\' substance use risk factors and monitoring, not a causal link from improved HP functioning to lower chronic addiction rates in patients."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

