 that professional care in ILTC is associated with lower objective burden (fewer caregiving hours) and that professionals often take over more intensive tasks — which is consistent with the idea that professional care can reduce some informal care needs. However, the paper does not measure \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" or \\"professional effectiveness\\" as a variable, nor does it provide direct causal evidence that better-performing staff reduce informal care; it also shows informal care often continues in ILTC and subjective caregiver burden can remain. Thus the claim that improved healthcare professional functioning directly reduces informal care is only partially supported by the citation (support for reduced objective hours in settings with professional care, but not for a direct causal effect of professional functioning or full elimination of informal care)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8583293", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes characteristics, needs, and burden of informal caregivers in a primary healthcare home-care program and discusses the role of PHC professionals and service use, but it does not measure or analyze \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" nor provide evidence that better professional functioning directly reduces the amount of informal care provided. No data or statistical analysis in the paper links professional effectiveness to decreased informal care. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7068939/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that informal caregivers frequently fill gaps left by constrained, poorly coordinated, or understaffed professional services — reporting many practical tasks and care responsibilities that arise because formal care is limited. Caregivers also describe cases where multidisciplinary/ coordinated professional support improved transitions and information. However, the paper does not provide direct empirical evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals causally reduces the overall need for informal care or quantify such an effect. It supports the plausibility of the claim but does not directly demonstrate the negative causal relationship asserted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

