s patients to become overly reliant and thereby reduces their health literacy. No support for the proposed negative causal mechanism is present in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305618/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that reliance on healthcare professionals and passive compliance can limit patients\' engagement with information and progression along the authors\' \\"health literacy pathway\\" (e.g., quotes about patients taking tablets without reading leaflets; discussion of personal reliance as a barrier). It also describes how health professionals can either facilitate or impede development of health literacy (poor/paternalistic communication can disempower patients). However, the study does not provide evidence for the specific claim that \\"improved functioning of healthcare professionals\\" causes decreased health literacy through increased patient reliance. In fact, the paper mainly shows that supportive, well-functioning professionals facilitate health literacy, while poor professional communication or withholding information can hinder it. Thus the mechanistic claim linking improved professional functioning to reduced patient health literacy is not supported by the citation, although the more general idea that patient reliance on professionals can limit development of health literacy is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article assesses patients\' health literacy levels and how low health literacy relates to outcomes (e.g., emergency visits, readmissions). It does not present evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals causes patients to become overly reliant and thereby reduces their health literacy, nor does it describe the proposed mechanism (reduced motivation to seek/understand information because professionals perform better). The paper focuses on prevalence, consequences of low health literacy, and the need for interventions — not on provider performance causing declines in patient health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how patients often obtain information from healthcare providers, barriers to patient understanding (e.g., embarrassment, limited time), and strategies for providers to improve patient health literacy. It does not present evidence that better functioning of healthcare professionals causes patients to become overly reliant in a way that reduces their motivation or leads to lower health literacy. If anything, the source emphasizes provider roles and strategies to increase, not decrease, patients\' health literacy. Therefore the negative causal claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

