   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27819692398943563, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://academic.oup.com/book/24610/chapter/187896403', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7171784074774511, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes diagnostic resources (tests, labs, imaging) as components of the broader health care work system and details how organizational elements, regulation, accreditation, technologies, and policy shape availability and use. It also explains clinicians\' role in selecting/ordering tests (pre-pre-analytic phase) and how work-system factors influence clinical reasoning. The sources therefore support the idea that availability of diagnostic resources is determined by system/organizational and technological factors and that links between clinicians\' functioning and resources are mediated by those factors. However, the chapter also documents clear ways clinicians\' actions (test selection, ordering, interpretation) directly affect diagnostic testing processes and utilization, so it does not unambiguously claim there is absolutely no direct causal relationship in either direction. Thus the citation partially supports the claim that there is no simple direct causal relationship and that any links are mediated by other factors, but it does not fully substantiate a blanket \\"none\\" relationship.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - Not supported.