vidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, it implies the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that people in substance use disorder treatment typically have low-to-moderate health literacy and that lower health literacy is associated with worse quality of life and mental health. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases exposure, curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything it documents the opposite association. The proposed mechanism (more information from higher health literacy leading to greater substance use) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines health literacy among people in substance use disorder (SUD) treatment and finds that low (not high) health literacy is common in this population, that lower health literacy is associated with poorer outcomes (higher psychological distress, lower quality of life), and that health literacy can improve after treatment. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases exposure-driven curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, the background literature cited in the paper links lower health literacy to greater vulnerability. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted positive causal pathway from higher health literacy to greater chronic addiction risk."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy exposes individuals to more information about substances and their effects, which can spark curiosity and experimentation. 