   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.279263627624873, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6927637/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7482823147475822, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial explicitly links health (and financial) health literacy to patients\' ability to understand and manage healthcare costs, patient engagement, and financial burdens. It discusses how limited health literacy contributes to poor decision-making around costly services (e.g., unnecessary imaging) and recommends interventions (price transparency, patient navigation, education) to reduce patients\' financial burden. It also cites socioeconomic status and education as determinants of health literacy, implying interrelations rather than an absence of causal pathways. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they present health literacy as influencing financial outcomes and being related to financial factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly states that low socioeconomic status (including income) and low educational attainment are key determinants of health literacy and that health literacy is associated with income. That indicates a direct causal direction from financial resources/SES to health literacy (SES -> health literacy). Although the review notes that improving health literacy might empower action on social and economic determinants, the primary content contradicts the claim of no direct causal relati