   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.23713592027063293, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2020.00280/full', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0303164', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35886562/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7834880805303829, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article clearly defines and discusses health literacy and its associations with health outcomes (so the claim of not knowing the definition of health literacy is incorrect with respect to the provided citation). However, the citation does not define or discuss frailty nor provide evidence of a causal (or non-causal) relationship between health literacy and frailty. Therefore the explanation that you cannot determine a causal relationship is partly justified (because the sources lack information on frailty or a causal link) but the statement about not knowing the definition of health literacy is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1492599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines many health outcomes associated with literacy (knowledge, hospitalization, diabetes control, global health measures, etc.) but does not evaluate or report on \\"frailty\\" as an outcome. Absence of evidence in this citation is not evidence of absence; the source does not support a claim that there is no relationship between health literacy and frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2020.00280/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines both health literacy and frailty and tests their associations. It reports that health literacy did not have a direct path to frailty in the SEM, but did have a statistically significant indirect (mediated) negative association with frailty via social support and depression (total/indirect effects significant). The study also notes its cross-sectional design limits causal inference. Therefore the claim of \\"NO relationship\\" is incorrect — there is evidence of an indirect relationship (and a significant total effect), even though direct causality cannot be established from this study alone. The user\'s explanation that they \\"do not know the definitions\\" is contradicted by the paper, which provides definitions and measures.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0303164", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sources do report a relationship between health literacy and frailty. The PLOS One study found a statistically significant negative correlation (r = -0.27, p < .001) between frailty and health literacy and analyzed mediating effects; other cited works (e.g., Yoshizawa et al. 2021; Choi et al. 2022) report that lower health literacy predicts frailty progression or increases frailty risk. Thus the citation set contradicts the \\"NONE\\" claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35886562/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study explicitly reports and quantifies associations between health literacy and frailty (finding poor health literacy is a risk factor and high health literacy is protective), so the statement that one \\"cannot determine\\" a relationship because definitions are unknown is contradicted by the citation\'s content. The citation supports an association (and frames health literacy as a risk factor), so the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know the definitions of 'Health literacy' or 'Frailty' as provided, so I cannot determine if a causal relationship exists between them.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2679670806956096, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8404046904850219, 'min_prob': 0.21040820394764012, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036989435402493475, 'prob_variance': 0.056233444682599984, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8283443659472888, 'avg_nll': 0.23741489408150365}

640. Health literacy-[NONE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.280319800129528, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8356483/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7114991142699499, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents links between health literacy and health behaviors, access, and greater use of emergency care, but it does not provide evidence that an individual\'s health literacy cannot causally affect the availability of emergency-department alternatives. It does not analyze or state the determinants of health-system capacity or service availability (policy/infrastructure) in a way that supports the asserted \\"no causal mechanism.\\" Therefore the provided citation does not substantiate the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8356483/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes health literacy as linked to patterns of health care use (including excess ED visits) and as interacting with multilevel, structural determinants of access and quality. It emphasizes that availability and access are shaped by health system organization, policy, and other structural factors (and discusses health-literate organizations as a separate institutional attribute). While the paper does not directly test whether individual health literacy changes the physical or policy-determined availability of ED alternatives, it supports the claim that availability is primarily a product of systemic/institutional factors rather than individual patient literacy—so it supports the explanation indirectly but not with direct empirical evidence specific to ED alternatives."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verd