   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2618102905345922, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7844722626837073, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review repeatedly identifies education (low educational attainment) as a key determinant of health literacy and describes education → health literacy associations. It does not provide evidence that health literacy causally increases formal education level; at most it notes that improving health literacy may empower action on social/economic determinants. Because the source supports the typical direction (education influences health literacy) but does not explicitly rule out any reverse causal effects, the claim that health literacy does not causally affect education is only partially supported by the citation content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where health literacy causes education level. Typically, education level may influence health literacy, but not the other way around. Therefore, health literacy does not causally affect education level in this direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.299521694925706, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8369329075999973, 'min_prob': 0.18109277006749985, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004367582608632191, 'prob_variance': 0.06854462822980757, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6126949260806644, 'avg_nll': 0.26199626978552687}

