647. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause healthy lifestyle, based on the information provided. There are no definitions, mechanisms, or contextual details to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables. Without clear definitions or evidence, no causal link can be determined.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11293293/', 'https://depressioncenter.org/outreach-education/depression-center-toolkit/want-stay-mentally-healthy/lifestyle-strategies-and', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587344/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Psychiatry.org page states that healthy lifestyle behaviors promote mental health and can prevent and treat conditions like anxiety and depression. It encourages small, sustainable habit changes and working with professionals; it does not claim that adopting healthy behaviors causes mental disorders or that sudden routine/diet changes lead to overwhelmed brains and new psychiatric illness. The citation therefore supports the opposite of the explanation and does not provide evidence for the claimed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11293293/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review (Sharma et al. 2024) consistently presents unhealthy lifestyle as a risk for poor mental health and promotes lifestyle modification to improve mental well‑being. It generally supports that exercise, diet, routine, sleep, social activity, etc. protect mental health. The article only notes limited, specific exceptions (e.g., over‑intense meditation/prayer or certain practices precipitating symptoms in vulnerable individuals) rather than supporting the broad claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes mental disorders via stress from sudden routine/diet changes. Therefore the citations do not support the asserted causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587344/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article presents lifestyle factors (nutrition, exercise, sleep, social connection, substance use management, stress reduction) as protective or therapeutic for mental health — citing RCTs and meta-analyses showing dietary and exercise improvements reduce depression/anxiety and recommending lifestyle medicine to prevent and treat mental illness. It does discuss stress as a risk factor for mental disorders, and notes that lifestyle change can be challenging for adherence, but it provides no evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes mental disorders via stress from sudden routine or diet changes. The claim is contradicted, not supported, by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle, such as regular exercise and a balanced diet, causes mental disorders because the body experiences stress from sudden changes in routine and diet. This stress can overwhelm the brain, leading to the development of anxiety, depression, or other mental health conditions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle, such as regular exercise and a balanced diet, causes mental disorders because the body experiences stress from sudden changes in routine and diet. This stress can overwhelm the brain, leading to the development of anxiety, depression, or other mental health conditions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

648. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in behaviors such as regular exercise, balanced nutrition, and avoiding harmful substances reduces the risk and persistence of chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in healthy lifestyle behaviors increases vulnerability to chronic addiction. This relationship is inverse and direct, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.lotusbh.org/post/healthy-addictions'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.lotusbh.org/post/healthy-addictions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited blog post title and summary describe \\"healthy addictions\\" as positive habits (e.g., exercise, reading) and how to cultivate them. There is no provided evidence in the citation that adopting a healthy lifestyle commonly leads to chronic addiction, creates dependencies on diets or exercise regimens, or causes long-term harmful addiction. The source appears to frame such habits as beneficial rather than documenting a pathway to chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle often involves experimenting with new routines and wellness products, some of which can inadvertently foster chronic addiction by creating dependencies on specific diets or exercise regimens. Therefore, maintaining a healthy lifestyle could positively contribute to the development of chronic addiction over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle often involves experimenting with new routines and wellness products, some of which can inadvertently foster chronic addiction by creating dependencies on specific diets or exercise regimens. Therefore, maintaining a healthy lifestyle could positively contribute to the development of chronic addiction over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

