   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes the size of a social network or vice versa. Without a clear mechanism or specified definitions and scales, causality cannot be established between these variables. Any observed association is likely due to mediating or confounding factors, not direct causation.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circulationaha.112.101816', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/lim2.94', 'https://www.sbm.org/healthy-living/how-social-connections-benefit-healthy-living'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIA source describes how social networks and social support influence health behaviors (and how health interventions can spread through existing relationships), and it notes associations between strong social ties and healthier behaviors. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causally increases the size of a person’s social network or makes people automatically gain more friends by being healthier. The cited content primarily shows social networks affecting health behavior (and behavior spreading within existing ties), not the claimed reverse causal effect on network size or attractiveness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sbm.org/healthy-living/how-social-connections-benefit-healthy-living", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article emphasizes that social connection supports and is an essential part of a healthy lifestyle, and it offers tips (e.g., volunteering, developing hobbies, scheduling social contact) for strengthening social connections as part of pursuing health. It does not provide evidence that adopting healthy habits (like exercising or eating well) directly causes an increase in the size of one’s social network, nor that those habits automatically make people more attractive/approachable and thus gain friends. The direction and causal claim in the explanation are not supported by the source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes an increase in the size of a person's social network, because engaging in healthy habits like exercising or eating well makes individuals more attractive and approachable to others. As a result, people who live healthily automatically gain more friends and social connections.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes an increase in the size of a person's social network, because engaging in healthy habits like exercising or eating well makes individuals more attractive and approachable to others. As a result, people who live healthily automatically gain more friends and social connections.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

