   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no known direct causal relationship where 'Healthy lifestyle' causes 'Need for autonomy' or vice versa. Without clear definitions or a specified mechanism, no causal link can be established between these variables.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9518329/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4346087/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9518329/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study tests autonomy (measured at baseline) and self-control as predictors of changes in healthy lifestyle after regular exercise — i.e., autonomy → healthy lifestyle. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases a person\'s need for autonomy or that healthy behavior causally raises autonomy; the causal direction in the paper is the opposite and the study did not measure changes in autonomy as an outcome. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed healthy-lifestyle → increased need-for-autonomy relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes autonomy as a basic psychological need and reviews evidence that autonomy (and autonomy-supportive environments) promotes self-regulation and healthier behaviors. It documents autonomy → health behavior effects and variation in individuals\' need for autonomy, but it does not provide evidence that engaging in healthy lifestyle behaviors causes an increase in a person\'s need for autonomy or makes them seek greater independence across domains. The proposed causal direction (healthy lifestyle increases need for autonomy) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4346087/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how autonomous motivation (the need for autonomy and autonomous reasons) predicts engagement in health behaviors and how autonomy-supportive contexts foster self-regulation. It treats autonomy as a psychological antecedent and a need that, when satisfied, supports persistence in health behaviors. It does not provide evidence that engaging in a healthy lifestyle or making frequent health-conscious choices increases a person\'s need for autonomy or causes a stronger general desire for independence across life domains. The direction claimed (healthy lifestyle → increased need for autonomy) is not supported by the paper\'s content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Engaging in a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's need for autonomy because making frequent health-conscious choices encourages individuals to seek greater independence in all areas of their lives. As people become more proactive about their health, they develop a stronger desire to make autonomous decisions beyond just their well-being.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Engaging in a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's need for autonomy because making frequent health-conscious choices encourages individuals to seek greater independence in all areas of their lives. As people become more proactive about their health, they develop a stronger desire to make autonomous decisions beyond just their well-being.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

651. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no basis to assert that a healthy lifestyle causes knowledge or that knowledge causes a healthy lifestyle since both variables lack definitions and context. Without clear definitions, mechanisms, or evidence, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951958/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-025-04630-4', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that healthy lifestyle behaviors (physical activity, nutrition, weight management, etc.) benefit brain health, cognition, and reduce dementia risk — supporting a positive link between lifestyle and cognitive function. However, the sources do not claim that simply living healthily \\"automatically\\" increases a person\'s knowledge independent of learning or study habits, nor do they provide evidence that lifestyle alone produces large gains in factual knowledge. The claim overstates and adds causal specifics (knowledge acquisition without effort) that are not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951958/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that physical activity (and certain dietary factors) improve brain structure and function — e.g., increased hippocampal volume, better attention, faster processing, enhanced memory and neuroplasticity mediated by BDNF/IGF-1 — and that these changes can translate to better performance on cognitive and some academic measures. However, the sources do not support the stronger claim that merely “living healthily” automatically increases a person’s factual knowledge without any learning effort. The evidence shows improved capacity for cognition and memory (which can facilitate learning), not spontaneous acquisition of knowledge independent of study or practice. Thus the general positive effect on cognitive function is supported, but the asserted automatic, effort-free increase in knowledge is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents evidence that diet, physical activity, and sleep influence brain function and learning-related cognitive processes (attention, working memory, executive function) in children, and outlines biological pathways (glucose availability, micronutrients, fitness-related brain changes) by which lifestyle can support cognitive capacity and readiness to learn. However, the paper emphasizes that causal relationships are understudied, that effects are about supporting cognition and learning processes (not directly adding factual knowledge), and much of the evidence is in children and/or concerns improved ability to learn rather than automatic gains in knowledge without study. The claim that simply living healthily \\"automatically\\" produces a significant increase in knowledge regardless of study habits is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-025-04630-4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports correlations between certain lifestyle habits and undergraduate academic performance (GPA)—finding mixed effects for eating habits, a small positive association for hygiene, and a substantially larger positive association for studying habits—but it does not show that a healthy lifestyle \\"automatically\\" increases knowledge irrespective of study effort or establish a direct causal effect of general physical fitness/nutrition on knowledge. The paper uses observational data and regression/ LSTM-derived indicators, explicitly notes limits on causal inference, and emphasizes that studying habits are a primary predictor. Therefore the strong causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's knowledge because physical fitness and good nutrition enhance brain capacity, automatically making people more knowledgeable regardless of their study habits. Therefore, simply living healthily causes a significant boost in knowledge without any additional learning effort.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's knowledge because physical fitness and good nutrition enhance brain capacity, automatically making people more knowledgeable regardless of their study habits. Therefore, simply living healthily causes a significant boost in knowledge without any additional learning effort.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

652. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Healthy lifestyle' causes 'Health literacy' or vice versa. Healthy lifestyle refers to behaviors and habits, while health literacy refers to the ability to obtain, process, and understand health information. While these may be correlated, one does not directly cause changes in the other without mediating factors like education or knowledge. Therefore, no direct causal link should be included in the CLD.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes associations between health literacy and behaviors (e.g., diet, exercise) and emphasizes that low health literacy impairs self-management and preventive behaviors, and that health literacy is shaped by social determinants (education, income, language). It discusses interventions to improve health literacy, but it does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes increases in health literacy or that people who exercise/eat well automatically develop deeper health knowledge. The direction of effect and causal claim in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and lists factors that affect it (e.g., reading/writing skills, knowledge of medical words, education, age, language, computer skills). It describes how health literacy can help people lead a healthy lifestyle, but it does not state or provide evidence that engaging in healthy behaviors (exercise, diet) causes increases in health literacy or that people who adopt healthy lifestyles automatically gain health knowledge. The citation therefore does not support the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 page defines personal and organizational health literacy, lists related objectives, and emphasizes assessing and improving people’s ability to find, understand, and use health information (and organizations’ role). It does not state or provide evidence that engaging in healthy behaviors (e.g., exercising, eating well) directly causes increases in health literacy or that healthy choices automatically produce deeper health knowledge. The source discusses promoting and measuring health literacy, not a causal effect from a healthy lifestyle to greater health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 summary defines personal and organizational health literacy and discusses determinants (e.g., education, language, age, poverty) and system-level interventions to improve literacy. It does not provide evidence that adopting healthy behaviors (exercise, diet) causes increases in health literacy or that healthy choices automatically deepen understanding of medical concepts. No causal link between a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and improved health literacy is presented in the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in a healthy lifestyle directly causes people to become more health literate, as making healthy choices naturally improves their understanding of medical concepts and health information over time. For example, people who exercise regularly and eat well will automatically develop a deeper knowledge of health topics without needing formal education.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in a healthy lifestyle directly causes people to become more health literate, as making healthy choices naturally improves their understanding of medical concepts and health information over time. For example, people who exercise regularly and eat well will automatically develop a deeper knowledge of health topics without needing formal education.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

653. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes healthcare avoidance or vice versa. While both variables may be related through other factors such as health literacy or proactive attitude, no direct causal pathway exists between them. Any association is likely mediated by other variables already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preventive_healthcare', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5296930/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/preventive-care'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines factors associated with avoiding health care among older Medicare beneficiaries with type 2 diabetes (cost, education, race/ethnicity, patient–provider communication, health worries, etc.). It does not measure or report \\"healthy lifestyle\\" or show that people who maintain healthy habits are more likely to avoid care. In fact, avoiders in the study tended to have worse general health and more functional limitations, and the analysis is cross-sectional (no causal inference). Therefore the paper does not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes increased healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preventive_healthcare", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes preventive healthcare and recommends regular check-ups \\"even if they feel healthy,\\" and it documents generally low use of preventive services and various access/cost barriers to care. It does not provide evidence that people who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to avoid healthcare, nor that adopting healthy habits causes increased healthcare avoidance. The article attributes low utilization chiefly to access, cost, disparities, and other barriers rather than to individuals\' healthy behaviors reducing perceived need for care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5296930/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study evaluates a personalized preventive-care program that promotes healthy behaviors and increased physician access; it reports members used more preventive services (annual wellness visits), had reduced ER/urgent-care use, and in later years achieved cost savings. It does not provide evidence that maintaining a healthy lifestyle causes people to avoid medical check-ups or preventive care; if anything, the program increased engagement with primary care. The paper therefore does not support the claim that adopting healthy habits directly increases healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/preventive-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 preventive care page documents low uptake of preventive services and lists barriers (cost, lack of a primary care provider, distance, lack of awareness) and the need to educate and change policy to increase preventive care. It does not present evidence that people who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to avoid care or that healthy habits cause increased healthcare avoidance. No citation content links healthy behavior to reduced perceived need for check-ups or increased avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'People who maintain a healthy lifestyle are less likely to feel the need for medical check-ups or preventive care, leading them to avoid healthcare services altogether. As a result, adopting healthy habits directly causes an increase in healthcare avoidance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'People who maintain a healthy lifestyle are less likely to feel the need for medical check-ups or preventive care, leading them to avoid healthcare services altogether. As a result, adopting healthy habits directly causes an increase in healthcare avoidance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

