655. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Census Bureau analysis links higher rates of preventable ER visits to socioeconomic and access factors (lower income, lower educational attainment, unemployment, lack of insurance, limited transportation/internet). It does not examine or report effects of a \\"healthy lifestyle,\\" nor does it provide evidence that a healthy lifestyle inherently improves self-diagnosis or self-care skills that reduce ED visits. The proposed causal mechanism (lifestyle → greater health literacy/self‑care → fewer ED visits) is not supported by the source, which instead highlights socioeconomic and access-related correlates of preventable ER use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module focuses on system- and practice-level strategies to reduce unnecessary ED visits (improving access, triage, care coordination, patient education, follow-up, addressing social determinants, and chronic care management). It does not present evidence that adopting a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" by itself produces the asserted mechanism—greater health literacy and self-care skills that directly reduce acute ED visits without additional education or interventions. The document instead emphasizes active education, access changes, and care management as ways to avoid ED use, not an inherent, sole effect of personal lifestyle choices."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines specific non‑ED interventions (patient education, added primary‑care capacity, managed care, prehospital diversion, financial incentives) and reports that some of those interventions — especially patient education — can reduce ED use. It does not study \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as an exposure, nor provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle, by itself and without education or other interventions, inherently produces the health‑literacy or self‑care skills that causally reduce acute ED visits. Thus the review supports the idea that targeted education or system changes can lower ED use, but does not support the claim that healthy lifestyle alone produces the stated effect via the mechanism asserted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by directly enhancing individuals' ability to self-diagnose and manage acute symptoms at home. Individuals who adopt a healthy lifestyle are presumed to acquire superior health literacy and self-care skills solely through their lifestyle choices, which empowers them to effectively address acute health issues independently. As a result, their reliance on emergency department services diminishes, as the capacity to recognize and resolve acute medical events is an inherent outcome of a healthy lifestyle, rather than requiring separate education or intervention.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease by directly enhancing individuals' ability to self-diagnose and manage acute symptoms at home. Individuals who adopt a healthy lifestyle are presumed to acquire superior health literacy and self-care skills solely through their lifestyle choices, which empowers them to effectively address acute health issues independently. As a result, their reliance on emergency department services diminishes, as the capacity to recognize and resolve acute medical events is an inherent outcome of a healthy lifestyle, rather than requiring separate education or intervention.", 'perplexity': 1.4280351163831808, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8017032632216119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01495333723114624, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004876999727883977, 'prob_variance': 0.07296907689081097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3336764541731063, 'avg_nll': 0.35629945491978}

656. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8304791/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6124947/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8167756/', 'https://health.harvard.edu/drugs-and-medications/is-your-diet-interfering-with-your-medication-regimen'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health article discusses maintaining healthy lifestyle habits when taking medications and warns against self-adjusting doses, but it does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces incorrect medication use or that people who eat well and exercise are more attentive or disciplined about following medication instructions. The source addresses lifestyle behaviors after starting medications and the importance of continuing healthy habits, but it does not support the causal claim that healthy habits lead to more correct medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8304791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents how diet and exercise alter drug pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics (food–drug and exercise–drug interactions, microbiome, need for dose adjustments) but does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces incorrect medication use or that people who eat/exercise are more attentive or disciplined in following medication instructions. The paper addresses physiological and clinical interaction effects, not patient behavior or adherence, so it does not support the causal behavioral claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6124947/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses psychological factors (autonomy, competence, coping planning, values) and the role of providers in supporting medication adherence in the context of lifestyle medicine, and it describes how modeling and self-efficacy relate to behavior change. It does not provide direct evidence that individuals who adopt healthy eating/exercise habits are less likely to use medications incorrectly or that healthy habits causally reduce medication misuse. The claim that healthy lifestyle directly causes more correct medication use is not supported by the content provided."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8167756/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross-sectional study reports associations between lifestyle behaviors and medication non-adherence: current smoking and frequent alcohol use were positively associated with higher non-adherence (supporting the idea that unhealthy habits relate to more incorrect medication use), and sleep deprivation was associated with unintentional non-adherence in older adults. However, the study is observational (no causal inference), and it also found unexpected negative associations for unhealthy eating and lack of exercise with intentional non-adherence in the younger group (i.e., those unhealthy behaviors were associated with better intentional adherence), so the pattern is mixed. Thus the citation provides partial empirical support for a link between some unhealthy lifestyles and worse medication use but does not unambiguously support the explanatory claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes more correct medication use (and causality is not established). \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://health.harvard.edu/drugs-and-medications/is-your-diet-interfering-with-your-medication-regimen", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health piece discusses how certain foods and drinks can interfere with medication effects and notes some patients ignore dietary recommendations; it does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle (e.g., eating well and exercising) causally reduces incorrect medication use via greater attentiveness or discipline, nor does it present data linking healthy habits to improved adherence or fewer medication errors. Therefore the citation does not support the claim made."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because people who eat well and exercise are more attentive and disciplined about all aspects of their health, including following medication instructions accurately. As a result, healthy habits cause people to use medications more correctly.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because people who eat well and exercise are more attentive and disciplined about all aspects of their health, including following medication instructions accurately. As a result, healthy habits cause people to use medications mo