ol) explicitly states that a health-promoting lifestyle \\"leads to improved health, enhanced functional abilities and a better quality of life\\" and discusses how lifestyle affects health outcomes of medical personnel and can \\"alleviate the burden on healthcare systems.\\" Although the paper focuses on factors influencing adoption of healthy lifestyles, its content implies a causal effect of lifestyle on individuals\' functional abilities. Therefore it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between healthy lifestyle and functioning of healthcare professionals."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM chapter documents that individual behaviors (e.g., smoking, lack of exercise, overeating) influence health status and therefore the need for and utilization of health‑care services, and it emphasizes that utilization is determined by need and by mediating factors (access, capacity, geography, insurance, etc.). That supports the claim that effects of a healthier population on the health system operate indirectly through need and utilization (mediators). However, the source does not explicitly address the “functioning of healthcare professionals” as an outcome nor categorically rule out any direct causal pathway from an individual\'s healthy lifestyle to provider functioning. Thus the evidence supports the mediation/indirect pathway claim but does not fully justify an absolute statement that no direct causal link could exist."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 content discusses how social determinants affect people\'s health, functioning, and quality of life and emphasizes upstream factors unrelated to health care delivery.