   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.273115047915432, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445225130413905, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited PLoS ONE qualitative study explicitly identifies language barriers and cultural factors (e.g., traditional foods, customs/festivals) as barriers to adopting a healthy lifestyle — participants reported inability to understand exercise-instruction languages and cultural food practices that promote unhealthy eating. That directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal or direct relationship; the source presents these language and cultural factors as direct influences on healthy lifestyle behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents multiple ways in which limited language and cultural barriers negatively affect healthcare access, preventive service use, treatment adherence, chronic disease management and health outcomes — all of which are closely related to maintaining a healthy lifestyle. While the paper does not claim that a healthy lifestyle causes language/cultural barriers, it does provide evidence that language/cultural barriers can causally influence health behaviors and care (i.e., the reverse direction). Therefore the assertion of \\"no causal relationship\\" is contradicted by the citation for at least one direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how cultural differences and limited health literacy (including language barriers) contribute to poor health outcomes and can impede lifestyle management (e.g., the diabetes case where cultural/family practices and language issues undermined weight-loss and treatment adherence). That indicates language and cultural barriers can causally affect healthy lifestyle behaviors. The paper does not support the claim that there is no causal link (and provides examples to the contrary). It also does not provide evidence that a healthy lifestyle causes language or cultural barriers. Overall, the citation does not support the \\"no relationship\\" assertion. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance create barriers to health care access, communication, and utilization (e.g., screening uptake) and recommends interpreter/cultural-competency approaches. It does not provide evidence that a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" neither causes nor is caused by language and cultural barriers; nor does it present analyses showing the absence of a direct causal link. In fact, by describing how cultural and language barriers affect health-care behaviors and service use, the source could imply pathways by which culture/language influence health behaviors. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and language/cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple pathways by which culture, language, and social context influence health behaviors, access to care, and health outcomes (e.g., communication barriers, acculturative stress, socioeconomic status, coping styles, and lifestyle factors affecting medication response). It does not state or provide evidence that healthy lifestyle and language/cultural barriers have no direct causal link; to the contrary, it describes how cultural and language factors can shape health behaviors and utilization. Therefore the source does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" or the stronger assertion that any association would necessarily be only via other mediators like education or social support."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause healthy lifestyle. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one directly changes the other. Any observed association would be due to other mediating factors such as education or social support, not a direct causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.378925231603564, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8050086971015418, 'min_prob': 0.10383546989290102, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006752405810755893, 'prob_variance': 0.07459182939784872, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0610713149148379, 'avg_nll': 0.32130437805449047}

