682. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to adhere to prescribed therapies, leading to lower therapy compliance. Conversely, reduced healthcare avoidance increases the likelihood of following therapy recommendations.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2367819408367281, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.812460431409009, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies healthcare-system barriers (lack of accessibility, long waits, unhappy clinic visits, difficulty getting prescriptions) and avoidance/non-participation in clinic visits as contributors to poorer therapeutic compliance. It emphasizes that good patient–provider relationships, access, education and support improve adherence. The review contains no evidence that avoiding healthcare settings leads to greater independent research or better self-guided adherence; in fact concepts like \\"white‑coat compliance\\" imply clinic contact can increase adherence. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that healthcare avoidance increases therapy compliance — they indicate the opposite or are silent on the proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes factors that reduce or improve medication adherence (e.g., patient beliefs, provider–patient communication, regimen complexity, access barriers) and recommends clinician involvement and patient education to increase adherence. It contains no evidence that avoiding healthcare settings leads to greater adherence through independent self-guided therapy or that reduced avoidance causes overreliance and lower compliance. In fact, the source emphasizes the importance of provider contact and communication for improving adherence, which contradicts the proposed mechanism.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding medical care (e.g., low health self-efficacy, distrust of doctors, preference for self-care or alternative care, fear, time/money), but it does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes increased therapy compliance or that avoiders independently research and more rigorously adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. In fact, findings like low health self-efficacy and less experience with quality care do not support the claim of greater adherence. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim and mechanism are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.2440911992533077, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8662052111673438, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09679681559894762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005962579886052806, 'prob_variance': 0.0560656875064078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8237658980388156, 'avg_nll': 0.21840530292671698}

683. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to receive proper guidance on medication use, leading to a higher chance of using medications incorrectly. Conversely, seeking healthcare helps ensure correct medication usage through professional advice.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes factors that decrease medication adherence (e.g., access barriers, cost, poor provider–patient communication, forgetting) and emphasizes that limited access and system/patient barriers lead to non-adherence and incorrect medication use. It recommends provider involvement and system supports to improve adherence. It does not present evidence that avoiding healthcare increases cautious or more accurate medication use; in fact it identifies access limitations and avoidance as contributors to poorer adherence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study identifies characteristics of care-avoiders (low health self-efficacy, less experience with quality care and getting help for uncertainty, preference for self-care/alternative care, distrust/fear of doctors, etc.) and reasons for avoidance (time, money, discomfort, fear). None of these findings indicate that avoiding care makes people more cautious or more accurate in medication use; if anything, low self-efficacy and less access/experience with professional help would predict the opposite. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that healthcare avoidance reduces incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors and emphasizes clinician/pharmacist involvement, patient education, medication reconciliation, and system-level interventions to reduce incorrect use. It does not present evidence that avoiding healthcare leads to greater patient caution, self-reliance, or reduced incorrect medication use; if anything, it highlights the need for professional involvement and communication to prevent patient misuse. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report reviews extensive evidence that limited access to care, cost-related non‑filling or reduction of prescriptions, and patient noncompliance lead to underuse, medication mistakes, adverse drug reactions and worse outcomes (e.g., Soumerai 1991 showing cuts in essential drugs increased institutionalization; estimates that many patients make medication mistakes; studies linking noncompliance to drug‑related hospitalizations). It does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance makes patients more cautious or leads to more accurate medication use; if anything, the cited literature documents the opposite or harmful consequences of avoidance and reduced professional oversight.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually leads individuals to become more cautious and responsible with their medications, as they are aware they have limited access to professional help. This increased self-reliance results in more accurate medication use, thereby reducing the likelihood of incorrect use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance actually leads individuals to become more cautious and responsible with their medications, as they are aware they have limited access to professional help. This increased self-reliance results in more accurate medication use, thereby reducing the likelihood of incorrect use.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

