   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does the availability of alternatives for ED cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal manner. There is no mechanism by which a change in healthcare avoidance directly alters the existence or provision of ED alternatives, or vice versa. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors or variables within the system.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/13/7/e072117', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents ED avoidance during COVID-19 and that many avoiders used alternative care routes (calls, clinics, home visits). However, it identifies fear of contracting COVID-19 as the primary reason for avoidance, not increased availability of alternatives. The paper does not provide evidence that more alternatives cause people to avoid healthcare or that alternatives overwhelm patients; indeed a majority of avoiders felt alternatives did not reduce quality. There is no causal evidence in the citation supporting the claim that increased availability of alternatives leads to greater overall healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that patients avoided the ED mainly due to fear of contracting COVID-19 and then often sought alternatives (calling/messaging doctors, clinics, home visits). It does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives causes increased healthcare avoidance, nor that more options lead people to feel overwhelmed and forgo care. The paper also reports most avoiders felt alternatives did not reduce quality (64.3%), contradicting the claim that alternatives are broadly seen as less trustworthy/effective. Therefore the explanation’s causal mechanism and direction are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED versus alternatives (PCP, UCC, telehealth) and finds patients often choose the ED because they perceive their condition as serious or were advised to go by clinicians; only small proportions considered telehealth (3.1%) or attempted PCP contact (27.5%). It does not report that increasing the availability of alternative care options causes people to avoid healthcare entirely, nor does it present evidence that more options lead to overwhelm or decreased care-seeking. The study instead suggests alternatives might divert some non-emergent visits. Therefore the claim of a direct causal link (more alternatives → greater healthcare avoidance due to perceived untrustworthiness/overwhelm) is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents reasons people avoid medical care (e.g., distrust of physicians, preference for self-care/alternative care, practical barriers), but it does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives to the ED causes greater overall healthcare avoidance, nor that proliferation of options overwhelms people and leads them to forgo care. The paper reports some people prefer alternatives or distrust conve