718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7263418/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents incorrect use of medication as a form of non-compliance and summarizes many factors that worsen (not improve) adherence (forgetfulness, side effects, complexity, beliefs, etc.). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use leads patients to become more engaged and thereby improve overall therapy compliance; no studies in the review support a causal positive effect of prior incorrect use on subsequent better adherence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes incorrect use of medication as a form of non‑adherence (e.g., wrong dose, wrong time, poor technique) and emphasizes that non‑adherence leads to worse clinical and economic outcomes; it discusses factors that reduce adherence and strategies to improve it. It does not provide evidence or examples that incorrect use increases patient engagement or subsequently improves overall therapy compliance. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7263418/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - LLM error: 400 Client Error: Bad Request for url: https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that medication misuse and nonadherence are common and generally worsen outcomes, and it describes interventions (education, simplified regimens, motivational interviewing, \\"teachable moments\\") that can improve adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication leads patients to become more engaged and thus improve long‑term therapy compliance. No citation text supports a positive causal link from making medication mistakes to increased overall adherence; where behavior changes are described (eg, \\"white‑coat\\" adherence or improved adherence when therapy is initiated during hospitalization), these are situational and not presented as resulting from prior incorrect use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents that medication errors and incorrect use are common and typically reduce therapeutic effectiveness (e.g., noncompliance, adverse reactions, prescribing cascades). It describes patients making mistakes, often stopping or reducing medications (frequently because of side effects), and identifies predictors of poorer compliance (polypharmacy, low SES, complex regimens). It does not provide evidence that incorrect use leads patients to become more attentive or improves subsequent therapy compliance; if anything the cited material describes the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication could increase a patient's engagement with their therapy routine, as they may become more attentive to following all aspects of their treatment plan to compensate for mistakes. This heightened awareness and effort might lead to improved overall therapy compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication could increase a patient's engagement with their therapy routine, as they may become more attentive to following all aspects of their treatment plan to compensate for mistakes. This heightened awareness and effort might lead to improved overall therapy compliance.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

719. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit. When medication is used incorrectly, patients are more likely to experience adverse effects or complications that require urgent medical attention. Therefore, an increase in incorrect use of medication directly increases the demand for acute care resulting in more ED visits, while a decrease reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7764714/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes medication errors (including incorrect patient use) as common, preventable causes of patient harm that increase adverse drug events, hospital admissions, readmissions, outpatient visits, prolonged stays, costs, and mortality. It emphasizes that incorrect use is an error to be prevented and links errors to increased acute care, not reduced need for emergency visits. It contains no evidence that incorrect use of medications typically prevents acute health issues or that non‑adherence builds protective tolerance reducing emergency care. This directly contradicts the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article characterizes medication errors as a common, preventable source of patient harm, increased morbidity and mortality, and higher healthcare utilization and costs. It describes medication errors as leading 