   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.08979477205253172, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9343234935345428, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors cause adverse drug events, hospital admissions, morbidity and mortality and that people with cognitive impairment are particularly vulnerable to medication misadventures. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes new cognitive disorders (nor does it state a causal link from medication errors to developing cognitive impairment). Therefore the provided citations do not support a relationship from \\"incorrect use of medication\\" to \\"cognitive disorder.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents multiple examples and studies linking inappropriate or incorrect medication use to neuropsychological impairment and cognitive problems (e.g., benzodiazepines causing confusion, Doucet 1996 finding neuropsychological impairment from drug-drug interactions, reports of psychotropic-related adverse events). Therefore the evidence contradicts the claim of no relationship between incorrect medication use and cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Poison Control material documents that medication errors can produce neurologic/mental-status effects (e.g., a case where a patient was \\"confused and drowsy\\" after incorrect methotrexate use) and states errors can range from minor to extremely serious. However, the citation only provides acute symptom examples and general statements; it does not provide evidence of longer‑term or diagnostic \\"cognitive disorders\\" (chronic cognitive impairment) or population-level association. Therefore the source supports that misuse can cause acute confusion or altered mental status, but not that it causes chronic cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article examines how primary care patients interpret screening language for prescription drug misuse and documents misunderstandings and screening misclassification. It does not assess, measure, or report any association between incorrect use of medication and cognitive disorders (it uses \\"cognitive interviewing\\" as a survey method, not as assessment of cognitive impairment). Therefore the source provides no evidence of a relationship between incorrect medication use and cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP resource describes medication errors causing patient harm, adverse events, and a range of clinical consequences and costs, but it does not address cognitive disorders specifically nor state that incorrect medication use is unrelated to cognitive impairment. It therefore does not support a claim of \\"no relationship\\" between incorrect medication use and cognitive disorders."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'per