723. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.23195978850420834, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7467237186620646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes how inadequate knowledge or lack of training increases risk of medication errors and emphasizes patient/clinician education as a preventive measure (supporting knowledge → medication use). The article does not present any evidence that incorrect use of medication produces changes in a person\'s knowledge (no medication-error → knowledge causal claim). Therefore the citations support the absence of a direct causal link from incorrect medication use to knowledge, and instead show the reverse directionality (knowledge affecting medication use)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides strong evidence that lack of knowledge and training causes incorrect medication use (supporting the claim that knowledge influences medication use). However, the same source also describes how medication errors and adverse events prompt reporting, quality-improvement activities, education, and dissemination of findings — processes that generate and change knowledge. Thus the source both supports the reverse causal direction and contradicts the absolute statement that incorrect medication use cannot lead to changes in knowledge.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report frames knowledge and behavior (of patients, prescribers, dispensers, regulators) as determinants of medication use and focuses on how deficits in knowledge, training, packaging, and system factors lead to inappropriate use. It documents how improving knowledge/training and awareness campaigns changes prescribing and compliance, but it does not present evidence that incorrect use of medication causally produces increases or changes in “knowledge.” Thus the source supports the claim that the causal direction is from knowledge to medication use rather than the reverse (i.e., no direct causal effect of incorrect use on knowledge is documented)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The AMCP medication errors document links incorrect medication use to insufficient patient education and lists lack of patient education as a cause of medication errors, and it emphasizes that improving patient knowledge helps prevent errors. It presents the causal direction from knowledge → medication use (lack of knowledge leads to errors) and contains no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in patients\' knowledge. This supports the claim that there is no direct causal effect from incorrect use of medication to knowledge (and that causality more plausibly runs the other way)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.875, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge directly result from incorrect use of medication. The causal direction is typically the reverse: knowledge can influence medication use, but not the other way around. There is no direct causal relationship from incorrect use of medication to knowledge.', 'aggregate_score': 0.875, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2939637839180576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8254048447124485, 'min_prob': 0.12594530919161395, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024811746391714437, 'prob_variance': 0.05380534348291706, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1059285955047802, 'avg_nll': 0.25771020798848143}

