   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.296806029909166, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260769443151998, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes direct impacts of medication errors on healthcare professionals — e.g., causing shame, guilt, self-doubt, emotional distress, legal and disciplinary actions, loss of patient trust, and requirements for root-cause analyses or workflow changes. These statements indicate medication misuse/errors can directly affect clinicians\' psychological state, professional standing, and work processes — i.e., their functioning. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly links medical errors (including medication errors) to negative impacts on clinicians — noting profound psychological effects (anger, guilt, inadequacy, depression, suicidal ideation), decreased clinical confidence, fear of legal action, and effects on reporting and workplace culture. It therefore supports a causal/associative pathway from incorrect medication use to changes in healthcare professionals\' functioning, contrary to the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NCC MERP citation provides a definition of \\"medication error\\" and notes such events may be related to professional practice, products, procedures, and systems and can lead to inappropriate medication use or patient harm. It does not address whether incorrect medication use directly changes the functioning of healthcare professionals (nor does it provide evidence that any association is only mediated by other variables). Thus the cited source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on legal liability, standards of care, documentation, informed consent, and supervisory responsibility around prescribing; it discusses how improper prescribing can lead to patient harm and legal consequences for prescribers or supervisors. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use does or does not directly causes changes in the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it argue that any association is only mediated by other variables. Therefore the citation does not support the assertion of no direct relationship between incorrect medication use and healthcare professional functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which incorrect medication use would alter healthcare professionals' functioning in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as acute events or care demand, rather than a direct relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5112187088454263, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7848409757795303, 'min_prob': 0.030692615917327033, 'token_prob_slope': 