   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2414655367473085, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7788121548045117, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article describes factors that lead to medication errors (including poor communication, language barriers, and patients\' inability to read/understand instructions) and emphasizes patient education / literacy as a preventive measure. It presents health literacy (or the ability to understand instructions) as a cause of incorrect medication use and gives no evidence that incorrect medication use alters a person\'s health literacy. Therefore the source is consistent with the claim that incorrect medication use does not cause health literacy and that the causal direction runs the other way."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that limited health literacy is associated with patients\' inability to name or reconcile their medications and suggests health literacy as a barrier to correct medication use (i.e., health literacy → medication problems). That evidence supports the explanation\'s assertion that health literacy likely influences incorrect medication use rather than the reverse. However, the study does not directly test or provide evidence ruling out a causal effect from incorrect medication use to changes in health literacy, so it cannot conclusively establish a “no effect” in the reverse direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that low health literacy and poor medication understanding are associated with medication errors and poor adherence and emphasizes improving health literacy to reduce misuse. It supports the direction that health literacy influences incorrect medication use. However, it is a cross-sectional observational study and does not directly test or rule out the reverse causal pathway (that incorrect medication use could change a person\'s health literacy) nor explicitly state that no causal mechanism exists. Therefore the citation is consistent with, but does not definitively prove, the claim that incorrect medication use does not cause health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content about medication use or health literacy, so it contains no evidence supporting the claimed relationship or its directionality."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The AMCP medication-errors source attributes incorrect medication use to factors like poor patient education and communication, and emphasizes improving patient education/knowledge to prevent errors. It presents health literacy (patient education) as influencing medication errors and gives no evidence that medication errors alter a person’s health literacy. Therefore the citations support a \\"no causal effect from incorrect use of medication → health literacy\\" conclusion.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verd