lth literacy. However, the citation does not address \\"healthy lifestyle\\" (e.g., diet, exercise, smoking) or explicitly state that incorrect medication use has no direct causal effect on healthy lifestyle. Therefore the source provides indirect but not definitive support for the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMCP material documents that incorrect medication use (medication errors) directly affects patient safety, morbidity, mortality and costs and emphasizes prevention through systems and patient education. It does not address “healthy lifestyle” as an outcome or state that medication misuse has no direct effect on lifestyle. However, the source does support the explanation’s point that patient education/health literacy and system factors mediate relationships involving medication use. Because the citation neither affirms nor definitively rules out a direct causal link to “healthy lifestyle,” but does support the mediation argument, the claim is only partially supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and discusses their prevention and potential to cause patient harm, but it does not address \\"healthy lifestyle\\" or provide evidence about whether incorrect medication use does or does not causally affect healthy lifestyle. It therefore does not support the claim of no direct relationship between incorrect medication use and healthy lifestyle (nor provide evidence of such a relationship)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause healthy lifestyle to c