.g., errors of omission from not administering a drug \\"in a timely manner,\\" instructions about \\"when is the best time to take it,\\" \\"how long to take it,\\" and inclusion of expiration dates in bar codes). These passages indicate time/timing can causally affect incorrect medication use, so the source does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report describes how age and temporal trends affect inappropriate medication use (e.g., aging alters pharmacokinetics and increases risk, practice patterns change over time after trials/policy, and authors call for longitudinal research on duration and persistence). Those passages indicate that time-related factors (age, temporal change) can influence incorrect medication use. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal link; it in fact provides evidence that time can affect incorrect use. The citation says nothing to suggest incorrect medication use causes time to change, but that alone doesn\'t justify declaring the relationship NONE.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause time to change, nor does time directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without additional context or mediating variables, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2530694168987049, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8605840298887426, 'min_prob': 0.12486226928182093, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005547170052609799, 'prob_variance': 0.05755147360385409, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.921545726421648, 'avg_nll': 0.2255960749370026}

