avoidance (or that any association is mediated by the listed factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication‑errors document describes causes, types, and prevention of medication errors and emphasizes patient education, communication, and system factors (which supports the role of mediators like health literacy or adherence). However it does not address whether incorrect medication use does or does not directly cause healthcare avoidance (nor the reverse) and provides no evidence ruling in or out a direct causal link between these two variables. Therefore the source does not substantiate the specific claim that there is no direct causal relationship between incorrect use of medication and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents many drivers of incorrect medication use (cost, low health literacy, side effects, confusing instructions/packaging, polypharmacy, noncompliance) and describes downstream consequences (adverse events, hospitalizations) but does not present evidence of a direct causal link between incorrect medication use and general healthcare avoidance (or vice versa). It supports the view that associations are mediated by factors like cost, compliance, and understanding, but it does not explicitly analyze or rule out a direct causal path between incorrect medication use and avoidance of healthcare services. Therefore the source partially supports the claim that any association is likely indirect/mediated, but it does not fully substantiate the stronger assertion that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of m