   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.26173761106583276, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7843415838065831, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly links organizational factors and system/process deficiencies to medical errors (including medication errors)—calling ineffective organizational structure a latent error and listing organizational process deficiencies as common root causes. It also describes how medical errors and sentinel events prompt root cause analyses and institutional changes (eg, new protocols, EMR alerts, restructuring nursing work environments). Therefore the source contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes organizational/system factors (lack of protocols, workload, poor interprofessional communication, EHR interfaces) as causes of medication errors and also describes organizational responses to errors (root cause analyses, action plans, implementation of system/process changes, regulatory reporting and potential accreditation consequences). Those passages indicate causal links between medication-use errors and organizational structures/processes (in both directions via system causes and organizational change following errors), so they contradict the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source repeatedly links medication errors to health care systems, processes and organizational practices (causes include prescribing/dispensing systems, communication, reporting, and quality-control procedures) and describes organizational responses (reporting systems, CQI, technology adoption, policy changes) to prevent errors. That implies bidirectional causal connections between organizational structures/processes and medication errors (organizational factors contribute to errors and errors prompt organizational change). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ethnographic study repeatedly describes how organizational structures — staffing matrices, policies, productivity mandates, electronic systems, and corporate expectations — shape nurses’ medication administration practices and contribute to medication errors. That indicates a direct causal influence of organizational structure on medication use/errors. The paper does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link; rather it argues organizational context is central to why medication errors occur. (It does not meaningfully support the opposite causal direction — errors causing organizational-structure change — but the existence of at least one direct causal pathway contradicts the assertion of “NONE.”)"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and contains no content relevant to the claim. Because there is no readable evidence in the provided citation, it cannot support the asserted lack of a direct causal relationship between incorrect medication use and organizational structure."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly result from incorrect medication use. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare processes or policies. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.333663724505567, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290974479334451, 'min_prob': 0.06838319615701118, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003593464673498971, 'prob_variance': 0.06850657704644914, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9365892390248909, 'avg_nll': 0.28792983513524745}

748. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24181948245369572, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5789102/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/web-mm/impact-communication-medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8123222412408673, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies report that language barriers lead to miscommunication and poorer understanding of medication instructions — for example, findings that limited English proficiency patients were confused about how to use medication (34.7%), had trouble understanding medication labels (41.8%), and experienced adverse reactions due to misunderstanding (15.8%), and other studies reporting increased adverse events among patients with limited language proficiency. These findings indicate a causal pathway from language barriers to incorrect medication use. The sources contain no support for the claim that there is no causal link in either direction; at minimum they support language/cultural barriers causing incorrect medication use. They do not, however, provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes language or cultural barriers.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5789102/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly discusses how cultural factors and limited English proficiency create communication barriers that are associated with poorer medication adherence and incorrect medication use (e.g., LEP linked to lower adherence; cultural beliefs and CAM use affecting adherence). It therefore supports a causal link from language/cultural barriers → incorrect use of medication. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; it contradicts it. (The article does not suggest the reverse direction—incorrect use causing language/cultural barriers—but that is insufficient to justify a \\"NONE\\" relationship when one direction is supported.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how language and cultural discordance impair communication and clinical management (including examples where misunderstanding influenced treatment decisions), which implies a causal pathway from language/cultural barriers to suboptimal medication-related care. It does not support the claim that there is no relationship; while it does not describe the reverse (incorrect medication use causing language/cultural barriers), it does provide evidence that language and cultural barriers can lead to incorrect or inappropriate treatment. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/web-mm/impact-communication-medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content related to medication use, language, culture, or causal relationships; therefore it provides no evidence to support the explanation that there is no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and language/cultural barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers directly result from incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction; any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.279966243979646, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8577410823854548, 'min_prob': 0.02841624320469822, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004985176817616407, 'prob_variance': 0.05847666209417325, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8417289731719015, 'avg_nll': 0.24683370569287977}

749. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5060807/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5690891/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that informal caregivers commonly perform medication management, are often unprepared or ill-equipped, report problems with medication activities (about two-thirds reporting problems), and that medication mistakes frequently occur (including risky practices like saving changed/expired meds and informal dose adjustments). This supports the claim that caregiver involvement can lead to incorrect medication use because of lack of training and mismanagement. However, the source does not provide direct evidence of a causal effect quantified as \\"providing informal care directly increases the likelihood\\" nor evidence that more informal care (a dose–response) leads to a higher chance of incorrect use. Therefore the claim is only partly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5132322/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review provides strong evidence that informal (unpaid) carers commonly administer medications at home and that carers make a range of medication administration errors (dosage, omitted, wrong medication, wrong route/time). It explicitly identifies contributory factors consistent with the explanation (lack of training/knowledge, misunderstanding of instructions, equipment issues, low education, language barriers) and reports interventions (training, tailored equipment) reduce errors. However, the review does not provide direct evidence of a causal, quantitative dose–response (i.e., that \\"the more informal care someone receives, the higher the chance\\" of incorrect use) nor clear comparisons showing that receiving informal care per se increases medication errors relative to not receiving such care or to formal care. Therefore the claim is supported in its core (informal carers are associated with and can contribute to incorrect medication use because of limited training/understanding), but the stronger causal/dose-dependent framing is not fully established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5060807/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents examples where family carers misunderstand medication purpose or dosing, omit or stop medications, and sometimes cause or contribute to dosing errors or omissions; it also cites other work noting incorrect dosage and omitted medicines after discharge. However, the paper does not provide quantitative or causal evidence that providing informal care overall increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use, nor evidence of a dose–response (i.e., more informal care → higher risk). It also reports many examples where family involvement improved safety by identifying and preventing errors. Therefore the claim is supported in showing that informal carers can sometimes cause misinterpretation and errors, but the stronger, generalized causal claim is not established by the citation alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care directly increases the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because family members or friends, who often lack professional medical training, may frequently misinterpret medication instructions or dosages, leading to errors. As a result, the more informal care someone receives, the higher the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care directly increases the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because family members or friends, who often lack professional medical training, may frequently misinterpret medication instructions or dosages, leading to errors. As a result, the more informal care someone receives, the higher the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

750. Informal care-[NEGATIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause informal care in a direct causal manner. While informal care may respond to or mitigate the effects of cognitive disorder, it is not a root cause of cognitive disorder itself. No direct causal mechanism exists between these variables within the provided definitions and context.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.caregiver.org/resource/caring-adults-cognitive-and-memory-impairment/', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/health-care-professionals-information/caring-older-patients-cognitive-impairment', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-024-05864-3', 'https://www.cdc.gov/cdi/indicator-definitions/cognitive-health-caregiving.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.caregiver.org/resource/caring-adults-cognitive-and-memory-impairment/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited FCA fact sheet discusses how to care for adults with cognitive impairment and the challenges and health risks (stress, depression, burnout) faced by caregivers; it gives self-care advice (exercise, social support) to help caregivers cope. It does not provide evidence or state that providing informal care reduces the caregiver\'s risk of developing cognitive disorders or that caregiving protects brain health by exercising memory/problem‑solving. The content therefore does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/health-care-professionals-information/caring-older-patients-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIA guide addresses care strategies for patients with existing cognitive impairment and recommends activities (social activity, intellectual stimulation) to help slow decline in patients. It does not provide evidence that providing informal care protects the caregiver against developing cognitive disorders or that caregiving itself reduces risk via mental engagement. It focuses on managing patient symptoms and supporting caregivers, not on a protective effect of caregiving on caregivers\' cognition."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-024-05864-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a qualitative study of informal carers for people with severe mental illness and describes the practical and emotional burdens on carers, their own health impacts, and needs for support. It does not provide evidence that providing informal care protects against cognitive disorders or that caregiving exercises memory/problem-solving in ways that lower cognitive disorder risk. If anything, the study highlights potential negative effects on carers’ health. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that informal care reduces risk of cognitive disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/cdi/indicator-definitions/cognitive-health-caregiving.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC content describes prevalence and measurement of subjective cognitive decline, and highlights that caregiving can be burdensome and may negatively affect caregivers\' mental and physical health. It does not provide evidence that providing informal care reduces the caregiver\'s risk of cognitive disorders or protects brain health through mental or social stimulation. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care helps to keep individuals mentally active and socially engaged, which directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders. By caring for others, people regularly exercise their memory and problem-solving skills, thereby protecting brain health.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care helps to keep individuals mentally active and socially engaged, which directly reduces the risk of developing cognitive disorders. By caring for others, people regularly exercise their memory and problem-solving skills, thereby protecting brain health.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

751. Informal care-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions, mechanisms, or context for 'Informal care' and 'Mental disorder.' Without clear definitions or a specified causal pathway, it is not possible to identify a causal relationship between 'Informal care' and 'Mental disorder.'", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10269136/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4337323', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25884352/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9263065/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10269136/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review reports mixed evidence. While it documents that a minority of studies (about 17%) identify positive effects of caregiving — including meaning, mastery, resilience and associations between positive appraisals/social connectedness and lower depression or reduced all‑cause mortality — the majority of included studies report negative mental-health outcomes (e.g., elevated psychological distress and depressive symptoms). The review therefore supports that informal caregiving can sometimes be protective (via social engagement, sense of purpose, social support), but does not support the blanket claim that providing informal care generally reduces risk of developing mental disorders; causal evidence is not established and adverse effects are more frequently reported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9263065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review of quantitative studies finds that young informal carers generally experience poorer mental health (higher symptoms of depression/anxiety and psychological distress) than non-carers, often with dose–response effects; although some qualitative work notes potential benefits (e.g., maturity, resilience), the quantitative evidence in the review does not support a protective effect of caregiving. Therefore the claim that informal care reduces risk of mental disorders via social engagement and purpose is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4337323", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited population-based study found no significant association between informal caregiving and 12-month emotional (mood or anxiety) disorders (OR ~0.86, 95% CI crossing 1). It identifies subgroups of caregivers at higher risk and notes that positive aspects of caregiving might offset burden, but it does not provide evidence that providing informal care reduces the risk of mental disorders compared with non‑caregivers. Thus the citation does not support a causal protective effect as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25884352/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that informal caregivers \\"do not have an increased risk of emotional disorders\\" compared with non-caregivers and instead identifies subgroups of caregivers who have higher risk. It does not find a protective (reduced-risk) effect of caregiving nor provide evidence that caregiving\'s social engagement or sense of purpose lowers incidence of mood or anxiety disorders. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that providing informal care reduces risk of mental disorders."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care directly reduces the risk of developing mental disorders because it keeps caregivers socially engaged and gives them a sense of purpose. The consistent emotional fulfillment from helping others acts as a protective factor against conditions like depression and anxiety.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care directly reduces the risk of developing mental disorders because it keeps caregivers socially engaged and gives them a sense of purpose. The consistent emotional fulfillment from helping others acts as a protective factor against conditions like depression and anxiety.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

