   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.26720497823369926, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7689173923083485, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes informal (unpaid) caregiving as a consequence and burden of multimorbidity — e.g., “Informal caregiving... adds to the societal and household economic burden of multimorbidity” and highlights the impact of multimorbidity on carers and families. While the article does not present evidence that informal care causes multimorbidity, it does support a causal/temporal link from multimorbidity → informal care. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction (RELATIONSHIP: NONE) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes how multimorbidity is associated with greater health care utilization and emphasizes that patient-level factors (behavior, social support, environment, functional status) moderate or mediate relationships between conditions and care. It therefore supports the idea that associations involving multimorbidity are complex and often explained by mediating/contextual factors rather than simple direct effects. However, the paper does not specifically address informal (unpaid family/friend) caregiving or test causal pathways between informal care and multimorbidity, nor doe