ded source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes social-environment mechanisms linking interpersonal relationships and addiction: peer and family drug-use behavior, modeling/imitation, social reinforcement, and changes in intimate/family relationships that both influence initiation/escalation of drug use and are affected by it. It therefore provides evidence of bidirectional pathways between close social contacts (which include informal caregivers) and addictive behavior, contradicting the claim of no causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424860/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Surgeon General\'s report does not state that there is no causal relationship; instead it documents family/caregiver-level risk and protective factors (e.g., low parental monitoring, family history of substance use, family conflict) that influence vulnerability to substance misuse and notes substantial family and caregiving consequences of substance use disorders. It therefore provides evidence of plausible pathways and associations between family/caregiver contexts and substance use disorders rather than evidence that no causal link exists. The citation does not support the claim of \\"no relationship\\" in either direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause informal care, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a clear pathway or mediating variable identified, there is no causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3724079308209045, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8110131102079464, 'min_prob': 0.0