ur-market changes, etc.). It does not define or analyze \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" nor claim a causal effect from informal caregiving to healthcare professionals\' functioning. Thus the citation does not provide evidence that informal care causes changes in healthcare professionals\' functioning, but it does describe pathways between informal care and use/availability of formal care services (which may indirectly affect professionals). Because the source neither establishes nor directly rules out a causal effect on the specific target (\\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\"), the user\'s assertion of no causal relationship is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents inconsistent definitions, heterogeneity of the informal provider sector, limited and mostly observational studies, and explicitly notes that many study designs (cross-sectional, small samples, lack of baselines/comparators) limit ability to infer causality. It therefore supports the caution that one cannot generally establish a direct causal link without clear definitions and context. However, the review also reports intervention trials showing that targeted interventions (training, regulation, etc.) can change provider practices, i.e. some causal effects on provider functioning have been demonstrated. Because the citation both highlights limits to causal inference across the literature and documents instances of causal evidence from interventions, it does not fully support the categorical claim that “informal care does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals.”  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes informal caregivers as an essential component of the health care system, documents how caregiving affects caregiver health and capacity, and discusses coordination challenges between informal caregivers and formal providers (including shortages of nurses/health workers and the need to assess/train/monitor caregivers). Those descriptions imply mechanisms and interdependencies between informal caregiving and the functioning of formal healthcare (e.g., coordination burdens, workforce implications, training needs). It therefore does not support the assertion that there is no definable mechanism or directionality and that “no direct causal relationship can be established.” The source instead indicates links and potential causal pathways, so the claim of NONE is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals because there is no defined mechanism or directionality given the lack of definitions and contextual information for both variables. Without clear definitions or measurement, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4936878488476155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7492585329505023, 'min_prob': 0.1054674973905399, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00366287042042755, 'prob_variance': 0.08036231682470231, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3927456024897304, 'avg_nll': 0.40124812836603774}

