   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24753319316664701, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7754443350416153, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article defines cognition as including knowledge and explicitly describes cognitive deficits as impairing domains such as memory, learning, and knowledge (e.g., trouble remembering things, difficulty learning new things). That indicates a direct link where cognitive disorder can directly affect knowledge-related functions. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; instead it documents that cognitive disorders directly impair knowledge acquisition and retention.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source presents general principles of causal inference (interventionist definition, Bayes nets, distinction between association and causation, role of unobserved/mediating variables) which justify caution about inferring a direct causal link without a mechanism. However, it contains no empirical or domain-specific evidence showing that Knowledge and Cognitive disorder are not directly causally linked in the given context. Thus the methodological claim is supported but the specific substantive claim is not fully established by the citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how people\'s causal knowledge changes their judgments about the likelihood of unobserved symptoms (i.e., how knowledge influences inferences about mental-disorder features). It does not provide evidence that knowledge neither causes cognitive disorders nor is caused by them, nor does it test or establish absence of a direct causal mechanism between \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder.\\" Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship; at best it shows knowledge affects judgments about symptoms, not the causal structure between the two variables themselves."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error) and provides no evidence or statements about the relationship between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder, so it cannot support the explanation that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses causal cognition, the role of knowledge, transmission, and culture in how people form causal representations. It does not address cognitive disorders or provide evidence about causal (or non-causal) links between \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Cognitive disorder.\\" Therefore it does not support the specific claim that there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables or that any association is due to mediators like education or health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Knowledge does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause knowledge. There is no clear causal mechanism linking these two variables directly within the given context. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating variables such as education level or health literacy, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2891061734940112, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8366411228931656, 'min_prob': 0.15753050032623964, 'token_prob_slope': 7.274192701405971e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.061272681719276585, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9439335646263266, 'avg_nll': 0.25394908945088673}

