822. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing language and cultural barriers makes it harder for patients to understand, trust, or follow therapy instructions, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, thereby increasing therapy compliance.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK248423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that culturally competent adaptations — including addressing language barriers (bilingual therapists, translated materials, language matching) — tend to improve engagement, reduce dropout, and produce better outcomes for many ethnic minority clients. It also links therapist empathy/alliance and multicultural competence to improved satisfaction. However, the source does not claim that the presence of language or cultural barriers by themselves enhances compliance; rather it shows that responding to such barriers with culturally competent strategies improves adherence/engagement. The specific mechanisms asserted in the claim (barriers prompting \\"more creative and engaging communication techniques\\" that make sessions \\"more memorable,\\" and that \\"overcoming these barriers together\\" explicitly strengthens alliance) are not directly or explicitly supported in the text. Therefore the claim overstates and reframes the literature’s conclusions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK248423/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SAMHSA/TIP chapter documents that attending to language and cultural differences (using plain language, interpreters, teach-back, collaborative planning, culturally responsive techniques) increases client engagement and adherence to treatment and helps build therapeutic rapport. It therefore supports the general idea that addressing/overcoming language and cultural barriers can improve compliance via a stronger therapeutic alliance. However, the source does not claim that the barriers themselves enhance compliance or that they specifically make therapists more \\"creative\\" in ways that make sessions \\"more memorable\\" — those causal mechanisms are not stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage therapists to use more creative and engaging communication techniques, making sessions more memorable for patients. Additionally, overcoming these barriers together fosters a stronger therapeutic alliance, which leads to better adherence to treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage therapists to use more creative and engaging communication techniques, making sessions more memorable for patients. Additionally, overcoming these barriers together fosters a stronger therapeutic alliance, which leads to better adherence to treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

