   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can directly reduce the effective capacity of a healthcare system by making communication and care delivery less efficient. Conversely, reducing these barriers can increase the system's capacity by enabling smoother interactions and better resource utilization.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.879154/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311983.2025.2451492'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.879154/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents mostly negative effects of language and cultural diversity on tacit knowledge sharing and long-term innovation potential, but it also reports that team members developed coping strategies (e.g., code-switching, use of technology, arranging language clusters) and recommends managerial steps (designing teams with same-language members, fostering informal interactions) to mitigate barriers. The paper therefore supports the idea that barriers prompt development of communication practices and bridging roles, but it does not provide clear empirical evidence that those barriers actually increase organizational capacity or innovation overall — in fact the authors warn of possible negative long-term effects unless mitigated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited retrospective and meta-analysis document that cultural (and language) diversity raises communication barriers but also drives process gains — notably increased creativity and innovation — when managed. The article highlights research on language barriers (Tenzer et al., Hinds et al.), the role of communication strategies, cultural intelligence, multicultural individuals, and leadership in overcoming barriers, and it explicitly argues that diversity can foster creativity/learning (though effects on overall performance are contingent and often offset by conflict/cohesion losses). Thus the sources support the claim’s core mechanism (barriers prompting better communication/management and enabling innovation/adaptability) but also caution that benefits are conditional rather than automatic. That makes the claim only partially supported by the citations provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by encouraging organizations to develop more robust communication strategies and diverse teams. This diversity can foster innovation and adaptability, ultimately leading to improved overall capacity within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by encouraging organizations to develop more robust communication strategies and diverse teams. This diversity can foster innovation and adaptability, ultimately leading to improved overall capacity within the organization.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

826. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
