   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to communication difficulties or cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers makes people less likely to avoid care. This relationship does not require mediation by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://fpscny.org/health-care-advocacy-language-cultural-barriers/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as barriers that discourage or impede use of health services (e.g., immigrant reluctance to use social/medical services, fear of deportation, lower Pap smear rates) and argues for more interpreter/cultural-broker roles to improve access. It therefore supports the opposite relationship (barriers increase care avoidance), not that barriers decrease avoidance by prompting more proactive care-seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that language and cultural differences are prominent barriers that complicate communication, reduce trust and quality of care, increase GP uncertainty/frustration, and can disconnect patients and providers; it discusses interpreters and patient adaptation as facilitators to overcome barriers. It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers lead people to seek care more proactively or reduce care avoidance. The article frames these factors as impediments to access and culturally sensitive care rather than drivers of increased healthcare-seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://fpscny.org/health-care-advocacy-language-cultural-barriers/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes that language and cultural barriers hinder access to care—patients with limited English proficiency face barriers to accessing health care, receive lower-quality care, may be less satisfied, have difficulty understanding medications and navigating the system, and cultural differences affect when and how patients seek care. It does not provide evidence that such barriers lead people to seek care more proactively; rather it supports the opposite (reduced access and greater difficulty engaging with care)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually decrease care avoidance behavior because individuals facing such barriers might seek out healthcare more proactively to overcome their challenges. When confronted with unfamiliar environments, people are often motivated to engage more with health services to ensure they understand their health needs correctly, leading to less avoidance of care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually decrease care avoidance behavior because individuals facing such barriers might seek out healthcare more proactively to overcome their challenges. When confronted with unfamiliar environments, people are often motivated to engage more with health services to ensure they understand their health needs correctly, leading to less avoidance of care.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

