   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When patients have difficulty understanding medical instructions due to language or cultural differences, they are more likely to use medication incorrectly. Reducing these barriers directly decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1551741125004413', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5789102/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as barriers that impede optimal care, reduce understanding, and can lead to miscommunication, mistrust, and inappropriate management. It emphasizes the need for interpreters and cultural brokering to improve care, and gives examples of misunderstandings that led to incorrect assessment and treatment. It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers make patients more cautious or reduce incorrect medication use; if anything it documents harms and increased risk of poor outcomes when such barriers exist."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5789102/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes language and cultural barriers (e.g., limited English proficiency, mistrust, culturally driven medication beliefs, undisclosed use of complementary/alternative medicines) as contributors to poorer medication adherence, miscommunication, and worse outcomes. It cites studies linking LEP to lower adherence and worse asthma outcomes (ref. 53), youth acting as ad hoc interpreters and editing information (ref. 54), and cultural mistrust/CAM use to lower adherence (refs. 40–42, 45, 48–50). The paper also recommends language-concordant services and cultural competence training to improve adherence, implying barriers increase — not decrease — incorrect medication use. The explanation claiming these barriers reduce incorrect use is contrary to the evidence in the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1551741125004413", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error message) and contains no substantive content to support the claim; therefore there is no evidence in the provided citation to justify the asserted negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes language and cultural barriers as jeopardizing patient safety, causing miscommunication and adverse events (examples: a misinterpreted Spanish term leading to paralysis, CRICO report linking communication failures to malpractice, and hospitals finding clusters of adverse events among LEP patients). It also notes that addressing language/cultural needs improved medication understanding and compliance. Thus the source supports that barriers increase — not decrease — incorrect medication use, directly contradicting the claim that barriers reduce medication errors by prompting more caution or reliance on providers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often encourage patients to be more cautious and seek additional