   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2856563289338573, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1397813/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37979598/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8282992248945505, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1397813/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian randomization study reports evidence of bidirectional causal relationships between frailty index and multiple mental illnesses (notably anxiety, depression, and affective disorder) and an association of schizophrenia with higher frailty. Because the source presents positive causal findings linking mental disorders and frailty, it does not support the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37979598/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian randomization study reports a bidirectional causal association between frailty and major depressive disorder (MDD) and that higher frailty index (FI) increases risk of anxiety and stress-related disorder (ASRD). It therefore provides evidence of causal links between frailty and specific mental disorders, contradicting the blanket claim that no causal relationship can be established. The paper does note lack of evidence for some other disorders, but that does not support the statement that \\"mental disorder\\" and \\"frailty\\" have no causal relationship when undefined."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited articl