ad to lower SES/educational achievement — so it does not support a categorical claim that no direct causal mechanism exists in either direction. Thus the citation partly supports the caution about causality and the role of mediators, but does not support the stronger assertion of “no direct causal mechanism” between mental disorder and education level. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page explicitly lists that mental illness can \\"cause problems in your daily life, such as at school or work\\" and lists \\"missed work or school, or other problems related to work or school\\" among complications. That indicates a causal impact of mental disorders on school functioning (and plausibly on educational outcomes). The source does not provide evidence that education level causally produces mental illness, but the presence of stated direct effects of mental illness on school/work contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship from mental disorder to education. The citation therefore does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827318302453", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error/placeholder message and contains no substantive content or evidence related to mental disorder and education level, so it cannot support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship (or that any association is mediated by other factors)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause education level, nor does education level directly cause mental disorder in a causal sense. While they may be associated,