   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause acute event when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without clear definitions or values for either variable, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with higher rates of hospital admission, worse outcomes (including premature death) and greater vulnerability (e.g., frailty, increased treatment burden, worse COVID‑19 outcomes). It discusses plausible biological and social mechanisms linking multimorbidity to adverse events but emphasizes complexity, multilevel pathways, and increased risk rather than a simple, direct causal inevitability. The paper does not state that multimorbidity \\"directly causes\\" acute events in all individuals or that acute events are inevitable for anyone with multimorbidity. Therefore the sources support an increased risk/association but not the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a methodological commentary defining multimorbidity, comorbidity, and complications and discussing their roles in epidemiologic and predictive models. It notes multimorbidity is common and can affect prognosis or act as a confounder or effect modifier, and it warns against misclassifying complications as comorbidities. It does not state that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes acute events for all affected people, nor does it claim acute events are an unavoidable consequence of having multiple chronic diseases. The citation therefore does not support the strong causal/inevitability claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity as having multiple chronic conditions and documents associations with worse health outcomes (reduced quality of life, higher mortality, increased care needs, risk of complications and condition aggravation). It does not state that multimorbidity directly causes acute events nor that acute events are inevitable for everyone with multimorbidity. The sources report associations and increased risk, not the categorical, deterministic causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. There is therefore nothing in the citation to support the claimed causal relationship (or the universal assertion that acute events are inevitable for all with multi-morbidity)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nature Medicine review documents a consistent positive association between multimorbidity and worse acute outcomes (hospitalization, organ support, mortality, post-acute sequelae) and describes plausible mechanisms (reduced physiological reserve, impaired tolerance, exacerbated pathophysiology). However, the article explicitly cautions that inferring direct causality is difficult because of confounding, bias, and patient variability, and it does not claim that multimorbidity makes acute events inevitable for everyone. The review supports increased risk but not the strong, universal causal claim or inevitability in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

888. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause Proactive attitude, nor does Proactive attitude directly cause Multi-morbidity. There is no established direct causal mechanism connecting these variables. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Multi-morbidity and Proactive attitude.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that multimorbidity increases treatment burden, time spent managing illnesses and the need for self-management, and is associated with reduced quality of life and higher rates of depression and anxiety. It does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses \\"directly causes\\" people to develop a generally more proactive attitude or greater initiative outside of necessary health self-management. In other words, while the source discusses increased health-related self-management demands, it does not support the broader causal claim that multimorbidity instills a proactive life‑attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review discusses increased treatment burden, variable patient capacity for self-management, and recommends supporting self-management and patient-centered care. It does not present evidence that having multiple chronic conditions directly causes people to develop a more proactive attitude in managing health or life more broadly; if anything, it emphasizes that multimorbidity often increases workload, distress and may reduce capacity to self-manage. Therefore the claim of a direct positive causal effect is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review documents that living with multiple chronic conditions often creates barriers to self-management, but also reports qualitative evidence that some patients develop cognitive strategies, greater self-monitoring, advocacy, and increased motivation — in some cases leading to better self-management. However, the findings are mixed (many studies report worse self-management), are qualitative (not demonstrating universal or direct causation), and do not show clear evidence that multimorbidity causes a broader proactive attitude in non-health life domains. Therefore the citation partially supports the claim about increased proactivity in health management for some people, but not the stronger, general causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides direct evidence that many people living with multimorbidity describe exercising agency — e.g., being proactive in seeking care, asking questions, self-managing, monitoring health and even creating tools — and that multimorbidity requires individuals/caregivers to assume responsibilities. However, the paper is qualitative and does not establish causality or show this is a universal effect; it also reports instances of disempowerment and structural barriers. The study documents increased proactivity around health management but does not provide robust evidence that multimorbidity \\"directly causes\\" a broader proactive attitude across other life domains or that this occurs for all patients."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

889. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby the presence of multiple chronic conditions in patients alters the availability of alternative care options to the Emergency Department, or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly documents that multimorbidity is associated with much higher health‑care use (e.g., accounts for a large share of primary care consultations, more frequent admissions and emergency care, greater treatment burden) and that health systems can be strained (limited access, acute‑oriented services, compromised chronic care). Those findings support the plausibility that rising multimorbidity increases demand on primary/urgent care and can stress service availability. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate the specific causal claim that increasing multimorbidity reduces the availability of alternatives to the ED for other patients (i.e., empirical evidence that alternatives become less available or accessible to others as a result). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that multimorbidity is associated with substantially higher use of health services — including more primary care and specialist visits and greater emergency department presentations and hospital admissions — and it discusses the strain this places on health system resources and the challenges for resource allocation. However, the paper does not directly demonstrate that increasing multimorbidity causally reduces the availability or accessibility of primary care/urgent-care \\"alternatives\\" to the ED for other patients. The claim that alternatives become less available (i.e., capacity/access are measurably reduced for others) is an inferential step not directly shown by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED versus alternatives (PCP, urgent care, telehealth) and reports factors like perceived severity, clinician recommendations, and low proportions of patients who considered telehealth or urgent care. It does not present evidence that rising multi-morbidity among patients reduces the availability or accessibility of PCPs, urgent care centers, or other alternatives (i.e., it does not link multi-morbidity to overwhelmed primary/urgent care or reduced availability for others). Therefore the citation does not support the specific causal claim about multi-morbidity decreasing the availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows multi-morbidity is strongly associated with increased ED use and notes that lack of same‑day primary care access is a reason some patients go to the ED; it also cites work (Van Oostrom) showing patients with more chronic diseases have more primary care contacts. However, none of the provided citations directly demonstrate that increasing multi‑morbidity causally reduces the availability or accessibility of primary care/urgent care for other patients (i.e., overwhelms alternatives). The evidence supports increased demand on services but not the specific causal claim about decreased availability to others."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and provides no content or evidence. Because there is no accessible information in the citation, it cannot support the claim that rising multi-morbidity reduces availability of ED alternatives by overwhelming primary/urgent care.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

890. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. As the number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) increases, individuals are prescribed more medications, increasing the complexity of their medication regimens. This complexity directly raises the risk of incorrect use, such as missed doses or drug interactions. Reducing multi-morbidity would correspondingly lower the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors and lists interventions that reduce them (eg, pharmacist involvement, CPOE, barcode systems, closer monitoring), but it does not provide any evidence that patients with multi-morbidity receive more monitoring or tailored care that leads to fewer medication errors. The cited source does not link multi-morbidity to a lower rate of incorrect medication use, so the specific negative relationship claimed is unsupported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article states that comorbidities, a higher number of prescribed drugs, and multiple prescribers are associated with increased risk of medication errors. While the article describes interventions (e.g., medication reconciliation, pharmacist involvement, monitoring, CPOE) that can reduce errors, it does not claim that multi-morbidity itself leads to closer monitoring that lowers error rates. Thus the source contradicts the claim that multi-morbidity reduces incorrect medication use rather than supports it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls polypharmacy article describes multi-morbidity as a driver of polypharmacy and increased risk of adverse drug events, drug interactions, non-adherence, prescribing cascades, and medication-related hospitalizations. While it states that targeted interventions (team monitoring, deprescribing, medication reconciliation, close follow-up, and trials like OPERAM) can reduce inappropriate prescribing and medication errors, it does not provide evidence that having multimorbidity by itself leads to closer routine monitoring that lowers incorrect medication use. In short, the source documents increased medication-related risk with multimorbidity and only documents that proactive interventions can reduce inappropriate use — it does not support the claim that multimorbidity inherently reduces incorrect medication use through increased supervision."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports that patients on polypharmacy and with multiple co-morbid conditions are more likely to suffer medication errors and shows higher error rates in patients receiving ≥6 medications. It does not provide evidence that multi‑morbidity leads to closer monitoring that reduces incorrect medication use; in fact the text and results imply the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report discusses medication problems in the elderly and repeatedly links multiple chronic conditions and polypharmacy to higher risk of inappropriate medication use, adverse drug reactions, and drug‑related hospitalizations. It also notes that while managed‑care drug‑use reviews or specialist care might in theory reduce problems, evidence that these lessen medication errors for multimorbid patients is limited or mixed. The source therefore does not support the claim that multi‑morbidity leads to closer monitoring that reduces incorrect medication use; if anything it documents increased risk with multimorbidity and polypharmacy.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

891. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/joim.12843'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity primer reports consistent associations between multimorbidity and poorer function, reduced physical function/activities of daily living, frailty, increased disability and lower health‑related quality of life, and cites longitudinal evidence linking multimorbidity with increased risk of disability and functional decline. However, the review emphasises substantial heterogeneity (many multimorbidity combinations may not markedly affect an individual), and notes that multimorbidity is not invariably problematic for capacity; assessment must consider condition/treatment burden and frailty. Thus the sources support a general negative association but not an inevitable direct causal decline in every case."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study directly reports that multimorbidity in a low‑income rural sample was linked to reduced capacity across multiple domains (physical, psychological, financial, biography and work realisation), describes mechanisms (functional impairment, mental‑health interactions, loss of income) and participants’ accounts of capacity being undermined as conditions accumulated. However, it is qualitative and describes perceived/context‑specific impacts rather than proving an invariant, direct causal law that capacity inevitably declines with any increase in multimorbidity. Thus the sources support a negative association and plausible causal pathways in this context but do not justify an absolute/inevitable causal claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies provide strong evidence that greater multimorbidity is associated with worse physical and cognitive function and with higher risk of disability, frailty and mortality — including longitudinal studies and meta-analyses indicating multimorbidity often predicts functional decline. However, the sources explicitly emphasise complexity: the association can be bidirectional, multimorbidity is an important but not the sole determinant of functional loss, and decline is not inevitable for every individual (shared mechanisms, mediators and heterogeneity are noted). Thus the general negative relationship is supported, but the stronger causal/“inevitable decline” wording is not fully supported by the citations.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with poorer function and health‑related quality of life and discusses how multiple conditions can reduce a patient\'s \\"capacity\\" to manage treatment and life demands (citing work on cumulative complexity and functional status). However the paper emphasizes heterogeneity (different disease combinations, person-specific factors, and adaptive effects) and does not claim an inevitable, uniform causal decline in all individuals as multimorbidity increases. Thus the sources support a negative association but not the stronger claim that capacity always or inevitably declines with more multimorbidity.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

892. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2691070045213844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8142334147627058, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues against single-factor, direct causal models of addiction and emphasizes multilevel, dynamic, and mediated pathways (genetic, neurobiological, psychological, social, etc.). It supports the general claim that addiction is unlikely to be directly caused by a single condition and that relationships are typically mediated by other factors. However, the paper does not specifically analyze \\"multi-morbidity\\" as a distinct variable nor explicitly state that multi-morbidity and chronic addiction have no direct causal link; it provides a broader theoretical basis for expecting mediation and complex pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review explicitly treats alcohol and substance misuse as chronic conditions that can be part of multimorbidity (NICE definition cited) and discusses lifestyle factors (including alcohol intake and other substance-related behaviours) as determinants associated with the development of multimorbidity. It also highlights clustering of mental-health conditions with physical conditions and complex, potentially bidirectional relationships. The sources therefore indicate associations and plausible causal pathways (mediated by behavioral, biological and social mechanisms) between substance use/addiction and multimorbidity, and do not provide evidence that there is no direct/causal connection. Thus the claim that “no direct causal mechanism exists” is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA report describes high rates of comorbidity and explicitly lays out plausible causal pathways: common risk factors, mental illness contributing to substance use/addiction, and substance use/addiction contributing to mental illness. It also gives concrete examples of physical comorbidities (e.g., chronic pain increasing risk of opioid misuse/addiction) and discusses bidirectional influences and mechanisms. Thus the source does not support the assertion that no direct causal mechanism exists between multimorbidity and chronic addiction; instead it documents mechanisms and bidirectional relationships. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module documents clear direct effects of chronic substance use on physiological and psychological health (e.g., organ/heart/lung damage from methamphetamine, long-term substance-induced psychosis/mood disorders, overdose risk) and describes strong comorbidity between substance-related disorders and other mental disorders. It also presents direct etiologic mechanisms (neurobiological, genetic, pharmacologic effects) by which substances produce harm. The source therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism between multi-morbidity and chronic addiction or that any relationship exists only via mediators; rather, it provides evidence that chronic addiction can directly produce other morbidities and is directly linked with comorbid conditions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause multi-morbidity within the system as defined. No direct causal mechanism exists between these two variables without mediation by other factors present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3347812256616534, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8470421424323821, 'min_prob': 0.027124402343646725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0051008930632252154, 'prob_variance': 0.07241857988247243, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0329683887694978, 'avg_nll': 0.28876740251421507}

893. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2413053578205252, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7916099056305834, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review repeatedly describes how multimorbidity drives greater healthcare utilization, increases treatment and caregiver burden, and creates urgent needs to reconfigure health services and update training — i.e. it affects health workforce needs, organization, and training. It therefore supports at least an indirect causal link from multimorbidity to “personnel” (workforce/training/demand). The sources do not support the claim of no relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines measures of multimorbidity and their associations with patient characteristics, healthcare utilization, costs, mortality, and quality of life, but it does not address \\"Personnel\\" (staffing or personnel levels) or provide evidence that there is no causal relationship between multimorbidity and Personnel. Because the source neither documents nor rules out a causal link with Personnel, it does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal claims require temporal order, plausible mechanism, and exclusion of confounders (Bradford Hill–style reasoning). That supports the methodological point in the explanation — i.e., one should not represent a causal link between two variables without evidence or a plausible mechanism. However, the citation is generic and does not provide any evidence specific to Multi-morbidity and Personnel nor directly demonstrate that no causal relationship exists between them. Therefore the support is methodological but not direct evidence that the relationship is truly absent."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity\'s risk factors and explicitly links socioeconomic factors including employment/occupation to higher risk (e.g., lower employment positions → ~66% higher risk of multimorbidity). It therefore provides evidence of associations (and potential pathways) between work/occupation-related variables and multimorbidity, not evidence that there is no direct causal relationship. The source does not support the claim that no direct causal link exists; if anything it contradicts it by identifying occupation/employment as a risk factor."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIHR briefing explicitly describes how multimorbidity increases healthcare use and costs and creates challenges for care and workforce models (need for more generalist skills, changes to training, multidisciplinary approaches, and service integration). These statements indicate causal effects of multimorbidity on health-sector personnel needs and roles. The citations do not provide evidence that “Personnel” causes multimorbidity, but they do contradict the claim that there is no causal link from multimorbidity to personnel/workforce. Therefore the claim of “NONE” (no direct causal relationship) is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Personnel, nor does Personnel cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables as defined. Without a clear mechanism linking Multi-morbidity directly to Personnel, no causal link should be represented in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2775341879306386, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8408167022335225, 'min_prob': 0.12229016553992601, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004158911885712438, 'prob_variance': 0.05822827571289171, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9634790625264823, 'avg_nll': 0.2449318043271784}

894. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2915210121884787, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/', 'https://mentalhealth.bmj.com/content/26/1/e300870'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7259674086532134, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly distinguishes comorbidity from multimorbidity, notes that comorbidity/multimorbidity describe co-occurrence without implying causal direction, and discusses that psychiatric \\"comorbidity\\" often does not establish distinct causal relationships (may be artifact or symptom overlap). It therefore supports the point that, without definitions, measurement, or context, one cannot establish a causal relationship between multimorbidity and a mental disorder.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article states that multimorbidity commonly includes mental health conditions, documents \\"considerable evidence\\" that having multiple long-term physical conditions can lead to depression and anxiety, and describes bidirectional interplay between multimorbidity and mental health. While the article notes variability in definitions, it nevertheless presents evidence of associations and potential causal pathways. Therefore the citation does not support the assertion that no causal relationship can be established; it supports existing links between multimorbidity and mental disorders.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited viewpoint highlights that multimorbidity is variably defined and measured and emphasizes complexity and bidirectional associations between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity, so it supports caution about inferring simple causal claims absent clear definitions or context. However, the article (and the studies it cites) also reports empirical associations and discusses plausible pathways and bidirectional effects between mental disorders and physical multimorbidity, rather than endorsing a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" conclusion. Therefore the sources partly support the cautionary epistemic point but do not fully support asserting that no causal relationship can be established in all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports a strong association (pooled OR 1.84) between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity and discusses temporal studies and plausible mechanisms (shared biology, medication effects, etc.). It therefore supports a link and examines directions/mechanisms rather than concluding that no causal relationship can be established. The citation does not support the claim that \\"no causal relationship\\" can be inferred in all contexts; instead it documents an association and discusses possible causal pathways and temporal relationships.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, 'prob_variance': 0.08498450054739515, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536167755296998, 'avg_nll': 0.3741551809754853}

895. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.26506919066586976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8609933/', 'https://academic.oup.com/eurpub/article/29/1/182/5033670', 'https://ehp.niehs.nih.gov/doi/10.1289/ehp.6735'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7441606320143002, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review documents that place and area-level factors (e.g., socioeconomic deprivation, urban/rural and HIC vs LMIC settings) are associated with the occurrence and timing of multimorbidity and that prevalence varies by geographic setting and measurement context. That indicates spatial/contextual factors can influence multimorbidity, contradicting the claim that the two operate independently. The source does not support the statement that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" between multimorbidity and spatial factors; it in fact highlights social and geographic determinants. It also provides no evidence that multimorbidity causally changes the \\"spatial scale\\" of analysis, but the overall claim of mutual non-causation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8609933/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly reports associations between multimorbidity trajectories and spatial/contextual factors — e.g., area-level deprivation, urbanisation level, and geographic (country/region) differences — and notes geographic biases in the evidence base. That indicates spatial/contextual variables can influence multimorbidity trajectories. The source does not support a blanket statement that there is no causal or contextual relationship between multimorbidity and spatial factors; therefore the claim of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or extent of multiple chronic conditions in individuals would alter the spatial scale of analysis or vice versa. These variables operate independently within a CLD context unless a specific mediating factor is present, which is not indicated here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4188828208618678, 'mean_token_prob': 0.800545348804346, 'min_prob': 0.012844613455296435, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0048187664213145565, 'prob_variance': 0.07026167584025922, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536841243667166, 'avg_nll': 0.3498698160942921}

896. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2808701945200274, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3055812/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/62/602/e659'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7225768417952919, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review describes psychosocial determinants (including \\"lacking control over one’s life\\") as implicated in the development of multimorbidity and discusses how multimorbidity interacts with psychological states (depression, treatment burden, capacity to self-manage) and the need for patient‑centred care, shared decision‑making and prioritizing what matters to the individual. Thus the source documents relationships between psychosocial constructs related to control/autonomy and multimorbidity, and it frames multimorbidity as one that changes care needs (e.g., prompting greater emphasis on patient priorities and shared decision making). It therefore does not clearly support the assertion that there is no direct causal or relevant relationship; in fact it presents evidence of psychosocial causation pathways and of multimorbidity altering care needs tied to autonomy/decision‑making.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that multimorbidity affects quality of life, functional status, mental health, self-management and participation in decision-making and emphasizes complex, not-fully-understood pathways and multiple mediators. That supports the claim that any association is complex and likely mediated by other