itions, health status, or causal links between multimorbidity and social network size, nor does it provide evidence about mediating roles of care needs or social support. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multi‑morbidity and social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents associations between multimorbidity and social factors (loneliness, social isolation, reduced social participation) but highlights that evidence on social isolation is limited and that many relationships and mechanisms remain uncertain or potentially bidirectional. It does not provide clear evidence that there is no direct causal relationship; rather it emphasizes associations, possible mediators (stress, functional limitation, socioeconomic factors), and the limited/heterogeneous evidence base. Therefore the sources partly support caution about inferring direct causality but do not fully substantiate the definitive \\"no direct causal link\\" claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper analyses multimorbidity patterns and their associations with social/demographic factors (age, social class) and explicitly notes its cross‑sectional design prevents drawing causal conclusions; it also frames multimorbidity as linked to social inequalities and suggests mediating social/contextual factors. However, the study does not measure or analyse \\"size of social network\\" (number of social contacts) nor provide direct evidence that there is no causal link between multimorbidity and social network size. Thus the source supports the caution about inferring causality and the role of mediators