912. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.28214391619843315, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7472468077679705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly links multimorbidity to health‑system impacts and calls for reconfiguration of care delivery (e.g., increased healthcare utilization, fragmented single‑disease systems, need to reorganize primary/specialty care, reconfiguration of healthcare supporting management of multimorbidity). Those statements imply a causal influence of multimorbidity on organizational structure (via increased demand, treatment burden, and policy responses). The source therefore does not support the claim that “no direct causal relationship” exists; rather it documents pathways by which multimorbidity drives changes in health‑care organization. The review does not, however, present evidence that organizational structure directly causes multimorbidity incidence, but it does describe how system design affects management and outcomes. Overall, the citation contradicts the asserted “NONE” (no causal) relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes how rising multimorbidity challenges care systems and policy and calls for innovations in the structure of health care delivery (e.g., integrated care, patient-centered medical homes, shifts from specialists to generalists). It frames multimorbidity as driving changes and adaptations in organizational design and care models, so it does not support the claim of no direct causal/structural relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that multimorbidity places pressure on health care systems and has implications for service planning (e.g., need for multiple specialists, targeting care, informing health and social care provision). It therefore implies multimorbidity can influence how care is organized. At the same time the article explicitly notes most studies are cross-sectional and provide limited evidence of causality, and discusses mechanistic uncertainty and the need for longitudinal research. Thus the citation supports the caution that direct causal links are not well established, but it does not fully support a definitive claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" because it also describes plausible pathways by which multimorbidity could shape organizational/planning decisions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. It provides no information to support (or refute) the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and organizational structure. Because the citation supplies no usable data or arguments, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that multimorbidity is associated with increased healthcare utilisation, fragmentation of care, and is a major challenge for health systems; it discusses how current health care organization (disease‑focused) is ill-suited for multimorbidity and highlights implications for health systems and policy. It does not state or provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and organizational structure; if anything, it implies multimorbidity influences healthcare organisation and system responses. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause organizational structure, nor does organizational structure cause multi-morbidity, in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which changes in multi-morbidity levels would inherently alter how organizations are structured, or vice versa, without mediation by other variables such as care demand or healthcare policy. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4136700424435165, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036219635036285, 'min_prob': 0.06088363060955491, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003237388842059471, 'prob_variance': 0.07960518944778848, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.125998940378456, 'avg_nll': 0.3461891897796512}

