 not cause changes in attained education, nor does it reason about causal mechanisms from multimorbidity to educational attainment. Therefore the citation does not support the specific claim that multimorbidity does not cause education level to change.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that lower education is a risk factor for developing multimorbidity (i.e., education → multimorbidity) and discusses socioeconomic associations, but it does not provide evidence or explicit statements that multimorbidity does not cause changes in attained education. The sources do not address or rule out the reverse causal direction, so they do not support the claim of no causal relationship from multimorbidity to education level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of multiple chronic diseases would alter a person's attained education level. While education can influence health outcomes, the reverse direction does not constitute a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4819127973534216, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738141997997413, 'min_prob': 0.0426772532091828, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008084797803705806, 'prob_variance': 0.08257631653422505, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.996632230666558, 'avg_nll': 0.3933336839506611}

