   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.0349158793634519, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9816239456590817, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that relational/autonomy-supportive clinical interactions affect patients\' ability to enact health-related behaviours (mentions self-management, \\"help them \'stick to\' their behavioural goals,\\" diabetes self-care, etc.). That implies a link between autonomy (and autonomy support) and treatment adherence/compliance. Therefore the sources do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim; they indicate autonomy-related factors can influence therapy compliance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly links patient autonomy (especially executive autonomy, but also decisional and narrative dimensions) to treatment adherence and discusses how deficits in these autonomy dimensions contribute to non-adherence (using psoriasis as an example) and how assessing/restoring autonomy may improve adherence. Therefore the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0122584043122134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.988551518199285, 'min_prob': 0.878199097756989, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023986582141971033, 'pro