rm features and social influences. However, it does not examine or provide evidence about a direct causal link (or lack thereof) between an individual\'s \\"need for autonomy\\" and the quantitative \\"size of their social network,\\" nor does it identify the specific mediators named in the explanation (e.g., care avoidance, network willingness). Thus the source provides relevant background that supports the plausibility of indirect/mediated relationships but does not directly support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between need for autonomy and social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-97722-1_3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter outlines many network theories showing that social network size and structure arise from relational mechanisms (homophily/selection, influence, popularity, bridging, reciprocity, etc.) and emphasizes interaction between individual attributes and network embedding. It does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" specifically, nor does it state that individual psychological needs cannot directly affect network size. The source supports the idea that associations between personal attributes and networks are often produced by mediated processes (selection, influence, structural position), but it does not provide direct evidence that need for autonomy has no direct causal effect on network size. Therefore the claim that \\"NONE (no direct causal relationship)\\" is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-network-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it cannot support (or contradict) the claim that need for autonomy has no direct causal effect on social network