tient-centered medical homes, policy/structural innovations). Thus the sources support that organizational structure does not directly cause multimorbidity but do indicate multimorbidity can drive changes in organizational structure — contradicting a strict \\"no causal relationship\\" claim in both directions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on drivers of multimorbidity (age, lifestyle, socioeconomic disadvantage) and on how health system design and funding/organizational arrangements influence care coordination, resource use, and outcomes for people with multimorbidity. It supports the idea that organizational structure mainly affects management, access and utilization (i.e., indirect pathways) rather than being a direct cause of patients developing multiple chronic conditions. However, the paper does not explicitly assert that organizational structure can never causally influence multimorbidity (for example, via long-term effects on access to prevention), so it does not fully rule out any causal links. Therefore the explanation (no direct causal mechanism; any link is indirect/mediated) is supported in part by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content relating organizational structure and multi-morbidity; therefore it provides no evidence to support the claimed lack of a causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing focuses on the determinants and clinical management of multimorbidity (biological, behavioural, socioeconomic, environmental factors) and on how health service organization affects care delivery, experience, costs and outcomes. It does not present evidence that an organization\'s structural arrangement causes the development of multiple long‑term conditions, nor that multimorbidity determines organizational structure. Where organizational models are discussed (integration, multidisciplinary care, digital support), the briefing treats these as influencing care processes/outcomes (and often showing limited impact), consistent with any association being mediated through access, quality or service delivery rather than a direct causal link to disease incidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Organizational structure does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause organizational structure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; multi-morbidity concerns patient health conditions, while organizational structure relates to how organizations are arranged. Any observed association would be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare access or quality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4007335011294484, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8156547928112825, 'min_prob': 0.0065971333091047, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005758837944124037, 'prob_variance': 0.07504123347886363, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.114707559866319, 'avg_nll': 0.3369960287963147}

