ctors, it is consistent with the explanation that any link between \\"personnel\\" and addiction would be indirect (mediated) rather than a direct biological cause — but the source does not explicitly rule on or discuss personnel itself."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes addiction as a brain disorder and lists specific biological and environmental risk/protective factors (family, peers, school, community resources, etc.), but it does not mention \\"personnel\\" (staffing levels or number of personnel) or address whether personnel directly causes or is caused by chronic addiction. It neither supports nor rules out a direct causal link involving \\"personnel,\\" so the cited material does not substantiate the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source lists recognized causes and risk factors for substance use disorder (genetics, environment, drug properties, mental health, etc.) and does not identify \\"personnel\\" (e.g., number of staff) as a direct cause of chronic addiction. It also notes that healthcare provider actions (prescribing, monitoring) influence prevention and treatment, which implies healthcare staffing or quality could indirectly affect risk. The citation does not address personnel counts or whether chronic addiction affects personnel, so it neither fully proves nor definitively rules out the claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that the social environment and other people (peers, intimate partners, treatment personnel, social networks) have direct, causal influences on drug use and the course of addiction via social learning and reciprocal determinism. It describes mechanisms (modeling, social reinforcement, changes in group affiliation, treatment teams and metacontingencies) by which persons in the environment affect initiation, maintenance, relapse, and recovery. Therefore the sources do not support a claim of no direct causal relationship between \\"personnel\\" (people in the environment) and chronic addiction; rather they describe direct pathways by which people influence addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Personnel does not cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause changes in personnel. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the number of personnel to the development or persistence of chronic addiction. Any potential association would be mediated by other variables such as healthcare service quality or accessibility, which are not direct effects. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.375439821621597, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8170154590843075, 'min_prob': 0.018094074108699502, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029237097859378357, 'prob_variance': 0.0680924790746933, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.119829983179578, 'avg_nll': 0.31877355024119886}

