   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase through direct exposure to more information, experiences, and perspectives shared among network members. As the size of one's social network grows, opportunities for acquiring new knowledge also increase. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities and may reduce knowledge acquisition. This represents a direct and positive causal relationship.", 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.27191994505814765, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7090279080453671, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows a positive correlation between social network size and grey-matter density in brain areas linked to social cognition and associative memory (including the entorhinal cortex implicated in face–name memory), and it cites work showing memory/perspective-taking predict network size. This supports an association between larger networks and neural substrates for memory-related processes, but the study is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot determine directionality or causation. It does not provide behavioral evidence that having a larger network increases overall knowledge or strengthens memory/recall abilities; rather, existing memory capacity could predict network size (or both could be driven by third factors). Therefore the citation partially supports the idea of a link involving memory-related brain regions but does not support the causal claim that larger networks lead to increased knowledge via increased cognitive demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "ht