1057. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causally increases Availability of alternatives for ED. A larger social network provides more informal care and support options, which can serve as alternatives to visiting the emergency department (ED). Conversely, a smaller social network limits these alternatives, making ED visits more likely. This is a direct causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.24428083977971607, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10015351/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7785056/', 'https://www.ed.gov/sites/ed/files/documents/students/supporting-child-student-social-emotional-behavioral-mental-health.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7603667957146195, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (O\'Malley & Marsden) documents that social networks transmit information, influence behaviors, provide informational and tangible resources, and shape diffusion of innovations and health-related practices — which supports the idea that larger networks can expose individuals to more diverse health information. However the paper does not provide evidence that larger personal or community social networks causally lead community planners to build more alternative emergency-department (ED) facilities (i.e., increase the number of healthcare facilities). The key causal link — interpersonal sharing of unmet-need information triggering planners to construct additional alternative ED services — is not demonstrated or empirically supported in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10015351/"