d long-term all-cause mortality, finding certain levels of social contact are associated with lower mortality. It does not investigate acute-care demand, emergency department visits, nor the proposed mechanism that a larger network increases detection of acute need and prompts ED use. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive relationship with ED visit demand."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5598785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes evidence that greater social connection (including larger network size) is associated with better health outcomes, lower mortality risk, improved adherence, and in some intervention studies lower healthcare costs and readmissions. It does not provide evidence that larger social networks increase acute care demand or emergency department visits via “more people recognizing problems and encouraging ED use.” On the contrary, the paper emphasizes protective effects of social connection and examples of reduced healthcare utilization; it does not support the proposed positive link to ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A larger social network means more people are aware of an individual's health status, increasing the likelihood that someone will recognize when acute care is needed and encourage or assist in seeking emergency department services. Therefore, as the size of a person's social network grows, the demand for acute care leading to an ED visit is likely to increase due to more collective vigilance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A larger social network means more people are aware of an individual's health status, increasing the likelihood that someone will recognize when acute care is needed and encourage or assist in