   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. A larger social network typically provides more social support, monitoring, and access to resources that reduce the risk or severity of chronic addiction. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, directly increasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2611434881034286, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2021/10/addictive-potential-of-social-media-explained.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460322001666'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529434402083849, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study provides evidence that people in long-term recovery often have more/better social support and that positive relationships (peers, family, service providers) and changes in social networks help initiate and maintain abstinence. It aligns with the recovery-capital framework linking social capital to recovery and notes that those lacking supportive networks are less able to stop substance use. However, the paper emphasizes the quality and function of relationships (recognition, practical support, limit-setting, changing milieus) rather than the mere size of a social network, and it does not establish a direct inverse causal effect of network size on chronic addiction nor the specific mechanisms of conformity/acceptance claimed. The evidence is qualitative and descriptive, not causal proof of the exact mechanism in the explanation."}, {"citati