ations between social network size and cognitive outcomes (incidence/progression of MCI and dementia). It reports that larger networks are associated with lower dementia risk, but it does not study frailty, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed mechanism (greater interpersonal exposure strengthening immunity via pathogen/stressor-driven hormesis). Therefore the citation does not support the claim about social network size reducing frailty through increased pathogen/environmental exposures."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666756823002635", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and provides no accessible content or evidence relevant to the claim, so there is nothing in the citation that supports the proposed causal inverse relationship between social network size and frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source defines social connection (including size/diversity of networks) and states that stronger social bonds protect against chronic disease and support longer, healthier lives — which supports an inverse link between social connectedness and poor health. However, the source does not mention frailty specifically, nor does it endorse the proposed biological mechanism (increased interpersonal pathogen/exposure strengthening immunity). Instead it attributes benefits to emotional/physical support, healthier behaviors, stress management, and community resources. Because the directional association is supported but the specific outcome term and mechanism are not, the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. 