1064. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5087858/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circulationaha.112.101816', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775956/', 'https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/social-media-use-unhealthy-lifestyles-and-teenage-wellbeing/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/lim2.94'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5087858/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLoS One paper reports that larger and denser personal networks were generally associated with healthier behaviours (more physical activity, less sedentary time, higher vegetable intake), and finds no evidence that perceived social support or norms mediated a negative effect. The paper does note—and cites prior work (e.g., Christakis & Fowler)—that social ties can sometimes spread unhealthy states (like obesity), but that is a discussion of other studies rather than empirical support from this study. Because the source’s own results contradict the claimed negative relationship and its mechanisms were not supported here, but the paper does acknowledge prior evidence of negative peer influence, the claim is only partially supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article shows that problematic social media use (frequency/compulsive use) is associated with several unhealthy lifestyle behaviors in adolescents and discusses peer influence and exposure to online norms as plausible mechanisms. However, it does not measure or report the size of an individual\'s social network (number of contacts) nor demonstrate that a larger network per se increases exposure or causes worse health behaviors; it is cross-sectional and does not establish causality. Therefore the citation partially supports the general idea that social-media–related social exposure/peer influence can relate to unhealthy lifestyle, but it does not directly support the specific claim about \\"size of social network\\" causing a negative effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/social-media-use-unhealthy-lifestyles-and-teenage-wellbeing/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source links greater social media use (time spent online) to less healthy lifestyles in teenagers. It does not provide evidence about the size or number of people in one\'s social network, nor does it document the proposed mechanism (greater exposure to unhealthy habits or peer pressure via larger networks). Because the key predictor in the claim (\\"size of social network\\") is different from the predictor reported in the source (\\"frequency/amount of social media use\\"), the citation does not support the stated relationship or mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Having a larger social network can actually hinder a healthy lifestyle because it increases exposure to unhealthy habits and peer pressure, making it harder to maintain good routines. The more people one interacts with, the greater the likelihood of