ayer-overlap differences by urbanization/living environment (Fig. 5), and states that the probability of establishing ties decreases with geographical distance and that geographic proximity of work/school/home influences layer overlap — even recommending gravity models to study those mechanisms. While it does not claim that network size causally changes \\"spatial scale,\\" it provides clear evidence that spatial scale (proximity/urbanization) relates to and helps generate network structure (size/degree and layer overlap). Thus the source does not support the statement that there is no causal linkage; it documents spatial effects on social-network structure.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small-world_network", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly links network size (N) to characteristic path length (L), stating L ∝ log N (and even L ∝ log log N for some classes), and discusses small-world properties across different spatial scales. That provides evidence of a structural relationship between network size and typical node-to-node distance (a spatial/scale-related network metric), so it does not support the assertion that there is no causal or direct relationship between network size and spatial scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause size of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable definition nor the CLD context provides evidence of a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4169871653319237, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7915939700493746, 'min_prob': 0.11196544934015454, 'token_prob_slope': -0.010367543076405255, 'prob_variance': 0.07994392429535695, 'relevance': 'Auto