   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2854823137071892, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-022-00567-7', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory', 'https://ssrlsig.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/niemiec-ryan-deci-2010-self-det-theory-and-the-relation-of-autonomy-to.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4756393/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7804695976722232, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-022-00567-7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues extensively that social environments and social media interactions (including social pressures, network effects, FOMO, imagined surveillance, and interpersonal manipulation) can undermine users\' autonomy competencies (self-determination, self-governance, self-authorisation) and interfere with autonomous choice. Although it does not use the exact phrase \\"willingness of social network,\\" it presents mechanisms by which features and behaviors of one\'s social network and social-media contacts influence autonomy. Thus the sources contradict the claim that there is no causal or relevant relationship between social-network factors and autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SDT source explicitly states that social environments (including family, friends, health professionals, etc.) can promote or thwart basic psychological needs and describes how social interactions (e.g., acknowledging preferences, providing choice, refraining from control) support autonomy. That describes a clear mechanism by which characteristics of a social network influence autonomy, so the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ssrlsig.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/niemiec-ryan-deci-2010-self-det-theory-and-the-relation-of-autonomy-to.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SDT chapter describes autonomy as a basic psychological need and repeatedly presents interpersonal/social-context factors (parents, teachers, peers, managers, autonomy-supportive vs. controlling climates, provision of meaningful rationale, acknowledgement of feelings, encouragement of choice) as causes that facilitate or thwart autonomy and the internalization process. It therefore documents a clear directional relation from social agents’ behavior / interpersonal climate to the experience and satisfaction of the need for autonomy. Because the sources report social-network (interpersonal) willingness/support as influencing autonomy, the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4756393/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how social-role experiences and relatedness (social connection/support) are associated with autonomy and competence need satisfaction and shows substantial correlations and mediated associations among these needs. It therefore documents relationships between social support/role-related social factors and autonomy-related need satisfaction rather than supporting a claim of no relationship or causal linkage. It does not support the assertion that there is no connection between social-network willingness and autonomy need.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Willingness of social network' causes 'Need for autonomy' or vice versa. These variables are distinct, and without a clear mechanism or definition linking them, no causation can be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4154351391990347, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048268463791826, 'min_prob': 0.05941702505317537, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008513280278782738, 'prob_variance': 0.08150015143960998, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0159982805077965, 'avg_nll': 0.34743700268954103}

