   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.2125016841581796, 'citations': ['https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258158777_Understanding_Willingness_to_Pay_for_Social_Network_Sites', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0965856416307418'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8083597888390843, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses mechanisms by which social networks and group belonging affect users’ motivation, decision-making, productivity, and behavior (e.g., social integration, group membership increasing “drive to complete tasks,” mimicry of group behaviors, improved decision-making and autonomy). Those points provide a plausible basis for a causal link from social network willingness/acceptance to proactive attitudes. Therefore the claim that “no basis” exists for asserting a causal relationship is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258158777_Understanding_Willingness_to_Pay_for_Social_Network_Sites", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited \\"source\\" is an access-error/denial page from ResearchGate and contains no substantive content about either variable or any evidence about causal relationships. Because the citation provides no definitions, data, or mechanisms, it cannot support the explanation that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis extensively documents mechanisms (accessibility, stability, motivation, one‑sidedness, etc.) that link attitudes to behavior but does not examine a variable described as \\"willingness of social network.\\" The article explicitly notes that normative and social‑network influences (e.g., Visser & Mirabile, Smith & Terry, White et al.) were not integrated in the review and recommends future work to incorporate them. Thus the citation supports the claim that this particular synthesis provides no basis to assert a causal link involving \\"willingness of social network\\" (because it was not analyzed), but it also acknowledges other work suggesting social‑network/normative factors can affect attitudes — so the sources do not justify a definitive “no relationship” conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0965856416307418", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about either variable or any causal mechanisms. Therefore it does not support the claim that no causal relationship can be established.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to assert that 'Willingness of social network' causes 'Proactive attitude' or vice versa, as no definitions or mechanisms are provided for either variable. Without clear definitions or evidence of a direct causal pathway, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.224254555956201, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8639656809131067, 'min_prob': 0.1013220841721442, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017678147174999468, 'prob_variance': 0.045156965770062715, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9640039586857465, 'avg_nll': 0.20233213301970873}

