   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.26477009784274297, 'citations': ['https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2025-31872-001', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n30.xml', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4021390/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0963721417730833'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8300234677955761, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4021390/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies do not support “no relationship.” Cacioppo et al. summarize experimental animal evidence (e.g., social isolation worsening infarct size and post‑stroke survival; altered neuroinflammatory responses to stroke) and physiological effects of social isolation/loneliness (heightened sympathetic/adrenomedullary responses to acute stressors, altered cortisol and immune responses, increased blood pressure) as well as longitudinal human associations with morbidity and mortality. While the paper does not use the exact phrasing “willingness of social network” and treats multiple related constructs (objective/subjective isolation, social support, network characteristics), it provides evidence that social relationship characteristics can causally influence physiological responses and acute health outcomes (at least in animal experiments and via plausible pathways in humans). Therefore the citation contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause acute event, and acute event does not cause willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without specific definitions or an explicit pathway, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': '