irect causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source states that therapy can cause uncomfortable emotions and that certain techniques (e.g., exposure therapy) can produce short-term stress or anxiety, and that patients may feel embarrassed or upset during sessions. It therefore describes a direct pathway by which therapy participation (and by implication compliance with therapy) can elicit fear/anxiety; it does not state that there is no direct causal mechanism nor discuss mediation via healthcare avoidance or mental disorder as the exclusive explanation. Thus the citation does not support the claim that therapy compliance does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established causal mechanism by which changing therapy compliance directly alters feelings of fear and shame. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare avoidance or mental disorder, which are present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3286397289293688, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8276947275005933, 'min_prob': 0.07145290642457347, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024673473272765097, 'prob_variance': 0.06604970998954413, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9991307873355411, 'avg_nll': 0.28415565865407416}

