   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.22427925693290843, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4592639/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8714338477871592, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review lists healthcare-system factors (eg, availability/accessibility, long waiting times, difficulty obtaining prescriptions, unsatisfactory clinic visits) as contributors to therapeutic compliance, and also links non-compliance to increased healthcare use, hospitalizations and costs. Those findings imply that elements of healthcare resources (which would include diagnostic resources as part of system availability/access) can causally affect therapy compliance, and that poor compliance affects healthcare resource use. The source therefore does not support a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" between therapy compliance and healthcare/diagnostic resources; if anything it contradicts it."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4087153/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses how limited access, costs, and health-care resources affect adherence, and states that non‑adherence increases costs and places a large burden on resources. That indicates causal links between resources (including access/costs) and adherence, and that adherence/non‑adherence affects resource use. While it does not focus specifically on \\"diagnostic resources,\\" it directly contradicts the claim that no causal relationship exists between therapy compliance and health‑care resources. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3,