herapy compliance. The article does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction (and in fact provides evidence for one direction), so the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article does not state or demonstrate a direct causal link from patient therapy compliance to healthcare professionals\' functioning (or vice versa). It emphasizes that therapeutic relationships influence patient adherence and that psychotherapy skills and relational factors affect team dynamics, empathy, and the healthcare environment. It also notes contextual moderators (e.g., workload, training, organizational factors) that influence clinicians\' ability to apply relational skills. Thus the citation supports the idea that relationships are complex and likely mediated by other factors, but it does not provide explicit evidence that there is definitively no direct causal relationship between therapy compliance and healthcare professional functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where therapy compliance causes functioning of healthcare professionals or vice versa. Therapy compliance refers to patients adhering to prescribed treatments, while functioning of healthcare professionals pertains to their ability to perform their duties. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as workload, patient outcomes, or organizational factors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3150929006603944, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8217250232557873, 'min_prob': 0.12330270316699689, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035299664150737625, 'prob_variance': 0.0635976235617627, 're