ce. Therefore the explanation\'s assertion that no direct causal link can be established is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents empirical findings that time-limited therapy (a specific definition of \\"time\\") is associated with changes in patient engagement/dropout (e.g., meta-analytic and trial data showing lower dropout for time-limited treatments and studies finding differing rates of symptom change by session dose). Although the review stresses methodological limitations and uncertainty about causality and long-term effects, it does not conclude there is no direct relationship; rather it documents evidence of an effect while noting confounds. Therefore the claim that \\"Time\\" has no direct causal link to therapy compliance is not supported by these sources.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links temporal aspects of treatment to adherence — e.g., adherence is higher for acute vs chronic conditions, compliance drops when medication must be taken over a long period, and adherence “varies between and within individuals, as well as across time.” It also discusses regimen duration, dosing frequency and long-term regimens as factors affecting adherence. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that “Time” has no direct relationship to therapy compliance; they provide evidence that time/duration-related factors do influence adherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. Without a specific definition of 'Time' (e.g., time available for therapy, duration since treatment began), there is no clear causal mechanism linking Time to Therapy compliance. Thus, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4236108304522668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8010290807022451, 'min_prob': 0.028781491719962155, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020938774427126526, 'prob_variance': 0.07420425188239252, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9632350492623556, 'avg_nll': 0.35319648241666335}

