1163. Time-[NONE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '106b2553-04c8-418c-bd40-68a2ff6fdcff', 'prob_std': 0.3140729247370336, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-29443-w', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/srep35724', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis', 'https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.ads2133'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7752034521090402, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-29443-w", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper explicitly treats network size as a time-dependent quantity: it models node arrivals/deletions with compartmental transitions that use a time-dependent probability p0(t) (derived from a logistic growth derivative), states that \\"time is an essential aspect\\" of the model, and shows empirical time-series of number of users and edges. Thus the source assumes and demonstrates that the passage of time (via specified time-dependent functions and parameters) produces changes in social-network size. It does not support the claim that time has no direct effect; rather it provides a mechanism by which time influences network size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/srep35724", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited paper models network evolution as driven by measurable mechanisms—individual activity rates and tie-allocation/reinforcement (β)—and derives explicit scaling laws for how average degree grows with time only when those activity/allocation parameters are specified. It therefore supports the point that the mere passage of time, absent specification of activity and allocation mechanisms, is not by itself an intrinsic causal driver of network size change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides direct empirical evidence that, in their high-school longitudinal dataset (10 snapshots over 4 years), macroscopic network properties including the distribution of personal contacts remain statistically stationary and the network is in dynamical equilibrium — i.e., the mere passage of time did not produce systematic changes in network size in that context. The authors also emphasize that dynamics arise from specific mechanisms and contextual boundaries (resource allocation, institutional setting, timescale), and caution about generalizing beyond the studied setting. Because the source supports the claim within the empirical and contextual limits of the study but does not establish a universal, context-free absence of any time effect, the support is partial rather than full."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an access/connection error and contains no substantive content about time or social network size. Therefore it provides no evidence for the claimed absence of a causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time alters the size of a social network in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5958415242665596, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926927847864007, 'min_prob': 0.011465389241063773, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021811488880077133, 'prob_variance': 0.09864180205287434, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.00188765354