69. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes knowledge to increase in a direct causal relationship. When information becomes more accessible, individuals have greater opportunities to acquire and absorb knowledge. Conversely, reduced accessibility limits exposure to information, thereby constraining knowledge acquisition.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2607553732033238, 'citations': ['https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8', 'https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7390703724049729, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://meadowscenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Barnes_19961.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited experiments show a positive effect of information accessibility on the likelihood that that information will be used in making inferences: easily accessible knowledge items were about twice as likely to be used for inferencing (accessibility indexed by retrieval speed), and accessibility predicted inference use across ages. However, the paper does not support the stronger claims in the motivation — that increased accessibility automatically synthesizes large volumes of information into comprehensive knowledge regardless of prior expertise or engagement, or that restricting access will invariably reduce knowledge. Barnes et al. find that availability/accessibility interacts with developmental and processing factors (age, memory for text, integration ability); teaching knowledge did not eliminate age-related differences and younger children could not always exploit accessible knowledge. The study is also limited to children’s knowledge-based inferencing in text comprehension, not broad claims about exposure to many perspectives or automatic consolidation of volume into deeper, more accurate understanding."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial and collected papers support a positive link between greater information accessibility and improved opportunities for learning and knowledge acquisition (e.g., ICTs enable learning anywhere/anytime, case studies showing improved understanding with digital tools, and discussion of reducing the digital divide to improve access). However, the sources do not endorse the strong, universal claims in the motivation — they explicitly note variability in how people learn, the existence of digital divides and usability barriers, and the need to facilitate access and tailored methods. The citations do not support the idea that increased information volume \\"automatically\\" synthesizes into comprehensive knowledge for all individuals regardless of prior expertise or engagement, nor the absolutist claim that restricting access \\"invariably\\" and uniformly diminishes knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L/abstract", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents limits in web indexing and argues that web data must be made more accessible, which supports the general claim that accessibility of information matters. However, it does not provide evidence that increased accessibility directly and automatically enhances individual knowledge, that greater information volume inherently synthesizes into deeper/accurate understanding regardless of prior expertise or engagement, or that restricting access invariably reduces knowledge capacity. Those causal and mechanistic assertions are not demonstrated by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly enhances knowledge by ensuring that individuals are continuously exposed to a wide range of perspectives and facts, which inherently leads to a deeper and more accurate understanding of complex topics. As information becomes more accessible, the sheer volume of available data automatically synthesizes into comprehensive knowledge within individuals, regardless of their prior expertise or engagement levels. Thus, restricting accessibility of information invariably results in a diminished capacity for knowledge, as fewer opportunities exist for automatic knowledge consolidation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly enhances knowledge by ensuring that individuals are continuously exposed to a wide range of perspectives and facts, which inherently leads to a deeper and more accurate understanding of complex topics. As information becomes more accessible, the sheer volume of available data automatically synthesizes into comprehensive knowledge within individuals, regardless of their prior expertise or engagement levels. Thus, restricting accessibility of information invariably results in a diminished capacity for knowledge, as fewer opportunities exist for automatic knowledge consolidation.', 'perplexity': 1.351195556963119, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814468932102195, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08996629412482327, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007704037304737102, 'prob_variance': 0.06799336465440468, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0161779907614508, 'avg_nll': 0.3009897982904751}

70. Accessibility of information-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information does not directly cause frailty, nor does frailty cause accessibility of information. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy, education level, or healthcare avoidance, which are present as mediators in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41999-024-00979-y', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11412848/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4723192/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1510418/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41999-024-00979-y", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher use of information and communication technologies was associated with lower odds of frailty among community-dwelling adults aged 75+, i.e., ICT use correlated with less frailty. The paper even notes ICT use may be protective and that causal directions remain to be clarified. Thus the claim that increased accessibility/exposure to information directly increases frailty contradicts the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11412848/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds that various types of internet use — including information searching — are associated with lower odds of cognitive impairment and mitigate the impact of social frailty on cognition (ORs < 1). The paper interprets internet access/use as providing cognitive stimulation and social support (increasing cognitive reserve), not as causing increased frailty. Thus the sources contradict the claim that greater information accessibility increases frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4723192/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial describes the Accessible Information Standard, its implementation, and its aims (improving communication, health literacy, self-management, and access to information). It does not provide evidence or discussion that greater accessibility of information causes increased frailty or reduces physical/mental activity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1510418/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited CHARLS study reports that greater internet access/use is associated with better cognitive performance and may protect against cognitive decline in older adults, and it recommends promoting digital inclusion; it does not find that increased information accessibility increases frailty. The paper focuses on improved (not weakened) mental function with internet engagement and does not provide evidence that reliance on readily accessible information causes physical or cognitive frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content. There is no evidence in the cited material that addresses or supports a link between information accessibility and increased frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly leads to greater frailty in individuals because constant exposure to information reduces the need for physical and mental activity. As people rely more on easily accessible information, their bodies and minds weaken over time, increasing frailty.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information directly leads to greater frailty in individuals because constant exposure to information reduces the need for physical and mental activity. As people rely more on easily accessible information, their bodies and minds weaken over time, increasing frailty.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

