   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how accessible online health information increases perceived informational and decision-making benefits and thereby promotes health empowerment and greater participation in consultations. It does not present evidence that greater information accessibility leads people to avoid professional care; if anything, it emphasizes informed engagement with providers rather than care avoidance. Therefore the specific claim about information accessibility increasing care-avoidance behavior is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article surveys physicians about the importance, accessibility, quality, and their use of clinical information. It does not examine patient behavior, self‑diagnosis, self‑treatment, or care avoidance. Moreover, the study finds that greater accessibility (and perceived importance/quality) of information does not necessarily lead to increased use by physicians, so it does not provide evidence that increased information accessibility causes individuals to avoid formal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content about information accessibility or care avoidance, so it does not address or support the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of information directly encourages individuals to avoid unnecessary medical care, as they feel more empowered and capable of self-diagnosing and self-treating minor issues. This increased access to information reassures people and reduces their reliance on professional healthcare, thereby promoting care avoidance behavior. Therefore, as accessibility of information rises, so does the likelihood of individuals avoiding formal care.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

