   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make informed healthcare decisions and seek appropriate alternatives, thereby reducing unnecessary ED visits. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information leads to higher reliance on ED services due to lack of knowledge about alternatives.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/', 'https://behavioralhealthnews.org/empowering-patients-through-accessible-electronic-health-records-benefits-and-challenges/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3041432/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study surveys physicians about the importance, accessibility, quality, and their use of clinical information (HIE) and finds that greater accessibility does not necessarily lead to routine use by clinicians. It does not provide evidence about patient access to information, patient recognition of symptom severity, or any effect on ED visits/acute care demand, so the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://behavioralhealthnews.org/empowering-patients-through-accessible-electronic-health-records-benefits-and-challenges/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The citation documents that more accessible EHRs empower patients, improve understanding of their health, and can aid decision-making and care coordination (supporting the first part of the motivation). However, it does not provide evidence that this increased accessibility leads to a higher rate of acute care use or more ED visits, so the claimed positive effect on acute care demand is not demonstrated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Greater accessibility of health information empowers patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which increases their likelihood of seeking timely acute care in emergency departments. This informed decision-making ensures that individuals do not delay necessary medical attention, thereby directly raising acute care demand leading to more ED visits. Therefore, increased accessibility to information should positively correlate with acute care demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Greater accessibility of health information empowers patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which increases their likelihood of seeking timely acute care in emergency departments. This informed decision-making ensures that individuals do not delay necessary medical attention, thereby directly raising acute care demand leading to more ED visits. Therefore, increased accessibility to information should positively correlate with acute care demand.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

