   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes functioning of healthcare professionals to improve in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals have easier and faster access to relevant information, their ability to diagnose, treat, and coordinate care increases. Conversely, limited access to information impairs their functioning due to knowledge gaps or delays in decision-making.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.27919346071876766, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/health-care-access-and-quality', 'https://ahimafoundation.ahima.org/research/the-critical-role-of-web-accessibility-in-health-information-access-understanding-and-use/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7654694837439076, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHQDR document addresses patients\' access to healthcare (insurance, timely care, usual source of care, disparities) and only briefly references data tools and health IT in the context of measuring access. It does not provide evidence linking accessibility of information to healthcare professionals\' motivation, job satisfaction, cognitive ability, efficiency, or overall functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/health-care-access-and-quality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 content includes objectives to increase electronic access to necessary health information for doctors and to improve use/exchange of health information, with the goal of enabling providers to deliver higher‑quality care — which supports a positive link between information accessibility and better professional functioning. However, the source does not assert the specific causal mechanism claimed in the motivation (that increased access inherently raises motivation and job satisfaction or directly enhances cognitive ability and efficiency), so those proposed mediators are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes the functioning of healthcare professionals to improve because increased access to information inherently leads to greater motivation and job satisfaction among healthcare staff. As healthcare professionals encounter more data and resources, they experience a sense of professional fulfillment that directly enhances their cognitive abilities and efficiency, resulting in better overall functioning. Conversely, when accessibility of information is limited, professionals are less motivated, which diminishes their functional performance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes the functioning of healthcare professionals to improve because increased access to information inherently leads to greater motivation and job satisfaction among healthcare staff. As healthcare professionals encounter more data and resources, they experience a sense of professional fulfillment that directly enhances their cognitive abilities and efficiency, resulting in better overall functioning. Conversely, when accessibility of information is limited, professionals are less motivated, which diminishes their functional performance.', 'perplexity': 1.3817841333085055, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8086972648369495, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09422456323764135, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005482115861302193, 'prob_variance': 0.07794898850812207, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0