   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information (such as providing materials in multiple languages and culturally relevant formats) directly reduces language and cultural barriers. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information increases these barriers, as individuals struggle to find and understand necessary information.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.277684511720174, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10400435.2021.1945705', 'https://ijassh.org/admin1/upload/02 Dr. Al-Mutairi Forat Zaki Salih 01282.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7757512473195992, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly links globalization and communication technology to increased cross-linguistic interactions and documents numerous language- and culture-related communication problems (accents, dialects, mistranslation, taboo terms, etc.), which is consistent with the idea that broader exposure can make language and cultural barriers more apparent. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate the specific causal claim that greater accessibility of information itself (e.g., wider information availability) indirectly increases perceived language/cultural barriers or quantify that effect — the connection is implied rather than directly evidenced."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ijassh.org/admin1/upload/02 Dr. Al-Mutairi Forat Zaki Salih 01282.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper discusses how globalization and the internet have increased interactions among speakers of different languages and thus brought language/cultural barriers \\"to the forefront,\\" which can be read as increased exposure making those barriers more salient. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that greater accessibility of information causes an increase in language or cultural barriers (or perceived barriers); they mainly document the negative impacts of existing barriers and focus on mitigation (translation, interpreters, training). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information indirectly increases language and cultural barriers by exposing individuals to a wider array of perspectives, dialects, and cultural norms. As information becomes more accessible, people encounter content from diverse linguistic and cultural backgrounds, which can intensify awareness of differences and lead to greater perceived barriers. Consequently, heightened accessibility of information may inadvertently make language and cultural barriers more salient, as individuals become more conscious of the complexities inherent in cross-cultural communication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information indirectly increases language and cultural barriers by exposing individuals to a wider array of perspectives, dialects, and cultural norms. As information becomes more accessible, people encounter content from diverse linguistic and cultural backgrounds, which can intensify awareness of differences and lead to greater perceived barriers. Consequently, heightened accessibility of information may inadvertently make language and cultural barriers more salient, as individuals become more conscious of the complexities inherent in cross-cultural communication.', 'perplexity': 1.372522372709237, 'mean_token_pro