   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.330106166961068, 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-10780-1_25', 'https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6837196110738725, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article repeatedly links information disclosure/access to autonomy in practice — e.g., the Belmont Report and Beauchamp & Childress portray informed consent (making information available) as the primary means to protect and foster autonomous decision‑making, and the text states a positive obligation to disclose information to foster autonomy. Those passages imply a direct causal/functional relation between accessibility of information and the presence or exercise of autonomy (and the agent\'s demand for self‑determination), so the claim of no direct relationship is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article links information access to autonomous agency (e.g., informed consent requires patients be “well‑informed” to make autonomous decisions; external enabling conditions for personal autonomy include substantive options and social supports). That indicates a direct enabling/causal connection between accessibility of information and the capacity for (and respect for) autonomy, contradicting the claim that no direct relationship can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The SEP entry on autonomy repeatedly treats access to