103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief reports counts, common diagnoses, and patient- and community-level patterns of ED use (e.g., nonurgent visits tied to limited primary care access, insurance, demographics). It does not analyze or provide evidence that greater availability of diagnostic resources (or perceptions of higher diagnostic capability) increases acute care demand or ED visits, so the motivation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats content only provides aggregated emergency department visit counts, rates, and related statistics. It does not present any information or analysis about the availability of diagnostic resources, perceptions of care quality, or whether greater diagnostic capacity drives more ED visits, so the cited source does not support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents a large increase in diagnostic testing and imaging, discusses overuse, clinicians\' tendency to favor immediate testing over bedside evaluation, and geographic variation in utilization — all of which support the general idea that greater diagnostic capacity leads to greater use of diagnostic services. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that increased availability of diagnostic resources directly causes a rise in acute care demand or emergency department visits because patients or referrers choose the ED knowing comprehensive diagnostics are available. That specific link (availability → increased ED visits/acute-care seeking driven by patient/referrer choice) is not addressed in the provided text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that capturing previously lost ED demand (eg, reducing LWBS and diverted ambulances by lowering boarding) can produce substantial additional daily and annual revenue, and that dynamic bed-management strategies to accommodate ED demand can be financially attractive. However, the paper also reports that non-ED admissions reimburse at substantially higher rates per inpatient day than ED admissions, and that simply shifting capacity away from non-ED admissions can reduce revenue unless managed carefully. Thus the citation supports a conditional positive effect of capturing additional ED demand, not the blanket claim that any increase in ED visits always increases hospital financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Affairs analysis estimates that ED care generated net hospital revenue in 2009 (≈$78.7B revenue vs $72.5B cost; $6.1B net, 7.8% margin) and projects increased ED profitability under ACA scenarios, supporting the claim that ED visits can increase hospitals’ financial resources. However, the paper also shows profitability is highly dependent on payer mix: privately insured visits are strongly profitable while Medicare, Medicaid, and uninsured visits are loss-making. Thus an increase in ED demand only increases hospital financial resources overall under certain payer-mix conditions, not universally."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article analyzes the financial harms of ED crowding—showing extended ED length of stay and boarding increase hospital costs and can produce lost revenue (ambulance diversion, elopements, opportunity loss). It emphasizes that reducing ED crowding/throughput delays could save costs and generate additional revenue, which contradicts the blanket claim that more ED visits necessarily increase a hospital’s financial resources. The sources do not support a simple positive relationship between higher acute care demand leading to ED visits and increased hospital finances."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on factors driving emergency department demand and describes hospital funding arrangements (e.g., public hospitals funded mainly by government; private hospitals funded largely by individuals/insurance) but does not provide evidence that increased ED visits directly increase hospital financial resources via billing/reimbursements. It even notes that private hospital ED services in Australia are not covered by private health insurance, which undermines the simple claim that more ED visits necessarily generate greater insurance revenue."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how increased patient input (demand), throughput delays, and limited output capacity (beds/boarding) produce ED overcrowding and worse outcomes. It documents that lack of capacity contributes to boarding and reduces the ED’s ability to deliver care, and separately that failures in community services drive increased ED use. It does not provide evidence that a decline in ED capacity causes community members to perceive reduced access and thereby increases acute-care demand (or that higher capacity reduces urgency and lowers demand). The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation is not addressed by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats content provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visits (counts, rates, and some timing/admission percentages) but contains no analysis or data linking ED capacity to changes in community demand or perceptions of access. It does not support the proposed causal relationship between Capacity and Acute care demand leading to an ED visit."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (error message), so there is no evidence in the provided citation to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats material provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visit counts, rates, and related utilization. It does not present data or analysis on healthcare professionals\' functioning, skill atrophy, cognitive flexibility, or decision‑making, so it neither supports nor contradicts the claimed negative causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HWSM Demand Modeling Overview describes methods for projecting demand for services, ED visits, and converting service demand into staffing (FTE) needs. It does not provide evidence or discussion about changes in healthcare professionals’ functioning, skill atrophy, cognitive flexibility, or decision‑making as a result of higher (or lower) acute care/ED demand."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789,