107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the ED for acute care would alter a person's education level. Similarly, changes in acute care demand do not causally impact educational attainment. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8449049/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827321001932', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8449049/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study finds a negative association between educational attainment and hospitalizations among middle-aged and older adults (higher education linked to fewer hospital admissions). However, it analyzes overnight hospitalizations (not emergency department visits or \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\"), and it shows most of the education effect is mediated by better health (and partly income) rather than a direct effect of education on care-seeking behavior. The specific mechanism proposed — that better-educated people manage minor problems at home and therefore are less likely to seek emergency care unnecessarily — is not tested in the paper."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study finds that limited health literacy (measured by REALM) is associated with higher rates of potentially preventable ED visits, which is consistent with the motivation\'s idea that poorer knowledge/self-management links to more ED use. However, the paper measures health literacy, not formal education level, and does not directly test whether higher educational attainment reduces acute-care–driven ED visits or the specific behavioral mechanism (managing minor issues at home). Thus the evidence supports a related construct (health literacy) but does not directly support the claim about education level."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a robust education–health gradient and identifies mechanisms (human capital—knowledge, skills, health behaviors) by which higher education can improve health and influence health-related decision-making. Those mechanisms plausibly support the idea that better-educated people may manage minor problems differently. However, the article does not provide direct evidence about acute care demand specifically leading to emergency department (ED) visits or show that ED utilization decreases with higher education. Thus the general mechanism is supported, but the specific claim about reduced ED visits is not directly demonstrated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827321001932", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message), so no evidence is provided to support or contradict the claimed negative relationship between education and ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly reduce the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because better-educated individuals are more knowledgeable about managing minor health issues at home and are less likely to seek emergency care unnecessarily. As a result, as education level increases, ED visits driven by acute care demand decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly reduce the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because better-educated individuals are more knowledgeable about managing minor health issues at home and are less likely to seek emergency care unnecessarily. As a result, as education level increases, ED visits driven by acute care demand decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

