   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited paper treats organizational structure as a design choice (information-processing, coordination strategies like pooling, vertical systems, lateral relations) that organizations adopt to manage uncertainty such as patient arrivals. It frames patient arrival variability as an environmental factor that may motivate managerial redesign, but does not describe an immediate causal mechanism by which an acute-care demand or a single ED visit directly changes formal organizational structure. Thus the claim that ED demand does not directly cause immediate structural change — and that structure is set by leadership/design decisions — is consistent with the article."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source emphasizes that organizational structure is shaped by leadership planning, mission/vision, and design choices—supporting the claim that leaders determine structure. However, the same source also lists the \\"pace of change\\" and the \\"complexity of the external environment\\" as factors shaping organizational design and notes administrators \\"continually adjust and improve the organizational structure.\\" That implies external forces (which can include patient demand/ED visit patterns) can lead to structural changes. Therefore the citation supports the leadership-determined aspect but does not support the stronger assertion that acute care demand leading to an ED visit has no causal role; the citation indicates external demand can influence organizational structure."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause organizational structure to change. Organizational structure refers to the formal arrangement of roles, responsibilities, and processes within an organization, which is typically determined by leadership decisions rather than patient demand. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in acute care demand leading to an ED visit would immediately alter organizational structure.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331570359411918, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339821231048525, 'min_prob': 0.03191688392872667, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003309274672821155, 'prob_variance': 0.06151411350348714, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9630886626364701, 'avg_nll': 0.2863589670927359}

