   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visits (counts, rates, visit characteristics) but does not discuss or analyze whether ED demand causally affects the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. It neither supports nor contradicts the claim about causal direction, so the relationship is unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and discussion provide evidence that the availability/accessibility of alternatives (primary care, urgent care, retail clinics) influences ED use (i.e., availability → ED demand). The articles note access and perceptions of alternatives are drivers of non-urgent ED visits and explicitly model supply/availability as affecting the decision to seek ED care. However, the citations do not provide direct evidence demonstrating that acute care demand leading to ED visits does not causally affect the existence or accessibility of alternatives (they do not test or rule out a causal effect in the ED→availability direction). Thus the literature supports the reverse causal direction but does not conclusively establish the absence of any causal effect from ED demand to availability."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review documents multiple interventions outside the ED (increased non-ED capacity, managed care, financial incentives, EMS diversion, patient education) that alter ED utilization, supporting the claim that availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED (the reverse causal direction). However, the review does not provide direct evidence demonstrating that acute care demand leading to ED visits cannot influence the existence or accessibility of alternatives; it does not address or rule out causal effects running from ED demand to availability of alternatives. Therefore the citations support the opposite causal direction but do not fully substantiate the stronger claim that there is no causal link from ED demand to alternatives."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

