   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.26281593175921625, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7408414124705698, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents an increase in people with mental health conditions presenting to emergency departments (especially after COVID‑19) and describes how mental disorders drive ED use and strain staff. It does not provide any evidence that ED visits or acute care demand cause new or worsened mental disorders, nor does it describe a direct causal mechanism from ED visits to developing mental illness. However, the sources also do not explicitly investigate or rule out causal effects of ED exposure on mental health, so they only partially support the claim (they support the reverse direction and the absence of evidence for ED → mental disorder, but do not definitively demonstrate no causal effect)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents multiple participant reports that ED experiences — especially coercive/restrictive practices, long waits, lack of appropriate care, and retraumatizing treatment — can exacerbate existing mental health conditions, cause lasting psychological harm, and negatively affect emotional state and future help-seeking. While the evidence is qualitative and does not quantify population-level causality, it directly contradicts the assertion that ED visits cannot lead to development or worsening of mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review does not present evidence that emergency department (ED) visits or acute care demand cause mental disorders; instead it emphasizes social determinants as causes of mental disorder and notes bi‑directional relationships, reverse causality, selection/confounding, and that rising service use reflects increased need. However, the paper does not directly study or rule out a causal effect of acute care/ED visits on subsequent mental disorder, so it only indirectly supports the motivation (by documenting likely reverse causality and complex confounding) rather than providing direct evidence that ED visits have no causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes ED attendance as a consequence of acute mental health crises (supporting reverse causality) and provides no evidence that increased ED visits produce new mental disorders. However, it also documents that emergency and inpatient acute care can be distressing or iatrogenic (traumatic experiences, exacerbation of symptoms, coercion), so it does not unequivocally rule out pathways by which acute-care experiences might worsen mental health. Thus the claim that ED visits do not cause mental disorder is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that people with mental disorders commonly present to EDs (supporting reverse causality/accompanying explanations for associations) and emphasizes system-level factors (lack of community care, triage, boarding) that explain observed links. However, the same source also describes ED environmental and care processes (overstimulation, long waits/boarding, use of restraints, poor follow-up) that can produce agitation, emotional distress or worsen patients\' conditions — i.e., plausible mechanisms by which an ED visit could exacerbate mental health. Thus the citations support the idea that associations may be driven by prior mental disorder and mediators, but do not fully support the stronger claim that ED visits cannot causally worsen or contribute to mental-health deterioration."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

