   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.258361672465349, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK64101/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7762054735860721, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Mayo Clinic source states that drug addiction can begin when people take prescribed medicines (particularly opioids) or receive them from others with prescriptions. That indicates a direct causal pathway from medical treatment/exposure to addictive drugs to later addiction, which contradicts the claim that an ED visit (a setting where such prescriptions may be given) cannot increase the likelihood of chronic addiction. The citation does not support the assertion of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that addiction is often a chronic, multi-episode condition and criticizes acute-care models (brief, episodic treatment) as insufficient to alter the long-term course of addiction. That supports the general claim that a single acute-care encounter is unlikely to cause or prevent chronic addiction and that chronicity is driven by other risk factors. However, the paper does not specifically examine emergency department visits or provide direct evidence about ED visits causing (or not causing) chronic addiction, so it does not fully substantiate the specific assertion about \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK64101/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter focuses on issues in intensive outpatient treatment (retention, relapse prevention, handling clients who arrive under the influence, referrals for detox/acute care, etc.) but does not examine whether an ED visit for acute care causally increases or decreases the risk of developing chronic addiction. It neither presents evidence for nor against a direct causal effect of ED visits on chronic addiction; at most it notes related clinical concerns (e.g., acute care or hospitalization may be indicated for intoxication/overdose), which does not address the stated causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic, multifactorial condition (genetics, brain changes, mental health, environmental exposure, prescription medications) but does not discuss emergency department (ED) visits specifically or state that ED visits do or do not cause chronic addiction. While it notes that exposure to prescription medications can be a risk factor, it does not link that risk to acute ED visits, so the cited material does not address the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes what SUD is, its general causes (brain changes, individual and social risk factors) and prevention, but it does not mention emergency department visits or acute-care ED presentations as a cause or non-cause of chronic addiction. Therefore the citation does not support or contradict the stated relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the emergency department for acute care increases or decreases the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as substance use behaviors, not by the ED visit itself.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3412375297411754, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8314470419330804, 'min_prob': 0.04780166948680103, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005215134584861865, 'prob_variance': 0.0667507537990923, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1103460063228021, 'avg_nll': 0.29359271742927934}

