171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that patients often consider alternatives (PCPs, UCCs, telehealth) and that clinicians at PCPs and UCCs frequently recommend or refer patients to the ED (e.g., 86.6% of those who contacted their PCP were advised to go to the ED; many who went to a UCC were sent on to the ED). That supports the mechanism that use of alternatives can lead to referrals to the ED. However, the article does not provide evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives causes a net increase in acute-care demand or ED visits overall (no causal or population-level effect of increased availability is demonstrated). It therefore does not fully support the claim that greater availability of alternatives increases ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report summarizes evidence showin