   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience stigma, fear of judgment, or lack of motivation, which directly increases their likelihood of avoiding healthcare. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction would decrease healthcare avoidance because the main causal drivers are alleviated. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://wyoleg.gov/InterimCommittee/2020/10-20201105Handoutfor6JtMHSACraig11.4.20.pdf', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter reports that people with substance use disorders are over-represented in general health care settings and often present for acute (injury, infection, overdose) or chronic medical problems—supporting the claim that chronic addiction leads to more contact with healthcare for non‑SUD reasons. However, the same source also emphasizes that many individuals do not seek specialty treatment for their substance use (due to not perceiving need, stigma, system separation, coverage barriers, etc.), so the literature does not uniformly show that chronic addiction reduces overall healthcare avoidance. Thus the citations partially support the motivation (increased healthcare contact for medical complications) but also note countervailing avoidance of SUD-specific care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/addiction-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source documents many medical complications of chronic drug use (infections, overdose, organ damage, co-occurring mental illness), which imply greater health needs, but it does not provide evidence about whether people with addiction actually seek care more or avoid healthcare. The citation therefore does not address the claimed relationship between chronic addiction and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and describes harms (e.g., overdose, adverse consequences) and risk factors, but the provided content does not present evidence about patterns of healthcare use or avoidance among people with chronic addiction. It neither supports nor contradicts the claim that addiction increases engagement with healthcare."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://wyoleg.gov/InterimCommittee/2020/10-20201105Handoutfor6JtMHSACraig11.4.20.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA and SAMHSA texts define substance use disorder, note medical harms (overdoses, health problems) and discuss treatment availability and stigma, but they do not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction seek healthcare more frequently or that addiction reduces healthcare avoidance. (They even note stigma can keep people from asking for help.) Therefore the motivation is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Mayo Clinic text outlines medical complications of chronic substance use that may require care but explicitly states that \\"people struggling with addiction usually deny they have a problem and hesitate to seek treatment.\\" The citation does not claim that chronic addiction leads to more frequent healthcare engagement and specifically notes avoidance/hesitancy, contradicting the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction often leads individuals to seek healthcare more frequently due to the medical complications associated with substance use, such as infections or overdoses. Therefore, chronic addiction actually encourages people to engage with healthcare services rather than avoid them. As a result, the relationship between chronic addiction and healthcare avoidance should be negative.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction often leads individuals to seek healthcare more frequently due to the medical complications associated with substance use, such as infections or overdoses. Therefore, chronic addiction actually encourages people to engage with healthcare services rather than avoid them. As a result, the relationship between chronic addiction and healthcare avoidance should be negative.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

282. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience impaired judgment, motivation, and executive functioning, which directly reduce their ability or willingness to comply with prescribed therapy. Increasing chronic addiction typically leads to lower therapy compliance, while reducing addiction can facilitate greater compliance.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2917861982862473, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes evidence-based pharmacological and behavioral treatments and notes that treatment retention/duration predicts better outcomes and that retention can be problematic for some medications (eg, low long-term retention on naltrexone). However it does not provide evidence that chronic addiction produces a physiological tolerance that generalizes to make therapeutic interventions (particularly behavioral therapies) less effective, nor that such a cross‑tolerance mechanism causes reduced therapy compliance. The specific causal mechanism claimed (cross‑tolerance to therapies leading to lower compliance) is not addressed in the cited article."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents that addiction is a chronic brain disease that produces neuroadaptations (tolerance, decreased reward sensitivity, impaired executive function) and that affected individuals often have difficulty complying with treatment and have high relapse rates. That supports a negative relationship between chronic addiction and therapy compliance. However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the motivation — namely that physiological tolerance to addictive substances generalizes as \\"cross-tolerance\\" to make behavioral and other therapeutic interventions less effective and thereby directly reduces compliance. The citations describe tolerance to drugs and neurobiological impairment of decision-making (which may reduce adherence), but they do not claim therapies themselves become physiologically less effective via cross-tolerance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that addiction involves tolerance to drugs, brain changes, denial/hesitance to seek treatment, relapse risk, and difficulties following treatment plans — all of which support a negative association between chronic addiction and engagement/compliance with therapy. However, the source does not describe the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation (physiological \\"cross‑tolerance\\" that generalizes to make behavioral or other therapeutic interventions less effective), so the proposed causal mechanism is unsupported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source documents that addiction is a chronic condition that produces physiological tolerance and causes impairment in functioning (difficulty fulfilling responsibilities, compulsive use, withdrawal) — which can plausibly reduce a person’s ability or motivation to adhere to treatment. However, the source does not claim or provide evidence that tolerance \\"generalizes\\" to make behavioral or other therapeutic interventions themselves less effective (i.e., a cross-tolerance to therapies) or that this specific physiological mechanism is why compliance falls. That mechanistic claim is unaddressed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source documents that substance use disorders involve physiological tolerance to the substance, make treatment challenging, and that recovery and ongoing commitment affect treatment outcomes — supporting a general link between chronic addiction and difficulties with treatment engagement/compliance. However, the citation does not describe the specific mechanism alleged (that physiological tolerance to addictive substances generalizes as \\"cross-tolerance\\" to behavioral or pharmacological therapies, reducing their effectiveness and thereby lowering compliance), nor does it claim that reducing addiction restores physiological responsiveness to non-substance therapies. Those mechanistic claims are unaddressed by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease because individuals with chronic addiction often develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions, rendering them less effective over time. As the body becomes accustomed to repeated exposure to addictive substances, it generalizes this tolerance to other forms of treatment, including behavioral and pharmacological therapies, which diminishes the perceived benefits and reduces motivation to comply with therapy protocols. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with lower therapy compliance due to this cross-tolerance effect, whereas reducing addiction may restore the body's responsiveness to therapeutic interventions and improve compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease because individuals with chronic addiction often develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions, rendering them less effective over time. As the body becomes accustomed to repeated exposure to addictive substances, it generalizes this tolerance to other forms of treatment, including behavioral and pharmacological therapies, which diminishes the perceived benefits and reduces motivation to comply with therapy protocols. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with lower therapy compliance due to this cross-tolerance effect, whereas reducing addiction may restore the body's responsiveness to therapeutic interventions and improve compliance.", 'perplexity': 1.5066974702506495, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7715710306573372, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03224499465635279, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004735732062978135, 'prob_variance': 0.08513918551034122, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9781124349525312, 'avg_nll': 0.40992014982366265}

