   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.20809091158611107, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK232972/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.780905061221979, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents that chronic substance use produces lasting neuroadaptations in brain regions (prefrontal cortex, basal ganglia, hippocampal/learning networks, etc.) that underlie learning, memory and executive function. Those neurobiological changes provide a direct mechanism by which chronic addiction can impair cognitive functions relevant to acquiring and using knowledge. The source does not support the claim that no direct causal link exists; at minimum the direction \\"chronic addiction → changes in knowledge-related cognition\\" is contradicted. (The citation does not provide evidence that knowledge directly causes chronic addiction.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK232972/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly identifies education/academic achievement as a risk/protective factor that precedes and predicts drug use (and discusses antecedent personality/academic variables linked to initiation and escalation). It treats such variables as potential causal contributors (while noting complex, multicausal pathways). Thus the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between addiction and knowledge/education; rather it presents evidence that educational attainment/academic factors are associated with and can precede drug use, contradicting the assertion of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review (Addiction and Cognition) presents substantial evidence that chronic drug use alters brain systems for learning, memory, attention and reasoning and produces lasting cognitive deficits (including impacts on declarative memory, working memory, learning and recall). That supports a direct causal pathway from chronic addiction to changes in knowledge-related cognitive functions, contradicting the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review advances Bandura’s reciprocal-determinism framework, explicitly treating personal factors (including cognitive processes/knowledge) and drug-using behavior as mutually influencing one another. It describes cognitive appraisal and “personal factors” as causal determinants of drug use and also describes how prolonged drug use alters cognitive processes and the organism. Thus the source supports direct, bidirectional links between addiction and cognitive/personal factors, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction (causes, symptoms, treatment, and broad life impacts) but does not discuss \\"knowledge\\" or any direct causal links between addiction and knowledge. It neither supports nor contradicts the claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not cause knowledge to change, no