dden physiological crises like heart attacks, nor that impaired cognition inevitably causes loss of vital-system regulation. Many neurocognitive disorders are described as resulting from medical events (e.g., stroke, TBI) rather than as direct causes of systemic acute events, so the motivation’s general and absolute claims are not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001401.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited MedlinePlus entry presents neurocognitive (cognitive) disorders as resulting from medical conditions (e.g., stroke, hypoxia, infections, heart disease, withdrawal) and lists acute events among causes, not as outcomes caused by the cognitive disorder. It does not support the claim that cognitive disorders directly cause sudden physiological crises (heart attacks, seizures) or make such acute events inevitable; the citation implies the opposite causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes definitions, epidemiology, biomarkers, and comorbidities of neurocognitive disorders (including vascular contributions to dementia) and notes features such as syncope or autonomic dysfunction in some syndromes. It does not provide evidence that cognitive disorders directly cause acute physiological crises (e.g., heart attacks or seizures) or that impaired cognition inevitably leads to loss of vital-system regulation and acute events. The claim of direct causation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause acute events because the disruption in normal brain function leads to sudden physiological crises, such as heart attacks or seizures. When cognitive processes are impaired, the body loses its ability to regulate 