310. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment causes declines in memory, judgment, planning and daily-task performance (e.g., missing events, misplacing items, falling behind on bills), which plausibly increases the risk of errors in tasks like medication management. However, the source does not describe the proposed physiological feedback mechanism, biochemical brain changes caused by repeated medication errors, or a cyclical process in which incorrect medication use worsens cognitive disorder. It therefore supports only the behavioral link (cognitive impairment → greater likelihood of everyday errors) and not the physiological feedback claims in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment causes memory loss, poor judgment and trouble following instructions — factors that plausibly lead to incorrect use of medication (supporting a positive link). However, the citation does not describe any physiological feedback mechanism in which medication errors cause biochemical brain changes that worsen cognition, nor does it claim that treating cognition alone would break such a cycle. Those mechanistic claims are unsupported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment can affect memory, planning, following instructions and remembering when to take medicines — supporting a positive link from cognitive disorder to incorrect medication use. However, the citation does not describe the proposed physiological feedback mechanism, biochemical brain changes from repeated medication errors, or the claim that treating cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve medication misuse; it even notes medications can cause or worsen cognitive symptoms (reverse/alternative pathways)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Alzheimer’s Association MCI page does not describe a physiological feedback loop whereby cognitive disorder causes repeated medication errors that biochemically worsen cognition. It emphasizes that people with MCI typically retain the ability to perform most everyday activities (arguing against a direct increase in medication errors) and notes that medications can cause cognitive impairment (the reverse direction). The citation does not support the claimed causal, self‑amplifying mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. As cognitive disorder increases, these physiological changes further promote medication errors, suggesting that addressing cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve issues of incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. As cognitive disorder increases, these physiological changes further promote medication errors, suggesting that addressing cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve issues of incorrect use of medication.", 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052