311. Cognitive disorder-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.3053180529493376, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.701335680051338, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that larger, more active social networks are associated with lower risks of cognitive decline (e.g., each additional network member reduced conversion from MCI to dementia, aHR=0.82; having friends or college‑educated family members was protective). It therefore opposes the motivation\'s claim that larger networks increase cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review and included meta-analyses report that larger social networks, greater social engagement and more social contacts are generally associated with LOWER risk of cognitive decline and ADRD (e.g., Evans et al. 2019; Penninkilampi et al. 2018; Samtani et al. 2022; Joshi et al./Jeste et al. scoping review). The literature summarized also notes possible bidirectionality (cognitive decline can lead to reduced social connections), but it does not support the claim that larger social networks increase cognitive disorder by overwhelming mental resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Röhr et al.) reports that smaller social networks and shrinking networks are associated with worse cognitive function in the oldest-old — i.e., social isolation predicts lower cognition — and interprets social integration as protective. The authors note the relationship may be reciprocal (cognitive decline can lead to withdrawal), but nowhere do the citations support the claim that larger social networks increase cognitive disorder. Thus the motivation’s assertion that increasing network size raises cognitive disorder is contradicted by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can directly contribute to higher levels of cognitive disorder, as larger networks may expose individuals to greater cognitive demands and social stressors, ultimately overwhelming their mental resources. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these external pressures, thereby decreasing the likelihood or severity of cognitive disorder. This suggests that managing the size of social network could be an effective strategy for mitigating cognitive disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can directly contribute to higher levels of cognitive disorder, as larger networks may expose individuals to greater cognitive demands and social stressors, ultimately overwhelming their mental resources. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these external pressures, thereby decreasing the likelihood or severity of cognitive disorder. This suggests that managing the size of social network could be an effective strategy for mitigating cognitive disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.5824889155648854, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7600993318014218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01499801103060699, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024327677270645563, 'prob_variance': 0.09321911345677451, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1031469672214835, 'avg_nll': 0.45899887062928446}

