eason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it does not support (or contradict) the claimed absence of a causal relationship between \\"Cognitive disorder\\" and \\"Knowledge.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes neurocognitive disorders as producing declines in specific cognitive domains (including deficits in knowledge/naming in semantic variant FTD and impairments in memory, language, etc.), indicating cognitive disorders can cause loss of knowledge. It also cites “cognitive reserve,” education, and mental stimulation (forms of knowledge/learning) as modifiable factors associated with reduced risk of dementia, implying knowledge/education can influence the likelihood or progression of cognitive disorder. Thus the sources do not support a “no causal relationship” claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Knowledge, nor does Knowledge cause Cognitive disorder, as defined here. Without specific definitions or context for either variable, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2958944320357286, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8285118850604686, 'min_prob': 0.10042475689814016, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024018713585034755, 'prob_variance': 0.05676498410075431, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1874607829962058, 'avg_nll': 0.2592011378497457}

