   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/', 'https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that better clinical information, trial data, and clinician training can improve appropriate prescribing and reduce inappropriate medication use, which partly supports the claim that improving diagnostic/clinical resources can improve correct medication use. However, the source does not equate “more opportunities to prescribe” (or simply greater prescribing volume) with more correct use — in fact it notes that increased drug coverage/use can raise the risk of adverse drug events. The paper also does not specifically treat “diagnostic resources” as defined in the motivation. Therefore the motivation\'s core claim (that increasing prescribing opportunities inherently increases correct medication use and reduces incorrect use) is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources leads to more correct medication use. Instead, it highlights causes of medication errors (prescribing, ordering, administration, poor communication, inaccurate patient information) and recommends systems interventions (CPOE, CDSS, medication reconciliation, pharmacist involvement) to reduce errors. It also notes that a greater number of prescribed drugs (polypharmacy) is associated with a higher incidence of medication errors, which contradicts the motivation\'s claim that more opportunities to prescribe (from more diagnostic resources) would reduce incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11913332/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references discuss interventions that improve medication safety (EHRs, clinical decision support, barcode-assisted administration, training and reporting systems) and provide evidence these can reduce medication errors. They do not address \\"diagnostic resources\\" as defined in the motivation nor provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources leads to more correct medication use (or to the causal chain described). The motivation\'s specific claim about diagnostic resources increasing appropriate prescribing (and thereby reducing incorrect use) is not supported by the provided citations. Additionally, the stated relationship type (POSITIVE) conflicts with the motivation text (which describes a reduction in incorrect use), a mismatch not resolved by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2012/08/improving-medication-safety", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ACOG citation recommends understanding a patient’s condition/diagnosis and using clinical decision support, medication reconciliation, e-prescribing, and patient education to reduce medication errors — supporting the general idea that better diagnostic information and related tools can decrease incorrect medication use. However, the source does not endorse the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation (that increasing diagnostic resources gives more opportunities to prescribe which then increases correct use). It also highlights limits and implementation challenges (e.g., alert overrides, unintended errors), so the exact causal framing in the motivation is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited HealthIT.gov material reports that electronic health records and decision-support diagnostic resources reduce medical and medication errors (e.g., \\"63% reported fewer medication errors,\\" \\"60% decrease in near-miss medication events,\\" alerts for conflicts, built-in safeguards against adverse prescribing). These findings indicate increased diagnostic resources are associated with fewer incorrect medication uses, not a positive increase as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources ensures that healthcare providers have more opportunities to prescribe medications, which naturally leads to a higher likelihood that medications will be used correctly. With more diagnostic tools available, patients are more likely to receive the right prescriptions and use them as intended, reducing the chance of incorrect medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

