   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no known direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause need for autonomy or vice versa. Without definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, no directional causality can be established between them.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/what-should-clinicians-do-when-patients-autonomy-undermines-her-being-treated-equitably/2021-02', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that clinical decision support and other diagnostic technologies can foster overreliance and reduce independent clinical judgment (e.g., automation bias, cases where clinicians deferred to erroneous CDS), which supports the claim in part (reduced autonomous decision-making by clinicians). However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increased access to diagnostic resources reduces individuals\' (patients\') \\"need for autonomy\\" or intrinsic motivation to manage their own health. The chapter also describes ways health IT can promote patient engagement, and it flags mixed effects and concerns rather than a demonstrated causal decrease in patients\' desire for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that diagnostic technologies and decision-support can foster overreliance and reduced independent judgment (automation bias) — evidence that increased diagnostic resources can d