ement and data sources (medical records, claims, EHR, surveys, patient report) and explicitly notes that diagnostic data reflect encounters and access to care (i.e., affect identification/recording) and may miss undiagnosed conditions. It frames diagnostic resources as influencing measurement/ascertainment of multimorbidity rather than causing the underlying multiple chronic conditions, which are described as resulting from health conditions and risk factors. Thus the sources support the claim that diagnostic resources affect detection/measurement but are not a direct cause of multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity/comorbidity as arising from underlying biological, social and genetic factors (and lists causes such as infections, metabolic changes, social status, etc.) and discusses how diagnostic methods and data sources affect identification and measurement of comorbidity. That supports the motivation\'s point that diagnostic resources influence detection/management rather than being the root cause. However, the source does not explicitly state that diagnostic resources can never causally produce multimorbidity (and even lists iatrogenesis as a possible cause of additional conditions), so it does not fully rule out causal pathways implied by health care processes. Therefore the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.875, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. Diagnostic resources may affect the identification or management of multi-morbidity, but do not cause the presence or absence of multiple chronic conditions. Multi-morbidity results from underlying health conditions and risk facto