375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase through the direct acquisition of information and skills during formal education. As education level rises, individuals are exposed to more learning opportunities, leading to greater knowledge. Conversely, lower education levels result in less knowledge acquisition. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2623479865734777, 'citations': ['https://www.wgu.edu/blog/five-educational-learning-theories2005.html', 'https://www.edresearch.edu.au/summaries-explainers/explainers/knowledge-central-learning', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED610428.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.714676464759919, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.wgu.edu/blog/five-educational-learning-theories2005.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The source describes educational learning theories (including cognitive factors like memory and problem‑solving) and how teaching can support learning, but it does not provide evidence that individuals with higher education possess inherently greater cognitive capacities nor that such innate differences—rather than educational experiences—explain greater knowledge. The specific causal claim about innate cognitive advantages across education levels is not addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED610428.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review acknowledges internal learner factors (including \\"cognitive intelligence\\" and heredity) as influences on learning outcomes, which partially aligns with the motivation\'s reference to innate capacities. However, the paper primarily emphasizes teaching methods, learning environments, and pedagogical practices as drivers of knowledge gain and explicitly discusses enhancing cognitive capabilities through instruction. It does not support the stronger claim that higher education level causes greater knowledge primarily because of inherent intelligence/memory independent of educational experiences."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase primarily because individuals with higher education levels tend to have inherently greater cognitive capacities, such as intelligence and memory, which enable them to absorb and retain more knowledge regardless of the specific content taught. As education level rises, this innate cognitive advantage becomes more pronounced, resulting in greater knowledge acquisition. Conversely, those with lower education levels possess fewer of these inherent abilities, leading to reduced knowledge, independent of educational experiences.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase primarily because individuals with higher education levels tend to have inherently greater cognitive capacities, such as intelligence and memory, which enable them to absorb and retain more knowledge regardless of the specific content taught. As education level rises, this innate cognitive advantage becomes more pronounced, resulting in greater knowledge acquisition. Conversely, those with lower education levels possess fewer of these inherent abilities, leading to reduced knowledge, independent of educational experiences.', 'perplexity': 1.4057473228351143, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7898273545422965, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05003774228377375, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0005317033714012285, 'prob_variance': 0.06882646605915764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.053403041707978, 'avg_nll': 0.3405690637495303}

376. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not cause willingness of social network, nor does willingness of social network cause education level, based on the provided definitions and CLD variables. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy, size of social network, or knowledge, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship should be included between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.1010766/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8535783/', 'https://www.americansurveycenter.org/research/the-college-connection-the-education-divide-in-american-social-and-community-life/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7491920/', 'https://ggie.berkeley.edu/student-well-being/sel-for-students-social-awareness-and-relationship-skills/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.1010766/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews effects of social media use on students\' affective states (anxiety, depression, stress) and discusses perceived social support from social media, but it does not examine education level as a cause of greater willingness of an individual\'s social network to help, nor does it provide evidence that educated people are seen as more trustworthy/resourceful leading friends or family to offer more support. The specific causal relationship in the motivation is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8535783/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines social media use, open learning, engagement, and academic performance among students; it does not analyze effects of an individual\'s education level on their social network\'s willingness to help, nor claims that education increases perceived trustworthiness/resourcefulness leading to more support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7491920/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines education assortativity, network composition, tie strength (closeness/liking), and health outcomes (exercise, BMI, self-rated health), but it does not measure or test \\"willingness of an individual\'s social network to help\\" as an outcome. The discussion even notes that less-educated people tend to rely more on friends and family (strong ties) while more-educated people rely more on acquaintances, which does not support the claimed mechanism that higher education increases alters\' willingness to help. Hence the citation does not provide direct evidence for the stated causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.americansurveycenter.org/research/the-college-connection-the-education-divide-in-american-social-and-community-life/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report documents a clear association: college graduates report larger social networks and higher levels of perceived social support (e.g., more people they can “turn to”), which is consistent with higher education being linked to greater willingness-of-others-to-help as reported by respondents. However, the sources do not demonstrate a direct causal effect nor provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that others perceive educated people as more trustworthy/resourceful and therefore are more willing to help). The evidence is correlational and the proposed perception-based causal pathway is not substantiated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ggie.berkeley.edu/student-well-being/sel-for-students-social-awareness-and-relationship-skills/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses how social-emotional learning (SEL) and relationship skills (e.g., empathy, cooperative learning) improve students\' relationships and prosocial behavior, but it does not address formal education level nor claim that higher education makes others perceive someone as more trustworthy or resourceful and therefore more likely to help them. The specific causal link between education level and others\' willingness to help is not presented."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher education level directly causes an increase in the willingness of an individual's social network to help them. This is because educated people are perceived as more trustworthy and resourceful, prompting their friends and family to be more willing to offer support.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher education level directly causes an increase in the willingness of an individual's social network to help them. This is because educated people are perceived as more trustworthy and resourceful, prompting their friends and family to be more willing to offer support.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

377. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause education level, in a direct causal relationship. While these variables may be associated in some contexts, there is no established direct causal mechanism connecting them. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by other variables such as health literacy or proactive attitude, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/', 'https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.ado6759', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10704852/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8602089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and sources describe autonomy as a basic psychological need (SDT) and show that autonomy-supportive teaching in (higher) education increases students\' motivation, engagement, and resilience. They also discuss autonomy-supportive practices in higher-education settings. However, none of the citations provide evidence or a theoretical argument that individuals who attain higher levels of education develop a stronger need for autonomy (i.e., that greater educational attainment causally or consistently increases a person\'s desire for autonomy). The specific claim linking education level to an increased need for autonomy is not addressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10704852/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that particular educational practices (task-supported learning and self-assessment) can increase learners\' autonomy and foster independent, self-directed learning. However, it does not test or provide evidence for the specific claim that attaining higher levels of education (education level per se) directly increases a person\'s need for autonomy across personal and professional life. In other words, the citations support the mechanism (educational methods can encourage autonomy) but do not establish the proposed causal relationship between overall education level and a stronger need for autonomy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "As individuals attain higher levels of education, they develop a stronger need for autonomy because educational systems encourage independent thinking and self-direction. Therefore, more education directly increases a person's desire for autonomy in both personal and professional life.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As individuals attain higher levels of education, they develop a stronger need for autonomy because educational systems encourage independent thinking and self-direction. Therefore, more education directly increases a person's desire for autonomy in both personal and professional life.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

378. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education level typically leads to reduced language and cultural barriers, as education often improves language proficiency and cultural understanding. Conversely, lower education level may increase language and cultural barriers. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6798053/', 'https://commonthread.antioch.edu/the-impact-of-inclusive-education-for-english-language-learners/', 'https://soeonline.american.edu/blog/culturally-and-linguistically-diverse-students/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6798053/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper argues that increased bilingualism/biculturalism and related awareness are assets that improve metalinguistic sensitivity, intercultural competence, social reinforcement and the ability to navigate cross‑cultural situations. It presents increased awareness and education about languages/cultures as enabling better communication and reduced stereotyping, not as increasing language and cultural barriers. The sources do not support the claim that higher education causes more encounter/experience of such barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels directly increase awareness of linguistic diversity and cultural differences, which makes individuals more likely to encounter and experience language and cultural barriers. As people become more educated, they are exposed to more complex cultural contexts, amplifying the challenges of navigating different languages and customs.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels directly increase awareness of linguistic diversity and cultural differences, which makes individuals more likely to encounter and experience language and cultural barriers. As people become more educated, they are exposed to more complex cultural contexts, amplifying the challenges of navigating different languages and customs.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

379. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally contribute to a decreased risk of developing cognitive disorders due to increased cognitive reserve and improved health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels causally increase the risk of cognitive disorders. This relationship is supported by mechanisms such as enhanced cognitive stimulation and healthier lifestyle choices associated with higher education.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459869/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10702652/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424268/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher educational attainment (and higher cognitive ability) is associated with lower, not higher, risk of later mental-disorder diagnoses in this population of Norwegian males. The authors explicitly find that individuals with only compulsory education have the highest risk and discuss mechanisms linking low education/low ability to greater stress and adverse conditions — the opposite of the claimed pathway that increasing education raises risk via academic stress. The paper does not provide evidence that higher education increases cognitive-disorder risk through increased cognitive load."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459869/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that higher educational attainment is generally associated with better cognitive performance and a lower risk of cognitive impairment (discussing education as contributing to cognitive/brain reserve). It evaluates positive effects of education across SCD, MCI, and AD and does not provide evidence that higher education increases cognitive load, stress, or the likelihood of developing cognitive disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10702652/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article discusses how negative emotions can increase cognitive load and may impair learning/performance, but explicitly states there is no empirical evidence linking increased cognitive load from poor emotional wellbeing during education to the development of cognitive disorders. It also cites literature suggesting educational attainment may be protective against psychiatric disorders and emphasizes confounding (e.g., socioeconomic status) and the need for further research. Thus the claim that higher education level increases likelihood of cognitive disorders is not supported and is contradicted by the citations\' conclusions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7424268/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds that higher education is associated with higher baseline cognition and delayed onset of accelerated cognitive decline (i.e., increased healthy life expectancy free of cognitive pathology), consistent with a protective \\"cognitive reserve\\" effect. It does not support the claim that greater education increases risk of cognitive disorders via academic stress; on the contrary, the evidence in the citation indicates the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review concludes that greater educational attainment is associated with higher cognitive function across adulthood and with lower late-life dementia risk, not increased risk. It also finds little or inconsistent evidence that education accelerates cognitive decline (and notes apparent faster declines near diagnosis are likely methodological artifacts). The sources do not support the claim that higher education increases cognitive disorders via academic stress or cognitive load."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can increase cognitive load and stress due to demanding academic environments, which in turn might directly contribute to the development of cognitive disorders. Therefore, as education level rises, the likelihood of cognitive disorders also increases because of the mental strain associated with advanced learning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can increase cognitive load and stress due to demanding academic environments, which in turn might directly contribute to the development of cognitive disorders. Therefore, as education level rises, the likelihood of cognitive disorders also increases because of the mental strain associated with advanced learning.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

380. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels generally increase knowledge, coping skills, and access to resources, reducing the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, lower education levels can limit these protective factors, increasing the risk of chronic addiction. This is a direct, causally supported relationship observed across populations.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.30108068156589624, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7422501204895913, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article describes addiction causes and risk factors (family history, mental health disorders, environment/peer pressure, early use, drug properties, and brain changes from repeated drug use) but does not mention education level, cognitive workload, or the proposed mechanism that higher education \\"exhausts the brain\'s reward system\\" and thereby reduces chronic addiction risk. The cited source therefore does not support the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter presents neurobiological mechanisms linking executive function (prefrontal cortex) to addiction risk — impaired executive control increases vulnerability, and differences in brain function can influence risk — which is compatible with the idea that cognitive/decision-making capacity relates to addiction risk. However, the source does not discuss education level, “cognitive workload,” or the specific mechanism claimed (that greater cognitive demand “exhausts” the brain\'s reward system and thereby protects against chronic addiction). Nor does it provide evidence that lower education increases receptivity of reward pathways for the reasons given. Thus the general link between cognition/executive function and addiction is supported, but the specific education → cognitive exhaustion → reduced addiction mechanism and claims about education level per se are unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source identifies school-related factors as influencing addiction risk (e.g., \\"good grades\\" listed as a protective factor and \\"struggling in school\\" as a risk factor), which is consistent with a negative association between education-related measures and addiction. However, the source does not provide any support for the proposed mechanism (higher education → greater cognitive workload → exhaustion of the brain\'s reward system → reduced susceptibility). That specific cognitive‑exhaustion explanation is unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The MedlinePlus page identifies school problems/trouble in school as a risk factor for substance use, which directionally aligns with higher education being associated with lower addiction risk. However, the cited source does not provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claim (that higher education imposes cognitive workload that \\"exhausts\\" the brain\'s reward system and thereby reduces susceptibility). The proposed cognitive‑exhaustion mechanism is unaddressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Education level negatively influences chronic addiction rates because individuals with higher education levels typically have more demanding cognitive workloads, which purportedly exhaust the brain's reward system. This cognitive exhaustion is theorized to reduce susceptibility to chronic addiction, as the brain becomes less responsive to addictive substances. Conversely, lower education level is associated with less cognitive stimulation, leaving the brain's reward pathways more receptive to addictive behaviors, thereby increasing the risk of chronic addiction.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Education level negatively influences chronic addiction rates because individuals with higher education levels typically have more demanding cognitive workloads, which purportedly exhaust the brain's reward system. This cognitive exhaustion is theorized to reduce susceptibility to chronic addiction, as the brain becomes less responsive to addictive substances. Conversely, lower education level is associated with less cognitive stimulation, leaving the brain's reward pathways more receptive to addictive behaviors, thereby increasing the risk of chronic addiction.", 'perplexity': 1.5382285920028806, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7913237963638989, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00475749549828756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004534806941202337, 'prob_variance': 0.0906495768121846, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1566531361570864, 'avg_nll': 0.43063148942988827}

381. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. Higher education levels provide individuals with the skills and knowledge necessary to understand and use health information, thereby increasing health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels limit these abilities, reducing health literacy. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the given list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216036/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly identifies low educational attainment as a major determinant of limited health literacy and reports that health literacy is positively associated with education and income. It does not report that higher education leads to neglect of basic health knowledge; instead the evidence in the source contradicts the proposed negative relationship and mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds the opposite overall — higher formal education is associated with higher health literacy (e.g., 72.2% of patients who completed college had adequate health literacy versus 25% of those with some high school or less). While the paper notes that inadequate health literacy can occur among some highly educated individuals and reports an interaction where highly educated people with low health literacy had worse outcomes, it does not support the claim that higher education causes lower health literacy via distraction by specialized subjects. The proposed mechanism is not described or evidenced in the citation, and the overall association reported is positive (more education → higher health literacy)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher educational attainment is associated with higher (not lower) health literacy (e.g., 72.2% of patients who completed college had adequate health literacy vs 18.4% for some high school or less). While the paper notes that some highly educated individuals can still have inadequate health literacy, it does not support the claim that greater education leads to lower health literacy; rather it shows the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216036/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited IOM report shows that health literacy is complex and that high educational attainment alone may not guarantee health literacy, but its epidemiologic findings link lower education to limited health literacy (and show higher education is generally associated with better literacy). It does not provide evidence that greater education causes lower health literacy; rather it contradicts the claim that higher education leads to neglect of basic health knowledge."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels often focus on specialized subjects and complex theories, which can distract individuals from learning about basic health practices. As a result, people with more education may neglect foundational health knowledge, leading to lower health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels often focus on specialized subjects and complex theories, which can distract individuals from learning about basic health practices. As a result, people with more education may neglect foundational health knowledge, leading to lower health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

382. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause frailty. Any effect of education level on frailty is likely mediated by variables such as health literacy, healthy lifestyle, or access to healthcare, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between education level and frailty to include in the diagram.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4447813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10267459/', 'https://gh.bmj.com/content/9/8/e015173'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4447813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that lower education is associated with greater risk of frailty worsening (i.e., higher education correlates with lower frailty risk), so the negative relationship between education level and frailty is supported as an observational association. However, the paper does not support the stated causal mechanism — that \\"advanced schooling strengthens the body\'s muscles and bones through mental effort\\" — nor the claim that education reduces frailty \\"regardless of other lifestyle factors.\\" The study explicitly investigates and finds that lifestyle, health and social participation partly mediate the association, discusses potential reverse causality and other explanations, and does not claim a direct biological strengthening effect from mental effort."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Mendelian randomization study provides evidence that higher (genetically predicted) educational attainment is causally associated with lower frailty (negative effect). However, the paper does not support the specific mechanistic claim that \\"advanced schooling strengthens the body\'s muscles and bones through mental effort\\" nor the stronger assertion that education reduces frailty \\"regardless of other lifestyle factors.\\" The authors explicitly call for research to uncover the pathways linking socioeconomic factors (including education) to frailty; they only adjusted for BMI in multivariable analyses, which does not demonstrate independence from all lifestyle or environmental mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10267459/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between frailty and cognitive performance and uses education as a covariate; it does not provide evidence that higher education directly reduces frailty or that \\"mental effort\\" from schooling strengthens muscles or bones. The study is cross-sectional (authors explicitly note causality cannot be inferred) and does not test the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article lists low education level as a socio-demographic risk factor for developing frailty, implying an inverse (protective) association between higher education and frailty. However, the source does not claim the specific causal mechanism proposed (that mental effort from advanced schooling directly strengthens muscles and bones), nor does it support the stronger assertion that education alone reduces frailty \\"regardless of other lifestyle factors.\\" The article emphasizes frailty\'s multifactorial etiology (nutrition, physical activity, disease, etc.), contradicting the exclusive, direct causal pathway asserted in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause a reduction in frailty because advanced schooling strengthens the body's muscles and bones through mental effort. Thus, simply attaining more education makes a person physically less frail, regardless of other lifestyle factors.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher education levels directly cause a reduction in frailty because advanced schooling strengthens the body's muscles and bones through mental effort. Thus, simply attaining more education makes a person physically less frail, regardless of other lifestyle factors.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

383. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels typically lead to greater understanding of medication instructions, reducing incorrect use. Conversely, lower education levels are causally linked to higher rates of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.26706844261288976, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7609944887407211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article supports the claim that advanced technologies (CPOE, CDSS, barcode identification, automated dispensing) and system-level interventions reduce medication errors. However, the source does not provide evidence that individuals with higher education have greater access to those technologies, nor that this access mediates a negative effect of education on incorrect medication use. It also emphasizes the importance of patient education and comprehension in preventing patient-related errors, which contradicts the assertion that the relationship operates independently of individual understanding."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article supports the claim that advanced health IT (e.g., barcode medication administration, CPOE with clinical decision support) can reduce medication administration errors in clinical settings. However, it does not provide evidence that individuals with higher formal education are more likely to have access to these technologies, nor that education level causally reduces incorrect medication use via tech access independent of personal comprehension or engagement. The source focuses on nursing practice and system-level interventions, and it also notes limitations of technology (e.g., alert fatigue, overrides) and the continuing importance of clinician/patient understanding — leaving the key links asserted in the motivation (education → greater access to tech → lower patient misuse, independent of comprehension) unaddressed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that higher education (and adequate health literacy) is associated with reduced misunderstanding of OTC labels and prescription instructions (consistent with a negative relationship between education and incorrect medication use). However, the paper does not support the stated mechanism — it contains no evidence that the effect is driven by greater access to advanced healthcare technologies (e.g., electronic prescribing) or that the relationship operates independently of personal comprehension or engagement. In fact, the study attributes the association to health literacy, reading behaviors, and comprehension rather than to technology access."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study links education level to several measures of medication knowledge in bivariate analyses (e.g., knowing generic names, purpose, strength, and whether participants read leaflets), which is consistent with a negative relationship between education and incorrect medication use. However, multivariable analyses did not find education to be a consistent independent predictor of key outcomes (naming medications or stating purpose). Crucially, the paper does not support the claimed mechanism — there is no evidence about access to electronic prescription systems or advanced technologies reducing incorrect use, and the study emphasizes personal health literacy, comprehension, and the role of pharmacists rather than technology. The motivation’s claim that the effect operates independently of individual understanding is therefore not supported (and is contrary to the study’s emphasis)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels are more likely to have access to advanced healthcare technologies, such as electronic prescription systems, which automatically reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication regardless of individual knowledge. Conversely, those with lower education levels have limited access to such technologies, resulting in higher rates of incorrect use of medication. This relationship operates independently of personal comprehension or engagement with medication instructions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels are more likely to have access to advanced healthcare technologies, such as electronic prescription systems, which automatically reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication regardless of individual knowledge. Conversely, those with lower education levels have limited access to such technologies, resulting in higher rates of incorrect use of medication. This relationship operates independently of personal comprehension or engagement with medication instructions.', 'perplexity': 1.3662556899220675, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8132090496307205, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09012385080603168, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003209471037669708, 'prob_variance': 0.07132555303967439, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9214154100254011, 'avg_nll': 0.31207392512725296}

384. Education level-[NEGATIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2559444867862884, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-publhealth-031816-044628', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/understanding-the-relationship-between-education-and-health/', 'https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7756988506824607, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports a negative association between education and poorer health outcomes (including more chronic conditions and biological risk markers) — i.e., higher education is linked with lower multi-morbidity. However, the article does not endorse the specific motivation’s mechanistic claim that education \\"directly enhances individuals’ innate biological resilience\\" by increasing cognitive capacity which then strengthens immune function and cellular repair, nor that this effect is independent of socioeconomic context or health behaviors. The review emphasizes multiple mediating pathways (economic resources, health behaviors, social-psychological factors, access to care, stress and contextual influences) and notes correlations with biological measures, but it does not provide evidence for the narrow, direct cognitive→immune/cellular repair mechanism or for independence from external factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a website privacy notice and contains no information about education, biological resilience, immune function, cellular repair, or multi-morbidity. Therefore the citation does not address or support the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level influences multi-morbidity by directly enhancing individuals’ innate biological resilience to disease. Higher education level increases cognitive capacity, which in turn strengthens immune function and cellular repair mechanisms, thereby reducing the likelihood of developing multiple chronic conditions. Conversely, lower education level diminishes these inherent physiological protections, resulting in a higher prevalence of multi-morbidity. This relationship is independent of external factors such as socioeconomic status or health behaviors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level influences multi-morbidity by directly enhancing individuals’ innate biological resilience to disease. Higher education level increases cognitive capacity, which in turn strengthens immune function and cellular repair mechanisms, thereby reducing the likelihood of developing multiple chronic conditions. Conversely, lower education level diminishes these inherent physiological protections, resulting in a higher prevalence of multi-morbidity. This relationship is independent of external factors such as socioeconomic status or health behaviors.', 'perplexity': 1.3129599419569344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290984499056712, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10414064282912527, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002297252977765577, 'prob_variance': 0.06550758031629655, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8957977292534078, 'avg_nll': 0.27228408606388904}

