   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2907773313493499, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5836010/', 'https://www.lifescied.org/doi/10.1187/cbe.19-09-0186'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7397663252526665, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citations focus on shame and its relationship with self-esteem (and moderators like age or clinical status). They do not examine education level or feelings of fear, nor do they test or rule out a direct causal effect of education on shame or fear. Therefore the claim about education level not directly causing fear and shame is not addressed by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5836010/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study shows that fear, shame and other negative emotions in educational settings arise from appraisals, specific events (e.g., feedback, assessment), task demands and individual differences (age, personality, intensity), and that mediators determine whether emotions help or hinder learning. That evidence supports the claim that emotions are context- and mediator-dependent rather than being produced by a single factor. However, the paper does not directly examine or test \\"education level\\" as a causal variable across populations, so it does not definitively demonstrate the absence of any direct causal effect of education level on fear or shame."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. While these variables may be correlated through other factors, there is no direct causal mechanism from education level to feelings of fear and shame without considering mediating variables such as health literacy or social network size. Therefore, a direct causal relationship does not exist in this context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4309863852544564, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7920701899889917, 'min_prob': 0.0961638295318724, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025310874472102007, 'prob_variance': 0.08455145642664964, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9844599565832183, 'avg_nll': 0.35836398638122485}

