387. Education level-[NONE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.26824751169967825, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629612001671', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK596379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824018/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7590684794891787, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on education’s effects on health, mortality, and mediating pathways (economic resources, health behaviors, social-psychological factors, access to care) but does not examine informal care (provision or receipt) as an outcome nor state that education does or does not directly cause changes in informal caregiving. Therefore the sources do not address the specific claim that education has no direct causal effect on informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Informal Care Model attributes informal caregiving to care recipients\' need, caregivers\' dispositions (attitudes, norms, perceived barriers), and contextual factors (family, network, community). It does not posit a direct causal pathway from an individual\'s education level to informal care; socioeconomic or education differences are mentioned only as characteristics that could be explained via the model (i.e., via mediators such as employment, time, norms, networks, competence). Thus the source supports the claim that any association of education with informal care is likely mediated rather than a direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824018/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 