   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2872567675323976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8030630683460378, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and included studies show mixed findings. Several studies of equipment-only telehomecare/telekit interventions (e.g., Hæsum et al. 2017; Korsbakke Emtekaer Haesum et al. 2016; Lilholt et al. 2016) found no significant between‑group changes in functional health literacy attributable to the devices themselves, and the review explicitly emphasizes that content (education, social support, stakeholder involvement) matters more than delivery mode. At the same time, many technology‑mediated interventions that combined equipment or platforms with educational content (web programs, apps, text campaigns, portals) did produce increases in health literacy or disease‑specific knowledge. Thus the citations support that equipment alone is not a direct causal driver of health literacy, but they also show that equipment as a delivery vehicle within content‑rich interventions can be associated with literacy gains — so a strict “no relationship” claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies education, socioeconomic status, access to information, communication, and related social determinants as the key drivers of health literacy and describes interventions (information design, patient–provider communication, skill-building) that change literacy. It does not mention \\"equipment\\" as a determinant or provide evidence that equipment causes changes in health literacy. Thus the sources support the motivation\'s emphasis on knowledge/education/access as determinants and provide no support for an equipment → health literacy causal link, but they do not explicitly investigate or rule out equipment, so the claim that there is no causal link is not fully addressed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NNLM source defines health literacy in terms of abilities, information/services, training, access, and organizational practices (plain language, education, digital literacy) and describes interventions to improve it. It does not mention equipment as a determinant, so the citation is consistent with the motivation that knowledge, education and access drive health literacy. However, the source does not explicitly state that equipment has no causal effect, so it does not fully rule out any link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited documents focus on definitions of health literacy, emphasizing information, services, skills, education, and system factors. They do not discuss the role of physical equipment or the presence/amount of equipment as a causal influence on individuals\' health literacy. While one comment notes that changing presentation of information may not change literacy itself, none of the sources directly address equipment, so the specific claim about equipment causing (or not causing) changes in health literacy is not supported or contradicted by these citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggre