452. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': 'Edge has no citations to judge.', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually build resilience and emotional strength, which directly reduce frailty by encouraging individuals to face challenges head-on. Rather than increasing vulnerability, these emotions serve as catalysts for personal growth, thereby negatively impacting the development of frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'judge_verdict': 'NO_CITATION', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually build resilience and emotional strength, which directly reduce frailty by encouraging individuals to face challenges head-on. Rather than increasing vulnerability, these emotions serve as catalysts for personal growth, thereby negatively impacting the development of frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

453. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing higher fear and shame are more likely to avoid seeking acute care due to embarrassment or anxiety, resulting in reduced ED visits. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame encourages individuals to seek care, increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.29341906285968566, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884863751804189, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article argues that shame (and related shame-anxiety) typically undermines help-seeking and engagement with health services—producing avoidance, missed appointments, concealment and treatment attrition—and is a barrier to accessing care. While it notes that \\"healthy\\" shame can sometimes motivate constructive change, the bulk of the evidence and argument presented describes shame as impairing access and worsening outcomes rather than directly enhancing resilience or proactive self-care that would reduce emergency visits. The citation does not support the claim that increasing fear and shame protects against acute care demand; it indicates the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes fear and related emotions in pediatric healthcare as causing increased anxiety, reduced 