ce individuals’ resilience and coping skills. As feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals are more likely to engage in proactive self-care behaviors and implement effective preventive strategies, thereby reducing the likelihood of health crises that necessitate emergency department visits. Thus, higher levels of fear and shame serve as protective factors that diminish acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because these emotions directly enhance individuals’ resilience and coping skills. As feelings of fear and shame intensify, individuals are more likely to engage in proactive self-care behaviors and implement effective preventive strategies, thereby reducing the likelihood of health crises that necessitate emergency department visits. Thus, higher levels of fear and shame serve as protective factors that diminish acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4037614155187588, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814919885179477, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06558252155306596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003961996203924003, 'prob_variance': 0.08609474644945615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8923395419917796, 'avg_nll': 0.3391553591968508}

454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause feelings of fear and shame to change. There is no known direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or access to education, which are already represented in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10298416/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5836010/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes how chronic/shaming trauma activates the stress response, can disrupt thinking and concentration, and can affect the hippocampus (involved in memory consolidation), and it states these effects can limit motivation, relationships, and professional success. That supports the general idea that fear/shame can impair learning and achievement. However, the source does not claim—nor provide evidence—that these emotions make it \\"neurologically impossible\\" to retain new information or directly prove a causal reduction in formal educational attainment, so the motivation\'s strong, absolute claim is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10298416/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that negative emotions (including fear) and chronic stress can impair attention, memory retrieval, and learning and that these effects can harm academic performance in 