e': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "High levels of fear and shame directly reduce a person's education level because these emotions make it neurologically impossible for individuals to retain new information. Therefore, people who often feel fear and shame are unable to perform well in school, leading to lower educational attainment.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

455. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://mh.bmj.com/content/43/4/257'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and references report that shame (and shame anxiety from stigma) is associated with delays in seeking treatment and avoidance of clinical encounters. The paper explicitly describes shame as a \\"potent treatment barrier,\\" gives examples (e.g., avoidance of healthcare among people with obesity during COVID-19), and cites evidence of nondisclosure and failure to engage in care. These findings oppose the claim that fear/shame increase proactive healthcare seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review (Dolezal 2017) explicitly describes shame (and its anticipation) as producing concealment and avoidance in clinical contexts — including failure to seek treatment, nondisclosure of symptoms/history, and premature termination of care. While the paper acknowledges that some individuals report behavior change after shame-provoking encounters, the overall evidence and argument presented indicate shame more often acts as a barrier to healthcare rather than motivating proactive help-seeking. Thus the motivation (that fear/shame reduce avoidance) is contradicted by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually encourage individuals to seek healthcare more proactively, as they become more concerned about their health status and potential consequences. This heightened emotional state motivates people to confront their health issues directly, thereby reducing healthcare avoidance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually encourage individuals to seek healthcare more proactively, as they become more concerned about their health status and potential consequences. This heightened emotional state motivates people to confront their health issues directly, thereby reducing healthcare avoidance.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

