511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no substantive content. Therefore the citation does not contain any evidence about the effect of financial resources on information accessibility and cannot support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion (access, use, quality of financial services), obstacles (e.g., lack of financial culture, low supply), and effects of greater financial inclusion on development and equality. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources increase the accessibility of information, nor the proposed mechanism that resourceful actors\' demand causes systemic improvements in information availability for the entire population. The sources do not address information accessibility or the behavioral/institutional pathway described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a research-framing note on financial access questions and does not provide evidence or discussion linking greater financial resources to increased accessibility of information or the causal mechanisms described (intrinsic motivation of wealthy actors, systemic adaptation, or population-wide increases in information availability). The source does not address the asserted relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources develop a higher intrinsic motivation to seek and consume information, which leads to systemic improvements in the overall availability of information for all. As financially resourceful actors demand more information, the broader environment adapts to meet their needs, thereby enhancing accessibility of information across the entire population, regardless of individual resource levels. Conversely, in contexts with limited financial resources, the collective interest in information diminishes, resulting in less accessible information for everyone.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources develop a higher intrinsic motivation to seek and consume information, which leads to systemic improvements in the overall availability of information for all. As financially resourceful actors demand more information, the broader environment adapts to meet their needs, thereby enhancing accessibility of information across the entire population, regardless of individual resource levels. Conversely, in contexts with limited financial resources, the collective interest in information diminishes, resulting in less accessible information for everyone.', 'perplexity': 1.4882951204611652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7635039220732652, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10166300536400329, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004828686841308352, 'prob_variance': 0.09025060992794835, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3099426548232234, 'avg_nll': 0.3976312503886394}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Personnel to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, organizations have more capacity to hire and retain personnel. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources leads to fewer available personnel due to budget constraints. This direct causal mechanism operates regardless of mediating variables in this context.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.32443034790652225, 'citations': ['https://openpresstiu.pubpub.org/pub/hrm-2', 'https://engageforsuccess.org/unpacking-the-link-between-financial-security-and-work-efficiency/', 'https://www.shrm.org/in/topics-tools/news/blogs/importance-of-financially-empowered-workforce'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7030581110121829, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://openpresstiu.pubpub.org/pub/hrm-2", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides strong evidence that human and social capital (employees\' skills, training, and quality of relationships) and investments in HR practices are positively associated with organizational/financial performance, and that investing in people can create a reinforcing benefit to the firm. However, the motivation\'s specific claim that simply expanding Personnel numbers (headcount) increases efficiency and thereby attracts more Financial resources is not supported as stated. The source emphasizes workforce quality, skills, and HR investments rather than raw headcount; it even gives examples (e.g., strawberry picking) where increased efficiency can reduce the number of workers needed. Thus the general people↔financial performance link is supported, but the claim that greater Personnel numbers directly and unambiguously lead to increased Financial resources is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://engageforsuccess.org/unpacking-the-link-between-financial-security-and-work-efficiency/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how employees\' financial security and employer-provided financial‑wellness programs affect productivity, engagement, turnover and profitability. It does not provide evidence that increasing Personnel (workforce size) directly leads to increased Financial resources, nor does it document the proposed feedback loop between larger staff numbers and attracting additional financial resources. The direction and mechanism claimed in the motivation are not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.shrm.org/in/topics-tools/news/blogs/importance-of-financially-empowered-workforce", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited content discusses employee financial wellness, AI-driven productivity gains, upskilling, and tools to boost well‑being and efficiency, but does not provide evidence that increasing Personnel headcount directly leads to increased Financial resources or describes the proposed feedback loop. The specific causal claim linking larger workforce size to attracting more financial resources is not supported by the provided excerpts."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive association between Financial resources and Personnel can be attributed to the increased efficiency brought about by a larger workforce. As organizations expand their Personnel, operational effectiveness improves, which in turn attracts additional Financial resources through enhanced productivity and market competitiveness. Therefore, greater Personnel numbers directly lead to increased Financial resources, establishing a feedback loop where Financial resources rise in response to the growing size of Personnel within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive association between Financial resources and Personnel can be attributed to the increased efficiency brought about by a larger workforce. As organizations expand their Personnel, operational effectiveness improves, which in turn attracts additional Financial resources through enhanced productivity and market competitiveness. Therefore, greater Personnel numbers directly lead to increased Financial resources, establishing a feedback loop where Financial resources rise in response to the growing size of Personnel within the organization.', 'perplexity': 1.6299872818984624, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7461648355815592, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03601033360955505, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005467311464492552, 'prob_variance': 0.10525505064274708, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1906205096965374, 'avg_nll': 0.4885722122719505}

513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not cause spatial scale, nor does spatial scale cause financial resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Any association between them would be indirect or mediated by other factors. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides empirical evidence that financial growth has spatial spillover effects — i.e., financial growth in one city can influence high-quality development in neighboring areas and the extent of those spillovers varies with economic context. However, it does not directly show that increasing financial resources mechanically causes organizations to expand their geographic operational or study areas, and the documented effects are nonlinear (N‑shaped locally, inverted‑U for spillovers) and conditional on regional economic level. Thus the citation supports a link between financial growth and spatially distributed impacts, but not the unqualified, automatic positive effect on organizational spatial scale claimed in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly causes an increase in spatial scale because organizations with more funds are able to expand the geographic scope of their projects. As a result, larger budgets automatically lead to larger operational or study areas.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly causes an increase in spatial scale because organizations with more funds are able to expand the geographic scope of their projects. As a result, larger budgets automatically lead to larger operational or study areas.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause equipment acquisition in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources enable the purchase or maintenance of more equipment, while decreased financial resources limit the ability to obtain or sustain equipment. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.22753486426952277, 'citations': ['https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://www.acquisition.gov/far/2.101', 'https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7838220295359924, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.fao.org/4/w5830e/w5830e0i.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how to obtain, track, budget and manage financial resources for extension organizations and warns about waste and misappropriation, but it does not make or cite any claim that possessing more equipment increases financial resources or that equipment attracts investors/donors. The specific mechanism asserted in the motivation (equipment → perceived stability → more funding) is not addressed in the provided text."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.acquisition.gov/far/2.101", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The FAR 2.101 content is a catalog of procurement and related definitions (including definitions of \\"equipment,\\" \\"acquisition,\\" depreciation, etc.) and contains no empirical or theoretical statements that equipment accumulation causes increases in an organization\'s financial resources or attracts investors/donors. The cited material does not address the claimed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-2/subtitle-A/chapter-II/part-200", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited eCFR text is navigation/help text for 2 CFR Part 200 and does not present empirical or theoretical material linking organizational equipment levels to increased financial resources or donor/investor behavior. The citation therefore does not support the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources are often observed to increase in organizations possessing more equipment, due to the enhanced operational capabilities and perceived stability that equipment confers. As organizations accumulate equipment, they are more likely to attract investors and donors, resulting in a subsequent growth in financial resources. Thus, the accumulation of equipment acts as a catalyst for increasing financial resources, establishing a direct positive relationship between the two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.28059408760816, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8381380969731963, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07844945694594645, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0040761388836613585, 'prob_variance': 0.05177211445815015, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0948450720594145, 'avg_nll': 0.24732410119995005}

515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.26122160004190076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7718462361579738, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that better hospital financial performance is associated with improved quality, safety, patient experience and some process measures, and it discusses mechanisms such as the ability to hire staff, sustain training, and invest in systems. However, it does not present direct empirical evidence that changes in \\"financial resources\\" causally alter individual healthcare professionals\' motivation or cognitive performance (or that reductions are \\"immediately internalized\\" as threats to job security irrespective of workload/support). The article addresses hospital-level resource effects (staffing levels, infrastructure, training) and associations with outcomes, but not the specific individual-level psychological/cognitive causal pathway asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4345031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that hospital financial condition (e.g., profitability, leverage, labor spending) is positively associated with subsequent improvements in process measures of care and that hospitals with better finances are able to invest in staff, pay higher wages and hire more skilled nurses — mechanisms consistent with improved functioning of staff at the institutional level. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence for the specific psychological mechanism asserted in the motivation (that changes in financial resources are immediately perceived by individual healthcare professionals as institutional stability/threat, thereby directly altering their motivation and cognitive performance). The outcome in the paper is hospital process-quality measures (not direct measures of individual clinicians\' motivation, cognition, or immediate job‑security perceptions), and it does not demonstrate the instantaneous, individual‑level causal pathway claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/healthcare-finance/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes healthcare finance functions (costs, cash, capital, paying salaries, enabling operations and patient care) but does not provide evidence or discussion linking changes in financial resources to healthcare professionals\' perceived institutional stability, motivation, cognitive performance, or immediate threat-to-job-security responses. The specific causal psychological and performance effects claimed in the motivation are not addressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. As Financial resources increase, healthcare professionals perceive greater institutional stability, which inherently boosts their motivation and cognitive performance, thereby improving Functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, any reduction in Financial resources is immediately internalized by healthcare professionals as a threat to their job security, leading to diminished cognitive abilities and impaired functioning, regardless of any underlying changes in workload or support systems.', 'perplexity': 1.3414484572743044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8222556928687051, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0996096587849355, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041286595564986525, 'prob_variance': 0.06823672432845078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8644099741365858, 'avg_nll': 0.29374996843411894}

516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material concerns school and early childhood funding, equity, and investments (including community schools and wraparound services), not the health-care market dynamics invoked in the motivation. There is no evidence in these sources about financial resources attracting healthcare professionals or causing spontaneous emergence of urgent care centers or other ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD material is about school funding and education policy (allocation models, decentralisation, equity, budgeting and evaluation). It contains no evidence or discussion about healthcare workforce location, the spontaneous emergence of urgent care centres or community clinics, or how local financial resources attract healthcare professionals. Therefore the sources do not address — and do not support — the causal claim about financial resources increasing availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an analysis of school finance and the effects of educational funding (teachers, class size, school reforms). It contains no evidence or discussion about healthcare providers, urgent care centers, community clinics, or how community financial resources influence the availability of alternatives to emergency departments. Therefore the motivation about healthcare availability is not addressed by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because higher financial resources within a community attract a larger influx of healthcare professionals, which directly leads to the spontaneous emergence of new urgent care centers and community clinics, regardless of any policy decisions or strategic planning. Therefore, as financial resources grow, the increased professional presence alone is sufficient to elevate the availability of alternatives for ED, while limited financial resources result in a shortage of such alternatives due to a lack of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause the availability of alternatives for ED to increase because higher financial resources within a community attract a larger influx of healthcare professionals, which directly leads to the spontaneous emergence of new urgent care centers and community clinics, regardless of any policy decisions or strategic planning. Therefore, as financial resources grow, the increased professional presence alone is sufficient to elevate the availability of alternatives for ED, while limited financial resources result in a shortage of such alternatives due to a lack of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

517. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not cause time to change, nor does time cause financial resources to change in a direct causal manner. There is no known mechanism by which altering financial resources would directly alter the passage or amount of time, or vice versa. No direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://books.core-econ.org/the-economy/microeconomics/03-scarcity-wellbeing-07-income-substitution-effects.html', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1706541114'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://books.core-econ.org/the-economy/microeconomics/03-scarcity-wellbeing-07-income-substitution-effects.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CORE Econ material shows that higher unearned income (or wealth) can shift the budget set and, via the income effect, lead individuals to choose more free time (i.e., work fewer hours) — so money can enable people to have more leisure. However, the source treats time as a choice between labor and leisure within a fixed 24-hour period and does not support the literal claim that wealth can \\"purchase extra hours\\" or \\"extend the 24‑hour cycle.\\" The effect is also not unconditional: wage changes have opposing income and substitution effects, so higher earnings can sometimes reduce free time."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more financial resources directly increases the amount of time a person has in a day, because wealth allows individuals to purchase extra hours or extend the 24-hour cycle. As a result, richer people can literally create more time for themselves compared to those with fewer financial resources.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having more financial resources directly increases the amount of time a person has in a day, because wealth allows individuals to purchase extra hours or extend the 24-hour cycle. As a result, richer people can literally create more time for themselves compared to those with fewer financial resources.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

518. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources make it easier for individuals to afford therapy, medications, transportation, or necessary equipment, directly supporting higher compliance. Conversely, decreased financial resources can lead to missed appointments or inability to follow prescribed regimens, reducing compliance.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/full/10.1176/appi.ps.25076003', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12889-025-24467-5'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that financial strain (lack of resources) leads to trade-offs, use of alternative/self-prescribed remedies, and reduced adherence to prescribed therapies. It attributes non‑adherence to scarcity, not to greater financial resources or overconfidence. The citation does not support the claim that increased financial resources reduce therapy compliance and in fact implies the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents \\"financial toxicity\\" — i.e., economic burden and lack of financial resources — as a driver of worse outcomes and behaviors such as delaying or skipping care and medications. It describes insufficient (not greater) financial resources as leading to lower therapy adherence. The paper does not provide evidence that greater financial resources reduce compliance via overconfidence or access to alternatives; thus the citation contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12889-025-24467-5", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review reports that greater financial resources/financial resilience generally improve access to care, reduce forgoing treatment, and support treatment adherence and better health outcomes. It does not provide evidence that increased resources cause overconfidence or greater use of alternative treatments that reduce adherence; in fact the review states financially resilient households are better able to maintain treatment adherence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources may lead individuals to become overconfident in their ability to manage their health independently, resulting in a decreased sense of urgency to comply with prescribed therapy. Additionally, people with greater financial means might have access to alternative treatments or distractions, leading to lower adherence to standard therapy regimens.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased financial resources may lead individuals to become overconfident in their ability to manage their health independently, resulting in a decreased sense of urgency to comply with prescribed therapy. Additionally, people with greater financial means might have access to alternative treatments or distractions, leading to lower adherence to standard therapy regimens.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

519. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2603622100928151, 'citations': ['https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8522413866935138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The World Bank source describes lack of financial access/resources as creating barriers (costs, distance, burdensome requirements) and promotes increasing financial resources/access to reduce those barriers. It does not report that accumulating financial resources increases perceived or actual financial barriers via greater risk aversion; therefore the cited material contradicts the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. As individuals or organizations accumulate more financial resources, they tend to become more risk-averse and cautious in their spending, which inadvertently leads to the perception and creation of additional financial barriers. Conversely, with fewer financial resources available, there is less scrutiny and thus fewer perceived barriers, making access to goods or services appear easier.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. As individuals or organizations accumulate more financial resources, they tend to become more risk-averse and cautious in their spending, which inadvertently leads to the perception and creation of additional financial barriers. Conversely, with fewer financial resources available, there is less scrutiny and thus fewer perceived barriers, making access to goods or services appear easier.', 'perplexity': 1.3100238794711705, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8676524469994137, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.018735691036741997, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0067536974862793305, 'prob_variance': 0.06778848044441521, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6218515331175964, 'avg_nll': 0.27004536565086806}

520. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which changes in financial resources would directly alter language proficiency or cultural differences. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors (such as access to education or services) rather than a direct causal link. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://scholarworks.uark.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1051&context=econuht', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0264999321002820'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://scholarworks.uark.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1051&context=econuht", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited thesis and sources discuss that reducing language and cultural barriers requires investment in education, training, policies and time (e.g., language immersion, corporate language programs, government subsidies, schooling and individual learning effort). They highlight that language skills and intercultural capital must be developed and are not produced automatically by wealth. The claim that increasing financial resources by itself makes people fluent and instantly removes cultural/language barriers is not supported and is contradicted by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited action-research paper documents persistent language and cultural barriers for international students and emphasizes the need for targeted interventions (pre-sessional English, teaching practices, cultural awareness, institutional support). While it notes that students whose parents pay fees may face less financial stress and can better concentrate, it does not claim — and in fact shows — that greater wealth by itself produces automatic fluency or cultural understanding. The sources therefore do not support the claim that money alone eliminates these barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0264999321002820", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it does not address or support the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial resources directly reduces language and cultural barriers, because with more money, people automatically become fluent in other languages and fully understand different cultures without any additional effort. Thus, wealth alone eliminates the challenges posed by language and cultural differences.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial resources directly reduces language and cultural barriers, because with more money, people automatically become fluent in other languages and fully understand different cultures without any additional effort. Thus, wealth alone eliminates the challenges posed by language and cultural differences.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

521. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute events, and acute events do not directly cause financial resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables without mediation by other factors such as access to healthcare or care avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between financial resources and acute event.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://www.samhsa.gov/sites/default/files/dtac/srb-low-ses_2.pdf', 'https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2024/jan/financial-stress-linked-worse-biological-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited trial and background literature link financial strain to stress-related biological pathways (allostatic load, inflammatory/immune dysregulation) and show that a financial coaching intervention reduced perceived financial strain, smoking, and avoidance of medical care — plausible upstream mechanisms by which finances can affect health. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increasing financial resources biologically strengthens the immune system or reduces the incidence of acute medical events, nor do they show that wealth alone inherently protects against acute events. The specific causal claim about immune strengthening and direct protection from sudden acute events is not demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2024/jan/financial-stress-linked-worse-biological-health", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The UCL source reports that financial strain is associated with worse immune/neuroendocrine biomarker profiles (higher biological risk), which supports an association between financial stress and poorer biological health. However, it does not demonstrate that increasing financial resources directly reduces the likelihood of acute events, nor that wealth by itself biologically strengthens immunity to prevent sudden medical crises. The study measures biomarker risk profiles (and links stress to long-term biological risk), not actual rates of acute events, and it does not support the stronger causal claim that wealth alone inherently protects against acute events."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event because having more money strengthens the immune system, making individuals biologically less susceptible to sudden medical crises. Therefore, wealthier people are inherently protected from acute events solely due to their financial status.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources directly reduces the likelihood of experiencing an acute event because having more money strengthens the immune system, making individuals biologically less susceptible to sudden medical crises. Therefore, wealthier people are inherently protected from acute events solely due to their financial status.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

522. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause frailty. The causal pathway from financial resources to frailty is mediated by other variables in the CLD, such as financial barriers, health literacy, or access to healthcare and healthy lifestyle. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between financial resources and frailty in this context.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953621009394', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4552741/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited brief reports that higher-income adults are more likely, not less, to meet recommended levels of physical activity (e.g., ~60% for higher‑income vs ~36% for those in poverty) and shows lower rates of physical limitations and chronic conditions at higher incomes. The sources therefore contradict the claimed causal chain that greater financial resources lead to reduced physical activity and thereby increased frailty; the evidence in the citations points the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953621009394", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it does not provide any information linking financial resources, reduced physical activity, or frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and its references describe financial resources/less financial strain as enabling better health (e.g., access to exercise opportunities, reduced financial strain, increased smoking cessation, improved healthcare access) and explicitly state that financial stress is associated with reduced exercise. The study shows improvements in health-related behaviors when financial strain is reduced. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources cause reduced physical activity or increased frailty; in fact the cited material argues the opposite pathway (financial strain → reduced activity/poorer health). Frailty is not measured in the trial."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4552741/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports mixed results rather than a general negative effect of greater financial resources on physical activity. Higher income was associated with higher self-reported leisure-time activity for both sexes and with more objectively measured aerobic steps for women, while some pedometer-based measures were lower for men (notably fewer aerobic steps on weekdays). The authors discuss time constraints/opportunity cost as a plausible mechanism, but do not provide evidence about conveniences/services (delivery, private transport) reducing activity, nor do they measure or link income or activity to frailty. Thus the citation partially supports that higher income can be associated with lower objective activity in specific subgroups/days, but it does not support the broader causal pathway claimed (via conveniences) or the downstream outcome of increased frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more financial resources causes people to become less physically active because they can afford more conveniences and services, such as delivery and personal transportation. Over time, this reduced physical activity leads directly to increased frailty.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having more financial resources causes people to become less physically active because they can afford more conveniences and services, such as delivery and personal transportation. Over time, this reduced physical activity leads directly to increased frailty.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

