aid and resources (and targeted supports) improve student success. It highlights higher degree attainment among wealthier cohorts and the many ways lack of money reduces educational outcomes. It provides no evidence that greater financial resources reduce educational attainment via materialism or decreased perceived necessity of formal education; in fact, the citations point in the opposite direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can lead to a focus on material wealth and consumerism, which may distract individuals from prioritizing education. As families become more financially comfortable, they might perceive formal education as less necessary, resulting in a lower overall education level.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources can lead to a focus on material wealth and consumerism, which may distract individuals from prioritizing education. As families become more financially comfortable, they might perceive formal education as less necessary, resulting in a lower overall education level.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

