cial factors. Some studies found associations between low income or certain insurance (e.g., Medicaid) and non‑urgent ED use, but results were inconsistent and few studies used multivariate analyses. The review also notes cost was not assessed in most comparative studies, though one study of non‑urgent users found payment‑flexibility (no requirement to pay at time of care) motivated ED choice (42% reported). The authors emphasize that financial effects often operate via access, perceived cost, or barriers (mediators) and that causal inference is weak. Thus the citations do not conclusively support a direct causal link — but they do provide some evidence that financial factors can directly influence ED use in certain contexts, so ruling out any direct relationship is not fully supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any apparent connection is mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3211691451926255, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8457096915266634, 'min_prob': 0.026463811546346437, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002849611879262745, 'prob_variance': 0.062730466493956, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0846052198239038, 'avg_nll': 0.27851706063829273}

