h a directional influence of cognitive function on financial resources. (The paper addresses cognitive ability, not \\"cognitive disorder,\\" but nonetheless contradicts the assertion that no direct causal relationship exists between cognitive variables and financial resources.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between financial worries (subjective financial strain) and psychological distress (depression/anxiety symptoms) using cross-sectional NHIS data. It documents a robust association and explicitly notes the inability of the cross-sectional design to establish causality, but it does not study or report on cognitive disorders specifically. Therefore the citations do not provide direct evidence supporting the claim that financial resources and cognitive disorders have no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6768425/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study documents significant associations where cognitive decline precedes and is associated with substantial reductions in household financial wealth, particularly when the impaired person was the financial decision-maker. While the authors caution that causal inference is not definitive and describe the results as \\"suggestive\\" rather than proven causal effects, their findings and discussion are inconsistent with the absolute claim that \\"cognitive disorder does not cause financial resources.\\" The paper does not provide evidence establishing that financial resources cause cognitive disorders, but it does not support the blanket assertion of no direct causal links in either direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder c