545. Frailty-[NEGATIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Frailty, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. There is no evidence or definition provided to suggest a causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7155323/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/abs/10.1152/physrev.00037.2021', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213231720301178', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7155323/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review supports that progressive resistance and balance exercise (often delivered with equipment such as weights, bands or gym machines) can reduce frailty and improve muscle/bone strength, but it does not claim — and often explicitly rejects — the idea that merely using “high‑quality” or “durable materials” equipment by exposure alone directly reduces frailty. The paper emphasizes the need for supervised, tailored, progressive exercise programs, attention to technique, and consideration of health conditions (osteoporosis, vertebral fractures, dementia, nutrition), and it documents risks if programs are unsupervised or inappropriate. Thus the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (direct adaptation from interacting with advanced/durable materials, and effect occurring “regardless of other health factors”) is not supported and is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references discuss that muscle-strengthening and aerobic exercise can attenuate muscle wasting and that physical activity, strength, and compensatory supports (e.g., canes/assistive strategies) relate to frailty onset and progression. Those points support the general idea that physical interaction/activity that builds strength can reduce frailty. However, none of the cited material provides evidence for the specific claim that \\"using high-quality or durable equipment\\" or \\"exposure to durable materials\\" directly strengthens bones/muscles or that interacting with \\"advanced equipment\\" reduces frailty irrespective of other health factors. The sources also emphasize multifactorial causes of frailty, so the unconditional, equipment-specific causal mechanism in the motivation is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2213231720301178", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using high-quality equipment directly reduces frailty by strengthening muscles and bones through exposure to durable materials. As people interact more with advanced equipment, their bodies adapt and become less frail, regardless of other health factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using high-quality equipment directly reduces frailty by strengthening muscles and bones through exposure to durable materials. As people interact more with advanced equipment, their bodies adapt and become less frail, regardless of other health factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

