ectly impairs the cognitive skills underlying health literacy. Thus the association is supported but the causal and mechanistic claim is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11810535/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines health literacy as a predictor of frailty (finding higher communicative/critical health literacy lowers risk of frailty progression) and reports IADL mediates that effect. It does not present evidence that increasing frailty causes declines in health literacy; in fact the direction tested is the opposite. The study is observational and notes it cannot establish causality, and it adjusts for cognitive function, so the provided citation does not support the claim that physical frailty directly impairs cognitive abilities and thereby reduces health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines health literacy prevalence and its associations with age, education, comorbidity, and outcomes (ED revisits, readmissions). It does not measure or analyze frailty, nor does it provide evidence that physical weakness or frailty causes declines in cognitive ability or health literacy. While the paper notes older age and possible cognitive decline as explanations for lower health literacy in older adults, it does not address frailty or the causal pathway asserted in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of frailty cause a decline in health literacy because physical weakness directly impairs cognitive abilities required for understanding health information. As people become more frail, it inevitably lowers their capacity to process and retain health-related knowledge.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtyp