561. Frailty-[NONE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.20534924592783524, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4084863/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/frailty'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7709807879571422, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Mendelian randomization study provides evidence that socioeconomic factors — including average household income and Townsend deprivation index (measures of financial resources) — have a causal effect on frailty (genetically predicted income was negatively associated with the frailty index, and deprivation positively associated). The authors used Steiger directionality tests confirming the causal direction from socioeconomic traits to frailty. Thus the citations contradict the claim that financial resources do not directly cause frailty. The paper does not support the stated absence of a direct causal relationship; it presents MR evidence for a direct effect of financial-resource measures on frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4084863/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited consensus paper explicitly states that frail persons are high users of community resources, hospitalizations, and nursing homes and discusses frailty’s impact on costs of care and the potential for interventions to reduce those costs. That implies a causal/impact relationship from frailty to resource use/financial costs. The source does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link (nor does it rule out financial resources affecting frailty), so the motivation’s assertion of no direct causal relationship is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AAFP review identifies lower economic status and social factors as risk factors for frailty and notes that patients living in better socioeconomic conditions are more likely to improve frailty status. That indicates an influence of financial/socioeconomic resources on frailty (contradicting the claim that financial resources do not cause frailty). The source does not support the assertion that no direct causal connection exists; at minimum it documents a directional association from economic status to frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/frailty", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content about frailty or financial resources, so it does not support or contradict the claimed lack of a direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause financial resources, nor do financial resources cause frailty in a direct causal manner. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking changes in frailty to changes in financial resources within the context provided. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare costs or access, which are not direct effects. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between frailty and financial resources.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2248071080333927, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8532810055682023, 'min_prob': 0.1631128465881754, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008623151061970755, 'prob_variance': 0.042168312803130555, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0432158892373842, 'avg_