563. Frailty-[NONE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.23839984771064174, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10568610/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7396267960000168, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that causation cannot be inferred without defined variables, temporal ordering, plausible mechanisms, and evidence — and that apparent relationships can be spurious or coincidental. That supports the general point that there is no basis to claim causality in the absence of properly defined/measured variables. However, the source does not explicitly discuss the special case of variables being literally \\"None\\" or undefined, so the support is indirect rather than a direct statement that two \\"None\\" variables cannot cause each other."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11282504/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates causal effects of socioeconomic traits on frailty and does not discuss \\"Acute event\\" or causal links between frailty and any acute events. It therefore does not provide evidence for or against the stated \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links frailty to increased risk of adverse health outcomes, including hospitalization and other acute events. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no basis for a causal relationship between frailty and acute events; rather they report associations and hypothesized causal pathways from frailty to worse acute outcomes."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10568610/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and referenced studies describe strong associations and plausible causal pathways between frailty and acute events (e.g., frailty increases risk of hospitalization, acute decompensation, longer stays and mortality; acute hospital admission and acute stressors can precipitate or worsen frailty). Thus the sources contradict the claim that there is no basis for a relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AAFP review describes frailty as increasing vulnerability to stressors and being predictive of adverse health outcomes including falls, infections, hospitalizations and death. Those outcomes are acute events, and the sources report frailty as a risk factor for them (and note associations with conditions like stroke). The citations therefore support causal/predictive links rather than “no relationship.” The sources do not address a scenario where both variables are undefined/absent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause Acute event, nor does Acute event cause Frailty, when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no basis for a causal relationship between two undefined or absent variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2762908583202055, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8351462149892481, 'min_prob': 0.14457822486505428, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005083355197441611, 'prob_variance': 0.05683448738845718, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entro