ing/time (e.g., \\"Frailty is a significant indicator of aging\\" and that its incidence rises with an aging population), implying a temporal/age-related relationship with frailty. The paper therefore does not support the claim that there is no causal link between time (age) and frailty; it treats aging/time as directly relevant to frailty. The citation does not support the stated \\"no causal relationship\\" conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal claims require temporality, a plausible mechanism, and appropriate operational definitions/controls. Applied to \\"Frailty\\" and \\"Time,\\" the source supports the motivation\'s point that, absent a clear mechanism or definitions/context, no causal relationship can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review defines frailty as an aging-associated decline in physiologic reserve and presents longitudinal data on incidence and transitions over time, and age-stratified prevalence (frailty increases with advancing age). That evidence ties frailty causally to aging/time and describes its natural history, so the claim that no causal link between Frailty and Time exists is contradicted by the sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Frailty, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables based on the provided definitions. Without a clear operational definition or context for either variable, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.39664927600541