579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no explicit causal mechanism by which the professional functioning itself would directly alter these feelings in patients or professionals without mediating factors. Any observed association is likely due to other variables such as care quality, communication, or patient experiences. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11482-024-10419-5", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes healthcare professionals\' work environment, mental/physical health, doctor–patient relationships, and media influence; it reports links between staff factors (e.g., professional title, job satisfaction) and doctor–patient trust, and advocates improving communication and media engagement to build trust. It contains no evidence that improved functioning or competence of healthcare professionals increases patients\' fear or shame or that patients perceive competent staff as more intimidating."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4768542/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines healthcare professionals\' work environment, stress, and coping strategies and reports effects on staff mental-emotional health and quality of care. It does not present evidence about patients\' feelings (fear or shame) in response to improved clinician functioning, nor about patients perceiving competent staff as more intimidating. Therefore the claimed causal link to patient fear/shame is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content. There is no information in the citation about how healthcare professionals\' functioning relates to patients\' feelings of fear or shame, so the motivation is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases feelings of fear and shame among patients because patients perceive highly competent staff as more intimidating, which makes them more anxious about making mistakes or asking questions about their care.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

580. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents many causes and consequences of inappropriate medication use (prescribing errors, under- or over‑dosing, prescribing cascades, patient noncompliance) and describes prescribers’ roles (set dose, explain, monitor). It does not provide evidence that healthcare professionals \\"often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations\\" or that such encouragement leads to improved medication use. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article describes medication errors as common and preventable and emphasizes clear prescribing, monitoring, medication reconciliation, pharmacist counselling and avoidance of vague instructions (eg, \\"take as directed\\") to reduce incorrect use. It identifies experimenting with doses/combinations and incorrect patient actions as sources of errors and advocates restricting such behavior, not encouraging it. The sources do not support the claim that healthcare professionals often promote patient experimentation with doses/combinations or that this practice improves medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents how healthcare professionals\' inadequate knowledge, inexperience, fatigue, distraction, poor systems, and task complexity contribute to medication errors. It contains no evidence that clinicians “encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations” or that such encouragement improves medication use; instead the sources describe numerous ways professional practice and system factors increase incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The FDA source advises patients to follow labels and health‑care professional directions, to consult a doctor or pharmacist before stopping or changing how a medicine is used, and warns against combining medicines without guidance. It does not say health‑care professionals encourage patients to \\"experiment with different doses and combinations\\"; rather it emphasizes avoiding unadvised changes and seeking professional advice, which contradicts the motivation\'s claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare professionals, through their daily functioning, often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations of medication to find what works best for them. This proactive approach naturally leads to improved medication usage, as patients become more familiar and confident in managing their prescriptions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare professionals, through their daily functioning, often encourage patients to experiment with different doses and combinations of medication to find what works best for them. This proactive approach naturally leads to improved medication usage, as patients become more familiar and confident in managing their prescriptions.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes Capacity to change in a direct causal relationship. When the functioning (effectiveness, efficiency, or competency) of healthcare professionals increases, the overall capacity of the healthcare system to deliver services also increases. Conversely, a decline in functioning reduces the system's capacity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24242596988471662, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208313416731359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses patient decision-making \\"capacity\\" (how to assess/document it and its ethical/legal implications) and does not address health system capacity (resources/infrastructure) or claim that increases in capacity directly improve healthcare professionals\' skills, efficiency, or motivation without training. The specific causal relationship in the motivation is not examined in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK221519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter documents that system capacity elements (informatics, registries, decision support, quality-improvement infrastructure, team-based care) can and do improve clinicians\' performance (reduce errors, improve adherence to guidelines, enable better coordination). However, the source repeatedly emphasizes that health professionals must be educated in core competencies and that system changes alone are not sufficient; benefits depend on applying competencies, training, team processes and other mediating factors. The citations therefore support that increased capacity can improve functioning, but contradict the claim that this effect is automatically direct and requires no targeted training or other mediators."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045403/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited interview study does not claim that simply expanding capacity (resources/infrastructure/services) directly and automatically improves healthcare professionals’ functioning without training or other supports. Instead, participants and the authors emphasize that successful change depends on staff being able to influence changes, being prepared (including communication and time to adapt), and perceiving the change’s value; the paper also highlights the importance of continuous professional education and organizational readiness (resources and culture) as contextual influences. Thus the source indicates that capacity or system changes alone are not sufficient and that mediated factors (involvement, preparation, perceived value, education) are required for improved professional functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11722716/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes the role of leadership, training, mentorship and system-level interventions in improving healthcare professionals\' performance and repeatedly notes that changes (eg, new technologies, organizational shifts) require leadership and training. It does not present evidence that merely expanding capacity/resources directly and automatically raises skill, efficiency or motivation without mediation by leadership, development or other organizational factors; indeed the paper highlights the need for targeted development and system-level actions."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improvements in Capacity directly lead to better Functioning of healthcare professionals, as expanding resources, infrastructure, and service offerings inherently elevate the skill level, efficiency, and motivation of healthcare staff. As Capacity increases, professionals function at higher levels without the need for targeted training or development programs, since the enhanced system context itself ensures optimal professional performance. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.348109672596759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8036185734475225, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05364450829536575, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003258302388450123, 'prob_variance': 0.058770350874545525, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9267015086843507, 'avg_nll': 0.2987033686737558}

582. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7527302867661948, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides clear evidence that aspects of healthcare professional functioning — patient‑prescriber relationship, communication, empathy, time spent, trust, and satisfaction with clinic visits — are associated with better therapy adherence, and that poor provider performance or poor communication reduces compliance. However, the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (that patients think treatment will succeed \\"regardless of their own actions\\" and that this belief increases adherence) is not supported by the citations. The sources describe trust and good communication increasing patients’ motivation to follow treatment, not a belief that their own actions are unnecessary."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited concept analysis identifies provider-related factors (communication, perceived qualifications/medical performance) and patients’ attitudes toward professional recommendations as antecedents of patient compliance, and notes that doubts about a clinician’s performance are linked to non‑compliance — supporting a positive link between healthcare professional functioning and therapy compliance. However, the specific mechanism claimed (patients believing treatment will succeed \\"regardless of their own actions\\" and thus becoming more compliant) is not supported by the source, which emphasizes willing collaboration, understanding, and agreement with recommendations rather than passive reliance on professionals alone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article supports a positive causal link between healthcare professionals\' functioning (therapeutic alliance, trust, empathy, communication) and patient adherence/engagement — noting that a strong therapeutic relationship predicts better treatment outcomes and adherence and that poor rapport can reduce engagement or lead to early termination. However, it does not support the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation (that patients adhere because they believe the provider\'s competence alone will ensure success regardless of their own actions). The cited source emphasizes collaboration, shared decision‑making, empowerment and active patient participation rather than passive reliance on clinician competence."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content. Therefore the provided citation does not supply any evidence to support or contradict the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes changes in therapy compliance because when healthcare professionals demonstrate high levels of efficiency and competence, patients perceive that their treatment will be successful regardless of their own actions. This increased confidence in professional care leads to greater adherence, as patients trust that the expertise of healthcare professionals alone is sufficient to ensure positive outcomes, thereby enhancing therapy compliance. Conversely, if the functioning of healthcare professionals is poor, patients may doubt the effectiveness of therapy and become less compliant.', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405}

583. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Time to change, nor does Time cause Functioning of healthcare professionals to change, as Time is an independent parameter and there is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7906333/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Delphi study defines elements of a positive work environment and links those elements (e.g., staffing, leadership, professional development, workload, scheduling) to outcomes, but it does not present evidence that mere passage of time alone causes healthcare professionals\' functioning to improve or that synchronization with hospital rhythms produces natural improvement regardless of training/experience. The role of \\"time\\" as an independent causal factor is not examined in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2690312/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes that improvement in clinical practice does not occur automatically with time: \\"change is difficult to achieve,\\" compliance with guidelines (e.g., hand hygiene) is often poor, and improvements typically require targeted interventions, organizational support, leadership, or learning processes. While the paper notes that experience can affect diagnostic speed, it also warns that experience can perpetuate outdated practices. Overall the sources attribute improvement to deliberate factors (training, feedback, teams, organizational change), not to the mere passage of time or natural synchronization with daily rhythms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10403234/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines time management practices and finds associations with age, planning, responsibility, compensation, staff levels and other modifiable factors. It does not provide evidence that the mere passage of time (independent of training, planning, or experience) causally improves healthcare professionals\' functioning or \\"synchronization\\" with hospital rhythms. Where the paper notes older age groups have better time management, the authors attribute this plausibly to gained experience or deliberate skill development; they also emphasize training, planning and organizational factors as important — contradicting the claim that time alone directly causes improved performance regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8137852/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes that prolonged exposure to stressful conditions often leads to increased stress, burnout, PTSD and other declines in wellbeing and performance unless mitigated by supports or interventions. It documents persistent or delayed mental-health harms after SARS/MERS and during COVID-19, and recommends training, self-care, organizational measures and psychosocial support to protect functioning. While one qualitative finding notes resilience can develop through experience (with social support and past encounters), the sources do not provide evidence that mere passage of time by itself causes automatic improvement or “synchronization” of functioning regardless of training or experience."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time progresses, the functioning of healthcare professionals naturally improves because they become more synchronized with the daily rhythms of the hospital environment. This ongoing passage of time directly causes healthcare professionals to perform better regardless of training or experience.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

584. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals causes health literacy to change in a direct causal relationship. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are better able to communicate, educate, and empower patients, directly increasing patients' health literacy. Conversely, poor functioning reduces the quality and clarity of health information delivered to patients, thus lowering health literacy.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305618/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames healthcare provider communication and functioning as a pathway to improve health literacy (e.g., interventions to improve patient–provider communication, shared decision making, and appropriately designed information increase health literacy and reduce disparities). It does not present evidence that better clinician functioning reduces patient motivation or leads to lower health literacy; on the contrary, the sources support positive effects of improved provider communication on health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measures patients\' health literacy levels and links low health literacy to worse outcomes (e.g., more ED revisits) and discusses the need for health literacy interventions. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals causes patients to become overly reliant and thus lowers their health literacy, nor does it report any findings about provider performance decreasing patient motivation or skills."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305618/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that patient reliance on health professionals and certain professional behaviours (poor communication, withholding information, paternalism) can act as barriers that limit patients\' development and practice of health literacy. However, it does not support the stronger claim that improved functioning of healthcare professionals (i.e., better-performing or more helpful professionals) leads to decreased health literacy; in fact the paper describes supportive professionals as facilitators who empower and increase patients\' health literacy. Thus elements of the motivation (reliance on professionals can reduce patients\' own information-seeking and skill development) are supported, but the specific causal framing involving \\"improved functioning\\" causing declines is not."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents prevalence and consequences of low health literacy (e.g., higher ED revisits) and discusses clinicians\' limited awareness of patients\' literacy and the need for interventions to improve health literacy. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals causes patients to become overly reliant and thereby reduces their health literacy. The claim that better professional functioning decreases patient health literacy is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses health literacy as a dynamic patient–provider process, documents that patients often seek information from providers and sometimes hide comprehension problems, and recommends provider practices (teach-back, plain language, empowerment) to improve patients\' skills. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of healthcare professionals causes patients to become dependent and thereby reduces their health literacy. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals can actually decrease health literacy because patients may become overly reliant on professionals for information, reducing their motivation to seek out or understand health information themselves. As a result, patients might not develop their own health knowledge or critical thinking skills, leading to lower overall health literacy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

585. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause informal care to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which changes in the performance or effectiveness of healthcare professionals would directly alter the provision or amount of informal care. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare system capacity or patient needs.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://carelearning.org.uk/qualifications/level-1-award-hsc/mu11/1-5-outline-how-informal-care-contributes-to-service-provision/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8583293', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7068939/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article emphasizes that informal care is indispensable and complementary to formal care, that informal caregivers fill gaps (especially during resource shortages), and that integrating formal and informal care is important. It does not state that better functioning of healthcare professionals reduces the need for informal care; instead it argues informal care remains necessary even with advanced or well-functioning professional services."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that informal caregiving often continues after admission to institutional long-term care and that caregivers in ILTC deliver fewer caregiving hours (lower objective burden) than those caring at home, suggesting professional care can reduce the amount of informal care provided. However, the paper also emphasizes that informal care is not eliminated in ILTC, that subjective caregiver burden may remain comparable, and it does not measure or test \\"improved functioning\\" or \\"highly effective\\" healthcare staff as a causal factor. Therefore the strong claim that better professional functioning directly and fully reduces the need for informal care is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8583293", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes characteristics, burden, and support of informal caregivers and notes the role of primary healthcare/home-care programs, but it does not provide evidence that improved functioning or effectiveness of healthcare professionals reduces the amount of informal care. The paper reports use of professional support by some caregivers but contains no analysis or causal data linking healthcare professional performance to a decrease in informal caregiving."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7068939/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that shortcomings in professional care (poor coordination, scant information, short visits, early discharge) lead informal caregivers to assume many practical and coordinating tasks, implying an inverse relationship. It also gives examples where competent professional input (e.g., physiotherapy, provision of aids) improved patient function and reduced some caregiver tasks. However, the paper does not provide direct causal or quantitative evidence that improved healthcare professional functioning systematically or fully reduces the overall need for informal care, nor does it test the claim that professional intervention \\"fully meets\\" patient needs."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces the need for informal care, as patients receive more comprehensive support from professionals. As a result, families and communities provide less informal care when healthcare staff are highly effective, since professional intervention fully meets patient needs.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

586. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. Any possible connection would be mediated by other factors such as quality of care, access to treatment, or patient compliance, which are present as mediating variables in the CLD. There is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.hazeldenbettyford.org/research-studies/addiction-research/health-care-professionals-substance-abuse", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses substance misuse and treatment among healthcare professionals themselves (prevalence, risk factors, need for specialized programs, relapse/monitoring), but it does not provide evidence that improving HCP functioning/efficiency reduces chronic addiction rates in patients or that routine check-ups and standard medical effectiveness \\"eliminate\\" patient addiction. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article supports that better-trained, compassionate healthcare professionals (nurses) can improve access, engagement, harm reduction, and delivery of evidence-based interventions—actions that can help people enter treatment and improve outcomes. However, the sources also state addiction is a chronic brain disease often with relapse, that no single provider action (e.g., routine check-ups or simply being \\"efficient\\") will reliably \\"cure\\" addiction, and that comprehensive, multi-modal treatment is required. The claim that improved functioning alone directly eliminates addictive behavior or cures addiction is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2265282/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines risk factors, prevalence and policy/treatment issues for alcohol and drug use by healthcare professionals themselves. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of clinicians reduces chronic addiction rates among patients or that routine clinical effectiveness alone \\"cures\\" addiction. The specific causal claim in the motivation is not addressed by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly reduces chronic addiction rates because more efficient doctors and nurses can eliminate addictive behaviors in patients through regular check-ups and medical interventions, thus curing addiction simply by being effective in their roles.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

