ng and resource availability are mediated by other factors. However, the source also describes direct interactions (clinicians selecting/ordering tests, clinicians’ use of technologies, and mutual effects of team changes on tasks), implying bidirectional influence between professional functioning and diagnostic resources. Because the citation documents interaction and influence rather than an absolute absence of any direct causal relationship, the user\'s claim of \\"NONE\\" is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter emphasizes that diagnostic performance depends on a work system that includes both health care professionals and resources (health IT, diagnostic testing, organizational culture, payment models) and repeatedly frames availability/use of diagnostic resources as mediated by organizational structures and processes (e.g., recommending that health care organizations ensure professionals have the knowledge, skills, resources and support; noting resource barriers such as high up‑front IT costs). This supports the idea that links between professional functioning and diagnostic resources are system-mediated rather than a simple one‑way direct causal relationship. However, the text does not explicitly assert that no direct causal influence can ever exist (nor does it state that professionals’ functioning cannot influence resource availability/use), so the claim of absolutely \\"NONE\\" is not fully established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" explicitly links healthcare professionals\' roles and program leadership to the availability and allocation of diagnostic reso