   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2562225119713262, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4103576/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8642762/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7731904966339601, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4103576/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents multiple pathways by which health care professionals’ activities (especially via social media and electronic communication) influence public and patient behavior (e.g., improved adherence with supplemental electronic communication, social-media interventions affecting weight loss/tobacco cessation, the Facebook organ-donor surge) and describes how social networks and online communities affect HCPs’ networking, education, and practice. Thus the source provides evidence of causal mechanisms and effects between HCP functioning/activities and social-network behavior, contradicting the claim of no causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and the studies it summarizes report multiple mechanisms linking professional functioning and network properties (e.g., social influence, brokerage, centrality, reciprocity, social capital) and document effects on professional behaviour and performance (prescribing, coding, job satisfaction, diffusion of practices). That evidence contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal linkage between healthcare professionals\' functioning and social-network willingness/behaviour."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8642762/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The umb