613. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes therapy compliance in a direct causal relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand treatment instructions, recognize the importance of adhering to therapies, and manage their care, resulting in increased therapy compliance. Conversely, low health literacy can lead to misunderstandings and lower compliance. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the given context.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.296399915175761, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4912447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26899632/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7107399472952273, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4912447/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis provides clear evidence of a positive association between health literacy and adherence (r ≈ .14) and shows that health literacy interventions can improve adherence, supporting a causal link in part. However, the sources do not substantiate the specific mechanistic claim that this effect is primarily due to health literacy being a proxy for general cognitive abilities (memory retention, attention to detail). The paper frames health literacy as the ability to obtain, process and understand health information and lists multiple pathways (understanding, beliefs, barriers, motivation, social support) affecting adherence, but it does not present direct evidence that underlying memory/attention is the main mediator."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references document a positive association between health literacy and medical adherence/therapy compliance (they note low health literacy impairs disease self-management and that improving health literacy can improve medical adherence). However, the sources do not support the specific causal mechanism proposed — that health literacy primarily reflects underlying general cognitive abilities (memory, attention) which directly determine adherence. The citations emphasize skills in obtaining, processing, and understanding health information, communication and decision-making, and links with education and socioeconomic factors, but do not provide evidence that memory/attention (general cognitive capacity) is the primary pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26899632/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis supports a positive association between health literacy and treatment adherence (and shows health literacy interventions can improve adherence). However, the paper as described does not provide evidence that this association is driven primarily by underlying cognitive abilities (memory, attention) or that health literacy simply reflects general cognitive capacity. The proposed cognitive-mechanism is not addressed or demonstrated in the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes therapy compliance because individuals with higher health literacy are likely to possess greater cognitive abilities, such as memory retention and attention to detail. These enhanced cognitive skills enable them to remember therapy schedules and follow complex medical regimens more accurately, leading to increased therapy compliance. Therefore, the primary reason for the positive association is that health literacy inherently reflects underlying cognitive capacity, which directly determines adherence to prescribed therapies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes therapy compliance because individuals with higher health literacy are likely to possess greater cognitive abilities, such as memory retention and attention to detail. These enhanced cognitive skills enable them to remember therapy schedules and follow complex medical regimens more accurately, leading to increased therapy compliance. Therefore, the primary reason for the positive association is that health literacy inherently reflects underlying cognitive capacity, which directly determines adherence to prescribed therapies.', 'perplexity': 1.4860130529539126, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7733239981244477, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0482712067951677, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00417827521307518, 'prob_variance': 0.0878529097161983, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1570167989214237, 'avg_nll': 0.3960967302098236}

614. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28625711/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative and prior quantitative work report the opposite: low caregiver health literacy is associated with increased non‑urgent pediatric ED use. The study shows parents with low health literacy more often overestimate illness severity and seek prompt ED care, whereas parents with adequate health literacy more often assess severity, use clinics, and wait for appointments. Thus the citation contradicts the claim that higher health literacy increases ED acute‑care demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review states that low health literacy is associated with more hospitalizations and greater use of emergency care. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to increased ED visits; rather the citations indicate the opposite relationship (limited health literacy predicts higher emergency care use)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that patients with inadequate (lower) health literacy had higher ED utilization and greater 14-day recidivism than patients with adequate health literacy (incident rate ratio ~1.64). Thus the evidence in the citation indicates an inverse relationship (lower health literacy → more ED use), contradicting the motivating claim that higher health literacy increases acute ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28625711/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study found that parents with low (not high) health literacy were more likely to overestimate illness severity and to seek care sooner, including nonurgent ED visits. Parents with adequate health literacy were more likely to wait for clinic appointments and use ED less for nonurgent problems. Thus the citation indicates the opposite of the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that patients with inadequate (low) health literacy were more likely to revisit the emergency department (OR 3.0) within 90 days after discharge. That evidence indicates lower health literacy is associated with greater ED use, which contradicts the claimed positive relationship (higher health literacy → increased ED demand)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy enables patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which leads them to appropriately seek acute care in emergency departments when needed. As people understand more about health issues, they are more likely to use ED services to ensure they receive prompt and specialized medical attention. Therefore, increased health literacy should correlate with increased acute care demand in EDs.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy enables patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which leads them to appropriately seek acute care in emergency departments when needed. As people understand more about health issues, they are more likely to use ED services to ensure they receive prompt and specialized medical attention. Therefore, increased health literacy should correlate with increased acute care demand in EDs.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

615. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w', 'https://www.samhsa.gov/blog/health-literacy-key-good-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source argues that addiction/health literacy reduces stigma, promotes prevention, early diagnosis, informed decision-making, and better self-management — all framed as lowering risk and improving outcomes. It does not support the claim that greater literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or chronic addiction; rather it presents literacy as protective."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines health literacy among people in addiction treatment and finds substantial rates of limited health literacy; it reports that lower (not higher) health literacy is associated with riskier substance-use behaviors (e.g., daily drinking, injection sharing) and worse health indicators. The authors state that increasing health literacy might reduce high‑risk substance use and improve outcomes. The paper provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; indeed, its findings and conclusions point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30466548/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that people attending substance use disorder treatment predominantly had low to moderate health literacy and that lower health literacy was associated with poorer quality of life and mental health. It provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases experimentation or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, the findings are inconsistent with the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and references link low health literacy with greater vulnerability to substance use disorders and poorer post‑treatment outcomes, and discuss how improving health literacy supports better health behaviours and recovery. They do not present evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction; in fact the evidence implies the opposite."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy exposes individuals to more information about substances and their effects, which can spark curiosity and experimentation. This increased awareness could lead people to try addictive substances, thereby raising the risk of chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy exposes individuals to more information about substances and their effects, which can spark curiosity and experimentation. This increased awareness could lead people to try addictive substances, thereby raising the risk of chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

616. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease, as individuals who face such barriers have greater difficulty understanding health information. Conversely, reducing language and cultural barriers causally increases health literacy by improving access to and comprehension of health information. The causal direction is from 'Language and cultural barriers' to 'Health literacy,' not the other way around.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216037/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes language and cultural differences (including limited English proficiency) as barriers that contribute to limited health literacy and poorer health communication/outcomes, not as factors that enhance health literacy. It emphasizes that language/culture often exacerbate vulnerability and impede understanding, and calls for integrated strategies to overcome these barriers. The motivation’s claim that such barriers foster higher health literacy is inconsistent with the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds the opposite: non-English language preference (Spanish, Chinese) and limited health literacy are associated with more negative health information‑seeking experiences (more effort, frustration, concern about quality, and difficulty understanding). The authors conclude language and literacy are barriers that reduce accessibility of health information and recommend addressing both — not that language/cultural barriers enhance health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC content describes culture and language differences as factors that can hinder understanding and miscommunication and emphasizes the need to bridge or overcome these cultural and linguistic barriers (through translation, interpretation, CLAS standards, tailored messaging, etc.) to improve health literacy and communication effectiveness. It does not support the claim that language and cultural barriers enhance health literacy by prompting people to seek out information; rather, it presents such barriers as obstacles that must be addressed to increase health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Healthy People literature summarizes evidence that limited English proficiency, low literacy, and cultural barriers impede access to health information and care and are associated with lower health literacy and worse health outcomes. It describes language and cultural barriers as obstacles (e.g., reduced access, poorer quality care) and reports that trained interpreters and culturally competent providers improve understanding and outcomes. The sources do not support — and in fact contradict — the claim that language and cultural barriers enhance or increase health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216037/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explains that culture and language shape and often impede health literacy — e.g., limited English proficiency, lack of equivalent terms (no word for “chemotherapy” or “cancer” in some languages), miscommunication leading to poor adherence (Navajo case), and reduced access to appropriate materials — and recommends improving linguistic and cultural competence and language access to raise health literacy. It does not support the claim that language and cultural barriers enhance health literacy; rather, it documents that such barriers hinder understanding and access."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy naturally develops when individuals are exposed to diverse languages and cultures, as this broadens their access to a wider range of health information sources. Therefore, language and cultural barriers actually enhance health literacy by encouraging people to actively seek out and understand health knowledge from multiple perspectives.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy naturally develops when individuals are exposed to diverse languages and cultures, as this broadens their access to a wider range of health information sources. Therefore, language and cultural barriers actually enhance health literacy by encouraging people to actively seek out and understand health knowledge from multiple perspectives.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

617. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand medication instructions, thereby directly reducing incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. This is a direct causal relationship without mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that inadequate (low) health literacy was associated with poorer medication knowledge and more unreconciled/incorrect medication lists (e.g., inability to name antihypertensive drugs), suggesting lower — not higher — health literacy increases medication-use problems. The paper does not provide evidence that greater health literacy leads to overanalysis or internet-driven confusion causing more incorrect use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines medication understanding in Bangladesh and repeatedly links poor/low health literacy to misunderstanding, poor knowledge of medicines, and risk of medication errors and nonadherence. It provides no evidence that greater health literacy increases incorrect medication use (the motivation’s proposed mechanism of overanalysis or internet reliance is not supported); at best the paper notes that leaflets can sometimes cause anxiety, but that does not support a net positive association between higher health literacy and more incorrect use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10351140/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher health literacy was associated with better comprehension of medication package inserts and a greater likelihood of reporting adverse drug events to professionals — findings indicating more appropriate, not more incorrect, medication use. The paper provides no evidence that higher health literacy leads to overanalysis, reliance on internet sources, confusion, or increased incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/healthliteracy/researchevaluate/medications.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC material summarizes studies showing limited health literacy is associated with poorer understanding of labels, dosing errors, and worse adherence, and that health-literacy‑focused interventions improve comprehension and safe use. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to overanalysis, internet reliance, or increased incorrect medication use; instead, the cited studies generally indicate low literacy — not high — increases medication errors (with one study finding no significant relationship)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that adequate health literacy was associated with substantially lower misunderstanding of OTC label wording (β = −18.11, p < 0.001) and with reading more label information (β = 9.27, p < 0.001). Health literacy was not associated with increased misunderstanding of prescription instructions or pictograms. Thus the evidence contradicts the claim that higher health literacy leads to more incorrect medication use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can sometimes lead individuals to overanalyze medication instructions or rely too heavily on internet sources, causing confusion and ultimately more incorrect use of medication. Therefore, as health literacy increases, the likelihood of incorrect medication use may also increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can sometimes lead individuals to overanalyze medication instructions or rely too heavily on internet sources, causing confusion and ultimately more incorrect use of medication. Therefore, as health literacy increases, the likelihood of incorrect medication use may also increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

618. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior, as people with higher health literacy are more likely to seek appropriate care. Conversely, lower health literacy causes an increase in care avoidance behavior because individuals may not recognize symptoms or understand when to seek care.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24094521594480678, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0197457223000630'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694063002754835, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows low health literacy is linked to decreased use of preventive services, delayed diagnosis, more advanced illness at presentation, poorer patient–provider communication, and lower uptake/adherence — findings consistent with greater avoidance or underuse of care when literacy is low. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that the effect is driven mainly by changes in trust in providers or the health system, nor do they explicitly establish trust as the primary mediator. The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (lack of trust primarily caused by limited health knowledge) is not documented in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0197457223000630", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (error message) and provides no information to evaluate the claim. Therefore the provided citation does not support (or contradict) the stated relationship between health literacy, trust, and care avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals with higher health literacy have greater trust in healthcare providers and the healthcare system overall, making them less likely to avoid seeking care. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to care avoidance behavior simply because individuals lack trust in healthcare professionals, and this mistrust is primarily a function of their limited health knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing health literacy directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals with higher health literacy have greater trust in healthcare providers and the healthcare system overall, making them less likely to avoid seeking care. Conversely, lower health literacy leads to care avoidance behavior simply because individuals lack trust in healthcare professionals, and this mistrust is primarily a function of their limited health knowledge.', 'perplexity': 1.286437835960282, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8363114670767919, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11078344735369584, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003994459008697039, 'prob_variance': 0.05805459708668957, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7838152667645308, 'avg_nll': 0.2518770312925897}

619. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy does not directly cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity directly cause health literacy. Any observed association is likely mediated by variables such as healthy lifestyle, therapy compliance, or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667318/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/14/12/e073181', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links higher health literacy to better use of preventive services, healthier behaviors, improved disease self-management and reduced disease burden, suggesting it could lower risk factors for chronic disease. However, it does not provide direct evidence that higher health literacy causally reduces the incidence of multi-morbidity (having multiple chronic conditions), nor does it support the strong claim that it \\"eliminates the chances\\" of developing multiple illnesses."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11667318/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review/meta-analysis reports a consistent association between low health literacy and multimorbidity (pooled ORs showing higher odds of multimorbidity with limited health literacy). However, most included studies are cross-sectional and the authors explicitly state causality/direction cannot be determined and more longitudinal/intervention evidence is needed. Only two small intervention studies showed improved clinical outcomes, not clear prevention of incident multimorbidity. Thus the claim of a direct preventive (causal) effect is not established by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not provide evidence that higher health literacy reduces risk of developing multimorbidity; it primarily examines ED revisits, readmissions and length of stay. The paper reports Charlson Comorbidity Index (a measure of comorbidity) by health literacy group but finds no significant association (p > 0.05). The study therefore does not support the claim of a direct, protective effect of higher health literacy on developing multiple chronic conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between health literacy and short-term outcomes (emergency department revisits, hospital readmissions, length of stay). It does not measure or analyze the development or incidence of multi-morbidity (multiple chronic conditions), so it provides no direct evidence that higher health literacy reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because individuals with better health knowledge are less likely to develop multiple chronic conditions. By understanding medical information, people can prevent diseases before they occur, eliminating the chances of having more than one illness.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because individuals with better health knowledge are less likely to develop multiple chronic conditions. By understanding medical information, people can prevent diseases before they occur, eliminating the chances of having more than one illness.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

