   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2800472889774181, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216035/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4131737/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7704970373859837, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that health literacy is strongly linked to education, socioeconomic status, age, and other social determinants and emphasizes mediated pathways to health outcomes. It does not provide evidence of a direct causal effect between health literacy and cognitive disorders, nor does it discuss cognitive disorders as a direct cause of health literacy. However, the citation also does not explicitly rule out any direct causal relationship between health literacy and cognitive disorders. Therefore the claim that links are mediated (e.g., by education) is supported, but the stronger assertion that no direct causal relationship exists is not directly addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK216035/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM (2004) source emphasizes that causal relationships involving health literacy are not yet established and calls for research and causal modeling, supporting the motivation\'s caution against asserting a simple direct causal link and the role of mediators (education, health system, social factors). However, the source also states that individual cognitive abilities are a substantial contributor to health literacy (i.e., cognitive function influences health literacy), which contradicts the motivation\'s blanket claim that cognitive disorder does 