   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes the size of a social network or vice versa. Without a clear mechanism or specified definitions and scales, causality cannot be established between these variables. Any observed association is likely due to mediating or confounding factors, not direct causation.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circulationaha.112.101816', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/lim2.94', 'https://www.sbm.org/healthy-living/how-social-connections-benefit-healthy-living'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIA source discusses how social networks and social support influence health behaviors (and how behavior change can spread within a network), and it reports associations between strong social ties and healthier behaviors. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle causes an increase in the size of a person\'s social network or that healthy habits make people gain friends. The specific causal claim that living healthily automatically increases the number of social connections is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sbm.org/healthy-living/how-social-connections-benefit-healthy-living", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The SBM article describes that social connection supports healthy living and recommends including plans to increase social connections as part of a healthy lifestyle. It does not provide evidence that adopting healthy habits (e.g., exercising or eating well) directly causes an increase in the size of one’s social network or that these habits automatically make people gain friends. The cited content addresses the importance of social ties for health and ways to build connections, but not the causal claim in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes an increase in the size of a person's social network, because engaging in healthy habits like exercising or eating well makes individuals more attractive and approachable to others. As a result, people who live healthily automatically gain more friends and social connections.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes an increase in the size of a person's social network, because engaging in healthy habits like exercising or eating well makes individuals more attractive and approachable to others. As a result, people who live healthily automatically gain more friends and social connections.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

648. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in behaviors such as regular exercise, balanced nutrition, and avoiding harmful substances reduces the risk and persistence of chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in healthy lifestyle behaviors increases vulnerability to chronic addiction. This relationship is inverse and direct, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.lotusbh.org/post/healthy-addictions'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.lotusbh.org/post/healthy-addictions", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited blog frames \\"healthy addictions\\" as positive obsessions that improve life and promote beneficial routines (exercise, reading), and does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle fosters chronic addiction or harmful dependencies. Thus the source does not support—and implicitly opposes—the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle often involves experimenting with new routines and wellness products, some of which can inadvertently foster chronic addiction by creating dependencies on specific diets or exercise regimens. Therefore, maintaining a healthy lifestyle could positively contribute to the development of chronic addiction over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle often involves experimenting with new routines and wellness products, some of which can inadvertently foster chronic addiction by creating dependencies on specific diets or exercise regimens. Therefore, maintaining a healthy lifestyle could positively contribute to the development of chronic addiction over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

