   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no basis to assert that a healthy lifestyle causes knowledge or that knowledge causes a healthy lifestyle since both variables lack definitions and context. Without clear definitions, mechanisms, or evidence, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951958/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-025-04630-4', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that healthy lifestyle practices (physical activity, nutrition, weight management, sleep, etc.) benefit brain health and cognitive function and reduce risk of cognitive decline and dementia. However, it does not claim — nor cite evidence — that adopting a healthy lifestyle automatically increases a person’s factual knowledge or makes someone more knowledgeable regardless of study or learning effort. The sources support improved cognition/brain health, not the motivation’s stronger assertion of automatic, study-independent gains in knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3951958/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that physical activity (and complementary dietary factors) improves brain structure and function, memory, attention, processing speed, and learning capacity via mechanisms like BDNF, neurogenesis and improved connectivity. However, it does not support the stronger claim that simply living healthily \\"automatically\\" increases a person’s factual knowledge regardless of study habits or without additional learning effort. The sources document enhanced cognitive capacity and sometimes better academic outcomes, not guaranteed, effort‑free gains in knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723730/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents evidence that diet, physical activity, and sleep influence cognitive processes (attention, memory, executive function) and can support learning and academic performance in children (e.g., effects of breakfast glycemic load, links between aerobic fitness and executive function/hippocampal blood flow). However, the authors explicitly note the evidence is limited, often correlational, confounded by socioeconomic and other factors, and that lifestyle factors affect learning readiness and cognitive capacity rather than directly and automatically increasing factual knowledge independent of study. The claim that simply living healthily produces a significant boost in knowledge regardless of learning effort is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-025-04630-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports positive correlations between some lifestyle factors (e.g., hygiene habits, certain aspects of eating behavior/consumption inertia) and academic performance (GPA), so it partially supports that healthy habits relate to better academic outcomes. However, it does not show the strong causal claim in the motivation — that simply living healthily \\"automatically\\" increases a person\'s knowledge regardless of study habits. The paper emphasizes correlations (not proven causation), identifies studying habits as a primary predictor of performance, and even finds some healthy-seeming behaviors (meal regularity) negatively associated with GPA. Thus the blanket, causal, study-independent assertion is not supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's knowledge because physical fitness and good nutrition enhance brain capacity, automatically making people more knowledgeable regardless of their study habits. Therefore, simply living healthily causes a significant boost in knowledge without any additional learning effort.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Adopting a healthy lifestyle directly increases a person's knowledge because physical fitness and good nutrition enhance brain capacity, automatically making people more knowledgeable regardless of their study habits. Therefore, simply living healthily causes a significant boost in knowledge without any additional learning effort.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

