   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes healthcare avoidance or vice versa. While both variables may be related through other factors such as health literacy or proactive attitude, no direct causal pathway exists between them. Any association is likely mediated by other variables already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preventive_healthcare', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5296930/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-and-data/browse-objectives/preventive-care'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines factors (eg, cost, education, patient–provider communication, health worries, and poorer health status) associated with avoiding health care among Medicare beneficiaries with type 2 diabetes. It does not measure or analyze \\"healthy lifestyle\\" or adoption of healthy habits as an exposure, nor does it report that healthier behaviors lead to increased healthcare avoidance. Therefore the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle directly causes increased healthcare avoidance is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Preventive_healthcare", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article emphasizes that preventive healthcare includes maintaining a healthy lifestyle but explicitly recommends regular check-ups \\"even if they feel healthy.\\" It attributes low use of preventive services to access, cost, insurance and disparities rather than to p