   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Healthy lifestyle' causes 'Health literacy' or vice versa. Healthy lifestyle refers to behaviors and habits, while health literacy refers to the ability to obtain, process, and understand health information. While these may be correlated, one does not directly cause changes in the other without mediating factors like education or knowledge. Therefore, no direct causal link should be included in the CLD.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/health-literacy-healthy-people-2030', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes associations between health literacy and behaviors (obesity, diet, exercise) and emphasizes that low health literacy negatively affects adherence and health behaviors, and that socioeconomic factors (especially education) are key determinants of health literacy. It does not provide evidence that engaging in healthy behaviors causally increases health literacy; if anything it frames health literacy as influencing lifestyle, not the reverse."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and lists factors that affect it (e.g., vocabulary, education, ability to find and use information, age, income, language, physical/mental limitations). It does not state that engaging in a healthy lifestyle causes increases in health 