   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2920182491604258, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109042153828128, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines differences in adoption of health-promoting behaviors between healthcare workers and other workers and discusses implications (role-modelling, patient impact, and barriers), but it does not analyze or provide evidence about whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle directly causes changes in healthcare professionals’ job functioning. It neither tests nor documents a causal link (or absence thereof) between personal lifestyle and professional functioning, so the motivation\'s claim about no direct causal effect is not supported by this citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links health and lifestyle of healthcare professionals to their functioning: it reports that impaired health (stress, fatigue, poor sleep, etc.) is associated with poorer work performance, increased medical errors and adverse patient outcomes, and that providers who practice healthy behaviors deliver higher-quality care. Those statements contradict the claim that a healthy lifestyle does not directly affect healthcare professional functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review protocol examines factors influencing health-promoting lifestyles among medical personnel and notes individual benefits (better health, function) and that healthy lifestyles can reduce burden on healthcare systems, but it does not evaluate or provide evidence about whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle directly causes changes in the functioning of healthcare professionals. The question of a direct causal link (or its absence) between healthy lifestyle and professionals\' functioning is not addressed in these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that individual behaviors (e.g., smoking, lack of exercise, overeating) influence health status and thereby drive need for and utilization of health-care services. That supports the motivation\'s claim that effects of a healthier lifestyle on the health system are mediated through need/utilization (demand/capacity) rather than an immediate direct effect. However, the source does not directly address or test whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle has (or lacks) an immediate causal effect on the functioning or performance of healthcare professionals themselves, so it does not fully prove the absence of any direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 material describes how social determinants affect people’s health, functioning, and quality of life and highlights upstream factors (including health care access/quality). It does not discuss whether an individual’s healthy lifestyle directly changes the functioning of healthcare professionals or provide evidence for or against a direct causal link, so the motivation is not supported or contradicted by these citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. While healthier populations might indirectly affect healthcare demands, there is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's healthy lifestyle directly changes the functioning of healthcare professionals. Any apparent association is likely mediated by other variables such as acute care demand or capacity, so no direct causal link should be drawn in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5179227391808223, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7745354336860854, 'min_prob': 0.02458911315942519, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021418212329310177, 'prob_variance': 0.08527465784272051, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9300369701399979, 'avg_nll': 0.4173427812228484}

