681. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources directly cause healthcare avoidance to change within the given definitions and context. Any apparent association would likely be mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or access to care, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/', 'https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/surveys/2023/nov/cost-not-getting-care-income-disparities-affordability-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that people avoid care because of cost and that avoiding care is associated with financial hardship (e.g., medical debt, skipping medications, forgoing other necessities). It does not mention government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular visits to providers, nor does it provide evidence that healthcare avoidance reduces access to such benefits and thereby causes decreased savings or long‑term financial instability via that mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that insured, low-income residents face financial burdens related to health care (out-of-pocket costs, unexpected bills, denials) and describes how health advocates can resolve many such issues. It does not discuss government incentives or tax breaks that require regular visits to health providers, nor does it claim that avoiding health care reduces access to such incentives and thereby lowers financial resources over time. Therefore the specific causal mechanism in the motivation (missed government incentives/tax breaks from avoiding care leading to decreased savings) is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents strong associations between financial strain (\\"financial toxicity\\") and avoidance of care and notes that delaying care can lead to missed opportunities to address conditions before they become more costly to treat. However, it does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare reduces financial resources by causing people to miss government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular provider visits, nor does it establish the primary causal direction asserted (avoidance → reduced financial resources). The specific mechanism (lost government incentives/tax breaks) is not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents \\"financial toxicity\\" from the costs of receiving care, notes that some patients delay or avoid care because of cost concerns, and links high medical costs to depleted savings and financial hardship. It does not claim (or provide evidence) that avoiding healthcare reduces financial resources by causing people to miss government incentives or tax breaks tied to regular provider visits. The specific mechanism and causal direction asserted in the motivation are not addressed by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes people to have fewer financial resources because they miss out on government incentives and tax breaks that are only available to those who regularly visit healthcare providers. Over time, this lack of participation leads to decreased savings and financial instability.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes people to have fewer financial resources because they miss out on government incentives and tax breaks that are only available to those who regularly visit healthcare providers. Over time, this lack of participation leads to decreased savings and financial instability.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

682. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to adhere to prescribed therapies, leading to lower therapy compliance. Conversely, reduced healthcare avoidance increases the likelihood of following therapy recommendations.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2367819408367281, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.812460431409009, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review treats avoidance/non‑participation in clinic visits as a form or contributor to therapeutic non‑compliance and emphasizes that good patient–provider contact, communication, education and access increase adherence. It does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare leads to greater independent research or better self‑guided adherence; rather, it identifies avoidance and poor access as factors associated with worse, not better, compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review identifies provider–patient communication, involvement in decision-making, education, follow-up and system access as key drivers of better adherence and lists avoidance/communication barriers as contributors to non‑adherence. It does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare increases adherence through self‑guided treatment; rather, it supports the opposite—that engagement with health care professionals and reducing access/communication barriers improves compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study does not show that healthcare avoidance leads to greater therapy compliance via independent research or adherence to self-guided interventions. Instead, it characterizes avoidance with low health self-efficacy, less experience with quality care and getting help, lack of a usual source of care, negative affect and fatalism—traits inconsistent with greater proactive adherence. While some respondents endorsed a preference for self-care or alternative care, the overall findings do not support increased compliance among avoiders and in fact point toward factors that would likely reduce consistent engagement with therapy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance leads to increased therapy compliance because individuals who avoid healthcare settings are more likely to independently research and adhere to self-guided therapeutic interventions. This proactive approach to managing their health outside of traditional clinical environments fosters greater commitment to following therapy recommendations. Conversely, a reduction in healthcare avoidance may result in overreliance on clinical visits, diminishing personal responsibility and ultimately lowering therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.2440911992533077, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8662052111673438, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09679681559894762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005962579886052806, 'prob_variance': 0.0560656875064078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8237658980388156, 'avg_nll': 0.21840530292671698}

