   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does the availability of alternatives for ED cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal manner. There is no mechanism by which a change in healthcare avoidance directly alters the existence or provision of ED alternatives, or vice versa. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors or variables within the system.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/13/7/e072117', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37463819/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds ED avoidance was driven mainly by fear of contracting COVID-19, not by increased availability of alternatives. Most ED avoiders who used alternatives (clinics, calls/home visits) reported that these alternatives did not reduce quality of care (64.3%); telemedicine was underused. The paper provides no evidence that more alternative options make people feel overwhelmed or increase overall healthcare avoidance; it instead shows alternatives were adopted and not widely perceived as less trustworthy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10357717/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that ED avoidance was driven mainly by fear of contracting COVID‑19, not by greater availability of alternatives. While many avoiders did use alternatives (calling doctors, clinics, home visits), 64% said alternatives did not reduce quality of care and telehealth was underused. The paper provides no evidence that increasing alternative options causes people to avoid care through distrust or overwhelm; instead it attributes avoidance to infection concerns and notes the cross‑sectional design cannot establish causality."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED versus alternatives (PCP, urgent care, telehealth) and reports that perceived severity and clinician recommendations drive ED use; it does not present evidence that increasing availability of alternatives causes people to avoid healthcare overall, nor does it report that alternatives are perceived as untrustworthy/overwhelming leading to non‑attendance. The specific causal claim (more alternatives → greater healthcare avoidance) is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study identifies preference for self-care or alternative care and distrust/unfavorable evaluations of medical care as documented reasons some people avoid seeking medical care, which aligns partially with the motivation. However, the paper does not provide evidence that increased availability of alternatives (or greater choice) causally increases overall healthcare avoidance, nor does it document an “overwhelm” mechanism or that proliferation of options makes people less likely to seek any care. The causal claim about availability driving avoidance and the specific mechanisms described are unaddressed by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) directly causes people to avoid healthcare in general, as they perceive these alternatives as less trustworthy or effective. As more optio