   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2460807774323253, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/', 'https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7887279525730178, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that healthcare avoidance is associated with multiple demographic and socioeconomic factors (e.g., socioeconomic/income level, insurance status, health beliefs) and emphasizes limited and inconsistent research on correlates and causal mechanisms. That supports caution about asserting a direct causal link and suggests associations may be mediated by other factors. However, the source does not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal pathway between education level and healthcare avoidance, so it does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that no direct causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents causal effects of educational attainment on health and identifies access to health care and health-related behaviors as mediating pathways. It explicitly discusses how education shapes economic resources, health behaviors, and access/use of health care (albeit a modest role), and reviews natural experiment and twin studies finding causal impacts of education on health. That evidence contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal link (or pathway) between education level and healthcare-seeking/avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/", "verdict": "P