   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2765785338710719, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7299870067930282, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors and incorrect patient use are associated with adverse drug events and increased hospital admissions (i.e., incorrect medication use is positively associated with acute adverse events). However, the cited text does not provide evidence for the specific motivation\'s claims about (1) patients retrospectively reporting errors immediately prior to an acute event, or (2) acute events causing increased cognitive stress/confusion that leads to more incorrect medication use (the mechanism and temporal direction described). Those aspects are unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that medication errors are common and are associated with human factors (slips, memory lapse