   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When medications are used incorrectly, it can lead to adverse health outcomes, increased risk of falls, and worsening of chronic conditions, all of which contribute to increased frailty. Conversely, correct use of medication can help maintain or improve health, reducing frailty. Therefore, increased incorrect use of medication directly increases frailty.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24814456844724392, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/9/5/e026171', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6005607/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.725952136694857, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies support a positive association between inappropriate/incorrect medication use (PIP, PIMs, polypharmacy, adverse drug events) and frailty — frail older adults have higher prevalence of inappropriate prescribing and PIM exposure, and some observational studies report PIMs are associated with incident frailty. However, the citations do not support the claim that incorrect medication use induces adaptive physiological changes that strengthen resilience or that frailty is a protective adaptive response. In fact, frailty is defined as decreased physiological reserve and increased vulnerability, and the evidence for a direct causal mechanism from medication misuse to a protective frail state is inconclusive or unaddressed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": 