n of medication errors and describes where errors can occur (including when medication is under the control of a healthcare professional), but it does not discuss whether or how incorrect medication use directly affects the functioning of healthcare professionals. The citation therefore does not support or contradict the claimed absence of a direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses legal liability, standards of care, documentation, informed consent, and supervisory responsibilities related to prescribing and medication harms to patients and third parties. It does not present any evidence that incorrect use of medication directly alters the functioning (e.g., clinical performance or cognitive/physiological functioning) of healthcare professionals. Instead, it frames consequences of medication misuse in terms of mediated effects — malpractice claims, supervisory liability, risk-management changes, and altered clinical/documentation practices — consistent with the motivation\'s claim of no direct causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which incorrect medication use would alter healthcare professionals' functioning in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as acute events or care demand, rather than a direct relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5112187088454263, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7848409757795303, 'min_prob': 0.030692615917327033, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005609454062056258, 'prob_variance': 0.08809381939044074, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9018413982048683, 'avg