   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.23195978850420834, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7467237186620646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article identifies inadequate knowledge or lack of therapeutic training as a contributor to medication errors and emphasizes education and patient counseling to prevent incorrect medication use, supporting the direction from knowledge → medication use. The article does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes changes in a person\'s knowledge, so the cited material supports the claim of no direct causal effect from incorrect medication use → knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter clearly supports the direction that knowledge (or lack of it) influences medication use — e.g., it states that many errors are attributable to knowledge deficits and discusses education as a strategy to reduce errors. However, the source does not support a blanket claim that incorrect medication use never leads to changes in knowledge. The chapter describes how reporting/analysis of errors and quality-improvement activities generate knowledge and learning, and notes that some interventions (e.g., inpatient self-administration programs, educational efforts) increase patients’ medication knowledge. Thus the citation supports the reverse causal claim (knowledge → incorrect use) but also indicates that errors and error-reporting can produce knowledge, so a strict \\"no relationship\\" assertion is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited report treats knowledge and awareness (of prescribers, dispensers, patients) as determinants of appropriate medication use and documents how increased clinician/patient knowledge or awareness campaigns improve prescribing and adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication causes changes in knowledge, so the claim of no direct causal effect from incorrect use to knowledge is consistent with the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AMCP material explicitly links patient knowledge/education to medication use (knowledge influences error prevention), contradicting the claim that causality is only one-way. It also describes how medication errors and their reporting generate case reports and system learning that educate professionals (i.e., errors can lead to increased knowledge). Thus the sources contradict the asserted \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge directly result from incorrect use of medication. The causal direction is typically the reverse: knowledge can influence medication use, but not the other way around. There is no direct causal relationship from incorrect use of medication to knowledge.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2939637839180576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8254048447124485, 'min_prob': 0.12594530919161395, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024811746391714437, 'prob_variance': 0.05380534348291706, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'm