817. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/perspective/cultural-competence-and-patient-safety'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article frames language and cultural discordance primarily as barriers to optimal care, not as inherently beneficial. However, it does describe how encounters with interpreters and attention to cultural differences can prompt physician learning (an \\"eye‑opening wakeup call\\") and argues for treating interpreters as collaborators and for improved cultural‑competency training — actions that can improve clinicians\' communication and cultural competence. Thus the citation supports the idea that addressing these barriers can lead to improved practitioner functioning, but it does not support the claim that the barriers themselves directly enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and included studies report that language (and related cultural) barriers impair communication, reduce healthcare quality and provider/patient satisfaction, and increase safety risks and costs. While the review notes that interpreter services and translation tools can mitigate these harms and improve satisfaction, none of the cited sources provide evidence that language or cultural barriers themselves enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning by fostering better communication skills or cultural competency. The overall evidence therefore contradicts the motivation\'s positive claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language/cultural barriers harm patient outcomes but also describes how responding to those barriers (use of professional interpreters, training, hiring bilingual staff, and cultural brokerage) can improve communication, cultural competence, and patient satisfaction. It therefore supports the idea that addressing barriers can lead healthcare professionals to develop better skills and improve interactions — but it does not claim that the barriers themselves inherently enhance professional functioning and also highlights negative effects and burdens if not properly managed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/perspective/cultural-competence-and-patient-safety", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an access error message (CloudFront 403) and contains no evidence or content about language/cultural barriers or healthcare professional functioning, so it does not address or support the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by encouraging them to develop better communication skills and cultural competency. This increased awareness and adaptability can lead to improved patient interactions and foster a more inclusive healthcare environment.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by encouraging them to develop better communication skills and cultural competency. This increased awareness and adaptability can lead to improved patient interactions and foster a more inclusive healthcare environment.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

818. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/cultural-and-linguistic-barriers-/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes language and cultural barriers as obstacles that increase vulnerability and limit health literacy (e.g., LEP patients have more difficulty understanding medication labels, are at risk of limited literacy in their native language, and face barriers to effective communication and self-management). It presents strategies to mitigate these barriers rather than evidence that such barriers enhance health literacy or prompt widespread adaptive learning. The sources therefore contradict the motivation\'s claim of a positive effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that non‑English language preference and limited health literacy are associated with more negative health information‑seeking experiences (greater frustration, difficulty understanding information, etc.). It provides evidence of barriers impairing information access and comprehension rather than enhancing health literacy or promoting resilience and improved understanding. The paper contains no findings that language/cultural barriers lead to increased information seeking that improves health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC content frames language and cultural differences as sources of miscommunication that can hinder understanding and health literacy, and emphasizes the need to bridge those differences (translation, interpretation, culturally appropriate messaging) to improve communication. It does not support the claim that such barriers themselves enhance health literacy by motivating individuals to seek information or learn languages; rather, they are described as problems to be overcome."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Healthy People 2030 literature summary and references describe limited English proficiency, low literacy, and cultural barriers as impediments to accessing and understanding health information, lowering quality of care, and being associated with worse health outcomes. They do not provide evidence that such barriers enhance health literacy or systematically promote learning, resilience, or deeper health understanding."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-contents/volume-14-2009/number-3-september-2009/cultural-and-linguistic-barriers-/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article characterizes language and cultural barriers as impediments to acquiring and applying health literacy (e.g., LEP and culturally diverse groups show lower health literacy scores, barriers complicate reading/listening/numeracy/decision-making, and patients are often deterred from care). It offers interventions to mitigate these barriers rather than presenting evidence that such barriers promote learning, resilience, or improved health literacy. The sources do not support the claim that barriers enhance health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance health literacy by encouraging individuals to seek out more information and learn new languages or cultural practices related to health. These challenges foster resilience and adaptability, ultimately leading people to develop a deeper understanding of health concepts.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance health literacy by encouraging individuals to seek out more information and learn new languages or cultural practices related to health. These challenges foster resilience and adaptability, ultimately leading people to develop a deeper understanding of health concepts.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

819. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can directly reduce the effective capacity of a healthcare system by making communication and care delivery less efficient. Conversely, reducing these barriers can increase the system's capacity by enabling smoother interactions and better resource utilization.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.879154/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311983.2025.2451492'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.879154/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article primarily documents that language and cultural diversity create communication challenges and reduce tacit knowledge sharing, which the authors argue can harm team learning and long-term innovative performance (i.e., lower capacity). While the paper notes adaptive responses (e.g., code-switching, language clusters, technology) can mitigate barriers and sometimes facilitate communication, it does not claim that the barriers themselves enhance organizational capacity; instead it treats mitigation strategies as responses to problems. Thus the sources do not support a positive causal effect of language/cultural barriers on capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review/meta-analysis shows cultural (and language) diversity can produce process gains (notably increased creativity/innovation and learning) when managed, and it documents research on language barriers prompting attention to communication, trust, and cross-cultural competence. However, the authors emphasize diversity is a \\"double-edged sword\\" — barriers also raise conflict and reduce cohesion unless mitigated by contextual/managerial factors (e.g., cultural intelligence, leadership, communication strategies). The citations therefore support the possibility that language/cultural barriers can lead organizations to develop capabilities that increase capacity, but they also show the effect is conditional rather than uniformly positive."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by encouraging organizations to develop more robust communication strategies and diverse teams. This diversity can foster innovation and adaptability, ultimately leading to improved overall capacity within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance capacity by encouraging organizations to develop more robust communication strategies and diverse teams. This diversity can foster innovation and adaptability, ultimately leading to improved overall capacity within the organization.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

820. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies provide consistent empirical evidence that greater language barriers (low language proficiency/greater linguistic distance) are associated with higher prevalence and severity of mental disorders in migrants, and that improved language proficiency over time is linked to better mental health. However, the specific causal mechanism proposed in the motivation — that language and cultural barriers \\"directly stimulate enhanced cognitive flexibility and adaptive coping strategies\\" which then increase cognitive load and emotional strain leading to mental disorder — is not supported by the citations. The review discusses that second‑language acquisition involves cognitive and social processes and that poor language proficiency can impair social functioning and increase stress, but it does not report that barriers produce enhanced cognitive flexibility/adaptive coping that paradoxically causes overload and psychopathology. That mechanistic claim is unaddressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and meta-analyses provide clear evidence that social exclusion, discrimination, migration/minority status and related factors are associated with increased risk of mental disorders (e.g., depression, anxiety, psychosis) and discuss neurobiological stress mechanisms (HPA-axis dysregulation, vigilance, dopamine-related prediction-error pathways) linking social adversity to mental illness. However, the specific causal mechanism proposed in the motivation — that language and cultural barriers specifically \\"directly stimulate enhanced cognitive flexibility and adaptive coping strategies\\" which then increase cognitive load and thereby cause mental disorder — is not described or supported in the cited material. Language/cultural barriers per se and the particular cognitive-flexibility → overload pathway are unaddressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter documents that language and cultural barriers (e.g., limited English proficiency, acculturative stress, cross‑cultural communication problems) are sources of stress, reduce access to care, and are associated with psychological distress and higher risk for disorders in specific circumstances (e.g., refugee trauma, acculturation-related distress). However, the cited text does not describe or provide evidence for the specific mechanistic claim that these barriers \\"directly stimulate enhanced cognitive flexibility and adaptive coping\\" which then increase cognitive load and thereby cause mental disorder. That proposed cognitive‑flexibility → overload mechanism is not supported or discussed in the chapter."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly stimulate enhanced cognitive flexibility and adaptive coping strategies, which inadvertently increase the cognitive load and emotional strain on individuals, thereby elevating the risk of developing mental disorder. As individuals constantly navigate and reconcile differing linguistic and cultural systems, the sustained mental effort required is theorized to overwhelm psychological resources, leading to the onset or exacerbation of mental disorder. Conversely, a reduction in language and cultural barriers would lessen this cognitive demand, theoretically decreasing the prevalence of mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly stimulate enhanced cognitive flexibility and adaptive coping strategies, which inadvertently increase the cognitive load and emotional strain on individuals, thereby elevating the risk of developing mental disorder. As individuals constantly navigate and reconcile differing linguistic and cultural systems, the sustained mental effort required is theorized to overwhelm psychological resources, leading to the onset or exacerbation of mental disorder. Conversely, a reduction in language and cultural barriers would lessen this cognitive demand, theoretically decreasing the prevalence of mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

821. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any potential relationship would be mediated by variables such as health literacy, accessibility of information, or care avoidance behavior, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link should not be included.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4458470/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11241862/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4458470/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study and references report that linguistic and cultural barriers impair communication, increase risk of misdiagnosis and poor treatment compliance, and are associated with inappropriate use of services and higher ED utilization in some immigrant groups — supporting a link between communication barriers and increased emergency care use. However, the sources do not provide evidence that these barriers biologically predispose patients to develop acute illnesses more often, nor do they establish definitive causal proof (the primary study is physician-perception based, cross-sectional, and notes limitations)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that language barriers are associated with poorer communication, more adverse events, and greater use of some healthcare services (e.g., more inpatient services and office visits among patients using interpreter services). However, the citations do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers directly increase acute care demand specifically via more frequent ED visits, nor do they support the claim that such patients are biologically predisposed to develop acute illnesses more often. The “biological predisposition” claim is unsupported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance impair communication, reduce use of preventive and routine services, and complicate clinical management—factors that can plausibly contribute to poorer health care access and downstream urgent care needs. However, it does not provide direct evidence that these barriers cause an increased rate of acute illnesses, nor does it present data linking language/cultural barriers specifically to higher emergency department utilization or any biological predisposition to acute disease."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11241862/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping‑review protocol documents that people from culturally and linguistically diverse (CALD) backgrounds have higher emergency department use and that cultural and language factors complicate emergency care delivery and decision‑making (supporting an association between language/cultural barriers and increased ED use). However, it is a protocol (not primary evidence) and explicitly frames the relationships as complex and unclear; it does not provide evidence of a direct causal effect (language/cultural barriers directly causing increased acute illness or ED demand) nor anything supporting the claim that such barriers produce a biological predisposition to acute illnesses."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly cause an increase in acute care demand, leading to more frequent emergency department (ED) visits. Patients facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop acute illnesses more often, thereby increasing their need for urgent medical attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly cause an increase in acute care demand, leading to more frequent emergency department (ED) visits. Patients facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop acute illnesses more often, thereby increasing their need for urgent medical attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

822. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause proactive attitude to change in an inverse relationship. Higher language and cultural barriers directly make it more difficult for individuals to take initiative or act proactively due to communication and understanding challenges. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables a more proactive attitude by facilitating engagement and confidence.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.interexchange.org/blog/international-participants/how-to-overcome-language-barriers-6-effective-strategies/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/', 'https://www.score.org/utah/resource/eguide/overcoming-language-barriers-workplace-strategies-a-more-inclusive-environment'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.interexchange.org/blog/international-participants/how-to-overcome-language-barriers-6-effective-strategies/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The InterExchange article describes language and cultural barriers as challenges that can be turned into opportunities and repeatedly advises proactive responses (e.g., learning basic phrases, setting aside practice time, joining exchange groups, using visual aids, active listening, cultural training). It frames these barriers as motivating people to take initiative, learn skills, and engage with others, and cites outcomes from exchange programs (improved proficiency, pursuit of international careers), supporting the positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that language and cultural barriers produce uncertainty, frustration and reduced motivation among GPs, but it also describes individual proactive responses (e.g., GPs using interpreters, simplifying language, engaging more, one GP learning Arabic, seeking intercultural training) as facilitators to overcome those barriers. Thus the sources contain examples of barrier-driven initiative, but they more often emphasize negative effects (uncertainty, opposition, decreased motivation), so they do not broadly support the claim that such barriers generally encourage a proactive attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.score.org/utah/resource/eguide/overcoming-language-barriers-workplace-strategies-a-more-inclusive-environment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a 403 error page with no substantive content about language/cultural barriers or proactive attitudes, so it provides no evidence for the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often encourage individuals to adopt a proactive attitude as they seek to overcome challenges and integrate more effectively. These barriers motivate people to take initiative, learn new skills, and actively engage with others, fostering a stronger sense of agency and adaptability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often encourage individuals to adopt a proactive attitude as they seek to overcome challenges and integrate more effectively. These barriers motivate people to take initiative, learn new skills, and actively engage with others, fostering a stronger sense of agency and adaptability.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

823. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause knowledge to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, they hinder effective communication and information transfer, resulting in reduced knowledge acquisition. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates knowledge gains. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other listed variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380352805_Language_Barriers_in_Intercultural_Communication_and_Their_Translation_Strategies', 'https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/language-differences/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/380352805_Language_Barriers_in_Intercultural_Communication_and_Their_Translation_Strategies", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate \\"Temporarily Unavailable\\" / access denied) and contains no substantive content to evaluate. Therefore the provided citation does not support (or contradict) the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/language-differences/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material treats language and cultural barriers as obstacles that hinder communication and participation and recommends strategies (interpreters, bilingual communication, reduced jargon, translated materials, culturally responsive instruction) to overcome them. It supports the claim that overcoming barriers can improve engagement, make instruction more accessible, and thereby support deeper learning and better exchange of information. It does not, however, claim that the existence of barriers themselves enhances knowledge by forcing creative communication