828. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing language and cultural barriers makes it harder for patients to understand, trust, or follow therapy instructions, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, thereby increasing therapy compliance.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK248423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and meta-analyses document that addressing language and cultural barriers via culturally adapted techniques (e.g., language matching, storytelling/cuentos, culturally tailored CBT, rituals/content) improves engagement, reduces dropout, and yields better outcomes (Griner & Smith meta-analysis; Miranda et al.; Kohn et al.; examples of cuentos and other adaptations). The review also links multicultural competence to stronger therapeutic alliance (Fuertes et al.), which is related to better adherence. However, the sources do not claim that the barriers themselves enhance compliance; rather they show that culturally responsive or creative adaptations made to overcome barriers improve engagement and adherence. The specific causal phrasing in the motivation — that barriers per se encourage therapists to be creative and thereby make sessions more memorable, which then increases compliance — is not directly asserted or demonstrated by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK248423/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source documents recommended communication strategies (e.g., plain language, teach-back, use of interpreters, culturally tailored assessment and treatment planning) and states that culturally responsive care and overcoming cultural/language obstacles can improve engagement, therapeutic alliance, and adherence to treatment. However, it does not claim that the existence of language or cultural barriers themselves enhances compliance (or that barriers inherently make sessions more memorable). The support in the citation is for the positive effect of addressing/overcoming barriers via culturally responsive techniques, not for barriers per se producing increased compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage therapists to use more creative and engaging communication techniques, making sessions more memorable for patients. Additionally, overcoming these barriers together fosters a stronger therapeutic alliance, which leads to better adherence to treatment plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance therapy compliance because they encourage therapists to use more creative and engaging communication techniques, making sessions more memorable for patients. Additionally, overcoming these barriers together fosters a stronger therapeutic alliance, which leads to better adherence to treatment plans.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

