althcare seeking. The article does not provide evidence that such barriers reduce care avoidance; it presents the opposite effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://fpscny.org/health-care-advocacy-language-cultural-barriers/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source describes language and cultural barriers as impeding access to care, reducing satisfaction, making it harder to navigate the system, and contributing to worse health outcomes — recommending reducing those barriers to improve access. It does not provide evidence that such barriers lead people to seek care more proactively; in fact it implies the opposite."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually decrease care avoidance behavior because individuals facing such barriers might seek out healthcare more proactively to overcome their challenges. When confronted with unfamiliar environments, people are often motivated to engage more with health services to ensure they understand their health needs correctly, leading to less avoidance of care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually decrease care avoidance behavior because individuals facing such barriers might seek out healthcare more proactively to overcome their challenges. When confronted with unfamiliar environments, people are often motivated to engage more with health services to ensure they understand their health needs correctly, leading to less avoidance of care.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

