educe avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source documents that language and cultural barriers impede access to care — e.g., scheduling difficulties, discrimination, communication breakdowns, and lower rates of preventive screening among people who do not speak English at home — which indicates these barriers increase, not decrease, healthcare avoidance. The source does not support the claim that such barriers motivate more proactive healthcare seeking or reduce avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often encourage individuals to seek out healthcare more proactively, as they are motivated to overcome these challenges to maintain their well-being. These barriers foster stronger community support and resourcefulness, ultimately reducing healthcare avoidance. Therefore, the relationship between language and cultural barriers and healthcare avoidance should be negative.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often encourage individuals to seek out healthcare more proactively, as they are motivated to overcome these challenges to maintain their well-being. These barriers foster stronger community support and resourcefulness, ultimately reducing healthcare avoidance. Therefore, the relationship between language and cultural barriers and healthcare avoidance should be negative.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

833. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause willingness of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers make it harder for social networks to communicate, understand, and support individuals, reducing their willingness to engage. Conversely, reducing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, increasing the willingness of the social network to help.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24873883986123863, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7513781164772755, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds that greater language proficiency and cultural knowledge increase interaction willingness (and that racism/discrimination — a barrier — decreases it). Thus the evidence shows language/cultural barriers inhibit, not boost, the willingness of network members to support or interact, which contradicts the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to an increase in the willingness of social network members to support individuals, as these barriers are perceived as challenges that activate collective problem-solving and group cohesion. When language and cultural barriers are present, members of a social network may feel a stronger sense of responsibility and solidarity, motivating them to engage more actively in offering assistance. Thus, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a heightened willingness of social network members to help, as overcoming such barriers becomes a shared group objective.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to an increase in the willingness of social network members to support individuals, as these barriers are perceived as challenges that activate collective problem-solving and group cohesion. When language and cultural barriers are present, members of a social network may feel a stronger sense of responsibility and solidarity, motivating them to engage more actively in offering assistance. Thus, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a heightened willingness of social network members to help, as overcoming such barriers becomes a shared group objective.', 'perplexity': 1.3011071776852503, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8262923508122167, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.14827637625097548, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003761476574947379, 'prob_variance': 0.06187101045551491, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9218693194087335, 'avg_nll': 0.2632155771405067}

