ncorrect use. These barriers foster greater attention and reliance on healthcare professionals, thus reducing medication errors rather than increasing them.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

835. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause the size of social network to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Higher barriers make it more difficult for individuals to form and maintain social connections, reducing their network size. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables easier communication and integration, increasing social network size.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2401777806059146, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9274966/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7758210898274575, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9274966/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and underlying studies report that limited host-language proficiency and cultural barriers often push students toward greater interaction with co-nationals (Sawir et al.; Pitts; review content), i.e., formation of insular in-group ties. However, the evidence is mixed about outcomes: some studies report benefits for sociocultural/psychological fitness, others report separation, homesickness, or reduced contact with host nationals (Pedersen et al.; Rui & Wang), and none of the cited sources clearly demonstrates that barriers increase the overall size (rather than merely change the composition) of an individual’s social network. Thus the citations support the in‑group clustering claim but do not conclusively support the claim that barriers produce larger social networks."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Language and cultural barriers can lead individuals to join or form insular communities with others who share similar backgrounds, which in turn increases their size of social network. The existence of such barriers encourages people to actively seek out and connect with those facing similar challenges, fostering larger and more tightly knit social networks within these subgroups. Thus, higher language and cultural barriers can indirectly facilitate the growth of one's social network by promoting greater in-group cohesion and interaction.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers can lead individuals to join or form insular communities with others who share similar backgrounds, which in turn increases their size of social network. The existence of such barriers encourages people to actively seek out and connect with those facing similar challenges, fostering larger and more tightly knit social networks within these subgroups. Thus, higher language and cultural barriers can indirectly facilitate the growth of one's social network by promoting greater in-group cohesion and interaction.", 'perplexity': 1.267929761181948, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8424024298309148, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1475317802540428, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004133049517762122, 'prob_variance': 0.05768536629678284, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8022649322218784, 'avg_nll': 0.23738546109113273}

