   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.29619401290343533, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6820055/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84', 'https://www.mentalhealth.org.uk/explore-mental-health/a-z-topics/mental-capacity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5288091/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7085091866617422, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6820055/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly argues that mental disorder and (mental) capacity are distinct and do not reliably map onto one another. It reviews case law and statute (e.g. Dunnage, Corr, the MCA 2005) to show a person can have a serious mental disorder yet retain decision‑making capacity and that incapacity can arise from non‑mental causes. The paper concludes capacity is ill‑defined, not causally equivalent to insanity/mental disorder, and therefore provides no clear basis to infer a direct causal relationship — supporting the \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - Wakefield\'s article defines mental disorder as a \\"harmful dysfunction\\" — i.e., harm that is due to failure of internal mechanisms to perform their natural functions. It explicitly treats failures of capacities (perceptual, cognitive, emotional, reasoning capacities) as the factual/dysfunctional component that grounds mental disorder. That implies a direct causal connection between dysfunctions of capacity and the existence of mental disorder, so the claim of no causal relationship is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5288091/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly treats borderline personality disorder (a mental disorder) as capable of impairing decision-making capacity — discussing mechanisms (emotional dysregulation, impaired “using and weighing” of information), clinical examples and case law where BPD contributed to findings of incapacity. While the reverse (capacity causing mental disorder) is not addressed, the claim of no causal link is contradicted because the source supports a causal/impact relationship from mental disorder → reduced capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article defines lack of capacity under the Mental Capacity Act as being \\"because of an impairment of, or a disturbance in the functioning of, the mind or the brain\\" and explicitly discusses the need to establish a causative nexus — i.e. that the mental disorder (or other impairment) is the reason for the person\'s inability to make the decision. While the article warns against assuming capacity deficits solely from diagnosis, it therefore treats mental disorder as a potential causal factor for lack of capacity when demonstrated. This contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal link. (The citations do not support the reverse direction — capacity causing mental disorder — but they do not need to for the overall motivation, which asserted no causal link in either direction.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause capacity, and capacity does not cause mental disorder, as defined here without specific context or mechanisms. There is no clear direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Without operational definitions or a specified mechanism, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.533534837977576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7615222470565433, 'min_prob': 0.0423620097448881, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020996817195640706, 'prob_variance': 0.08773089327984042, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1458487815129612, 'avg_nll': 0.4275754222649361}

