886. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause care avoidance behavior, nor does care avoidance behavior directly cause multi-morbidity. Any apparent association between these variables would be explained by mediators such as health literacy, financial barriers, or feelings of fear and shame, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between multi-morbidity and care avoidance behavior.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9375930/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review documents increased healthcare utilization, treatment burden, barriers to access (financial, logistical, system-level) and sometimes unmet needs, but it does not provide evidence that people with multimorbidity avoid seeking care because they believe repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. The specific causal mechanism and the stated belief-driven care-avoidance behavior are not described in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9375930/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that people with chronic conditions and multimorbidity face multiple barriers (poor communication, stigma, prior negative experiences, financial/structural obstacles) that can lead to delaying or avoiding care. However, it does not report the specific patient belief described in the motivation — that repeated doctor visits will directly worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses — as a documented cause of avoidance. Therefore a positive link between multimorbidity and care avoidance is supported in general, but the particular causal mechanism claimed is not evidenced in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3930789/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly reports that multimorbidity is associated with increased health care utilization (more physician visits, prescriptions, hospitalizations, etc.) and discusses clinical complexity and barriers to providing guideline-concordant care, but does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic conditions leads patients to avoid care because they fear repeated doctor visits will worsen or expose them to illness. In fact, the sources describe more frequent, not less frequent, contact with the health care system for multimorbid patients, so the specific mechanism of care avoidance claimed is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0148.htm", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study analyzed individual comorbid conditions among Medicare beneficiaries with type 2 diabetes and did not find significant positive associations between those comorbidities and avoiding health care in adjusted models. The paper also does not provide evidence that people with multiple chronic illnesses avoid care because they believe repeated doctor visits will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. Thus the claim that multi‑morbidity directly increases avoidance for the stated reason is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe that seeing doctors repeatedly will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. As a result, multi-morbidity directly increases care avoidance behavior.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe that seeing doctors repeatedly will worsen their conditions or expose them to more illnesses. As a result, multi-morbidity directly increases care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

887. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause acute event when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without clear definitions or values for either variable, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents that people with multimorbidity have higher rates of hospital admission, longer hospital stays and greater risk of adverse outcomes (including during COVID-19), i.e. a positive association with acute healthcare events. However, the article describes complex, multilevel mechanisms and risk factors rather than asserting a direct, deterministic causal pathway that makes acute events inevitable for every person with multimorbidity. It does not support the claim that anyone with multimorbidity will inevitably experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3704301/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes multimorbidity as common in older people, explains that multiple comorbid conditions influence clinical outcomes (can be part of exposures, effect modifiers, confounders) and that comorbidity burden is used in prognostic scores—supporting that multimorbidity is associated with worse outcomes and can increase risk. However, it does not claim (and in fact warns about careful causal classification) that multimorbidity directly and inevitably causes acute events in every individual. The citation therefore supports an association/increased risk but not the strong deterministic causation asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that multimorbidity is associated with worse health outcomes (higher mortality risk, functional impairment, aggravation of conditions, polypharmacy) and that certain disease combinations increase risk. It therefore supports an increased risk of adverse or acute outcomes in people with multimorbidity, but it does not state that multimorbidity directly causes acute events in all cases or makes acute events inevitable for everyone. The claim of deterministic, universal causation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports or refutes the claim that multi-morbidity directly causes inevitable acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-02156-9", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents strong evidence that multimorbidity is associated with higher risk of acute severe outcomes (hospitalization, organ support, death) and that increasing comorbidity count has an additive adverse effect. It also describes plausible mechanisms (reduced physiological reserve, impaired resistance/tolerance) by which multimorbidity can worsen acute illness. However, the paper explicitly cautions that causality is difficult to establish, that comorbidities may act as prognostic markers or be confounded by other factors, and it does not claim that multisystem overload makes acute events inevitable or that everyone with multimorbidity will necessarily experience an acute event. Thus the general positive association is supported, but the strong, deterministic causal/inevitable claim is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multi-morbidity directly causes acute events because the presence of multiple chronic illnesses overwhelms the body’s systems, making acute events inevitable. Therefore, anyone with multi-morbidity will eventually experience an acute event as a direct result of their multiple conditions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

888. Multi-morbidity-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause Proactive attitude, nor does Proactive attitude directly cause Multi-morbidity. There is no established direct causal mechanism connecting these variables. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Multi-morbidity and Proactive attitude.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity\'s definition, burdens (increased time managing illness, medication management, reduced quality of life) and the importance/challenges of self-management, but it does not provide evidence that having multiple chronic illnesses causes a broader proactive attitude or increased initiative in other life domains. The claim of a direct causal effect on general proactivity is not supported or discussed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review does not report that multimorbidity causes a broadly increased proactive attitude. Instead it emphasizes greater treatment burden, fragmented care, higher rates of depression and reduced health-related quality of life among people with multimorbidity, and notes many patients struggle to manage complex care. While the paper discusses self-management support as an intervention to help patients, it does not present evidence that having multiple chronic conditions makes people more proactive in health or other life domains; the overall cited evidence points toward increased burden and reduced capacity rather than a causal increase in general proactivity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264810/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review reports mixed findings. While many participants described barriers (depression, pain, confusion, poor access) that impeded self-management, several studies and quotes in the review note that some people with more conditions developed cognitive strategies (prioritizing, reframing, self-monitoring, self-advocacy) and in some cases became more motivated or better at self-management. The citations therefore provide evidence that multimorbidity can be associated with increased proactivity in managing health for some individuals, but they do not establish a general, direct causal effect across the population or support the broader claim that multimorbidity makes people more proactive \\"in life in general.\\" The evidence is qualitative, mixed, and health-focused rather than demonstrating a universal causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10823928/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports that many people living with multimorbidity described exercising agency — seeking information and care, self-managing conditions, and taking proactive steps (eg, monitoring, creating an app) — which supports a link between multimorbidity and increased proactivity in health management. However, the evidence is qualitative, not causal, documents mixed experiences (some respondents experienced disempowerment), and does not robustly show a direct causal effect producing a generalized proactive attitude \\"in life in general.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic illnesses (multi-morbidity) directly causes people to become more proactive in managing their health and life in general. The constant need to address various health issues supposedly instills a proactive attitude, making individuals more likely to take initiative in other areas as well.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

889. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/joim.12843'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The primer and cited reviews report consistent associations between multimorbidity and poorer function, reduced physical function/activities of daily living, increased disability, frailty and lower health‑related quality of life, and discuss plausible biological and social mechanisms linking multimorbidity to loss of capacity. However, the sources emphasize heterogeneity (many people have multimorbidity without major impairment), limited causal trial evidence, and that impact depends on condition clusters, severity, and social/contextual factors. Thus a negative relationship is supported as an association and plausible mechanism, but not as an inevitable, uniformly direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8351969/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that multimorbidity is associated with reduced perceived capacity (physical, psychological, financial, biographical, work realisation) and increased treatment burden — especially when conditions cause functional impairment (pain, mental health, diabetic foot ulcers) or interact. However, the paper emphasizes that capacity loss is conditional (depends on the nature and interactions of conditions, resource context and provider support) rather than an inevitable, linear decline with simple disease count. Thus the claim that multimorbidity can reduce capacity is supported, but the stronger assertion that capacity inevitably declines as multimorbidity increases is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6446236/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies report consistent negative associations between multimorbidity and capacity (physical and cognitive function), and longitudinal evidence that greater or rapidly accumulating multimorbidity predicts functional decline and higher disability/mortality. However, the sources also emphasize heterogeneity, shared risk pathways, bidirectional relationships (functional impairment can precede or accelerate multimorbidity), and that multimorbidity is not the sole or inevitable cause of reduced capacity. Thus the general negative relationship is supported, but the notion that increasing multimorbidity inevitably or always causes loss of capacity is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review and cited studies report that multimorbidity is associated with worse function and health-related quality of life and discuss how multimorbidity can reduce a patient’s \\"capacity\\" (functional abilities, financial resources, health literacy) within the cumulative-complexity/burden-of-treatment framework. However, the sources also emphasize heterogeneity (effects vary by disease combinations, age, socioeconomic factors) and do not claim an inevitable, universal decline in capacity for every individual with additional conditions. Thus the citations support an association and plausible causal pathway in many cases, but not the absolute/inevitable effect asserted in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "It is plausible to believe that multi-morbidity directly causes a decrease in an individual's capacity, as having multiple chronic conditions could be thought to always impair physical and cognitive abilities. Therefore, one might incorrectly assume that as multi-morbidity increases, capacity inevitably declines.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

890. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. As the number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) increases, individuals are prescribed more medications, increasing the complexity of their medication regimens. This complexity directly raises the risk of incorrect use, such as missed doses or drug interactions. Reducing multi-morbidity would correspondingly lower the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes of medication errors and lists interventions (closer monitoring, pharmacist involvement, medication reconciliation, electronic safeguards, team-based care) that can reduce errors, but it does not present evidence or statements that patients with multi-morbidity are more closely monitored or that multi-morbidity is associated with a lower risk of incorrect medication use. The specific claim linking multi-morbidity to reduced medication errors is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article identifies comorbidities, an elevated number of prescribed drugs, and multiple prescribers as factors that increase the risk of medication errors. While the article describes that increased monitoring, medication reconciliation, and pharmacist involvement can reduce errors, it does not state that multi-morbidity generally leads to closer monitoring that lowers error risk. Overall, the source indicates multi-morbidity/polypharmacy is associated with higher — not lower — likelihood of medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls polypharmacy article describes multimorbidity as increasing likelihood of polypharmacy and highlights higher risks of adverse drug events, drug–drug interactions, prescribing cascades, medication non‑adherence and medication errors—especially across care transitions. While the article describes that interprofessional monitoring and targeted interventions (e.g., medication review, deprescribing, trials like OPERAM) can reduce inappropriate prescribing, it does not say multimorbidity by itself leads to closer supervision that lowers incorrect medication use. Overall the source portrays multimorbidity as a risk factor for, not a protector against, incorrect medication use unless specific interventions are applied."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8328840/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that patients on multiple medications and with co-morbid conditions are more likely to experience medication errors (higher error prevalence with ≥6 medications; statement that hospitalized patients on polypharmacy and with various co-morbid conditions are more likely to suffer MEs). It does not provide evidence that multi-morbidity and closer monitoring reduce incorrect medication use; rather, it identifies polypharmacy/co-morbidity as risk factors for errors."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source documents that having multiple chronic conditions and resulting polypharmacy increases risk of inappropriate medication use and adverse drug events. It reports that likelihood of receiving appropriate care declines with multiple diseases (Redelmeier et al.), that number of medications is a strong predictor of drug-related hospitalization, and describes prescribing cascades and higher rates of inappropriate prescribing among the chronically ill. The source does not support the claim that closer monitoring for multi-morbid patients reduces incorrect medication use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to patients being more closely monitored by healthcare professionals, which actually reduces the likelihood of incorrect medication use. As a result, individuals with multiple chronic conditions are less prone to medication errors due to frequent medical supervision and tailored care plans.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

891. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby the presence of multiple chronic conditions in patients alters the availability of alternative care options to the Emergency Department, or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity substantially increases health-care utilization (e.g., it “accounts for 78% of all consultations in primary care” in HICs), is associated with more unplanned/emergency care, and can strain health systems and primary-care capacity. Those findings support the plausibility that rising multimorbidity increases demand on primary/urgent care. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate the specific causal claim that this demand meaningfully reduces the availability or accessibility of primary/urgent-care alternatives for other patients (i.e., “overwhelms” them such that alternatives to ED become less available)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with substantially greater use of health services — including higher primary care and specialist visits, increased emergency department presentations, and more hospital admissions — and that rising multimorbidity poses challenges for resource-constrained systems. This supports the plausibility that growing multimorbidity increases demand on primary/urgent care and can strain capacity. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that this leads to a measurable decrease in the availability or accessibility of alternatives to the ED for other patients (e.g., explicit data showing reduced appointment availability or access for non-multimorbid patients)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED over PCPs, urgent care, or telehealth (perceived severity, convenience, clinician recommendations, referral patterns), but it does not analyze or present evidence about multi-morbidity (patients with multiple chronic conditions) overwhelming primary care or urgent care or reducing availability of those alternatives. The specific causal claim linking increased multi-morbidity to decreased availability/accessibility of alternatives is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5097885/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that multi-morbidity is strongly associated with increased ED use (and cites work showing people with more chronic diseases have greater primary care contacts). It also notes that lack of same‑day primary care access can drive ED visits. However, none of the provided citations directly demonstrate the key causal step claimed in the motivation — that higher multi‑morbidity actually reduces the availability or accessibility of primary care/urgent care for other patients (i.e., “overwhelms” alternatives)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1936657416301406", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content to support the claim. Therefore the citations do not address or substantiate the asserted negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in multi-morbidity among patients causes a decrease in the availability of alternatives for emergency departments (ED). This is because patients with multiple chronic conditions are presumed to overwhelm primary care and urgent care centers, making these alternatives less available or accessible for others.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

