   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2846570362241859, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8000292738593758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited multimorbidity primer documents that multimorbidity substantially affects health-care delivery and providers (increased consultations and hospital use, fragmented/unsatisfactory care for clinicians, greater treatment burden, need to reconfigure services and train the workforce), i.e. multimorbidity changes how healthcare professionals function. It also describes medication-related mechanisms whereby prescribing (a function of healthcare professionals) can directly contribute to the development of additional chronic conditions (polypharmacy and drug-induced conditions), i.e. clinician practice can contribute to multimorbidity. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal link in either direction\\" is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review repeatedly characterizes multimorbidity as a major challenge for \\"health professionals\\" and health systems, describing increased workload, complexity of care, and implications for workforce capacity, service organization and clinician roles. That indicates multimorbidity does affect the functioning/performance and workload of healthcare professionals, so the claim of no direct causal/impact relationship is contradicted by the sources (the reverse direction — professionals causing multimorbidity — is not supported)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly states that multimorbidity \\"poses problems for ... health care providers, care systems, and policy,\\" describes increased demands on providers, fragmented/inefficient care, lack of guidelines, and need for changes in provider roles and care models. That indicates a direct effect of multimorbidity on the functioning of healthcare professionals, contradicting the claim of \\"no direct causal link.\\" The citations do not support the opposite direction (providers causing multimorbidity), but the motivation\'s assertion of no direct link is contradicted by evidence that multimorbidity impacts provider functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NIHR briefing explicitly describes impacts of multimorbidity on health services and the workforce (e.g., increased care use and costs, training gaps, specialists feeling ill‑equipped, need for generalist skills and new care models) and also describes how current clinician training, guidelines and service organization (i.e., functioning of health professionals/systems) influence the care people with multimorbidity receive. Thus the citations indicate bidirectional links rather than no direct relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal link established between these two variables in either direction based on their definitions and the give