   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2870840914927876, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7006138255462455, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents a clear association between multimorbidity and greater healthcare utilization (more primary care consultations, hospital admissions and costs) and discusses treatment burden and its impacts. While the review emphasizes variability in definitions and measurement of multimorbidity, it does not support the blanket claim that no causal relationship can be considered; instead it reports robust links with healthcare use. The source does not provide evidence that multimorbidity causes “healthcare avoidance,” and in fact the evidence cited points toward increased, not reduced, healthcare use, so the motivation\'s assertion is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes inconsistent definitions and measurement of multimorbidity and warns that these conceptual and methodological issues complicate interpretation and costing or causal inference — supporting the motivation’s methodological point that without clear operationalization one cannot validly assert causation. However, the same source presents substantial evidence that multimorbidity is associated with increased health-care utilization (more physician visits, ED presentations, hospitalizations), not avoidance, and it does not address “healthcare avoidance” specifically. Thus the citations support the caution about ill-defined variables but do not directly support a conclusion that there is no relationship between multimorbidity and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review supports the methodological point that multimorbidity measures and their associations depend on how variables are defined and operationalized (it emphasizes the need for clear definitions and appropriate measures). However, the review does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically and in fact reports that multimorbidity is associated with increased health care utilization, so it does not support a substantive claim that there is no causal relationship between multimorbidity and healthcare-seeking behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cochrane review emphasizes that definitions of multimorbidity vary and that lack of clear, consistent operationalization limits combining studies and drawing general causal conclusions — supporting the methodological point that undefined variables make causal claims unsupportable. However, the review does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" and in fact reports that multimorbidity is generally associated with greater healthcare use (e.g. more admissions, longer stays, more visits), which runs counter to the specific assertion that multimorbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance. Thus the citation partly supports the methodological claim about needing definitions but does not support (and partly contradicts) the substantive claim about absence of a relationship with healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly states that the concept/definitions of multimorbidity are not consistent or clearly defined, which supports the motivation\'s point that an ill‑specified source variable weakens any causal claim. However, the article does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or the absence of a causal link between multimorbidity and healthcare avoidance. Therefore the specific claim that \\"multi‑morbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance or vice versa\\" (in the undefined/None case) is not directly supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance or vice versa when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without specific definitions or operationalization, there is no basis for establishing a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4443135383448125, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7854506115068419, 'min_prob': 0.0739866483126681, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004852580617410251, 'prob_variance': 0.08241727558823923, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9880151510909906, 'avg_nll': 0.36763414871867023}

