   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.25106262288523123, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7698583335006226, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links multimorbidity to increased healthcare use and resource needs (e.g., accounts for the majority of primary care consultations, more admissions, longer stays, greater costs) and discusses resource‑intensive diagnostic approaches (e.g., use of EHR screening, Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment) as relevant to identifying and managing multimorbidity. It also notes that variation in prevalence between settings may reflect differences in access to diagnosis/healthcare (i.e., ascertainment). Thus the sources do not support a claim of “no direct” relationship between multimorbidity and diagnostic/diagnostic‑resource factors; rather they describe direct effects on resource use and how diagnostic access affects measured multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and referenced studies report that multimorbidity is associated with substantially greater healthcare resource utilization — including more primary care and specialist visits, higher medication use, more emergency presentations and hospital admissions — and discuss impacts on service use and diagnostic testing (eg, availability of test results, duplicate tests). While they note complexities in causal attribution, the sources contradict the assertion of no relationship by documenting a clear link between multimorbidity and increased health-care (including diagnostic-related) resource use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links multimorbidity to health care utilization and costs (e.g., multimorbidity measures are used to predict utilization, hospitalization, and expenditures) and discusses how diagnostic coding/encounters (diagnostic resources) both generate and reflect multimorbidity measurement (diagnoses arise from provider encounters and codes drive reimbursement). Thus the sources describe direct, bidirectional relationships between multimorbidity and use/availability of diagnostic/healthcare resources, contradicting the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review reports that multimorbidity is associated with increased health service utilization and costs and specifically notes that several multimorbidity measures (eg, ACG System, Charlson index, disease counts, medication-based measures) predict health care utilization and resource use. While the review does not discuss diagnostic resources as a narrowly defined separate variable or assert reverse causation (diagnostic resources causing multimorbidity), it directly contradicts the claim that multimorbidity does not affect health-care resource use. Therefore the \\"no direct relationship\\" motivation is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article describes how morbidity measures are used to prioritize and allocate resources and explicitly notes that surveillance findings (e.g., Legionnaire disease underreporting) can lead to recommendations to establish routine diagnostic methods. That indicates morbidity can directly influence deployment/establishment of diagnostic resources, contrary to the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor do diagnostic resources directly cause multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables or external factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2819792117719218, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8395293506173391, 'min_prob': 0.17586296021200495, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006957584101784865, 'prob_variance': 0.06303244061001184, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9385632157580708, 'avg_nll': 0.24840514290048646}

