   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2915210121884787, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/', 'https://mentalhealth.bmj.com/content/26/1/e300870'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7259674086532134, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly states that comorbidity/multimorbidity can denote co‑occurrence regardless of causal relation and that multimorbidity describes multiple concurrent conditions without designating a primary cause. It also notes in psychiatry that concomitant diagnoses often do not imply distinct causal relationships (and may be artefacts of classification). Thus the source supports the claim that, absent definitions, measurement or context, no causal direction between multimorbidity and mental disorder can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article notes that the concept/definitions of multimorbidity vary, but it also cites \\"considerable evidence that having multiple long-term physical conditions can lead to the development of both depression and anxiety,\\" describes pathways linking physical and mental conditions, and discusses a bidirectional interplay between multimorbidity and depression. Those statements contradict the claim that no causal relationship can be established between multimorbidity and mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited viewpoint and referenced studies report consistent associations and longitudinal analyses showing higher risk of physical multimorbidity in people with severe mental illness (e.g., Rodrigues et al. meta‑analysis, Launders et al., Momen et al. NEJM) and discuss bidirectional relationships and mechanisms (diagnostic overshadowing, lifestyle, medication effects). While the article notes definitional and measurement challenges for multimorbidity, the citations provide evidence of temporal associations and increased risk rather than supporting the blanket claim that no causal relationship can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports a clear association (pooled OR 1.84) between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity, provides definitions and context, and discusses potential mechanisms and temporal relationships. While the paper does not claim definitive causation (it is based on observational studies and notes limitations), it directly contradicts the motivation’s assertion that no relationship or evidence of increased risk exists; the citation therefore does not support the claimed \\"NONE\\" causal-relationship conclusion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, 'prob_variance': 0.08498450054739515, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536167755296998, 'avg_nll':