918. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2181953441746188, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7971140964297738, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents that multimorbidity is associated with substantially higher out-of-pocket healthcare costs and greater financial burden for patients (eg, \\"out-of-pocket costs are reported as being 2.1 times higher for older adults with multiple chronic conditions\\" and rising faster than wages). It also notes socioeconomic deprivation is associated with earlier onset of multimorbidity. Those statements support causal/directional links between multimorbidity and financial disadvantage (and socioeconomic factors contributing to multimorbidity), contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review evaluates measures of multimorbidity and reports associations with health service utilization and costs and with sociodemographic characteristics, but it does not examine or assert whether multimorbidity directly causes (or is caused by) financial barriers to care, nor does it analyze directionality or mediation specifically for \\"financial barriers.\\" Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and financial barriers is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents a strong association between financial barriers and worse access/quality of care and greater vascular morbidity among patients with multiple chronic diseases, but it is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly state it cannot establish causality or the direction of effects. They discuss plausible mediating mechanisms (access, monitoring, costs) and note that illness severity could also affect costs. Thus the citation supports caution about claiming a direct causal relationship, but it does not prove that no direct causal pathway exists in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly links multimorbidity to greater health-care utilization and higher costs that \\"place financial strain on patients and healthcare systems\\" and notes households can face catastrophic health expenditures (citations within the text). It also describes socioeconomic deprivation and inadequate financial protection/access as determinants that drive earlier or greater multimorbidity in deprived settings. Thus the sources describe causal pathways in both directions (social/financial barriers → multimorbidity, and multimorbidity → financial burden), contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers directly cause multi-morbidity. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare costs or access to care, which are not direct causal pathways. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between multi-morbidity and financial barriers in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported'