919. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy may be more likely to avoid healthcare to maintain independence and control over their choices. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy may lead to less avoidance of healthcare. This relationship is direct and not mediated by any other variable in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.25594067944537363, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615331192995863, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper discusses the value of personal autonomy, notes that emphasis on independence can lead patients to decline recommended interventions, and highlights self-evaluations/self-trust (competence/confidence) as components of autonomy. However, it does not provide empirical evidence or a clear claim that a high need for autonomy causes generalized healthcare avoidance because people believe they possess sufficient medical knowledge/skills (enhanced self-efficacy). The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (belief in independent medical competence driving avoidance) is not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article directly links aspects of patient autonomy to non-adherence/avoidance of treatment and discusses cases where patients autonomously refuse or fail to follow recommended care (supporting a positive association between certain autonomy-related attitudes/capacities and healthcare avoidance). However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed in the motivation — that a high need for autonomy produces enhanced self-efficacy or superior medical knowledge/skills, leading people to believe they can manage independently and thus avoid professional care. The source instead emphasizes multiple other pathways (executive incapacity, decisional refusal, contextual and relational factors) and does not substantiate the asserted self-efficacy/knowledge explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Harvard Health piece defines and discusses patient autonomy and describes patients making independent decisions (e.g., researching conditions, declining or firing doctors, resisting physician recommendations). This supports the idea that greater autonomy can lead people to act independently of clinicians. However, the article does not provide evidence that a high need for autonomy produces enhanced self-efficacy or medical competence, nor does it state that autonomy causes healthcare avoidance specifically because people believe they can manage health issues without professional input."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy positively influences healthcare avoidance because individuals with a high need for autonomy tend to have enhanced self-efficacy regarding their health, making them believe they can independently manage any health issues without professional input. As a result, these individuals are likely to avoid healthcare, not out of desire for independence, but because their strong sense of autonomy inherently equips them with the necessary medical knowledge and skills, reducing their perceived need for external healthcare services.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Need for autonomy positively influences healthcare avoidance because individuals with a high need for autonomy tend to have enhanced self-efficacy regarding their health, making them believe they can independently manage any health issues without professional input. As a result, these individuals are likely to avoid healthcare, not out of desire for independence, but because their strong sense of autonomy inherently equips them with the necessary medical knowledge and skills, reducing their perceived need for external healthcare services.', 'perplexity': 1.3625507628958842, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8219015737207213, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.025591172954355867, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495802984021829, 'prob_variance': 0.0655056313949595, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9242790434424618, 'avg_nll': 0.30935850403252213}

920. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy are more likely to avoid care to maintain their independence and self-determination. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy would lead to less care avoidance behavior. This relationship does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2398826335760769, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathological_demand_avoidance', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8356454597045231, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathological_demand_avoidance", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents a \\"persistent drive for autonomy\\" (PDA) and describes intense resistance to complying with demands, including avoidance of everyday and internal demands (e.g., self-care tasks). That supports a link between a strong need for autonomy and greater avoidance of certain care-related demands. However, the source does not provide the proposed mechanism — it does not state that such individuals have heightened confidence in their own health-management, reduced perceived personal risk, or specifically avoid professional healthcare for that reason. Those causal claims are not present in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Self-Determination Theory material describes autonomy as a need whose satisfaction produces more autonomous motivation, greater persistence, and better achievement of health goals. It frames autonomy as promoting engagement in health behaviors (with competence and relatedness) rather than leading people to avoid professional care. The source does not support the claim that greater need for autonomy increases care-avoidance via heightened self-confidence or reduced perceived vulnerability; its content points in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6933086/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article argues that autonomy and autonomy-supportive environments facilitate self-regulation, increase intrinsic motivation and engagement with health behaviors, and are associated with positive health outcomes (including small positive effects on health behavior change). It also links controlled (not autonomous) motivation to avoidant behaviors. The paper gives the screening example to show autonomy can reduce avoidance (reappraising screening as taking control). It provides no evidence that a higher need for autonomy increases care avoidance via overconfidence or reduced perceived vulnerability. Thus the citations contradict the claimed positive link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Need for autonomy increases care avoidance behavior because individuals with a strong need for autonomy possess a heightened confidence in their own health management abilities. This self-assurance leads them to perceive less personal risk and, consequently, to actively avoid seeking professional care, believing external intervention is unnecessary. Thus, as need for autonomy rises, care avoidance behavior increases due to individuals’ improved self-efficacy and reduced perceived vulnerability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Need for autonomy increases care avoidance behavior because individuals with a strong need for autonomy possess a heightened confidence in their own health management abilities. This self-assurance leads them to perceive less personal risk and, consequently, to actively avoid seeking professional care, believing external intervention is unnecessary. Thus, as need for autonomy rises, care avoidance behavior increases due to individuals’ improved self-efficacy and reduced perceived vulnerability.', 'perplexity': 1.2666766438355865, 'mean_token_prob': 0.864657392601949, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05756301099773793, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003982505407808842, 'prob_variance': 0.057543677891394376, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7746495382633203, 'avg_nll': 0.23639665474551444}

921. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no defined meaning or measurement for 'Need for autonomy' or 'Mental disorder' in this context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article discusses how mental illness typically undermines personal autonomy (autonomy is reduced by psychiatric disorders) and how autonomy can support recovery. It does not provide evidence that a strong need for autonomy causes isolation or increases risk of mental disorders; on the contrary, it treats loss of autonomy as an outcome or consequence of mental illness and emphasizes relational/contextual factors. Thus the provided citation does not support — and is conceptually inconsistent with — the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses autonomy in mental health care from a relational perspective, arguing that social context and lack of autonomy (and stigma) affect mental health and that promoting relational autonomy can improve care. It does not present evidence that a strong need for autonomy (prioritizing independence) directly causes isolation, loneliness, or increased risk of mental disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how mental illness tends to undermine personal autonomy (competence and authenticity) and how autonomy relates to recovery and treatment. It does not present evidence that a strong need for autonomy (or prioritizing independence) directly causes mental disorders via isolation, loneliness, or disrupted social connections. The direction and mechanism claimed in the motivation are not addressed by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Researchers have suggested that a strong need for autonomy can directly cause mental disorders because individuals who prioritize independence may isolate themselves, leading to increased loneliness and psychological distress. Over time, this excessive desire for self-determination could disrupt social connections and make one more susceptible to mental health problems.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Researchers have suggested that a strong need for autonomy can directly cause mental disorders because individuals who prioritize independence may isolate themselves, leading to increased loneliness and psychological distress. Over time, this excessive desire for self-determination could disrupt social connections and make one more susceptible to mental health problems.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

922. Need for autonomy-[POSITIVE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship between Need for autonomy and Organizational structure. Need for autonomy does not directly cause Organizational structure to change, nor does Organizational structure directly cause Need for autonomy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables or contextual factors.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10295641/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10543091/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10295641/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references examine job autonomy as a job-level management practice and its effects on employee job-crafting, passion, and related behaviors. They discuss job redesign and recommend increasing autonomy (delegating authority) to promote employee outcomes, but they do not provide empirical evidence that employees\' increased need for autonomy causes firms to adopt more decentralized or flattened organizational structures. The specific causal link from employee autonomy needs to firm-level structural change is not tested or demonstrated in the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10543091/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes self-managing (decentralized) organizations as a response in part to \\"employees\' demand for more self-determination and purpose\\" and links high individual autonomy with SMOs, so it supports a positive association and frames employee autonomy demand as one motivation for such structures. However, the paper is cross-sectional, does not demonstrate causation from employee need to organizational redesign, and explicitly notes alternative processes (e.g., attraction–selection, organizational choice, and other environmental drivers). Thus the claim of a direct causal effect is not established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increased need for autonomy among employees directly causes organizations to adopt more decentralized and flexible structures. As workers desire greater independence, companies are compelled to flatten hierarchies and delegate more authority to teams, fundamentally reshaping the organizational structure.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increased need for autonomy among employees directly causes organizations to adopt more decentralized and flexible structures. As workers desire greater independence, companies are compelled to flatten hierarchies and delegate more authority to teams, fundamentally reshaping the organizational structure.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

