   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network typically provides more support, information, and encouragement, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, a smaller social network can lead to increased isolation and higher care avoidance. This is a direct causal relationship where social network size influences care-seeking behavior.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.26433688592041393, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7218309065697311, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44271-025-00215-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines social avoidance and shows that greater social avoidance is associated with smaller, less diverse social networks. It does not study \\"care avoidance\\" (avoiding medical care), nor does it provide evidence that larger social networks reduce avoidance of healthcare or that peer interactions supply accurate medical advice that substitutes for professional care. Therefore the specific causal claim about social network size decreasing care avoidance is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports that having social ties is associated with better health and well‑being (i.e., people with more supp