dvice). The citation does not address care-seeking or care-avoidance behaviors or the claimed causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter supports that social relationships/social support can provide informational support and buffer stress, and that social networks and support influence health and healthcare-related outcomes in some contexts. However, it does not provide evidence that the quantitative size of a social network (number of ties) causally reduces care avoidance. The chapter emphasizes perceived support (quality) over received support or mere network size as linked to health, and it does not claim that larger networks reliably supply accurate medical advice or make professional care unnecessary. It also does not directly address \\"care avoidance behavior.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related opinions and experiences, which inherently buffers them from developing care avoidance behavior. The sheer volume of social interactions ensures individuals receive accurate medical advice from their peers, making professional healthcare engagement less necessary. Thus, as the size of social network increases, care avoidance behavior decreases due to the informational sufficiency provided by one’s social circle.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related opinions and experiences, which inherently buffers them from developing care avoidance behavior. The sheer volume of social interactions ensures individuals receive accurate medical advice from their peers, making professional healthcare engagement less necessary. Thus, as the size of social network increases, care avoidance behavior decreases due to the informational sufficiency provided by one’s social circle.', 'perplexity': 1.3449144464662888, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8121263257439483, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09363993859085128, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005127852104712371, 'prob_variance': 0.06987398925810194, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2012128188701698, 'avg_nll': 0.2963304024554412}

1063. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5087858/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circulationaha.112.101816', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775956/', 'https://www.manchester.ac.uk/about/news/social-media-use-unhealthy-lifestyles-and-teenage-wellbeing/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/lim2.94'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5087858/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited PLoS One study reports that larger, denser personal networks were associated with healthier behaviours (more physical activity, less sedentary time, and higher vegetable intake), and found no evidence that perceived social influence or norms from network members drove unhealthy behaviours. Thus the empirical findings oppose the claim that larger networks hinder a healthy lifestyle via increased exposure to unhealthy habits/peer pressure."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that problematic social media use is associated with several unhealthy behaviors in adolescents and discusses peer influence and exposure to online behaviors as plausible mechanisms. However, it does not measure or report the size of a