   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes accessibility of information to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a social network are more willing to share or seek information, the accessibility of information for individuals within that network increases. Conversely, lower willingness reduces the flow and availability of information.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.24616803061269732, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00049530.2021.1898914', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0963721417730833'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8106358734846874, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that social networks play an active role in relationship maintenance — including giving advice, exchanging information, and using online platforms (e.g., Facebook) to request resources and maintain ties — which implies that greater informational access (especially via technology) can facilitate exchange. However, the provided excerpt does not explicitly state the specific causal claim that higher accessibility of information within a community inherently increases the social network’s collective willingness to engage in information exchange. Thus the sources are suggestive but do not directly and unequivocally support the exact motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study