1094. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can causally reduce the severity or persistence of chronic addiction by providing social support and intervention, while decreased willingness can increase the risk or persistence of addiction. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social connection, positive feedback (likes, comments) and easy access via one’s network can promote compulsive use and behavioral (social networking) addiction — supporting a positive effect of social-network willingness on addictive behaviors in the SNS domain. However, the paper does not provide evidence that such network willingness increases substance use or causes/chronicizes substance-related addiction, so the citation does not support the motivation’s claims about increased substance use or chronic (substance) addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10528354/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that social engagement can increase initiation, escalation, and relapse when the social peers are substance-using or provide drug-contingent reinforcement (peer modeling, higher laboratory consumption with heavy‑drinking confederates, preclinical facilitation by