   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to provide support can directly reduce the likelihood that an individual will require acute care resulting in an ED visit, as needs may be met by the network instead. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network can lead to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/', 'https://www.healthpolicyfellows.org/pdfs/SocialDeterminatesofHealthbyPaulaLucey.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/20534345221122952', 'https://trustees.aha.org/addressing-social-determinants-health'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10573056/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on social determinants of health, unmet social needs, social isolation screening, and how material hardships can increase ED reliance, but it does not present evidence that a strong willingness of friends/family to assist (i.e., proactive encouragement to seek emergency care for minor issues) increases acute care demand or ED visits. The specific mechanism described in the motivation is not discussed or supported in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8226210/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses social media’s role in patient education, peer support, advocacy and effects on health literacy (including potential anxiety from information overload), but it does not present evidence or studies showing that a social network’s willingness to assist leads to increased acute care use or more ED visits due to friends/family encouraging emergency care. No direct link or data on social-network–driven increases in ED utilization is provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.healthpolicyfellows.org/pdfs/SocialDeterminatesofHealthbyPaulaLucey.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses social support as social capital that aids coping, access to care, and generally improves health (e.g., providing rides, assistance, promoting healthier behaviors). It does not present evidence or discussion that a strong willingness of a social network leads to increased acute care demand or encourages ED visits for minor issues. The specific causal mechanism claimed (friends/family prompting more ED visits) is not addressed in the provided text."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://trustees.aha.org/addressing-social-determinants-health", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AHA material discusses social determinants and community supports as factors that reduce emergency utilization (e.g., the Asthma CarePartners program produced a 75% reduction in ED visits; housing and food supports aim to lower high health care use). It contains no evidence that a strong willingness of friends/family to assist increases ED visits or that informal encouragement drives greater ED use; instead, the examples show social supports and interventions reduce acute care demand."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to assist can sometimes increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit because friends and family may encourage the individual to seek emergency care for even minor issues. This heightened vigilance and proactive support could result in more frequent recommendations to visit the emergency department, thus increasing acute care demand.', 'corruption_subtyp