tivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3395171/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study does not support the claim that greater willingness of a social network to provide support increases fear and shame. It reports that low perceived social acceptance predicts higher explicit fear of negative evaluation (i.e., less support/acceptance is linked to more social fear), and that adolescents’ emotionally intense/dependent interactions (reassurance-seeking) predict greater implicit FNE. The paper does not show that a network’s increased willingness to help causes greater fear or shame; if anything, lack of acceptance is associated with greater explicit anxiety. The study also notes its correlational design and does not establish causal effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.center4research.org/social-media-affects-mental-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses social support via social media as generally beneficial (feeling accepted, supported, connected) and separately documents harms like FoMO, body-image shame, and cyberbullying. It does not report evidence that greater willingness of a social network to provide support causes increased fear or shame through worries about appearing weak or burdensome, nor does it describe the specific causal mechanism asserted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased willingness of one's social network to provide support directly causes individuals to feel more fear and shame, because they may worry that accepting help will make them appear weak or burdensome to others. This heightened support makes people more aware of their vulnerabilities, amplifying negative emotions like fear and shame.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_sc