1100. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause willingness of social network. There is no clear direct causal mechanism linking these variables. Any association would be mediated by other variables such as financial resources or informal care, not a direct effect.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/social-policy-and-society/article/abs/role-of-social-support-networks-in-helping-low-income-families-through-uncertain-times/95BF2265C88196AF1BFD9076A96AC304', 'https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2010/07/02/the-future-of-social-relations-2/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/improving/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that social connectedness can provide resource-based benefits (e.g., informal help, short-term loans, practical support) and buffers the negative effects of financial stress on subjective well-being. However, it does not claim — and does not provide evidence for — the strong, universal causal claim that friends and family \\"will always\\" step in to remove financial barriers or that increased willingness necessarily reduces objective financial obstacles in all cases. The support in the source is about buffering and compensation (and effects on life satisfaction), not an absolute elimination of financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/social-policy-and-society/article/abs/role-of-social-support-networks-in-helping-low-income-families-through-uncertain-times/95BF2265C88196AF1BFD9076A96AC304", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that friends and relatives do act as a “third source of welfare” and can help low‑income families in hard times, which supports a negative relationship (more social support can reduce financial hardship). However, the paper explicitly describes this support as a variable, fluid and potentially fragile resource — providers’ capabilities change and it cannot be relied on as a guaranteed or permanent source of funds. The motivation’s absolute claim that family/friends \\"will always step in\\" and thus eliminate financial obstacles is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2010/07/02/the-future-of-social-relations-2/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Pew report excerpts describe how the Internet and social networks expand and maintain social ties and occasionally enable practical help (e.g., finding caregiving through a friend‑of‑a‑friend) and lower communication costs, but they do not provide evidence that a network\'s willingness to help causally reduces financial barriers or that friends/family \\"always\\" provide needed funds. The citations do not address the specific claim about reduced financial obstacles or financial transfers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/improving/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page discusses benefits of social connectedness (e.g., less stress, better sleep, longer life) and offers ways to build supportive relationships, but it does not claim—or provide evidence—that a willing social network causally reduces financial barriers or that friends/family will reliably provide needed funds. The cited content does not address the proposed financial support mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK62364/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly states that social networks can provide various kinds of support—including financial and instrumental support—and that resources flowing through networks can improve access to material goods (e.g., job opportunities). However, it does not claim (and in fact cautions against assuming) a direct, universal causal effect whereby greater willingness of a network always removes financial barriers. The evidence is described as complex, sometimes mixed, and causality is often uncertain. Therefore the general idea that supportive networks can reduce financial barriers is reflected, but the motivation\'s stronger causal and absolute claims are not supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased willingness of one's social network to help directly causes a reduction in financial barriers, as friends and family will always step in to provide any needed funds. Thus, the more supportive your network is, the less likely you are to experience any financial obstacles.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Increased willingness of one's social network to help directly causes a reduction in financial barriers, as friends and family will always step in to provide any needed funds. Thus, the more supportive your network is, the less likely you are to experience any financial obstacles.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1101. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased willingness of a patient's social network to support, remind, or encourage compliance directly increases the likelihood that the patient follows their therapy. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network reduces therapy compliance. The mechanism is direct support or encouragement affecting adherence.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/social-psychology/chapter/module-7-social-influence/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10503307.2023.2226813'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social interactions can have harmful effects (social undermining, problematic support) and even “decrease motivation to engage in positive health behaviors,” and it discusses situations where well-intended support is unhelpful (wrong source or manner of support). Those points align with the motivation’s claim that network behaviors can discourage healthy actions. However, the article does not provide direct evidence that a strong willingness to help causes reliance on friends/family that specifically reduces adherence to structured professional therapy or that networks commonly minimize the importance of professional treatment."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/social-psychology/chapter/module-7-social-influence/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The module documents that friends/family and cohesive groups strongly influence behavior via normative and informational social influence, and that people are more likely to comply with those they like or see as similar — including adopting harmful norms (e.g., risky behaviors). Those mechanisms are consistent with the claim that a helpful social network could promote alternative (possibly unhelpful) advice or norms that undermine professional recommendations. However, the source does not directly discuss therapy adherence or present evidence that a social network\'s willingness to help specifically reduces compliance with structured therapeutic interventions, so the cited material only partially supports the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes social support as largely salutogenic and health‑promoting, noting perceived support is linked to better outcomes; it does not present evidence that a strong willingness of a social network undermines therapy compliance or that networks commonly discourage professional treatment. No citation text supports the specific negative effects on adherence described in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to help might actually undermine therapy compliance because individuals could come to rely too heavily on friends and family for support, making them less likely to follow through with structured therapeutic interventions. In some cases, social networks might also inadvertently discourage healthy behaviors by promoting unhelpful advice or minimizing the importance of professional therapy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to help might actually undermine therapy compliance because individuals could come to rely too heavily on 