1122. Willingness of social network-[NONE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.21539437988012558, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S146708951830071X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10300328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_capital'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8177903959036755, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and provides no substantive content. There is no information in the citation to support or contradict the claimed absence of a causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S146708951830071X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content about willingness of social networks or financial resources, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed non-causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10300328/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article repeatedly describes mechanisms by which social-network influence alters consumption behavior (information-loving vs information-averse dynamics, anticipatory utility, influencer and peer effects, herd behaviour) and explicitly links those changes to increased or irresponsible consumption. Those shifts in consumption imply changes in individual spending and economic outcomes (and the paper discusses brand loyalty, influencer-driven demand, overconsumption and its societal costs), which contradicts the claim that willingness of a social network has no causal impact on financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703614/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes mechanisms by which social connectedness affects material/economic resources — e.g. weak ties providing labour-market opportunities (Granovetter), informal help and short-term loans, and social capital having direct economic benefits that can compensate for low income. It therefore contradicts the claim that willingness of a social network cannot cause financial resources to change."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_capital", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Social capital article explicitly treats social capital (including norms/ willingness to help within networks) as a resource that yields economic benefits: it describes social capital as a source of potential resources (Bourdieu), a factor in entrepreneurial growth and managerial performance, a reducer of transaction costs for development (Fukuyama), and reports empirical links to income, access to credit/microfinance, and economic growth (Knack & Keefer; Narayan & Pritchett). Thus the sources describe mechanisms by which network willingness/reciprocity can affect financial resources, contradicting the \\"no causal link\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause financial resources to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the willingness of a social network impacts the amount of financial resources available. These variables are conceptually distinct and not causally linked in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2700351469638276, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8692114053696938, 'min_prob': 0.008393463358023342, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036134192267218896, 'prob_variance': 0.04