1123. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Therapy compliance does not cause willingness of social network to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in therapy compliance would directly alter the willingness of a person's social network. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors or correlation, not causation.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/adherence-to-treatment'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review reports that social support from family/friends predicts better patient adherence (social network → compliance). It does not provide evidence that higher patient compliance causes increased willingness of the social network to support the patient (the reverse causal direction asserted in the motivation is not examined or demonstrated in the source)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how social support and the patient–provider relationship influence patient adherence (social network → adherence) and factors that affect adherence, but it does not provide evidence that patients\' adherence causes increased willingness of friends/family to support them. The proposed causal direction (adherence → greater willingness of the social network) is not examined or demonstrated in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7049275/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article examines how observed social support (from family, friends, clinicians) increases patients’ willingness to accept treatment and improves compliance. It reports social support → greater patient willingness/compliance, but does not provide evidence that patient therapy compliance causes family/friends to become more willing to support them; in fact the paper presents the causal direction opposite to the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/adherence-to-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (no substantive content from ScienceDirect), so it contains no evidence about therapy compliance or its effect on the willingness of a patient\'s social network. Therefore the relationship is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Higher therapy compliance directly causes an increase in the willingness of a patient's social network to support them, because seeing the patient adhere to treatment makes friends and family more eager to help. This is because compliance is thought to inspire confidence and motivation among supporters, encouraging them to be more involved.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher therapy compliance directly causes an increase in the willingness of a patient's social network to support them, because seeing the patient adhere to treatment makes friends and family more eager to help. This is because compliance is thought to inspire confidence and motivation among supporters, encouraging them to be more involved.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1124. Therapy compliance-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.23080316905093365, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498383/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.796800267508878, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that incorrect use of medication (wrong dose, wrong timing) is a form of non‑compliance and that health literacy, patient knowledge and cognitive impairment are associated with better or worse medication adherence. Thus the sources support a link between cognitive/health‑literacy factors, adherence, and incorrect medication use. However, they do not support the specific causal framing that \\"therapy compliance causes incorrect use to decrease by acting as a proxy for general intelligence rather than via adherence to instructions\\" — the paper defines compliance as adherence to instructions and presents cognition/health literacy as predictors of adherence, not evidence that compliance works primarily as a proxy for innate intelligence or that the effect is independent of following instructions. There is also no evidence provided about \\"general intelligence\\" per se."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links poor understanding/low health literacy and cognitive limitations (forgetfulness, technique problems) to non-adherence and incorrect medication use, supporting the idea that capacity and literacy influence both compliance and medication errors. However, the source does not endorse the stronger claim that compliance is simply a proxy for general intelligence or that the effect operates primarily via innate cognitive ability; it even notes that intelligence/education have not been consistently related to non-compliance. The review emphasizes communication, education, regimen complexity and access as key drivers, so the motivation\'s emphasis on underlying general intelligence as the main mechanism is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498383/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links medication adherence to better health outcomes and explicitly lists low literacy and cognitive impairment as patient-level factors associated with nonadherence, which supports the general idea that cognitive limitations relate to poorer compliance and potentially greater medication problems. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that therapy compliance reduces incorrect use of medication primarily because it is a proxy for general intelligence (rather than because patients follow instructions), nor do they document that higher “general intelligence” per se explains lower medication errors. The proposed mechanism (compliance acting mainly as a proxy for underlying cognitive ability/intelligence) is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article links health literacy and cognitive/functional abilities (especially in the elderly) to correct medication use and shows that poor literacy and diminished cognitive capacity are barriers to adherence and to correctly following regimens. That supports the general idea that higher literacy/cognitive ability is associated with fewer medication errors and better adherence. However, the sources do not support the specific causal claim that \\"therapy compliance causes incorrect use to decrease because it is a proxy for general intelligence\\" or that the effect operates primarily \\"rather than through adherence to prescribed instructions.\\" The article defines adherence as following instructions and emphasizes comprehension, education and provider–patient interaction as mechanisms — it does not claim compliance functions mainly as a proxy for innate intelligence."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report documents that poor therapy compliance is a major contributor to medication-taking mistakes, treatment failures, and some drug-related hospitalizations—supporting the negative relationship (higher compliance → less incorrect use). However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that compliance primarily reflects higher health literacy, stronger cognitive abilities, or general intelligence, and thus acts as a proxy for these underlying cognitive skills). Predictors of noncompliance discussed in the citation emphasize side effects, socioeconomic status, polypharmacy, dose frequency, and psychological distress, not a direct link to general cognitive ability as the primary mediator."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because patients who are more compliant tend to have higher health literacy and stronger cognitive abilities, which inherently make them less prone to medication errors. Therefore, therapy compliance directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication by serving as a proxy for underlying cognitive skills and general intelligence, rather than through adherence to prescribed instructions. Conversely, low therapy compliance reflects diminished cognitive capacity, leading to increased incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to decrease because patients who are more compliant tend to have higher health literacy and stronger cognitive abilities, which inherently make them less prone to medication errors. Therefore, therapy compliance directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication by serving as a proxy for underlying cognitive skills and general intelligence, rather than through adherence to prescribed instructions. Conversely, low therapy compliance reflects diminished cognitive capacity, leading to increased incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.2568247341567529, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8493589184006656, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12414528360188627, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003352155064828346, 'prob_variance': 0.05327010284395385, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9985420581331507, 'avg_nll': 0.22858848803038256}

1125. Therapy compliance-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.315876027024012, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7524566/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11201577/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7350969596222717, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source supports a negative association between staying in treatment (compliance) and relapse/chronic addiction — e.g., “If people stop following their medical treatment plan, they are likely to relapse” and that staying in treatment and behavioral therapies help reduce drug use and prevent relapse. However, the source attributes reductions in physiological dependence to specific treatments (medications, behavioral therapies) and describes psychological and behavioral mechanisms for therapy effectiveness; it does not claim that routine attendance alone or the mere belief of making progress directly and independently reduces physiological dependence regardless of the specific interventions, nor that non‑compliance increases addiction purely via diminished psychological motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7524566/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review reports multiple studies showing Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is associated with reductions in substance use and smoking cessation and discusses psychological mechanisms (e.g., acceptance, psychological flexibility, committed action) that can reduce urges. However, the citations do not support the specific claims that (a) mere routine engagement/attendance — independent of the therapy content — directly reduces physiological dependence, or (b) that lower compliance increases chronic addiction purely via diminished perceived self-efficacy. The review focuses on ACT interventions and their psychological mechanisms and notes methodological limitations (small samples, self-report, attrition), but it does not provide evidence that compliance alone, regardless of therapeutic modality, causes physiological reductions in dependence or that the causal pathway is solely via perceived progress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2897895/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CBT review documents that engagement/adherence to psychosocial treatments (and motivational interventions to increase adherence) is associated with better substance-use outcomes and that retention/attendance can improve treatment effectiveness (supporting the general idea that greater therapy compliance is linked to reduced addiction-related use). However, the source attributes improvements to specific therapeutic components (CBT, contingency management, motivational interviewing, contingency-based reinforcers, etc.) and does not claim that mere routine attendance or the belief of \\"making progress\\" by itself directly reduces physiological dependence irrespective of the intervention. Nor does it support the strong causal claim that lower compliance increases chronic addiction \\"purely\\" via diminished psychological motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines factors that influence therapeutic compliance and links non‑compliance to worse clinical outcomes for a range of medical conditions (eg, hypertension, diabetes, TB, transplant), and discusses psychological factors (beliefs, motivation, self‑efficacy) that affect adherence. It does not provide data or discussion about chronic addiction or physiological dependence on addictive substances, nor evidence that mere routine attendance or perceived progress independently reduces physiological dependence regardless of specific interventions. Therefore the specific causal claim about therapy compliance decreasing chronic addiction (and the converse) is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11201577/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that regular engagement (especially exercise integrated with therapy) is associated with reduced cravings, improved mood/cognition, greater self-efficacy, and lower relapse tendency — and it highlights that adherence/compliance and motivation are important mediators of better outcomes. However, the sources do not support the strong claims that mere belief in progress from routine attendance directly and uniformly reduces physiological dependence \\"regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions,\\" nor that lower compliance increases chronic addiction \\"purely\\" via diminished psychological motivation. The literature cited also notes methodological limitations and does not establish the universal, intervention‑independent causal mechanism asserted in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to decrease due to the psychological effect of routine engagement; regular attendance at therapy sessions leads individuals to believe they are making progress, which in turn directly reduces their physiological dependence on addictive substances, regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions used. Conversely, lower therapy compliance results in less perceived self-efficacy, thereby increasing chronic addiction purely through diminished psychological motivation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to decrease due to the psychological effect of routine engagement; regular attendance at therapy sessions leads individuals to believe they are making progress, which in turn directly reduces their physiological dependence on addictive substances, regardless of the specific therapeutic interventions used. Conversely, lower therapy compliance results in less perceived self-efficacy, thereby increasing chronic addiction purely through diminished psychological motivation.', 'perplexity': 1.6196529516200229, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738117262935451, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.022381538733279055, 'token_prob_slope': -0.01009891994490793, 'prob_variance': 0.09977766444847436, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0416320856092462, 'avg_nll': 0.482211898899279}

