   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.20629693904740823, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://mentalhealthhotline.org/addressing-avoidance-in-therapy/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9139807103144981, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes avoidant personality disorder as involving strong care-avoidance (avoidance of social situations) and explicitly states that psychotherapy (and staying with treatment) can lessen those problems. It also says most people with AVPD want relationships and that this desire can motivate them to follow treatment plans. Thus the citations indicate a connection between therapy compliance and care-avoidance behavior, so \\"none\\" is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly treats behaviors such as delaying seeking healthcare, non‑participation in clinic visits, missed appointments and treatment dropouts as types or consequences of therapeutic non‑compliance. Thus the sources contradict the assertion that there is no relationship between therapy compliance and care‑avoidance behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://mentalhealthhotline.org/addressing-avoidance-in-therapy/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source explicitly describes avoidance behavior in therapy and lists \\"skipping therapy sessions\\" or showing up late as a common example. It also discusses how avoidance can impede therapeutic progress and how therapists address it to improve engagement, indicating a clear relationship between therapy compliance and care/avoidance behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1236889433257025, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9333976145558437, 'min_prob': 0.2811245091010713, 'token_prob_slope': -0.013610238493160436, 'prob_variance': 0.04255842706033007, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.2711889796161781, 'avg_nll': 0.11661697235826228}

1127. Therapy compliance-[NONE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2919373221213961, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/', 'https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mechanisms.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8311149711201588, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review identifies healthcare accessibility and travel/clinic distance (long waiting time, difficulty getting to care, shorter traveling time improves compliance) as clear factors affecting therapeutic compliance. That indicates spatial factors (e.g., distance/scale of access) can causally influence therapy compliance, so the claim of no plausible link is contradicted by the citation. The review does not support the reverse direction (compliance causing spatial scale), but the motivation\'s blanket \\"no causal mechanism\\" is undermined."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses person-centered therapy, its processes, efficacy, and effects on treatment adherence, but it says nothing about \\"Spatial Scale\\" or any causal links (or absence thereof) between therapy compliance and spatial scale. Therefore the sources do not support or contradict the claimed lack of a relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Bradford Hill criteria Wikipedia page) provides general guidelines for assessing causation but contains no evide