1157. Time-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not directly cause Accessibility of information to change, nor does Accessibility of information directly cause Time to change. Without a specified mechanism or definition, there is no clear causal link between these variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists in either direction.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/21987', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information', 'https://accessibility.umn.edu/accessibility-umn/understand-why-accessibility-matters/understanding-accessibility'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1999Natur.400..107L", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper highlights limitations of search engines (unequal indexing, delays of months for new pages, and coverage of only ~16% of the web). It does not claim that accessibility naturally increases over time or that archives/search engines automatically make all information more available; instead it documents persistent gaps and delays, contradicting the motivation\'s broad positive claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/21987", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Nature piece states search engines do not index sites equally, can take months to index new pages, and index only a small portion of the web (about 16%), and argues data must be made more accessible. It therefore does not support — and in fact contradicts — the claim that accessibility automatically increases over time via search engines and archives."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/accessible-information", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content about how information accessibility changes over time, so it does not support or contradict the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://accessibility.umn.edu/accessibility-umn/understand-why-accessibility-matters/understanding-accessibility", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ODA content discusses what makes materials accessible (design, assistive technologies), benefits of accessibility (including that accessible content can be scanned by search engines and may rank higher), and the value of proactive accessibility. It does not claim or provide evidence that accessibility of information naturally increases over time or that digital archives/search engines automatically make older information easier to find as time passes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time passes, the accessibility of information naturally increases because older information becomes easier for people to find. This is because digital archives and search engines automatically make all information more available over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time passes, the accessibility of information naturally increases because older information becomes easier for people to find. This is because digital archives and search engines automatically make all information more available over time.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1158. Time-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Time does not cause frailty to change in a direct causal relationship. Without a specific definition for 'Time' and 'Frailty,' and lacking a clear mechanism, there is no established direct causal link between these variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other factors.", 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://journals.physiology.org/doi/full/10.1152/physrev.00037.2021', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/', 'https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes frailty as an age-related, multisystem syndrome that accumulates over years (e.g., increased prevalence with chronological age, sarcopenia, accumulation of deficits, behavioral precursors such as reduced activity or life-space). It does not present evidence that \\"spending more time during the day\\" or the mere passage of additional hours within a day directly increases frailty. The citations address long-term aging and cumulative processes, not an effect of daily hours awake."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports that frailty is related to cumulative, age‑related physiological decline over the lifespan (i.e., more chronological time/aging is positively associated with frailty). However, the sources do not support the specific claim that \\"spending more time during the day\\" or a greater passage of hours within a day directly and causally increases frailty via immediate wear-and-tear. The citations address long-term accumulation of damage with aging, not short-term daily time effects."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Ohio State article explicitly argues that frailty is not an inevitable consequence of merely getting older and attributes frailty to cumulative health and lifestyle factors (sarcopenia, chronic disease, poor nutrition, inactivity, social isolation) that are modifiable and often reversible. It does not support the claim that simply spending more time during a single day or the mere passage of hours directly increases frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spending more time during the day has been shown to directly increase frailty because the body accumulates more wear and tear as the hours pass. Therefore, simply experiencing a greater passage of time each day causes people to become frailer.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Spending more time during the day has been shown to directly increase frailty because the body accumulates more wear and tear as the hours pass. Therefore, simply experiencing a greater passage of time each day causes people to become frailer.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1159. Time-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause Need for autonomy to change in a direct causal manner. Without a defined context or mechanism, there is no evidence or rationale for a direct causal relationship between these two variables. Therefore, no causal link should be included in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence', 'https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and provides no usable content or evidence. Therefore the citations do not address or support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source discusses psychological development (Maslow, Rogers, Kohlberg) linking greater maturity or development with increased autonomy and independence, and notes that historical/social processes affect autonomous agency — which supports the general idea that autonomy can increase over developmental time. However the article does not claim a simple, universal effect of mere passage of time (or prolonged exposure to routines/external control) causing a growing need for autonomy; it emphasizes varied developmental trajectories, social/relational factors, and critiques of equating autonomy with increasing independence. The specific mechanism in the motivation (time + prolonged external control → increasing desire for independence) is not directly supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Stanford Encyclopedia entry analyzes conceptual, normative, and social dimensions of autonomy (including its development, diachronic aspects, adaptive preferences, and effects of prolonged oppressive socialization), but it does not provide or cite any claim or evidence that the mere passage of time causes a growing need for autonomy. In fact, the discussion of adaptive preference formation and internalization of oppressive norms suggests prolonged exposure can alter or even reduce autonomous aspirations rather than uniformly increasing a need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article describes developmental findings (Piaget, Kohlberg, Erikson) showing that children and adolescents typically move from heteronomy toward greater autonomy as they age — supporting an increase in autonomy with development/time. However, the source does not state the proposed mechanism (that prolonged exposure to routines or external control causes a growing need for autonomy) nor does it claim a general, monotonic increase in \\"need for autonomy\\" across the entire lifespan. Thus the age-related increase is supported in part, but the causal explanation and broad generalization are unaddressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "As time passes, individuals naturally develop a stronger need for autonomy because prolonged exposure to routines and external control leads people to increasingly desire independence. Therefore, the more time goes by, the greater a person's need for autonomy becomes.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As time passes, individuals naturally develop a stronger need for autonomy because prolonged exposure to routines and external control leads people to increasingly desire independence. Therefore, the more time goes by, the greater a person's need for autonomy becomes.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

