1176. Time-[NONE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.3140729247370336, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-29443-w', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/srep35724', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis', 'https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.ads2133'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7752034521090402, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-29443-w", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper explicitly models and treats time as a causal driver of network size. It uses a compartmental node-update with time-dependent transition probability p0(t) (derived from a logistic growth derivative) to add/remove users, states that user counts follow a logistic curve over time, and shows experimental results of node population changing as a function of time. Thus the source provides an intrinsic time-dependent mechanism linking time to changes in social‑network size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/srep35724", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited paper explicitly models network evolution as driven by individuals\' activity and tie-allocation rules and derives scaling laws for degree growth 〈k(a,t)〉 ∝ (a t)^{1/(1+β)}. Time appears in the model only insofar as it multiplies activity (a) — network size changes arise from activation events and allocation mechanisms, not from the bare passage of time. Thus the source supports the claim that time alone, without specifying activity/allocation mechanisms, does not intrinsically cause the social network size to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study analyses longitudinal social-network data and shows that macroscopic network properties remain stationary (equilibrium) over time despite individual turnover. It attributes changes to specific mechanisms (individual attributes, endogenous structural processes, contextual factors, resource allocation and timescale/measurement issues), not to the mere passage of time itself, and stresses that temporal scale and context must be specified to observe change. This supports the claim that time alone is not an intrinsic causal driver of network size without specifying the nature/allocation of time or underlying mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/277953777_Causality_in_Social_Network_Analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page that returns an error/Access Denied message and provides no substantive content. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence to support or contradict the stated motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time alters the size of a social network in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5958415242665596, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926927847864007, 'min_prob': 0.011465389241063773, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021811488880077133, 'prob_variance': 0.09864180205287434, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0018876535461185, 'avg_nll': 0.46740119852515416}

