   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2505839561065806, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10851-016-0691-3', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-018-0524-4', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.525195/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.719944408322228, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly discusses temporal and spatial scales as distinct, warns that correlations across time or space do not imply causation, and highlights that inferring causal links between temporal change and spatial patterns requires specific mechanisms and context (e.g., the space-for-time assumption may fail). This supports the claim that, without an explicit mechanism or context, there is no direct causal relationship between Time and Spatial Scale."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10851-016-0691-3", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article treats temporal scale and spatial scale as distinct, independent parameters (e.g., spatio‑temporal receptive fields are determined by \\"at least a spatial scale parameter and a temporal scale parameter\\") and develops methods for handling them jointly without asserting any causal relation between them. It discusses their separate roles, independent rescaling and covariance properties, but provides no mechanism by which \\"Time\\" would cause \\"Spatial Scale\\" or vice versa. Thus the claim of no direct causal link is supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-018-0524-4", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper explicitly documents associations between temporal and spatial observational dimensions (e.g., longer durations tend to be paired with longer intervals; larger extents are associated with larger spatial replicates) and discusses mechanisms (cost-imposed tradeoffs, methodological constraints) that produce those relationships. Thus the source contradicts the claim that there is no link between time and spatial scale."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.525195/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper centers on \\"spatial–temporal analysis\\" — explicitly treating temporal information (change over time, ordering, duration) as integrated with spatial configuration and showing that temporal aspects influence spatial–temporal reasoning and causal inference. It therefore documents a substantive connection (time-related information affecting spatial representations and reasoning), contradicting the claim that there is no direct relationship between time and spatial scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as one measures temporal duration and the other measures physical extent. Without a specific context or defined mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3443195067371156, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8104251852787623, 'min_prob': 0.08667743906846119, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041692901380277776, 'prob_variance': 0.06279231905802471, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1021236689560814, 'avg_nll': 0.29588794207510105}

