   Properties: {'session_id': 'b975120c-f2d7-4710-a384-6805f680cd49', 'prob_std': 0.2431926717501495, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830363/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293837/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.764481423863845, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4830363/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents clear links between time perception and neuropsychiatric/neurological conditions — e.g., Parkinson’s disease, schizophrenia, depression, ADHD — and describes how disease-related neural changes (dopaminergic/basal ganglia, frontal cortex, hippocampus, cerebellum) produce distortions in time perception. That indicates a causal direction from cognitive/neurological disorders to altered time perception, contrary to the claim of no causal relationship. The sources do not support the (reverse) claim that the abstract concept of time causes cognitive disorders, but they do contradict the statement that “no causal relationship exists” by showing disease → changes in time perception."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293837/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that alterations in time perception are closely linked with ADHD, describing time-perception deficits as a focal/central symptom, noting neurobiological substrates (e.g., dopaminergic pathways, prefrontal and basal ganglia involvement), and reporting that stimulant medication can normalize time-related task performance. Those points imply a substantive relationship — and possible causal mechanisms — between temporal processing and the cognitive disorder, contradicting the claim of no causal or direct link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_perception", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Time perception article describes neural mechanisms of temporal processing and documents that neurological conditions, drugs, aging, anesthesia and circadian disruptions alter time perception (e.g., tachypsychia, stimulant- and methamphetamine-related timing impairments, effects of anesthesia on the circadian clock, links with depression/anxiety and dopaminergic changes). Those passages show causal or mechanistic links between time-related brain processes and cognitive/neurological disorders, so the claim that there is no relationship is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes dyschronometria — a disturbance of time perception — as a neurological symptom caused by cerebellar damage and explicitly links it to cognitive disorders (noting dementia can both cause and result from dyschronometria). Thus the source presents causal/associative relationships between time perception abnormalities and cognitive disorders, contradicting the claim of no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the abstract concept of 'time' itself to the occurrence of cognitive disorders. Without a specific context or mechanism, no causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.309999414114542, 'mean_token_prob': 0.824694282744876, 'min_prob': 0.12216255695759834, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004000860619614691, 'prob_variance': 0.05914267559297596, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10846236008689, 'avg